hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:
"8037","(8034) And it was narrated by 'Abd ar-Rahmad ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from 'Atā' ibn Yasār, from Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Three things do not break the fast of the one who is fasting: vomiting, wet dreams, and cupping."" And 'Abd ar-Rahmān is weak (in narration).

Abū al-Hasan — 'Alī ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān — informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid as-Saffār informed us; 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; Abū al-Jamāhir narrated to us; 'Abd ar-Rahmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

The preserved narration from Zayd ibn Aslam is the first one. [Munkar. See its predecessor.]

---"
"8038","(8035) As evidence, that which was narrated to us by Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Hāfiz; Abū Bakr — Ahmad ibn Sulmān al-Faqīh — informed us; 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad ar-Raqāshī narrated to us; Abū 'Āsim narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Abī 'Ubayd narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwā', that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a man from the tribe of Aslam to his people on the day of 'Āshūrā' and said: ""Command them to fast this day."" The man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I do not think I will reach them until they have already eaten."" He said: ""Whoever among them has eaten, let him fast the remainder of his day.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Sahīh from Abū 'Āsim, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Yazīd.

And it has been narrated in the hadith that he commanded making up (the fast), and that is in what... [Sahīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]

---"
"8039","(8036) Abū al-Hasan — 'Alī ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Minhāl narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zurai' narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatādah, from 'Abd ar-Rahmān ibn Muslimah, from his paternal uncle, that the tribe of Aslam came to the Prophet (ﷺ) on the day of 'Āshūrā' and he said: ""Have you fasted this day of yours?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Then complete the remainder of your day (in fasting) and make it up.""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from Muhammad ibn al-Minhāl.

And likewise Abū Qilābah narrated it from Muhammad ibn al-Minhāl, from Yazīd, from Shu'bah.

And that occurred in some copies as ""Sa'īd.""

And Sa'īd also narrated it, but he contradicted Shu'bah in both the chain (isnad) and the text (matn). [Munkar. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]

---"
"8040","(8037) Abū 'Abd ar-Rahmān as-Sulamī and Abū al-Qāsim 'Abd ar-Rahmān ibn Muhammad as-Sarrāj narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdūs at-Tarā'ifī informed us; 'Uthmān ibn Sa'īd ad-Dārimī narrated to us; Sa'īd ibn Abī Marwān narrated to us; Yahyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, from 'Abd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from his father, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever does not intend to fast during the night, there is no fast for him."" [Sahīh. Its grading has been given previously.]

---"
"8041","(8038) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Hāfiz and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr said: Abū al-'Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Asīd ibn 'Āsim narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Sufyān; al-A'mash and al-Hasan ibn 'Ubayd Allāh narrated to me, from Abū ad-Duhā, that a man said to Ibn 'Abbās: ""When should I stop eating the pre-dawn meal (suhūr)?"" A man said: ""When you become doubtful."" Ibn 'Abbās said: ""Keep eating as long as you are doubtful, until it becomes clear to you."" [Sahīh. Narrated by 'Abd ar-Razzāq.]

---"
"8042","(8039) Abū Tāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū 'Uthmān al-Basrī narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Ya'lā ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Habīb ibn Abī Thābit, who said: ""Ibn 'Abbās sent two men to look for the dawn. One of them said: 'The dawn has broken,' and the other said: 'No.' He said: 'You two have disagreed; bring me my drink.'""

And something similar has been narrated in this regard from Abū Bakr as-Siddīq, 'Umar, and Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them). [Da'īf.]

---"
"8043","(8040) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Hāfiz narrated to us; Abū 'Abd Allāh Muhammad ibn Ya'qūb al-Hāfiz narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn 'Ubayd Allāh narrated to us; Yahyā ibn Yahyā narrated to us; Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from az-Zuhrī, from Humayd ibn 'Abd ar-Rahmān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'I am ruined, O Messenger of Allah!' He said: 'What has ruined you?' He said: 'I had intercourse with my wife during Ramadān.' He said: 'Can you find (the means) to free a slave?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Can you fast two consecutive months?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Can you find (the means) to feed sixty poor people?' He said: 'No.' Then he sat down, and the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a 'raq (large basket) containing dates. He said: 'Give this in charity.' He said: 'Is there anyone more in need than us? There is no household between its two sides more in need of it than us.' The Prophet (ﷺ) smiled until his front teeth became visible, then he said to him: 'Go and feed it to your family.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Sahīh from 'Alī ibn 'Abd Allāh from Sufyān, and Muslim narrated it from Yahyā ibn Yahyā. [Sahīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]

---"
"8044","(8041) Abū al-Hasan — 'Alī ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Isḥāq al-Isfara'inī informed us; Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb al-Qādī narrated to us; Abū ar-Rabī' narrated to us; Jarīr ibn 'Abd al-Hamīd narrated to us, from Mansūr, from Muhammad ibn Muslim az-Zuhrī, from Humayd ibn 'Abd ar-Rahmān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'So-and-so had intercourse with his wife during Ramadān.' He said to him: 'Can you find (the means) to free a slave?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Can you fast two consecutive months?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Can you find (the means) to feed sixty poor people?' He said: 'No.' Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a 'raq containing dates — and it was a large basket (zambīl). He said: 'Feed this on your behalf.' He said: 'There is no household between its two sides more in need than us.' He said: 'Feed it to your family.'"" Muhammad ibn Muslim said: ""This was a concession specifically for this person. Whoever experiences what he experienced should do as he was commanded.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Sahīh from 'Uthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Jarīr. [Sahīh. See its predecessor.]

---"
"8045","(8042) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Hāfiz, Abū Dharr ibn Abī al-Husayn ibn Abī al-Qāsim al-Mudhakkir, and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr as-Sayrafī narrated to us, saying: Abū 'Abd Allāh Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allāh as-Saffār al-Isbahānī narrated to us; Abū Yahyā Ahmad ibn 'Isām ibn 'Abd al-Majīd al-Ansārī al-Isbahānī narrated to us; Mu'mal ibn Ismā'īl narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Mansūr, from az-Zuhrī, from Humayd ibn 'Abd ar-Rahmān, from Abū Hurayrah, that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had intercourse with my wife during Ramadān."" He said: ""Free a slave."" He said: ""I cannot find one."" He said: ""Fast two consecutive months."" He said: ""I am not able."" He said: ""Feed sixty poor people."" He said: ""I cannot find (the means)."" Then the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a miktal containing fifteen sā's of dates. He said: ""Take it and feed it on your behalf."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, there is no household between its two sides more in need of it than us."" He said: ""Take it and feed it to your family.""

And likewise Ibrāhīm ibn Tahmān narrated it from Mansūr ibn al-Mu'tamir, and he said in the hadith: ""A miktal containing fifteen sā's of dates,"" and he mentioned it.

And al-Awzā'ī and Muhammad ibn Abī Hafsah narrated it from az-Zuhrī likewise. And Hishām ibn Sa'd narrated it from az-Zuhrī from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah, similar to it.

And 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated it from al-Awzā'ī from az-Zuhrī, and he attributed this estimation to 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb. What seems most likely is that the estimation of the miktal as fifteen sā's is from the narration of az-Zuhrī from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb. And Allah knows best.

---"
"8046","(8043) 'Alī ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid as-Saffār informed us; 'Ubayd ibn Sharīk and Ibn Milhān said: Yahyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us.

(And) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Hāfiz informed me; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn 'Alī narrated to us; Yahyā ibn Yahyā narrated to us; al-Layth informed us.

(And) Abū 'Abd Allāh informed me; Abū al-Fadl Muhammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'īd narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Humayd ibn 'Abd ar-Rahmān ibn 'Awf, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that a man had intercourse with his wife during Ramadān and sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding that. He said: ""Can you find a slave (to free)?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you fast two months?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then feed sixty poor people."" Ibn Bukayr added in his narration: ""Consecutive.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Yahyā ibn Yahyā. [Sahīh. See its predecessor.]"
"8047","It was narrated that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: ""I have been ruined."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I have had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do you have a slave girl?"" He said: ""No, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Can you afford to fast two consecutive months?"" He said: ""No, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Can you afford to feed sixty poor persons?"" He said: ""I cannot find [the means]."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a tray of dates, and he said: ""Go and give this in charity."" He said: ""On those more needy than I? By Him who sent you with the truth, there is no one between these two mountains more in need of it than we are."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled until his molars became visible, then said: ""Go and feed your family with it."" 
It was narrated by Muslim from the hadith of Abd al-Razzaq, and it was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from the hadith of Abd al-Wahhab from Ma'mar, and most of the narrators of al-Zuhri narrated it, such as Ibrahim ibn Sa'd and Uqail ibn Khalid and others. 
And it was narrated by Ibn al-Athir from al-Zuhri. [Sahih. See his introduction.]"
"8048","It was narrated that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: ""I have been ruined."" They asked him: ""What has happened to you?"" He said: ""I have had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan."" A tray of dates, known as al-'arq, was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""Where is the ruined one?"" The man stood up and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Give this in charity."" 
It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Munir from Yazid ibn Harun, and it was narrated by Muslim from the hadith of Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi and Layth ibn Sa'd from Yahya ibn Sa'id. [Sahih.]"
"8049","It was narrated that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting under the shade of a mountain when a man from Banu Bayadhah came and said: ""I have been ruined; I have had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Free a slave."" He said: ""I cannot afford that."" He said: ""Then fast two consecutive months."" He said: ""I cannot do that."" He said: ""Then feed sixty poor persons."" He said: ""I do not have that."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a tray of dates, containing twenty sa' (a measure of volume), and he said: ""Give this in charity."" He said: ""We do not have food for tonight."" He said: ""Then take it and feed your family."" 
The Sheikh said: The additional details in this narration indicate the soundness of the memory of Abu Hurairah and those who narrated from him, and his statement of twenty sa' is eloquent, as narrated by Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr. 
It was narrated by Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Yasar from Muhammad ibn Ja'far, with some additions and omissions, and at the end he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far said: ""I was later told that this charity was twenty sa' of dates, and it has been narrated in the hadith of Abu Hurairah that it was fifteen sa', which is more authentic."" [Hasan. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8050","It was narrated that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have been ruined."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I have had intercourse with my wife while I was fasting."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do you have a slave girl you can free?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you fast two consecutive months?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you feed sixty poor persons?"" He said: ""No."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent. Abu Hurairah said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a tray of dates, known as al-'arq, was brought, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Where is the man who asked [for guidance] just now? Take this date and give it in charity."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is there anyone more needy than my family?"" By Allah, there is no one between these two mountains more in need than my family. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled until his molars became visible, then said: ""Feed them."" 
It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from the hadith of Abu al-Yaman. [Sahih. This is the wording of al-Bukhari.]"
"8051","It was narrated that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have been ruined."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Woe to you! What is the matter?"" He said: ""I have had intercourse with my wife in a day of Ramadan."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Free a slave."" He said: ""I cannot afford that."" He said: ""Then fast two consecutive months."" He said: ""I cannot do that."" He said: ""Then feed sixty poor persons."" He said: ""I cannot afford that."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a tray of dates, containing fifteen sa', and he said: ""Take it and give it in charity."" He said: ""On those more needy than I? By Allah, there is no one between the two mountains of Medina more in need than my family."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled until his molars became visible, then said: ""Take it and ask forgiveness from Allah and feed your family."" 
It was also narrated by Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak, al-Hakim ibn Ziyad, and Masrur ibn Sadak from al-Awzai, except that ibn al-Mubarak attributed the statement ""fifteen sa'"" to the narration of Amr ibn Shu'ayb, while al-Hakim and Masrur included it in the hadith as narrated by Duhaym from al-Walid, as narrated by al-Bukhari. [Sahih. Narrated by Ahmad.]"
"8052","It was narrated that Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim reported that Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr reported that he heard Abbad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam report that he heard Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) narrate: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: ""I have been ruined."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked: ""What is the matter?"" He said: ""I have had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan while fasting."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Free a slave."" He said: ""I cannot afford that."" He said: ""Then fast two consecutive months."" He said: ""I cannot do that."" He said: ""Then feed sixty poor persons."" He said: ""I cannot afford that."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a tray of dates, and he said: ""Take it and give it in charity."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I do not know anyone more in need than I am."" By Allah, there is no one between these two mountains more in need than my family. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled until his molars became visible, then said: ""Take it and ask forgiveness from Allah and feed your family."" 
It was also narrated by Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yasar from Muhammad ibn Ja'far, with some additions and omissions, and at the end he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far said: ""I was later told that this charity was twenty sa' of dates, and it has been narrated in the hadith of Abu Hurairah that it was fifteen sa', which is more authentic."" [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8053","It was narrated that Sa'id ibn Hashim ibn Marthad narrated: Duhaym narrated: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated: al-Awzai narrated to me: al-Zuhri narrated to me: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me: Abu Hurairah said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have been ruined."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Woe to you! What is the matter?"" He said: ""I have had intercourse with my wife while I was fasting in Ramadan."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do you have a slave girl you can free?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you fast two consecutive months?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you feed sixty poor persons?"" He said: ""No."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent. Abu Hurairah said: While we were in that state, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a tray of dates, and he said: ""Where is the man who asked [for guidance] just now? Take this and give it in charity."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is there anyone more needy than my family?"" By Allah, there is no one between these two mountains more in need than we are. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled until his molars became visible, then said: ""Feed them."" 
It was also narrated by Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Tha'ib, Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir, Nu'man ibn Rashid, Abd al-Rahman ibn Namir, Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, and others from al-Zuhri, and the narrations of this group agree with the narration of those mentioned earlier in this chapter, that the man's intention was to break his fast collectively, and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the expiation according to the wording that necessitates the sequence. 
It was also narrated from Aisha bint al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade deliberate fasting in Ramadan during the day. [Sahih. Meaning is close.]"
"8054","It was narrated that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab reported: A Bedouin man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his hair disheveled, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have come to my family in Ramadan."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to perform the expiation of the afternoon prayer. 
It was also narrated by Shu'bah from Ibrahim ibn Amr al-Qurashi. [Hasan. Narrated by Ahmad.]"
"8055","It was narrated that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: A man broke his fast in the month of Ramadan, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to free a slave, or to fast two consecutive months, or to feed sixty poor persons. He said: ""I cannot afford any of these."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a tray of dates, and he said: ""Take this and give it in charity."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I do not know anyone more in need than I am."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled until his molars became visible, then said: ""Eat it."" 
It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from the hadith of Muhammad ibn Rafi' from Ishaq ibn Isa from Malik, and it was also narrated by Ibn Jurayj with a similar meaning. [Sahih. Narrated by Malik.]"
"8056","It was narrated that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded a man who broke his fast in the month of Ramadan to free a slave, or to fast two consecutive months, or to feed sixty poor persons. 
It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from the hadith of Muhammad ibn Rafi', and it was narrated by Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari from al-Zuhri, and the narration of the group from al-Zuhri is considered more authentic, as it is closer to the wording of the religious scholar, and it is more likely to be accepted due to their sound memory and correct narration. It was also narrated by Hammad ibn Mas'adah from Malik from al-Zuhri in a manner similar to the narration of the group. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8057","(8054) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed me, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah narrated to us, from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said regarding a man who had intercourse with his wife during Ramadan: ""Free a slave."" The man said: ""I cannot find one."" He said: ""Then fast for two months."" He said: ""I am not able."" He said: ""Then feed sixty poor persons."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been narrated extensively.]"
"8058","(8055) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Abū Marwān narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, and he said: al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me, from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥumayd, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: ""Make up for one day in its place.""

And similarly it was narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī, from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd. Ibrāhīm heard the hadith from al-Zuhrī but did not mention this wording from him; rather, he mentioned it from al-Layth ibn Saʿd, from al-Zuhrī.

It was also narrated by Abū Uways al-Madanī, from al-Zuhrī. [Ḥasan due to other narrations.]"
"8059","(8056) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us, Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us, my father informed me that Muḥammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihāb informed him, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that Abū Hurayrah narrated to him: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded the one who broke his fast in Ramadan to fast one day in its place.

It was also narrated by ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn ʿUmar al-Aylī, from al-Zuhrī, and he is not strong. [Ḥasan due to other narrations. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8060","(8057) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn ʿUmar informed us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), plucking out the hair of his head and beating his chest, saying: ""The most wretched of people is doomed!"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What has caused your ruin?"" He said: ""I had intercourse with my wife today, and that was during Ramadan."" He said: ""Do you have a slave whom you can free?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you fast for two consecutive months?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you feed sixty poor persons?"" He said: ""No."" Then the man departed, and a man from among the people came with a large basket containing the charity of his wealth. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Where is the questioner?"" They said: ""He has departed."" He said: ""Bring him to me."" So the man was brought, and he said: ""Take this and give it in charity as expiation for what you have done."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, upon one who is more in need than I and my household? By Him in whose Hand is my soul, there is no one between its two mountain passes more in need than I and my household."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) laughed until his premolars became visible, and he said: ""Then eat it and feed your household, and make up for one day in its place."" [Ḥasan due to other narrations. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8061","(8058) He said: and Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn ʿUmar informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī informed me, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar narration.

And that was also narrated in the hadith of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather."
"8062","(8059) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Maslamah narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us, al-Ḥajjaj ibn Arṭāh narrated to us, from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿĀmir, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, and from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We were in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when a man came to him, plucking out his hair and calling destruction upon himself. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Woe to you! What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""Indeed, the wretched one has fallen upon his wife during Ramadan."" He said to him: ""Free a slave."" He said: ""I cannot find one."" He said: ""Then fast for two consecutive months."" He said: ""I am not able."" He said: ""Then feed sixty poor persons."" He said: ""I cannot find the means."" Then the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a basket containing fifteen ṣāʿ of dates, and he said: ""Take this and feed sixty poor persons with it."" He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, there is no household between its two mountain passes more in need of it than us."" He said: ""Then you and your family eat it."" [Ḥasan due to other narrations. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8063","(8060) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar narration to the hadith of al-Zuhrī from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Hurayrah, the hadith of the one who had intercourse. And ʿAmr added in it: ""And he commanded him to make up for one day in its place."" [Ḥasan due to other narrations. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]

It was also narrated by Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib, from Yazīd ibn Hārūn, and he said: ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb added in his hadith: ""He commanded him to fast one day in its place.""

It was also narrated by Hishām ibn Saʿd, from al-Zuhrī, except that he differed from the group in its chain of narration, saying: from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah."
"8064","(8061) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, they both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad al-Ṣaffār al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr al-Zubayrī al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and had intercourse with his wife during Ramadan. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Free a slave."" He said: ""I cannot find one."" He said: ""Fast for two consecutive months."" He said: ""I am not capable of that."" He said: ""Feed sixty poor persons."" He said: ""I cannot find the means."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a basket containing fifteen ṣāʿ, and he said: ""Take this and give it in charity."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I do not find anyone more in need of this than I and my household."" He said: ""Then you and your household eat it, and fast one day in its place, and seek forgiveness from Allah."" And similarly, a group narrated it from Hishām ibn Saʿd.

And that was narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from the Prophet (ﷺ), as a mursal narration. [Munkar in its chain. Al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8065","(8062) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us, from ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, who said: A Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), plucking out his hair and beating his chest, saying: ""The most wretched of people is doomed!"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What is the matter?"" He said: ""I had intercourse with my wife during Ramadan while I was fasting."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: ""Can you free a slave?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Can you offer a sacrificial camel (badanah)?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then sit down."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a basket of dates, and he said: ""Take this and give it in charity."" He said: ""I do not find anyone more in need than I."" He said: ""Then eat it and fast one day in place of what you have done.""

ʿAṭāʾ said: ""I asked Saʿīd: How much was in that basket?"" He said: ""Between fifteen and twenty ṣāʿ."" Thus did Mālik ibn Anas narrate it from ʿAṭāʾ.

And it was narrated by Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from ʿAṭāʾ, with the addition of the mention of fasting two consecutive months, except that he did not mention making up the fast nor the quantity of the basket. And it was narrated through other routes from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, and there was disagreement regarding the wording of the hadith. Reliance is placed upon the connected narrations, and with Allah lies the success. [Ḥasan due to other narrations. Narrated by Mālik.]"
"8066","(8063) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Tamīmī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab al-Arghiyānī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us, my father narrated to us. Ibn al-Musayyab said: and ʿAbd al-Salām, meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, narrated to us, ʿUmar and al-Walīd informed us, they said: al-Awzāʿī informed us, al-Zuhrī narrated to us, Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated to us, and Abū Hurayrah narrated to me, who said: While I was in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), a man came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined and have caused ruin!"" He said: ""Woe to you! What is your affair?"" He said: ""I had intercourse with my wife during Ramadan."" He said: ""Then free a slave…"" And he mentioned the hadith.

Our shaykh Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ (may Allah have mercy on him) weakened this wording (""and have caused ruin"") and attributed it to having been introduced into the narration of Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab al-Arghiyānī. For Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab through the first chain of narration without this wording. And al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd narrated it from ʿUqbah ibn ʿAlqamah without this wording. And Ḥudhaym and others narrated it from al-Walīd ibn Muslim without it. And all of the companions of al-Awzāʿī narrated it from al-Awzāʿī without it, and none of the companions of al-Zuhrī mentioned it from al-Zuhrī, except what was narrated from Abū Thawr from Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr, from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, from al-Zuhrī. Our shaykh used as evidence for its being an error in that narration that he examined the book ""al-Ṣawm"" compiled by Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr in a well-known handwriting and found this hadith in it without this wording. And all of the companions of Sufyān narrated it from him without it. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8067","(8064) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd informed me that his father said: al-Awzāʿī was asked about a man who had intercourse with his wife during Ramaḍān. He said: Upon them both is one expiation, except for fasting, for fasting is incumbent upon both of them together. It was said to him: What if he forced her? He said: Then fasting is upon him alone. [Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
"8068","(8065) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad informed us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us that Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us that Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, who said: I heard ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr narrating from Abī al-Muṭawwas — and Ḥabīb said: I saw Abī al-Muṭawwas — from his father, from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever breaks a day of Ramaḍān without a concession granted to him by Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will not have it compensated even if he fasts his entire life.""

And from what has reached me from Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī, that he said: I asked al-Bukhārī about this hadith, and he said: Abī al-Muṭawwas' name is Yazīd ibn al-Muṭawwas, and he is alone in this narration, and I do not know whether his father heard from Abū Hurayrah or not. Al-Bukhārī has cited its text in the heading of the chapter. [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8069","(8066) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār in Baghdād informed us that al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Mujashshir informed us that ʿAbīdah ibn Ḥumayd informed us that Manṣūr narrated from Wāṣil, from al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Yashkurī, who said: I was told that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said: ""Whoever breaks a day of Ramaḍān without any valid excuse, the fasting of an entire lifetime will not suffice him, until he meets Allah, Mighty and Majestic. If He wills, He will forgive him, and if He wills, He will punish him.""

It has also been narrated through another chain from Ibn Masʿūd. [Sound through other routes. Narrated by Ibn al-Najjār.]"
"8070","(8067) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us that Abū Usāmah narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik — and Abū al-Mughīrah al-Thaqafī narrated from ʿArfajah, who said:

ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said: ""Whoever deliberately breaks a day of Ramaḍān without any valid excuse, and then makes it up over the entire length of his life, it will not be accepted from him.""

ʿAbd al-Malik — I think he is Ibn Ḥusayn al-Nakhaʿī — is not strong. [Sound through other routes. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
"8071","(8068) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us that Saʿīd, meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, narrated from Abī Maʿshar, from Ibrāhīm and Yaʿlā, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, regarding a man who deliberately broke a fast during Ramaḍān for one day: They said: ""We do not know what his expiation is. He should fast a day in its place and seek forgiveness from Allah.""

A similar view has been narrated from Jābir ibn Zayd and al-Shaʿbī, in line with the statement of Saʿīd ibn Jubayr and Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī, that there is no expiation upon him. As for the hadith which... [Weak.]"
"8072","(8069) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us that Yaḥyā al-Ḥimmānī informed us that Hushaym narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Sālim, from Mujāhid, that the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered the one who broke a fast during Ramaḍān for one day to give the expiation of Ẓihār. [Weak. Narrated by Ibn ʿAbd al-Barr.]"
"8073","(8070) Abū al-Ḥusayn informed us that Abū Jaʿfar informed us that Ismāʿīl informed us that Yaḥyā informed us that Hushaym narrated from Layth, from Mujāhid, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar narration. This is an abbreviation that occurred from Hushaym in the hadith.

Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, Mūsā ibn Aʿyan, and ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd have all narrated it from Layth, from Mujāhid, from Abū Hurayrah, with an explanation in the context of the incident of intercourse with one's wife during the month of Ramaḍān.

And likewise, every hadith narrated in this chapter through an unrestricted chain has also been narrated through another chain with clarification and explanation in the context of the incident of intercourse. And nothing is established from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the one who breaks the fast by eating. [Weak. See the preceding.]"
"8074","(8071) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī informed us that Hishām narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When one of you forgets and eats or drinks while fasting, let him complete his fast, for it is Allah who has fed him and given him drink.""

Al-Bukhārī included it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Yazīd ibn Zuraʿī, and Muslim included it from the hadith of Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah, both from Hishām ibn Ḥassān. [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8075","(8072) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us that Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Karābīsī informed us that al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Bajalī informed us that Hawḍah ibn Khalīfah informed us that ʿAwf narrated from Khilās and Muḥammad, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""When one of you fasts a day and forgets and eats and drinks, let him complete his fast, for it is Allah who has fed him and given him drink.""

Al-Bukhārī included it from the hadith of Abū Usāmah from ʿAwf. [Sound. See the preceding.]"
"8076","(8073) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us that Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us that Abū al-Azhar informed us that Quraysh ibn Anas narrated from Ḥabīb ibn al-Shahīd, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""I ate and drink out of forgetfulness."" He said: ""Complete your fast, for Allah has fed you and given you drink.""

Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it from Ayyūb, Ḥabīb, and Hishām, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, with this wording. It has also been narrated from Abī Rāfiʿ, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8077","(8074) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd al-Rahman Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Tajir narrated to us — Abu Hatim Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated from Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever breaks his fast in Ramadan out of forgetfulness, there is no making up of the fast upon him, nor any expiation (kaffarah).""

Likewise, Muhammad ibn Marzuq al-Basri narrated it from al-Ansari, and this is among the unique narrations of al-Ansari from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, and all of them are trustworthy (thiqat). And Allah knows best. It has also been narrated in this regard from 'Ali and Ibn 'Umar as their own statement. Al-Daraqutni said: Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrates it, and it has been narrated from the hadith of Abu Hatim. And we have narrated from Mujahid and al-Hasan regarding this, and regarding sexual intercourse (jima') done forgetfully — that there is no making up of the fast upon him. And 'Ata' used to say regarding sexual intercourse done forgetfully: ""Upon him is the making up of the fast."" [Hasan. Al-Daraqutni narrated it.]"
"8078","(8075) In corroboration with what Abu al-Fath Hilal ibn Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us — al-Husayn ibn Yahya ibn 'Ayyash al-Qattan narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sabah narrated to us — Ibn Abi 'Adi narrated from Shu'bah —

And Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated — Shu'bah narrated —

And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated — Shu'bah narrated from al-Hakam from Ibrahim: that 'Alqamah and Shurayh ibn Arta'ah, a man from the Nakha', were in the presence of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her. One of them said to his companion: ""Ask her about kissing for the fasting person."" He said: ""I would not utter such foolishness in the presence of the Mother of the Believers."" She said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to kiss while fasting, and he would have sexual intimacy (yubashiru) while fasting, and he was the most able of you to restrain his desire (irb).""

This is the wording of the hadith of Sulayman, and the hadith of Abu Dawud is close to it. In the narration of Ibn Abi 'Adi, he said: from 'Alqamah and Shurayh ibn Arta'ah that they mentioned kissing for the fasting person in the presence of 'A'ishah, then he mentioned the hadith with its meaning. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari narrated it.]"
"8079","(8076) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Hafiz informed me — Abu Quraysh narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ma'bad narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated — Shu'bah narrated from al-Hakam from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from 'A'ishah, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to kiss and have sexual intimacy while fasting, and he was the most able of you to restrain his desire.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Sulayman ibn Harb in this manner. It is gharib (singular) in this chain, for the narration of the group from Shu'bah is from al-Hakam from Ibrahim from 'Alqamah and Shurayh, as has preceded. Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ibn 'Awn from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from 'A'ishah, and from the hadith of al-A'mash from Ibrahim from al-Aswad and 'Alqamah from 'A'ishah. [Sahih. It has preceded.]"
"8080","(8077) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated —

And Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Fasawi narrated to us — Makki ibn Ibrahim informed me — Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah from 'Azrah from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas that he said: ""Permission was granted to the elderly old man and the elderly old woman in that regard, even though they were capable of fasting — that they might break the fast if they wished, and feed one poor person (miskin) in place of each day."" Then this was abrogated by this verse: {And whoever among you witnesses the month, let him fast it} (Qur'an 2:185). And the ruling remained for the elderly old man and the elderly old woman when they are unable to fast, and for the pregnant woman and the nursing mother — if they fear, they shall break the fast and feed one poor person in place of each day."" This is the wording of the hadith of Makki. In the narration of Ruh: ""and the pregnant woman and the nursing mother if they fear,"" and the rest is the same. [Sahih. Ibn Jarud narrated it.]"
"8081","(8078) Muhammad ibn Abi 'Adi narrated from Sa'id, and he said in the hadith: ""and the pregnant woman and the nursing mother, if they fear for their children, they shall break the fast and feed.""

Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated — Abu al-Muthanna narrated — Ibn Abi 'Adi narrated from Sa'id, and he mentioned it. [Sahih. Abu Dawud narrated it.]"
"8082","(8079) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr and others besides them said — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us — al-Shafi'i narrated to us — Malik narrated from Nafi' that Ibn 'Umar was asked about a pregnant woman who fears for her child. He said: ""She breaks the fast and feeds one poor person (miskin) in place of each day — a mudd of wheat."" Abu Sa'id added in his hadith that al-Shafi'i said: Malik said: ""And the people of knowledge are of the view that upon her, in addition to that, is the making up of the fast."" Malik said: ""Upon her is the making up of the fast, because Allah the Most High says: {And whoever among you is ill or on a journey, then a number of other days} (Qur'an 2:185).""

The Shaykh said: And Anas ibn Iyyad narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from Ibn Labitah — or Ibn Abi Labitah — from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Uthman that a woman fasted while pregnant and became thirsty during Ramadan. Ibn 'Umar was asked about her, and he ordered her to break the fast and feed one poor person each day a mudd, and that it would not suffice her; and when she recovered, she should make it up.

Abu 'Ubayd mentioned it in al-Nasikh wa al-Mansukh from Ibn Abi Maryam from Anas ibn Iyyad. And this is the view of Mujahid: she breaks the fast, feeds, and makes it up.

In the narration of Qatadah from al-Hasan al-Basri: ""They both break the fast and make it up."" And in the narration of Yunus ibn 'Ubayd from al-Hasan: ""The nursing mother, if she fears, breaks the fast and feeds. And the pregnant woman, if she fears for herself, breaks the fast and makes it up, like the sick person."" [Sahih. Malik narrated it.]"
"8083","(8080) Abu Mansur Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Nuh al-Nakha'i in Kufah informed us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duham narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Garzah informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa and Abu Nu'aym narrated — from Abu Hilal — from 'Abd Allah ibn Sawadah — from Anas ibn Malik, a man from Banu 'Abd al-Ash'hal, the brothers of Qushayr, who said: ""The cavalry of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raided us, so I came to him and found him eating. He said: 'Come forward and eat.' I said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'Sit, I shall speak to you about fasting — or about the fast.' He said: 'Indeed, Allah has remitted for the traveler half of the prayer, and for the traveler, the pregnant woman, and the nursing mother the fast.'"" By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said both of them — or one of them — and alas for my soul, would that I had eaten from the food of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. And someone else, a man from Banu 'Abd Allah ibn Ka'b, the brothers of Banu Qushayr, said likewise.

Thus Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated it without mentioning his father in it. And Wahb narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Sawadah from his father from Anas ibn Malik. [Hasan. Abu Dawud narrated it.]"
"8084","(8081) Abu al-Husayn ibn Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us — al-Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us — Wahb narrated — 'Abd Allah ibn Sawadah al-Qushayri narrated from his father that Anas ibn Malik, a man from among them, said: ""His camels were lost, so he came to Madinah seeking his camels. He entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and found him having his midday meal. He said: 'Come to the meal.' He said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'Indeed, the fast has been remitted for the traveler, and half of the prayer, and for the pregnant woman and the nursing mother.'"" [Hasan.]"
"8085","(8082) And Abu al-Husayn ibn Fadl informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us — al-Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us — Wahb narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from a man from Banu 'Amir. Ayyub said: ""I met him and asked him, and he narrated to me from a man among them that he came to Madinah seeking his camels, and he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned the hadith with its like.""

And al-Thawri narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from Anas ibn Malik al-Ka'bi.

And Ma'mar narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from a man from Banu 'Amir that a man called Anas narrated to him.

And Khalid al-Hadha' narrated from Abu Qilabah and Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir from a man from Banu 'Amir that a man among them came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.

And Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated from Abu Qilabah from Abu Umayyah — or Abu al-Muhajir — from Abu Umayyah, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he was Abu Umayyah — Anas ibn Malik al-Ka'bi."" [Hasan. Al-Nasa'i narrated it.]"
"8086","(8083) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated — Nasr ibn 'Ali narrated to us — Abu Ahmad narrated — Isra'il narrated — from Abu al-'Anbas — from al-Agharr — from Abu Hurayrah that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about sexual intimacy (al-mubasharah) for the fasting person, and he granted him permission. Then another came and asked him, and he forbade him. And lo, the one to whom he granted permission was an old man (shaykh), and the one whom he forbade was a young man (shabb). [Hasan. Abu Dawud narrated it.]"
"8087","(8084) It was narrated that Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us, and others narrated to us, that Abu al-‘Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, and ‘Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us, and Sahl ibn Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Askarī narrated to us, and Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaydah narrated to us, and ‘Aban al-Bajali narrated to me from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs from ‘A’ishah that the Prophet (peace be upon him) permitted the elderly man who was fasting to kiss his wife, but he forbade the young man from doing so, and he said: “The elderly man controls his desire, but the young man ruins his fast.”"
"8088","(8085) It was narrated that Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, and Abu ‘Abdillah ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, and Ja‘far ibn ‘Awn narrated to us, and Mis‘ar narrated to us from ibn Abi Salamah from his father, who said: An elderly man asked Abi Hurayrah about kissing his wife while fasting, and he permitted it for him, but forbade it for a young man."
"8089","(8086) Through its chain, it was narrated that Mis‘ar narrated to us from Habib from Mujahid from ibn ‘Abbas, as was related."
"8090","(8087) It was narrated that Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us, and others narrated to us, that Abu al-‘Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, and Rab‘i ibn Sulayman narrated to us, and al-Shafi‘i narrated to us, and Malik narrated to us from Zayd ibn Aslam from ‘Ata’ ibn Yasar, that ibn ‘Abbas was asked about kissing a fasting woman, and he permitted it for the elderly man, but disliked it for the young man."
"8091","(8088) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Amr narrated to us, that Abu al-‘Abbas, who was the dumb, narrated to us, and Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us, and ‘Abd al-Wahhab ibn ‘Ata’ narrated to us, and ibn Jurayj narrated to us from ‘Ata’, that a man asked ibn ‘Abbas about kissing a fasting woman, and he said: “There is no harm in it if he turns away from her.” And a man grabbed her leg, and he said: “Forgive the fast.”"
"8092","(8089) It was narrated that Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran narrated to us, and Isma‘il al-Saffar narrated to us, and al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali ibn ‘Affan narrated to us, and ibn Numayr narrated to us from ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Umar from Nafi‘ from ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar, that he disliked kissing and direct contact with a fasting person."
"8093","(8090) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Amr narrated to us, that Abu al-‘Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, and Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us, and ‘Abd al-Wahhab ibn ‘Ata’ narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn ‘Amr narrated to us from Yahya ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, that a youth asked ibn ‘Umar about kissing while fasting, and he said: “No.” And an elderly man who was with him said: “Why do you trouble people and make it difficult for them? By Allah, there is no harm in it.” Ibn ‘Umar said: “As for you, accept it, for there is no harm in it for you.”"
"8094","(8091) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abdillah and Abu Sa‘id narrated to us, that Abu al-‘Abbas narrated to us, and Yahya narrated to us, and ‘Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, and Hisham al-Dastawai narrated to us from Hammad from Ibrahim from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, who said: I said to ‘A’ishah: “May a fasting person kiss?” She said: “No.” I said: “Was the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) not kissing?” She said: “He was the one who controlled his desire.”"
"8095","(8092) It was narrated that Abu ‘Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa‘id ibn Abi ‘Amr narrated to us, that Abu al-‘Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya‘qub narrated to us, and Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi narrated to us, and Abu ‘Usamah narrated to us from ‘Umar ibn Hamzah, and Salim narrated to us from ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar, who said: ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in a dream, and I saw that he did not look at me, so I said: “O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with me?” He turned to me and said: “Are you the one who kisses and you are fasting?” By the One in Whose hand is my soul, I have never kissed a woman while fasting.”

‘Umar ibn Hamzah narrated it alone, and if it is authentic, then ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was strong in this matter, as it is understood that the turning of the qiblah was due to desire, and Allah knows best."
"8096","(8093) It was narrated that Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-‘Alawi narrated to us, and Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Hafiz narrated to us, and ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashr ibn al-Hakam al-‘Abdi narrated to us, and Yahya ibn Sa‘id al-Qattan narrated to us from ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Umar, who said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrating from ‘A’ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to kiss while fasting, and you are the most capable of controlling your desire.”

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from another route through ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Umar, and it was also narrated through the hadith of al-Qamah from ‘A’ishah with these words."
"8097","It was narrated that Abū ‘Abdillāh al-Hāfiẓ said: “Abū Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqīh narrated to us: ‘Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: ‘Al-Humaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: I said to ‘Abdur-Rahmān ibn al-Qāsim: “Did you hear Abū Bakr narrate from ‘Ā’ishah that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to kiss her while he was fasting, and they were silent for an hour, then he said: Yes.”’ Muslim narrated it in his Sahīh on the authority of ‘Alī ibn Hujr and others on the authority of Sufyān."
"8098","It was narrated that Abū ‘Abdillāh al-Hāfiẓ and Abū Tahir al-Faqīh and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Ishaq and Abū Sa‘īd ibn ‘Amr said: “Abū al-‘Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya‘qūb narrated to us: Muhammad ibn ‘Abdillāh ibn al-Hakam narrated to us: Anas ibn ‘Iyāḍ narrated to us: Hishām ibn ‘Urwah narrated to us: ‘Urwah narrated to us: ‘Ā’ishah said: ‘If the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to kiss some of his wives while he was fasting, then she would laugh.’ ‘Urwah said: ‘I have never seen a kiss that calls to goodness.’” It was narrated in al-Bukhārī’s Sahīh on the authority of al-Qa‘nabī on the authority of Mālik."
"8099","It was narrated that Abū Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qāḍī said: “Abū al-‘Abbās narrated to us: Rabī‘ narrated to us: al-Shāfi‘ī narrated to us: Mālik narrated to us: Hishām ibn ‘Urwah narrated to us, and he mentioned it in a similar manner, except that he did not mention ‘Urwah’s statement in our narration, although he did mention it in the expanded version.” It was narrated in al-Bukhārī’s Sahīh on the authority of al-Qa‘nabī on the authority of Mālik, and it was narrated by Muslim from the hadith of Ibn ‘Uyaynah on the authority of Hishām."
"8100","It was narrated that Muhammad ibn ‘Abdillāh al-Hāfiẓ said: “Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salama narrated to us: ‘Abd al-Jabbār ibn al-‘Alā’ narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: Manṣūr narrated to us: Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Alqamah said: ‘They asked ‘Ā’ishah about kissing while fasting, and she said: “The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to kiss while he was fasting, and he was the one who controlled your desires.”’ Muslim narrated it in his Sahīh on the authority of ‘Alī ibn Hujr and others on the authority of Sufyān."
"8101","It was narrated that Abū Muḥammad ‘Abdillāh ibn Yūsuf al-Asbahānī said: “Abū Sa‘īd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Za‘farānī narrated to us: ‘Abīdah ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us: Muṭarrif ibn Ṭarīf narrated to us: ‘Amir narrated to us: Masrūq narrated to us: ‘Ā’ishah said: ‘If the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to fast and kiss from any side of my face wherever he wished until he broke his fast.’” It was narrated in the hadith of Aḥmad."
"8102","It was narrated that ‘Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn ‘Abdillāh ibn Bishrān al-‘Adl said: “Abū Ja‘far Muḥammad ibn ‘Amr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ja‘far narrated to us: al-Daḥḥāk (i.e., Abū ‘Āṣim) narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naḥshalī narrated to us: Ziyād ibn ‘Ilāqah narrated to us: ‘Amr ibn Maimūn narrated to us: ‘Ā’ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) said: ‘The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to kiss while he was fasting during the month of Ramaḍān.’” It was narrated by Muslim from the hadith of Bahz ibn Asad on the authority of Abū Bakr al-Naḥshalī."
"8103","It was narrated by Abū al-Aḥwass Salām ibn Sulaym on the authority of Ziyād ibn ‘Ilāqah on the authority of ‘Amr ibn Maimūn al-Awdī on the authority of ‘Ā’ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), who said: “The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to kiss during the month of fasting.” It was narrated by Abū Bakr ibn Furāk: “‘Abdillāh ibn Ja‘far narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Salām narrated to us, and he mentioned it.” It was narrated by Muslim from a group on the authority of Abū al-Aḥwass."
"8104","It was narrated that Abū ‘Abdillāh al-Hāfiẓ said: “Abū al-‘Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya‘qūb narrated to us: Ismā‘īl ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Sa‘d narrated to us: Abū Sa‘d narrated to us: Ṭalḥah ibn ‘Abdillāh ibn ‘Uthmān ibn ‘Ubaydillāh ibn Ma‘mar al-Taymī narrated to us: ‘Ā’ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) said: ‘The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) wished to kiss me, so I said: “I am fasting.” He said: “And I am fasting.” Then he kissed me.’”"
"8105","It was narrated that Abū Tahir al-Faqīh said: “Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl narrated to us: Buhr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlānī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Dīnār al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ‘Abdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ‘Ubayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-‘Abbās al-Mu‘addib narrated to us: ‘Affān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Dīnār narrated to us: Sa‘d ibn Aws narrated to us: Miṣda‘ (Abū Yaḥyā) added: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān married Abū Naḍrah from ‘Ā’ishah, who said: ‘The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) used to kiss her while he was fasting, and he would squeeze her tongue.’ ‘Affān added: A man said to him: “Did you hear it from Sa‘d?” He said: “Yes.”’” This is a weak hadith narrated by Abū Dāwūd."
"8106","It was narrated that Abū ‘Abdillāh al-Hāfiẓ said: “‘Abdillāh ibn Muḥammad al-Ka‘bī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to us: Hishām ibn Abī ‘Abdillāh narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us: Abū Salama narrated to us: Zaynab bint Abī Salama narrated to us from her mother, who said: ‘While I was with the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) in a tent, I menstruated and slipped out, and I took the clothes for my menstruation. He said: “Why did you sigh?” I said: “Yes.” So he called me and I entered with him in the tent. She said: “The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) and she used to bathe together from one vessel for janābah, and he used to kiss her while he was fasting.”’ It was narrated in al-Bukhārī’s Sahīh on the authority of Musaddad."
"8107","(8104) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Sarrāj informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū Mu'āwiyah narrated from al-A'mash, from Muslim, from Shutayr ibn Shakil, from Ḥafṣah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would kiss while he was fasting.""
Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8108","(8105) Abū 'Abdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Ḥusayn ibn Ḥusn ibn Muhājir and Muḥammad ibn Ismā'īl ibn Mihrān both said: Hārūn ibn Sa'īd al-Aylī informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, 'Amr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from 'Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'īd, from 'Abdullāh ibn Ka'b al-Ḥimyarī, from 'Umar ibn Abī Salamah al-Ḥimyarī, that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""May a fasting person kiss?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Ask this one"" — meaning Umm Salamah. So she informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Allah has forgiven you your past and future sins."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""By Allah, I am the most among you in taqwā of Allah and the most fearful of Him.""
Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Sa'īd al-Aylī. And we have narrated regarding its permissibility from Sa'd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and a group of the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8109","(8106) Abū 'Abdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib informed us, 'Amr ibn Marzūq informed us, Shu'bah narrated from Manṣūr, who said: I heard Hilāl, meaning Ibn Yasāf, narrating from al-Hazhāz, that Ibn Mas'ūd said regarding the kiss of the fasting person a severe statement — meaning that he should fast a day in its place. And according to us, this applies when he kisses and then ejaculates. [Ḥasan.]"
"8110","(8107) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhāb informed us, Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us, Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zā'idah narrated from 'Āmir, who said: Abū Mayṣarah narrated to me that Ibn Mas'ūd used to be intimate with his wife during the daytime while he was fasting.
And it has been narrated from Mujāhid, from Ibn Mas'ūd and Ibn 'Abbās, that they both saw no harm in the intimacy of a fasting person.
And in this there is an indication from Ibn Mas'ūd that what is intended by the first narration is other than what its apparent meaning suggests. And Allah knows best. [Da'īf.]"
"8111","(8108) Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn 'Abdillāh informed us, al-Qa'nabī narrated from Mālik, from Yaḥyā ibn Sa'īd, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from 'Alqamah ibn Waqqāṣ, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Actions are according to intentions, and a person shall have only what he intended. So whoever's hijrah was to Allah and His Messenger, his hijrah is to Allah and His Messenger; and whoever's hijrah was for worldly gain to be acquired or a woman to marry, his hijrah is to that for which he made hijrah.""
Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from al-Qa'nabī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8112","(8109) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī in Naysābūr and Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās in Makkah, may Allah the Most High protect it, both said: Abū Ḥafṣ 'Umar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Jumaḥī informed us, 'Alī ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz informed us, Abū Nu'aym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn informed us, al-A'mash narrated from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: 'Fasting is for Me, and I shall reward for it. He leaves his desire, his food, and his drink for My sake. Fasting is a shield. The fasting person has two joys: a joy at the time of breaking his fast, and a joy at the time of meeting his Lord. And the smell of the mouth of the fasting person is more pleasant to Allah than the fragrance of musk.'""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nu'aym, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from al-A'mash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8113","(8110) Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Ma'rūf informed us, Abū Sahl al-Isfarā'inī informed us, Abū Ja'far al-Ḥaddā' informed us, 'Alī ibn al-Madīnī informed us, al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulaymān al-Taymī, who said: I heard 'Ubaydullāh ibn 'Umar narrating from Nāfi', who said: Ibn 'Umar used to fast voluntarily and would be overcome by fainting, yet he would not break his fast.
The Shaykh said: This indicates that fainting during the fast does not invalidate it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8114","(8111) Abū 'Abdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Jawharī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā ibn Abān both said: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ja'far ibn Abī Kathīr informed me, Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from 'Iyāḍ ibn 'Abdillāh, from Abī Sa'īd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out on the day of al-Aḍḥā or al-Fiṭr to the muṣallā. He prayed, then turned and stood and admonished the people and commanded them to give charity. He said: ""O people, give charity."" Then he turned and passed by the women and said: ""O assembly of women, give charity, for I have seen you to be the majority of the inhabitants of the Fire."" They said: ""Why is that, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""You curse frequently and are ungrateful to your husbands. I have not seen anyone more deficient in intellect and religion who overwhelms the mind of a prudent man than any one of you, O assembly of women."" They said: ""What is the deficiency in our intellect and our religion, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Is not the testimony of a woman equal to half the testimony of a man?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""That is the deficiency in her intellect. And is it not so that when a woman menstruates, she neither prays nor fasts? That is the deficiency in her religion.""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from al-Ḥulwānī and al-Ṣaghānī from Ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8115","(8112) Abū 'Abdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad, meaning al-Ṣaydalānī, and Ja'far ibn Aḥmad, meaning the Ḥāfiẓ, both said: Muḥammad ibn Rāfi' informed us, 'Abd al-Razzāq said:
(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us, Ma'mar narrated from 'Āṣim al-Aḥwal, from Mu'ādhah al-'Adawiyyah, that a woman asked 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: ""Why is it that the menstruating woman makes up the fasts but does not make up the prayers?"" She said to her: ""Are you a Ḥarūriyyah?"" She said: ""I am not a Ḥarūriyyah, but I am asking."" She said: ""That used to happen to us during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we were commanded to make up the fasts but were not commanded to make up the prayers.""
Ma'mar said: And Ayyūb informed us from Abī Qilābah, from Mu'ādhah, from 'Ā'ishah, something similar.
Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abd ibn Ḥumayd from 'Abd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8116","(8113) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Muḥammad 'Abdullāh ibn Yūsuf both said: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Baghdādī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr informed us, Shu'bah narrated from —
(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, Ādam informed us, Shu'bah informed us, 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Ṣuhayb narrated, who said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Partake of suḥūr, for indeed in suḥūr there is blessing.""
This is the wording of Ādam's ḥadīth. And in the narration of Yaḥyā, he said: from Anas ibn Mālik.
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8117","It was narrated that Anas bin Malik said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Eat at dawn, for there is blessing in it."""
"8118","It was narrated that Abi Qais, the freed slave of 'Amr bin Al-'As, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The difference between our fasting and the fasting of the People of the Book is eating at dawn."""
"8119","It was narrated that 'Irbad bin Sariyah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) call for dawn food during the month of Ramadan, saying: ""Come to the blessed breakfast."""
"8120","It was narrated that Abi Hurairah said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""The best breakfast of a believer is dates."""
"8121","It was narrated that Sahl bin Sa'd said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""People will always be in goodness as long as they hasten in breaking the fast."" Narrated by Ibn Wahb: ""And they do not delay like the people of the East."""
"8122","It was narrated that Abi Hurairah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""The religion will always be prevailing as long as people hasten in breaking the fast. Indeed the Jews and Christians delay [breaking the fast]."""
"8123","It was narrated that Abi Hurairah said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Indeed Allah, the Exalted, said: 'Indeed my most beloved servants are those who hasten in breaking the fast.'"""
"8124","It was narrated that Abi Sa'eed Al-Khudri said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever breaks his fast at sunset will have a prayer that will be answered before he reaches his dwelling."" Narrated by Ibn Wahb: ""And he will have a prayer that will be answered before he reaches his dwelling."""
"8125","It was narrated that Abi Sa'eed Al-Khudri said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever breaks his fast at sunset will have a prayer that will be answered before he reaches his dwelling."" Narrated by Ibn Wahb: ""And he will have a prayer that will be answered before he reaches his dwelling."""
"8126","It was narrated that Abi Sa'eed Al-Khudri said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever breaks his fast at sunset will have a prayer that will be answered before he reaches his dwelling."" Narrated by Ibn Wahb: ""And he will have a prayer that will be answered before he reaches his dwelling."""
"8127","(8124) It was narrated that Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""We used to fast with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), then he would stand for the prayer. I asked: 'How much time is there between the adhan and the suhoor?' He said: 'As much as it takes to recite fifty verses.' This hadith is narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih on the authority of Muslim ibn Ibrahim, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Waki‘ on the authority of Hisham."
"8128","(8125) It was narrated that Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""We, the community of prophets, were commanded to hasten our breaking of the fast and delay our suhoor, and to place our right hand over our left in the prayer."" This hadith is known as that of Talhah ibn ‘Amr al-Makki, and it is weak; there is disagreement about it. Some say it is narrated on the authority of ‘Ata’ on the authority of Abu Hurairah, and it is narrated from another weak chain on the authority of Abu Hurairah, and from a weak chain on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar. It is also narrated on the authority of ‘Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she said: ""There are three things in prophethood,"" and she mentioned them, and this is the most authentic version that has reached us. It is included in the book on prayer."
"8129","(8126) It was narrated that Humaid ibn ‘Abdur-Rahman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""‘Umar and ‘Uthman (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to pray the Maghrib prayer while they were still looking at the black night, then they would break their fast after the prayer, and this was in Ramadan."" Imam al-Shafi‘i said in al-Mabsoot: ""It seems that they were delaying it as much as possible, not that they were neglecting the virtue of it after it had become permissible for them, and they would break their fast without food or drink, for the fast does not remain valid during the night."""
"8130","(8127) It was narrated that ‘Amr ibn Maimun (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) were the quickest to break their fast and the most delayed in their suhoor."""
"8131","(8128) It was narrated that Salman ibn ‘Amir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When one of you is fasting, let him break his fast with dates; if he cannot find dates, then let him break it with water, for water is pure."" This is narrated by Abu Dawud on the authority of Musaddad.

It is also narrated by Ibn ‘Awn and Hisham ibn Hasan on the authority of Hafsah, and Hisham al-Dustawai narrated it on the authority of Hafsah, but he did not raise it."
"8132","(8129) It was narrated that Salman ibn ‘Amir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When one of you is fasting, let him break his fast with dates; if he cannot find them, then let him break it with water, for it is pure."" I found this in al-Musnad, and Abu Dawud established its chain of narration. It is also narrated by Mahmud ibn Ghailan on the authority of Abu Dawud, without mentioning al-Rabab. It is also narrated by Rawh ibn ‘Ubadah on the authority of Shu‘bah, and Sa‘id ibn ‘Amir narrated it on the authority of Shu‘bah, but he made a mistake in its chain of narration."
"8133","(8130) It was narrated that Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever finds dates, let him break his fast with them; and whoever does not find them, let him break his fast with water, for it is pure."" Al-Bukhari said: ""In what I have narrated on the authority of Abu ‘Isa, the hadith of Sa‘id ibn ‘Amir is weak, and Sa‘id is confused in it; the correct hadith is that of ‘Asim on the authority of Hafsah bint Sirin."" The sheikh said: ""It has also been narrated on the authority of Anas ibn Malik from another chain."""
"8134","(8131) It was narrated that Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to break his fast before he prayed, with raisins, if they were not available, then with dried grapes, and if they were not available, then with water.

This is narrated by Abu Dawud on the authority of Ahmad ibn Hanbal."
"8135","(8132) It was narrated that Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would not pray the Maghrib prayer until they broke their fast, even if it were with a single mouthful of water.

This is narrated by Qasim ibn Ghussan on the authority of Ibn Abi ‘Arub."
"8136","(8133) It was narrated that Marwan ibn Salam al-Muqaffa‘ said: I saw Ibn ‘Umar, and he mentioned the hadith, and he said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) broke his fast, he would say: 'The thirst has passed, and the veins have been moistened, and the reward has been established, if Allah wills.'"" The wording of their hadiths is the same."
"8137","(8134) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbārī informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abu Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from Husayn from Mu'ādh ibn Zuhrah: that it reached him that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to say when he broke his fast: ""O Allah, I have fasted for You, and with Your provision I have broken my fast."" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8138","(8135) Abu Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abu al-'Abbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Hishām al-Dastawā'ī narrated from —
(Ḥ) And Abu al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hishām al-Dastawā'ī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) broke his fast at the house of a people, he would say to them: ""The fasting ones have broken their fast with you, the righteous have eaten your food, and the angels have descended upon you."" This is the wording of the hadith of Yazīd. This is a mursal hadith; Yaḥyā did not hear it directly from Anas, but rather he heard it from a man from the people of Basra known as 'Amr ibn Zunayb, or it is said Ibn Zubayb, from Anas. [Sahīh through other chains. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8139","(8136) Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us; Ma'mar narrated from Thābit from Anas, or someone else: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sought permission to visit Sa'd ibn 'Ubādah. He mentioned the hadith and said: Then they entered the house, and he (Sa'd) brought near some raisins, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ate. When he finished, he said: ""The righteous have eaten your food, the angels have prayed upon you, and the fasting ones have broken their fast with you."" [Sahīh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8140","(8137) Abu 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān narrated to us; Ḥusayn narrated from Zā'idah from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān from 'Aṭā' from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever provides food for a fasting person to break his fast shall receive a reward equal to that of the one who fasted, without diminishing anything from the reward of the fasting person. And whoever equips a warrior (in the cause of Allah) or stays behind to look after his family shall receive a reward equal to his, without diminishing anything from his reward."" [Sahīh through other chains. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
"8141","(8138) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Adhān informed us; Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us; Abu Ja'far al-Nufaylī narrated to us. He said: I read to Ma'qil ibn 'Ubayd Allāh from 'Aṭā' from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever provides food for a fasting person to break his fast shall receive a reward equal to his, without diminishing anything from his reward. And whoever equips a warrior in the cause of Allah shall receive a reward equal to his, without diminishing anything from his reward."" [Sahīh through other chains. See the preceding.]"
"8142","(8139) 'Alī informed us; Aḥmad informed us; 'Uthmān ibn 'Umar al-Dabbī narrated to us; Ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Sufyān ibn Sa'īd narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā from 'Aṭā' from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever equips a warrior, or stays behind to look after his family, or provides food for a fasting person to break his fast, shall receive a reward equal to his, without diminishing anything from his reward."" This is the preserved version from the hadith of al-Thawrī. However, Mu'mmil ibn Ismā'īl narrated it from al-Thawrī and contradicted the majority in its chain of narration. [Sahīh through other chains. See the preceding.]"
"8143","(8140) Abu 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abu Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abu Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us. They said: Abu al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Ḥumayd ibn 'Ayyāsh al-Ramlī narrated to us; Mu'mmil ibn Ismā'īl narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn Jurayj from 'Aṭā' from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever provides food for a fasting person to break his fast, or equips a warrior, shall receive a reward equal to his."" [Sahīh through other chains. See the preceding.]"
"8144","(8141) Abu Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāh al-Muzakkī informed us, along with others. They said: Abu al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfi'ī informed us; Mālik narrated from al-Zuhrī.
(Ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abu al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abdūs narrated to us; 'Uthmān ibn Sa'īd narrated to us; al-Qa'nabī narrated, from what he read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb from 'Ubayd Allāh ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Utbah ibn Mas'ūd from 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abbās: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out for Makkah during the year of the Conquest in the month of Ramaḍān. He fasted until he reached al-Kadīd, then he broke his fast and the people broke their fast with him. They used to follow the most recent of the commands of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and both (al-Bukhārī and Muslim) narrated it from the hadith of Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah from al-Zuhrī. [Sahīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8145","(8142) Abu 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abu al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Muḥammad ibn Rāfi' and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. Isḥāq said: ""He informed us,"" and the others said: ""He narrated to us."" 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us; Ma'mar informed us. He said: I heard al-Zuhrī say: ""'Ubayd Allāh ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Utbah informed me from Ibn 'Abbās: that the Prophet (ﷺ) set out in Ramaḍān from Madīnah, and with him were ten thousand Muslims. This was at the beginning of eight and a half years after his arrival in Madīnah. He travelled with those Muslims who were with him to Makkah, fasting and they fasting, until he reached al-Kadīd, which is between 'Usfān and Qudayd. Then he broke his fast and the Muslims broke their fast with him. They did not fast anything of the remainder of Ramaḍān."" Al-Zuhrī said: ""Breaking the fast was the latter of the two commands, and indeed only the most recent of the commands of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is to be taken."" Al-Zuhrī said: ""Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) reached Makkah in the morning of the thirteenth night that had elapsed of Ramaḍān."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān from 'Abd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfi'. [Sahīh. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
"8146","(8143) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbārī informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī narrated to us.
(Ḥ) And Abu 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abu Sa'īd Aḥmad ibn Ya'qūb al-Thaqafī narrated to us; Abu Shu'ayb 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Ḥamīd al-Madanī narrated to us. He said: I heard Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslamī mention that his father informed him from his grandfather Ḥamzah ibn 'Amr, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am a man who tends mounts — I manage them, travel on them, and hire them out. Sometimes this month, meaning the month of Ramaḍān, coincides with me while I have strength, and I am young. I find fasting easy for me, O Messenger of Allah, compared to deferring it and it becoming a debt upon me. O Messenger of Allah, should I fast, which is greater in reward for me, or should I break my fast?"" He said: ""Whichever you wish, O Ḥamzah."" This is the wording of the hadith of Abu 'Abd Allāh. In the narration of al-Rawdhbārī, it reads: ""Whichever you wish, O Ḥamz."" In this there is evidence indicating the permissibility of breaking the fast during a lawful journey. [Sahīh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8147","(8144) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Abū ʿImrān Mūsā ibn Sahl al-Jawnī narrated to us; Ibn Zughbah — meaning ʿĪsā ibn Ḥammād ibn Zughbah — narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Abū al-Khayr, from Manṣūr al-Kalbī, that Dihyah ibn Khalīfah set out from his village in Damascus to a distance equivalent to the village of ʿUqbah from al-Fusṭāṭ — that being three miles — during Ramadan. Then he broke his fast, and some people broke their fast with him, while others disapproved of that. When he returned to his village, he said: ""By Allah, I have seen a matter I never thought I would see — a people who have turned away from the guidance of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and his Companions."" He said this to those who had fasted. Then he said at that point: ""O Allah, take me to Yourself."" Al-Layth said: ""The matter upon which the people have reached consensus is that one should not shorten the prayer nor break the fast except at a distance of four barīds, each barīd being twelve miles.""

The Sheikh said: ""We have narrated in the Book of Prayer what indicates this from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar. As for what we have narrated from Dihyah al-Kalbī — if it is authentic — it is as though he went to the apparent meaning of the verse regarding concession in travel. By his statement 'they turned away from the guidance of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and his Companions,' he meant regarding the acceptance of the concession, not regarding the estimation of the distance of travel in which he broke his fast. And Allah knows best."" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8148","(8145) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; al-Muʿtamir narrated from ʿUbaydullāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he used to go out to al-Ghābah and would neither break his fast nor shorten the prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8149","(8146) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, among others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from Jābir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) set out for Makkah in the year of the Conquest during Ramadan. He fasted until he reached Kuraʿ al-Ghamīm, and the people fasted with him. It was said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, the people have found fasting difficult."" So he called for a cup of water after ʿAṣr and drank it while the people were watching. Some of the people broke their fast and some continued fasting. Then it reached him that some people had fasted, and he said: ""Those are the disobedient ones."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8150","(8147) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzzakkī informed me; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated it with the same meaning, and added in the hadith: ""They were only watching what you did."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"8151","(8148) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Miqdām ibn Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Faqīh in Ṭabarān informed us; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd said: We read it over Abū al-Yamān, and he informed me that he heard it from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Tanūkhī, from ʿAṭiyyah ibn Qays, from Qazāʿah ibn Yaḥyā, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us to set out in the year of the Conquest on the last two nights of the month of Ramadan. We set out fasting until we reached al-Kadīd. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us to break our fast. The people became divided — among them were those who fasted and those who broke their fast — until we reached the place where we would encounter the enemy. He then commanded us to break our fast, and we all broke our fast together.""

In the narration of Ibn Yūsuf: ""Until he reached al-Ẓahrān, he gave us notice of the encounter with the enemy and commanded us to break our fast, and we all broke our fast together."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8152","(8149) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzzakkī informed me; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Hāshim al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah — meaning Ibn Ṣāliḥ — narrated from Rabiʿah ibn Yazīd. He said: Qazāʿah narrated to me, saying: ""I came to Abū Saʿīd when he was surrounded by many people. When the people dispersed from him, I said: 'I will not ask you what these people asked you. I will ask you about fasting in travel.' He said: 'We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to Makkah while we were fasting. We stopped at a place, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""You have drawn near to your enemy, and breaking the fast is stronger for you."" So it was a concession — among us were those who fasted and among us were those who broke their fast. Then we stopped at another place, and he said: ""You will be encountering your enemy in the morning, and breaking the fast is stronger for you, so break your fast."" So it was a firm command, and we broke our fast. Then he said: ""I have indeed seen us fasting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) after that during travel.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"8153","(8150) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥīrī informed us, among others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik narrated from Sumayy, the freedman of Abū Bakr, from Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded the people during his journey in the year of the Conquest to break their fast and said: ""Strengthen yourselves against your enemy."" And the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) fasted. Abū Bakr — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — said: The one who narrated to me said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) at al-ʿArj pouring water over his head due to thirst or due to heat."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a group of people fasted when you fasted."" When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) reached al-Kadīd, he called for a cup and drank, and the people broke their fast."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8154","(8151) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Umm al-Dardāʾ, from Kaʿb ibn ʿĀṣim al-Ashʿarī, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'Fasting during travel is not from righteousness (al-birr).'""

Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā said: ""And I heard ʿAbd al-Razzāq on one occasion say: Maʿmar informed us from al-Zuhrī, from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Umm al-Dardāʾ, from Kaʿb ibn ʿĀṣim al-Ashʿarī, who was among the Companions of the Ship."" ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: ""A people who came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) from the delegation of Yemen."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'Fasting during travel is not from righteousness (al-birr).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī]"
"8155","(8152) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us by dictation; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ṣafwān ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Umm al-Dardāʾ, from Kaʿb ibn ʿĀṣim al-Ashʿarī, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Fasting during travel is not from righteousness (al-birr)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"8156","(8153) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — meaning Ibn Saʿd ibn Zurārah — from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥasan, from Jābir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was during a journey when he saw a man over whom shade had been provided. He asked about him, and they said: ""He is fasting."" He said: ""Fasting during travel is not from righteousness (al-birr)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8157","(8154) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed me; Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us; Adam narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd ar-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, saying: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Ali narrating from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah al-Ansari that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was on a journey when he saw a crowd and saw a man over whom shade had been provided. He said: ""What is this?"" They said: ""This is a fasting person."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Fasting while travelling is not from righteousness (al-birr).""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam, and Muslim narrated it from Ahmad ibn 'Uthman an-Nawfali from Abu Dawud at-Tayalisi.

[Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
"8158","(8155) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; Abu Ya'la informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us; Asim narrated to us. Abu Ya'la said: and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us — both from Asim — from Muwarriq al-'Ijli from Anas ibn Malik, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on a journey. Most of us that day were those who sought shade with a garment. As for those who broke their fast, they watered the mounts and tended to them and busied themselves. As for those who fasted, they did not tend to anything. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Those who broke their fast have taken the reward.""

This is the hadith of Isma'il. Abu Mu'awiyah said in his hadith: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) on a journey; among us were those who fasted and among us those who broke their fast. We alighted at a resting place on a hot day. Most of us who had shade were those with garments. Among us were those who shielded themselves from the sun with their hands. He said: The fasters collapsed, and those who had broken their fast stood up and erected the tents and watered the mounts. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Today, those who broke their fast have taken the reward.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu ar-Rabi' from Isma'il ibn Zakariyya, and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah.

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8159","(8156) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us, and among the others they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; ar-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; ash-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, that Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I fast while travelling?"" He used to fast frequently. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break your fast.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdillah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Hisham.

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8160","(8157) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Abu ar-Rabi' narrated to us; Hammad — meaning Ibn Zayd — narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am a man who fasts continuously (as-sard). Shall I fast while travelling?"" He said: ""Fast if you wish, and break your fast if you wish.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu ar-Rabi'.

[Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
"8161","(8158) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz, Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Fami, and Abu Sadiq Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-'Attar informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; ar-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us; 'Abdillah ibn Wahb narrated to us; 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, from Abu Murawwah, from Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): ""I find in myself the strength to fast while travelling. Is there any sin upon me?"" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""It is a concession from Allah, the Blessed and Most High. Whoever takes it has done well, and whoever loves to fast, there is no sin upon him.""

[Sahih. This is the wording of Muslim.]"
"8162","(8159) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us. It was recited to Ibn Wahb: ""'Amr ibn al-Harith informed you"" — and he mentioned it similarly.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu at-Tahir from Ibn Wahb.

[Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
"8163","(8160) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; Abu Ya'la narrated to us; Abu Khaythamah narrated to us; Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) travelled in Ramadan and fasted until he reached 'Usfan. Then he called for a vessel of water and drank during the daytime so that the people could see him, and he broke his fast until he arrived at Makkah. He (the narrator) said: Ibn 'Abbas used to say: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) fasted while travelling and also broke his fast. Whoever wishes may fast, and whoever wishes may break his fast.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madini, and Muslim narrated it from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim — both from Jarir.

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8164","(8161) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad; Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri informed us; Miqdam ibn Dawud narrated to us; 'Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us; Malik ibn Anas narrated to us.

(And) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus informed us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us; al-Qa'nabi narrated to us — from what he recited to Malik — from Humayd at-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: We travelled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) in Ramadan. The fasting person did not find fault with the one who broke his fast, nor did the one who broke his fast find fault with the fasting person.

This is the wording of the hadith of Abu 'Abdillah. In the narration of Abu al-Husayn it is: ""Among us were those who fasted and among us those who broke their fast, yet the fasting person did not find fault with the one who broke his fast, nor did the one who broke his fast find fault with the fasting person.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi.

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8165","(8162) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn an-Nadr al-Harashi and al-Hasan ibn 'Abd as-Samad both narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; Abu Khaythamah informed us, from Humayd at-Tawil, who said: Anas was asked about fasting Ramadan while travelling. He said: We travelled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) in Ramadan. No fasting person found fault with one who broke his fast, nor did one who broke his fast find fault with a fasting person.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya.

[Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
"8166","(8163) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Walid informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Humayd, who said: I went out fasting, and they said to me: ""Repeat (your fast)."" I said: ""Indeed, Anas informed me that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to travel, and the fasting person would not find fault with the one who broke his fast, nor would the one who broke his fast find fault with the fasting person."" Then I met Ibn Abi Mulaykah, and he informed me from 'A'ishah something similar.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah.

[Sahih. This is the wording of Muslim.]"
"8167","(8164) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi narrated to us — Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Hafiz narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashir narrated to us — Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated from — Asim narrated from — Abu Nadrah narrated from — Abu Sa'id al-Khudri and Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet ﷺ: that they were with him on a journey — the fasting person would fast and the non-fasting person would break his fast; the fasting person would not reproach the non-fasting person, nor would the non-fasting person reproach the fasting person.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Sa'id ibn 'Amr al-Ash'athi and others from Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8168","(8165) Abu 'Abd Allah Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us — they both said: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us — al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazid informed us — my father informed me — I heard al-Awza'i — Ziyad al-Numayri narrated to me — Anas ibn Malik said: ""The month of Ramadan coincided with a journey of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he fasted; and it again coincided with a journey of his, so he broke his fast."" [Da'if. Narrated by al-Daraqutni.]"
"8169","(8166) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us — Ruh narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from — Abu al-Fayd said: ""I was on a military expedition in Syria when Maslamah ibn 'Abd al-Malik addressed the people, saying: 'Whoever fasts during Ramadan while on a journey must make it up.' So I asked Abu Qirsa'ah, a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: 'If I were to fast, then fast, then fast' — he counted up to ten — 'I would not make it up.'""

It is also narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf that he said: ""The fasting person on a journey is like the non-fasting person at home."" This is a mawquf narration, and in its chain there is an interruption. It has also been narrated as marfu', but its chain is da'if. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Hakim.]"
"8170","(8167) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub dictated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' and al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated — Muhammad informed us, and al-Sari narrated — 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah narrated — Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated from — 'Uthman ibn Hayyan al-Dimashqi from — Umm al-Darda' said: Abu al-Darda' said: ""I saw us with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on some of his journeys on an intensely hot day, to the point that a man would place his hand upon his head due to the severity of the heat, and there was no one among us who was fasting except the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and 'Abd Allah ibn Rawahah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah, and al-Bukhari extracted it from the hadith of Isma'il ibn 'Ubayd Allah from Umm al-Darda'. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8171","(8168) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Mu'adhhi narrated to us — Ja'far ibn Ahmad ibn Nasr al-Hafiz narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Zurarah narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated — from al-Jurayri — from Abu Nadrah — from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: ""We used to go on military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during Ramadan. Among us would be the fasting person and among us would be the non-fasting person. The fasting person would find no fault with the non-fasting person, nor would the non-fasting person find fault with the fasting person. They considered that whoever found strength and fasted, that was good; and whoever found weakness and broke his fast, that was also good.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid from Isma'il. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8172","(8169) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Abu Qilabah narrated to us — 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith and Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated — they both said: 'Abd al-Samad ibn Habib al-'Awdi narrated to us — from his father — from Sinan ibn Salmah ibn al-Muhabbaq — from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever is on a journey, riding a mount that brings him to a place of rest, let him fast wherever Ramadan finds him.""

Al-Bukhari commented: ""'Abd al-Samad ibn Habib is munkar in hadith, gone (extremist); al-Bukhari did not consider this hadith to be of any account."" [Da'if. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
"8173","(8170) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ali al-Khusrawjirdi informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us — Abu Ja'far al-Hadrami narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us — Hasan ibn Salih narrated from — Asim — from Anas, who said: ""If you break your fast, it is the concession of Allah; and if you fast, it is more virtuous.""

That was also narrated from Hasan ibn Salih with his chain as marfu', but it is not anything (i.e., not established). [Sahih.]"
"8174","(8171) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Balwayh informed me — al-Kudaymi narrated to us — Ruh narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from — Asim — from Muhammad ibn Sirin — from 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As, who said regarding fasting on a journey: ""It is more beloved to me.""

And something to that effect was narrated from Ibn Mas'ud. Ibn 'Umar, however, considered breaking the fast to be more beloved to him. [Da'if.]"
"8175","(8172) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan informed us — Isma'il al-Saffar narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us — Ibn Numayr narrated from — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""That I should break my fast during Ramadan while on a journey is more beloved to me than that I should fast."" [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat).]"
"8176","(8173) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us — 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us — Sufyan narrated — al-Zuhri narrated from — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah — from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out during the month of Ramadan and fasted until, when he reached al-Kadid, he broke his fast. And only the last of his actions ﷺ is taken as the basis.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8177","(8174) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus reported from Ibn Shihāb, and he mentioned it similarly. Ibn Shihāb said: ""They used to follow the most recent of the most recent matters of his affair, and they considered it the abrogating, decisive ruling.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ; see its predecessor]"
"8178","(8175) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad reported from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, from Jābir: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out for Makkah during the year of the Conquest in Ramaḍān and fasted until he reached Kuraʿ al-Ghamīm — and we fasted with him. It was said: ""The people have found fasting difficult, and they are only waiting to see what you will do."" So he called for a vessel of water after ʿAṣr and drank it while the people were looking on, and the people broke their fast. But some of them continued to fast, and when it reached him that some people had fasted, he said: ""Those are the disobedient ones! Those are the disobedient ones!"" — twice.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. And likewise Ibn al-Hādī, Wuhaib, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī, and Ḥumaid ibn al-Aswad narrated it from Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ; narrated by Muslim]"
"8179","(8176) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī in Baghdād and Abū al-Azhar Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar both said: Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī narrated to us: Sufyān reported from al-Awzāʿī from Yaḥyya ibn Abī Kathīr from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah: that the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought food while he was at Marr al-Ẓahrān, and he said to Abī Bakr and ʿUmar: ""Eat!"" They said: ""We are fasting."" He said: ""Move on behalf of your two companions, work on behalf of your two companions, come close and eat!""

Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī is alone in narrating it from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ; narrated by al-Nasāʾī]"
"8180","(8177) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us: Yaḥyya ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us: Shuʿbah reported from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from Abī al-Bakhtarī, who said: ʿUbaydah said: ""When a man travels and has fasted some of Ramaḍān, let him fast what remains."" He then recited this verse: **""So whoever among you witnesses the month, let him fast it.""** Abī al-Bakhtarī said: Ibn ʿAbbās — and he was more learned than us — said: ""Whoever wishes may fast, and whoever wishes may break his fast."""
"8181","(8178) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd — Abū Dāwūd said:

(Ḥ) And Jaʿfar ibn Musāfir narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā al-Maʿnī narrated from Saʿīd, i.e., Ibn Abī Ayyūb. Jaʿfar added: and al-Layth. He said: Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb informed me: that Kulayb ibn Dhuhl al-Ḥaḍramī informed him from ʿUbayd — Jaʿfar said: Ibn Jabr — who said: ""I was with Abū Baṣrah al-Ghifārī, the Companion of the Prophet (ﷺ), on a ship from al-Fusṭāṭ during Ramaḍān. He handed over [his goods], then he brought close his midday meal."" Jaʿfar said in his ḥadīth: ""He had not yet passed beyond the houses when he called for the food-cloth."" He said: ""Come near."" I said: ""Do you not see the houses?"" Abū Baṣrah said: ""Do you turn away from the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?"" Jaʿfar said in his ḥadīth: ""Then he ate.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain; narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8182","(8179) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us: Shuʿbah reported from ʿAmr ibn ʿĀmir from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: Abū Mūsā said to me: ""Shall I not inform you — or shall I not give you the news — that you set out fasting and arrive fasting?"" He said: ""I said: 'Yes.'"" He said: ""When you set out, set out having broken your fast, and when you arrive, arrive having broken your fast."" [Ṣaḥīḥ; its narrators are trustworthy]"
"8183","(8180) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir informed me from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb, who said: ""I came to Anas ibn Mālik during Ramaḍān, and he intended to travel. His mount had been saddled, he had put on travelling clothes, and the setting of the sun was near. He called for food and ate from it, then mounted. I said to him: 'Is this a Sunnah?' He said: 'Yes.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ; narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8184","(8181) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Sufyān reported from Abī Isḥāq from ʿAmr ibn Sharḥbīl: ""He used to travel while fasting and would break his fast on that same day."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8185","(8182) By way of evidence, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh — informed us, Mūsā ibn Hārūn informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and Ibn Numayr both said: Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar informed us, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the crescent moon and said: ""When you see it, fast; and when you see it [at the end of the month], break your fast. And if it is obscured from you, then count thirty.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Ziyād from Abū Hurayrah, as has preceded. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim narrated it.]"
"8186","(8183) The Shaykh al-Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān informed us by dictation among others, and they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣamm narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd reported from his father, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fast upon seeing it, and break your fast upon seeing it. If it is obscured from you, then fast thirty days."" [Ṣaḥīḥ; see its predecessor]"
"8187","Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid informed us; 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us; Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah informed me; Kurayb informed me that he heard Ibn 'Abbas say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us to fast upon sighting the crescent and to break the fast upon sighting it. If it is obscured from us, then we should complete thirty days."" [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8188","Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim, Abu Yahya al-Bazzaz, narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us; 'Abbad, meaning ibn al-'Awwam, narrated from Abu Malik al-Ashja'i; Husayn ibn al-Harith al-Jadali — Judaylah of Qays — narrated that the governor of Makkah delivered a sermon and then said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) enjoined upon us that we perform the sacrifice upon sighting the crescent. If we do not see it and two upright witnesses testify, we shall act upon their testimony."" I asked Husayn ibn al-Harith: ""Who was the governor of Makkah?"" He said: ""I do not know."" Then he met me afterwards and said: ""He is al-Harith ibn Hatib, the brother of Muhammad ibn Hatib."" Then the governor said: ""Among you is one who is more knowledgeable of Allah and His Messenger than I,"" and he pointed with his hand toward a man. Husayn said: ""I asked an elder beside me: 'Whom is the governor pointing to?' He said: 'This is 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar.' And he spoke the truth — he was indeed more knowledgeable of Allah than him."" Then he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us with this."" [Hasan. Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
"8189","Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz said: Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi said to us: ""I asked Ibrahim al-Harbi about this hadith, and he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated it to us."" Then Ibrahim said: ""He is al-Harith ibn Hatib ibn al-Harith ibn Ma'mar ibn Habib ibn Wahb ibn Hudhafah ibn Jamah, and he was among the emigrants to Abyssinia."" 'Ali ibn 'Umar said: ""This is a connected, sound chain of narration."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Daraqutni]"
"8190","Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us; he said: It was recited to 'Abdullah ibn Wahb: ""Sufyan al-Thawri informed you from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir from Rabi'i ibn Hirash from some of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: 'The people began the day fasting for the thirtieth day.'"" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Hakim]"
"8191","Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Harun ibn Sulayman al-Isfahani narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Rabi'i ibn Hirash, from some of his Companions, who said: ""The people began the day at the completion of thirty days in a state of fasting. Then two Bedouins arrived and testified that they had seen the crescent the previous evening. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded the people to break their fast."" Abu 'Awanah also narrated it from Mansur. [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn al-Jarud]"
"8192","Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us; 'Umar ibn 'Umar narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Abu 'Awanah narrated from Mansur, from Rabi'i ibn Hirash, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The people differed on the last day of Ramadan. Then two Bedouins arrived and testified before the Prophet (ﷺ), by Allah, that they had seen the crescent the previous evening. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded the people to break their fast."" [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"8193","Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Nusayr al-Khalidi narrated to us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Isma'il al-Talaqani narrated to us; Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from Mansur, from Rabi'i ibn Hirash, from Abu Mas'ud, who said: ""The people began the day fasting at the completion of thirty days. Then two men came and testified that they had seen the crescent the previous day. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded the people, and they broke their fast."" Ibrahim ibn Bashar also narrated it from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah. [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"8194","Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; Abu 'Abdullah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Abu Ahmad — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab — narrated to us; Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us; al-A'mash narrated from Abu Wa'il, who said: ""The crescent of Ramadan appeared to us while we were in Khaniqin. Among us were those who fasted and among us were those who broke the fast. Then a letter from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to us: 'When you see the crescent during the day, do not break the fast until two Muslim witnesses testify that they saw it the previous evening.'"" The Sheikh said: ""By this he meant the crescent at the end of Ramadan."" [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn al-Banna]"
"8195","Abu Nasr — 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz — informed us; Abu al-Husayn — Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Quhistani — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub narrated to us; Hafs ibn 'Umar narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, who said: ""'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to us while we were in Khaniqin: 'Indeed, some crescents are greater than others. So when you see the crescent at the beginning of the day, do not break the fast until two upright witnesses testify that they saw it the previous evening.'"" This is a sound narration from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"8196","Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, Abu Tahir al-Faqih, Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr all said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Dawud al-Munadi narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Warqa' ibn 'Umar narrated from 'Abd al-A'la al-Tha'labi, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: ""I was with al-Bara' ibn 'Azib and 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them both) at al-Baqi'. He looked toward the crescent, and a rider approached. 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) went to meet him and said: 'Where have you come from?' He said: 'From the west.' He said: 'Did you see the crescent?' He said: 'Yes.' 'Umar said: 'Allah is the Greatest! Indeed, one man is sufficient for the Muslims.' Then 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stood, performed wudu', wiped over his leather socks, and prayed Maghrib. Then he said: 'This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do it.'"" [Da'if. Narrated by Ahmad]"
"8197","(8194) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Muḥammad narrated to us: Yazīd informed us: Isrāʾīl ibn Yūnus narrated from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Thaʿlabī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, who said: I was with ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) when a man came to him and said, ""I have seen the crescent of Shawwāl."" ʿUmar said, ""O people, break your fast."" Then he mentioned the hadith regarding wiping over the leather socks.

[Weak. See its predecessor.]"
"8198","(8195) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) accepted the testimony of a single man regarding the sighting of the crescent for the fast-breaking or the sacrifice.

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq said: I asked Abū Nuʿaym, ""Did Ibn Abī Laylā hear from ʿUmar?"" He said, ""I do not know.""

Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī said: I asked Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, ""Did Ibn Abī Laylā hear from ʿUmar?"" He did not affirm that.

ʿAlī said: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā is ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿĀmir al-Thaʿlabī; other narrators besides him are more reliable, and the hadith of Abū Wāʾil from ʿUmar has a more authentic chain of narration than this one. It was narrated by al-Aʿmash and Manṣūr from Abū Wāʾil.

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn was asked about ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā's narration from ʿUmar, and he said, ""He did not see him."" I said to him, ""The hadith which is narrated: 'We were with ʿUmar looking for the crescent'—"" He said, ""It is nothing.""

[Weak.]"
"8199","(8196) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Abī Bishr from Abī ʿUmayr ibn Anas from among his paternal uncles who were Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: The people of Madīnah woke up fasting on the last day of Ramadan during the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). A caravan arrived in the late afternoon and bore witness before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) that they had seen the crescent the previous evening. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded the people to break their fast and to proceed to their place of prayer the following morning.

Shuʿbah and Hushaym ibn Bashīr narrated something similar from Abī Bishr — Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ḥayshah — and this is a good chain of narration. Abī ʿUmayr narrated from his paternal uncles who were Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). All the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) are trustworthy, so it makes no difference whether they were named or not.

[Good. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8200","(8197) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir narrated from Shuʿbah from Qatādah from Anas that some of his paternal uncles from among the Anṣār bore witness before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding the sighting of the crescent, and he commanded them to go out for their Eid the following day.

Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir is the only one who narrated this from Shuʿbah, and he made an error in it. Shuʿbah actually narrated it from Abī Bishr.

[Denounced in its chain of narration. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8201","(8198) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us in it: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us: Rūḥ narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Bishr from Abī ʿUmayr ibn Anas from his paternal uncles who were Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: A caravan came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and bore witness that they had seen it — meaning the crescent — the previous evening. He commanded them to break their fast and to go out the following day. Shuʿbah said: I think it was in the late afternoon.

[Good. Meaning as above.]"
"8202","(8199) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Khalaf ibn Hishām al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Manṣūr from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: The people differed on the last day of Ramadan. Two Bedouins arrived and bore witness before the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), by Allah, that they had seen the crescent the previous evening. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded the people to break their fast and to proceed to their place of prayer the following morning.

[Sound. Meaning nearly as above.]"
"8203","(8200) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated to us. He said: I heard Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr say: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""We are an unlettered nation; we do not write and we do not calculate. The month is like this, and like this, and like this"" — meaning thirty. Then he said: ""And like this, and like this, and like this"" — and he tucked in his thumb, meaning twenty-nine. He said it once as thirty and once as twenty-nine.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ghandar from Shuʿbah.

[Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8204","(8201) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz informed us, from what I read to him from the original of his book in Baghdad: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Ḥāmid ibn Sahl al-Thaghfī narrated to us: Abū Ghassān Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Saʿīd narrated from ʿĀʾishah. She said: It was said to her, ""O Mother of the Believers, can the month of Ramadan be twenty-nine?"" She said: ""I did not fast twenty-nine days with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) more than I fasted thirty.""

And something similar has been narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd.

[Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8205","(8202) Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid narrated to us by dictation: Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Harawī narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sābiq narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Dīnār narrated to me: My father narrated to me that he heard ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith say: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd say: ""I did not fast twenty-nine days with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) more than I fasted thirty with him.""

[Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8206","(8203) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: al-Muʿtamir said: I heard Isḥāq ibn Suwayd and Khālid al-Ḥaddāʾ narrating from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakrah from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The two months of Eid do not fall short: Ramadan and Dhū al-Ḥijjah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān.

And what is meant by the hadith — and Allah knows best — is that even if they come out as twenty-nine days, they are both complete with respect to the rulings pertaining to them.

[Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8207","(8204) And Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī — informed us, that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Aṣbahānī — informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris — narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī — narrated to us, that Abū Nuʿaym — narrated to us, that Ḥumayd, meaning Ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Aṣamm al-Kūfī — heard al-Walīd saying: We fasted during the era of ʿAlī (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) for twenty-eight days, and then he commanded us to make up one day.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8208","(8205) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī — informed us, that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūsh al-Shaybānī al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn Naṣr, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Marwazī — narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā — informed us, that Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar — from Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥarmalah — from Kurayb — that Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith had sent him to Muʿāwiyah in Shām. He said: I arrived in Shām and fulfilled her need. Then Ramaḍān commenced while I was in Shām, and I sighted the new moon on the night of Jumuʿah. I then returned to Madīnah at the end of the month, and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās asked me about the new moon. He said: ""When did you sight the new moon?"" I said: ""We sighted it on the night of Jumuʿah."" He said: ""Did you yourself see it?"" I said: ""Yes, and the people saw it and fasted, and Muʿāwiyah fasted as well."" He said: ""But we sighted it on the night of Sabt (Saturday). We shall continue fasting until we complete thirty days, or until we see it."" I said: ""Is the sighting of Muʿāwiyah not sufficient for you?"" He said: ""No. Thus did the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) command us.""

Muslim reported it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

It is possible that Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) intended what has been narrated from him in another account: that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) extended the period for his sighting, or that the count be completed. However, the sighting from another land was not established before him through the testimony of two men until the count was completed based on his individual sighting. Since Kurayb was alone in reporting this narration, he did not accept it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim.]"
"8209","(8206) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us, that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us, that Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz — narrated to us, that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah — narrated us, that Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah — from Ayyūb — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu).

Ḥ: And ʿAlī — informed us, that Abū ʿUbayd al-Qāsim ibn Ismāʿīl — narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd — narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — informed us, that Ayyūb — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) said: The month is only twenty-nine days. Do not fast until you see it (the new moon), and do not break the fast until you see it. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count of thirty. Your day of breaking the fast is the day you break it, and your day of sacrifice is the day you sacrifice. Every ʿArafah is a place of standing, and every Minā is a place of slaughter, and every path of Makkah is a place of slaughter.

It has been reported from the Ḥadīth of Ḥammād ibn Zayd from Ayyūb, raised to the Prophet (marfūʿan), and it was followed by ʿAbd al-Wārith and Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim from Ibn al-Munkadir, raised (marfūʿan).

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"8210","(8207) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān — informed him, that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd — narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Jābir — narrated to us, that al-Ḥasan ibn Qazāʿah — narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-Wārith — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir.

Ḥ: And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith — informed us, that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar — narrated to us, that Ibn Ṣāʿid — narrated to us, that Azhar ibn Jamīl — narrated to us, that Ibn Sāwī — narrated to us, that Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim — from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Abū Hurayrah — from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam): ""Fast upon seeing it (the new moon)."" Then he mentioned the like of it in full, but he did not mention ""The month is twenty-nine days.""

It was reported from al-Maqburī from Abū Hurayrah, raised (marfūʿan).

[Ḍaʿīf. Reported by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8211","(8208) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh — informed us, that Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī — narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid — narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-Jabbār and Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr — both said: narrated to us, that Abū Saʿīd, the mawlā of Banī Hāshim — narrated to us, that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Makhramī — from ʿUthmān al-Aknasī — from al-Maqburī — from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: ""Your fasting is the day you fast, and your sacrifice is the day you sacrifice.""

[Ḥasan. Reported by al-Tirmidhī.]"
"8212","(8209) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us, that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿf ibn Durustawayh al-Naḥwī in Baghdād — narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayn — narrated to us, that ʿĀrim, Abū al-Nuʿmān — narrated to us, that Ḥammād ibn Zayd — said: I heard Abā Ḥanīfah narrating to ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who said: narrated to me ʿAlī ibn al-Aqmar — from Masrūq, who said: I entered upon ʿĀʾishah on the day of ʿArafah, and she said: ""Give Masrūq a drink of sawīq and increase its sweetness."" He said: I said: ""Nothing prevented me from fasting today except that I feared it might be the day of Naḥr (sacrifice)."" ʿĀʾishah said: ""Naḥr is the day the people perform sacrifice, and Fiṭr is the day the people break their fast.""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"8213","(8210) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us, that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā — narrated to us, that Aḥmad ibn Yūnus — narrated to us, that Zuhayr — narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — from Abū Salamah — from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā), who said: There would be upon me fasts from Ramaḍān which I was unable to make up except in Shaʿbān. Yaḥyā said: The preoccupation was from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam).

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both reported it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8214","(8211) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, that al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram — narrated to us, that Yazīd ibn Hārūn — narrated to us, that Shuʿbah — from al-Ḥakam — from Maymūn ibn Mihrān — from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding a man whom Ramaḍān overtook while he still owed fasts from another Ramaḍān. He said: He fasts this one (the current Ramaḍān), and for that one he feeds a poor person for every day, and he makes up the fasts.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Ahl al-Jamāʿah.]"
"8215","(8212) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — both said: narrated to us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib — said: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: Saʿīd, that is Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, was asked about a man upon whom two consecutive Ramaḍāns fell and he neglected what was between them. He informed us from Qatādah, from Ṣāliḥ, Abū al-Khalīl, from Mujāhid, from Abū Hurayrah, that he said: He fasts the one that is present, makes up the other, and feeds a poor person for every day.

And ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Saʿīd narrated to us from Qays ibn Saʿd, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah, with the like of it.

And Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ from Abū Hurayrah, and he said: A mudd of wheat for every poor person.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Ibn ʿUrwīyah.]"
"8216","(8213) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād — informed us, that Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān — narrated to us, that ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Shawārib — narrated to us, that Sahl ibn Bakkār — narrated to us, that Abū ʿAwānah — from Raqabah — said: ʿAṭāʾ claimed that he heard Abū Hurayrah say regarding a person who falls ill and does not fast during Ramaḍān, then recovers but does not make up the fasts until another Ramaḍān overtakes him: He fasts the one that overtakes him, fasts the other, and feeds a poor person for every night.

Ibrāhīm ibn Nāfiʿ al-Jallāb narrated this Ḥadīth from ʿUmar ibn Mūsā ibn Wajīḥ, from al-Ḥakam, from Mujāhid, from Abū Hurayrah, raised (marfūʿan), but it is nothing (i.e., it is not authentic). Ibrāhīm and ʿUmar are both abandoned (matrūkān).

It has been reported from Ibn ʿUmar and Abū Hurayrah regarding the one who was not well until another Ramaḍān overtook him: he feeds and there is no making up of fasts upon him. And from al-Ḥasan, Ṭāwūs, and al-Nakhaʿī: he makes up the fasts and there is no expiation (kaffārah) upon him. And we hold this view, by the decree of Allah the Most Exalted: {So a count of other days} [Sūrah al-Baqarah, 2:184].

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Its men are trustworthy (thiqāt).]"
"8217","(8214) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us: Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us on the authority of Hammam ibn Munabbih, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Leave me as I have left you, for those who were destroyed before you were destroyed due to their excessive questioning and their disagreement with their Prophets. So when I forbid you from something, avoid it; and when I command you with something, do it as much as you are able."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi', from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8218","(8215) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us: Ja'far ibn Awn informed us: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us: al-Qasim and Nafi' informed us that when Ibn 'Umar was asked about a man who died owing fasts of Ramadan or a vow, he would say: ""No one should fast on behalf of another, but give charity on his behalf from his wealth for fasting, one miskin for each day."" [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat).]"
"8219","(8216) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah al-Ansari informed us: Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil informed us: Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' informed me: Juwayriyyah ibn Asma' informed me: Nafi' informed us that 'Abd Allah used to say: ""Whoever breaks fast during Ramadan for days while ill, then dies before making them up, let a miskin be fed on his behalf for each day he broke fast from those days, a mudd of wheat. If the next Ramadan overtakes him before he fasts them and he is able to fast what overtakes him, let him feed for what has passed, a mudd of wheat for each day, and fast what remains."" This is the correct version, being a statement of Ibn 'Umar (mawquf). Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated it from Nafi' but erred in it. [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat).]"
"8220","(8217) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqi informed us: Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isfahani informed us: Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas informed us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn al-Bakhtari informed us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Sharik informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, regarding one who dies owing fasts of Ramadan and does not make them up, he said: ""Let a half sa' of barley be fed on his behalf for each day."" This narration is erroneous from two perspectives: First, he raised the hadith to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, whereas it is only a statement of Ibn 'Umar. Second, his saying ""half a sa',"" whereas Ibn 'Umar said ""a mudd of wheat."" It was also narrated from another route from Ibn Abi Layla in which there is no mention of the sa'. [Munkar. Narrated by Ibn Abi Layla.]"
"8221","(8218) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad: Abu 'Umar ibn al-Sammak informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Kamil al-Qurqasani informed us: Abu 'Asim al-Bajali informed us: Abthar ibn al-Qasim informed us: Ash'ath ibn Sawwar informed us: Muhammad, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was asked about a man who died owing fasts of a month, and he said: ""Let a miskin be fed on his behalf for each day."" [Munkar. Narrated by al-Tirmidhi.]"
"8222","(8219) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us: Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban said: Ibn 'Abbas was asked about a man who died owing fasts of the month of Ramadan and owing a vow of fasting another month. He said: ""Let sixty miskins be fed."" Thus did Ibn Thawban narrate it from him regarding both fasts together. [Sahih in other than this chain (sahih li-ghayrihi).]"
"8223","(8220) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us: Sa'id informed us: Ruh ibn al-Qasim informed us: 'Ali ibn al-Hakam informed us: Maymun ibn Mihran informed us: Ibn 'Abbas was asked about a woman or a man who died owing Ramadan fasts and a vow of a month's fasting. Ibn 'Abbas said: ""Let a miskin be fed on his behalf for each day, or let his guardian fast on his behalf for his vow."" Sa'id ibn Jubayr also narrated similarly from Ibn 'Abbas. [Sahih in other than this chain (sahih li-ghayrihi).]"
"8224","(8221) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu al-Walid al-Faqi informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Dawud informed us: Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us — and Abu 'Umar Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me: Abu Ya'la informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Isa al-Misri informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far informed us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr informed us: 'Urwah informed us: 'A'ishah informed us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever dies owing fasts, let his guardian fast on his behalf."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Musa ibn A'yan from 'Amr, then he said: Ibn Wahb followed him up from 'Amr, and Muslim narrated it from Harun ibn Sa'id and Ahmad ibn 'Isa. Al-Bukhari said: And Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated it from Ibn Abi Ja'far. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8225","(8222) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us: 'Umar ibn al-Rabi' ibn Tariq informed us: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far informed us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed us: 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed us that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever dies owing fasts, let his guardian fast on his behalf."" [Sahih. See the preceding narration.]"
"8226","(8223) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed us: Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us: 'Isa ibn Yunus informed us: al-A'mash informed us: Muslim al-Batin informed us: Sa'id ibn Jubayr informed us: Ibn 'Abbas informed us that a woman came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: ""My mother has died and she owed fasts of a month."" He said: ""Tell me, if she had owed a debt, would you have paid it?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""The debt of Allah is more deserving of being paid."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. And with its meaning, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan and Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid and 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated it from al-A'mash. And Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated it from al-A'mash. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8227","(8224) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Shabib al-Fami narrated to us, saying: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us, Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated from al-A'mash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my mother has died and she owed a month's fast. Should I fast on her behalf?"" He said: ""If your mother had a debt, would you pay it?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then the debt of Allah is more deserving of being fulfilled."" Sulaiman said: al-Hakam and Salamah said — and we were sitting when Muslim was narrating this hadith — ""We heard Mujahid narrate from Ibn 'Abbas this (report).""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim from Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Husayn al-Ju'fi from Zaidah.

And Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated it from al-A'mash. [Sahih; see its predecessor]"
"8228","(8225) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us, Abu Muhammad Dalaj ibn Ahmad narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abu Khalid narrated from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam and Muslim al-Batin and Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid and 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my sister has died and she owed two consecutive months of fasting."" He said: ""What do you think — if your sister had a debt, would you pay it?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then the right of Allah is more deserving.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashajj, and al-Bukhari said: ""And it is mentioned from Abu Khalid,"" and he mentioned it. [Sahih; narrated by Muslim]"
"8229","(8226) Shu'bah narrated it from al-A'mash, as Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us, 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated from al-A'mash, saying: I heard Muslim al-Batin narrating from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that a woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned to him that her sister had vowed to fast a month, and that she had embarked upon the sea and died without having fasted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to her: ""Fast on behalf of your sister.""

This wording is explicit regarding fasting on her behalf.

Similarly, Zaid ibn Abi Unaysah narrated it from al-Hakam ibn 'Utbah from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, except that he said: ""My mother died."" [Sahih; narrated by al-Sultani]"
"8230","(8227) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-Husayn 'Abd al-Baqi ibn Qani' al-Hafiz narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Shadhan al-Jawhari narrated to us.

(And) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us, Muhammad ibn Shadhan narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn 'Adi narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated from Zaid ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Hakam, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""My mother has died and she owed a fast of a vow."" He said: ""Would you have paid a debt on her behalf if your mother had owed one?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then fast on her behalf.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih. [Sahih; narrated by Muslim]"
"8231","(8228) From Ishaq ibn Mansur and 'Abd ibn Humayd and Ibn Abi Khalaf, from Zakariyya ibn 'Adi, and he added in its text: ""Should I fast on her behalf?"" He said: ""What do you think — if your mother had a debt and you paid it, would that fulfill it on her behalf?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then fast on behalf of your mother.""

Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us, Abu 'Abdullah ibn Yazid and Abu Ahmad ibn 'Isa said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to me, Ishaq ibn Mansur and Ibn Abi Khalaf and 'Abd ibn Humayd — 'Abd said: Zakariyya ibn 'Adi narrated to me, and he mentioned it.

And al-Bukhari narrated it as a report from Zaid ibn Abi Unaysah.

And Hammad ibn Salamah narrated it from Abu Bishr Ja'far ibn Abi Wahshiyyah from Sa'id, as an explicit text regarding the permissibility of fasting on her behalf. [Sahih; see its predecessor]"
"8232","(8229) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Ja'far ibn Abi Wahshiyyah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that a woman made a vow while she was at sea: if Allah saved her, she would fast a month. Allah saved her, but she died before she could fast. A relative of hers — either her sister or her daughter — came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him. He said: ""Fast on her behalf.""

Hushaym followed it up from Abu Bishr regarding fasting, and Shu'bah and Abu 'Awanah narrated from Abu Bishr regarding Hajj but not fasting. It is possible that the question was asked about both, and they transmitted one of them, while Hammad ibn Salamah and Hushaym transmitted the other. And 'Ikramah narrated it from Ibn 'Abbas regarding fasting. [Sahih; narrated by Abu Dawud]"
"8233","(8230) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Bakr ibn 'Abdullah informed me, al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated, saying: I read to al-Fudayl from Abu Hariz: regarding a woman who died and upon whom there was a fast. He said: 'Ikramah narrated to me from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my mother has died and she owed a fast of fifteen days."" He said: ""What do you think — if your mother had died and she owed a debt, would you pay it?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then pay the debt of your mother."" And she was a woman from Khath'am. Al-Bukhari said: And Abu Hariz said: 'Ikramah narrated to me, and he mentioned it.

The Sheikh said: And Buraydah ibn Husayb narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding both fasting and Hajj together. [Sahih; narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"8234","(8231) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far narrated to us, Ja'far al-Hafiz narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn 'Ata' al-Madani narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) when a woman came to him and said: ""I gave a maidservant in charity on behalf of my mother, but she has died."" He said: ""Your reward is confirmed, and the inheritance has returned it to you."" She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, she also owed a fast of a month. Should I fast on her behalf?"" He said: ""Fast on her behalf."" She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, she did not perform Hajj. Should I perform Hajj on her behalf?"" He said: ""Perform Hajj on her behalf.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali ibn Hujr.

Similarly, a group narrated it from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ata' — Sufyan al-Thawri, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah, 'Abdullah ibn Numayr, Marwan al-Fazari, Abu Mu'awiyah, and others — except that some of them said: ""A fast of two months.""

And 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated it from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ata' from Sulayman ibn Buraydah from his father. And he said: ""A fast of a month."" Thus, with these hadiths, the permissibility of fasting on behalf of the deceased is established.

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said in al-Qadim: ""Something has been narrated regarding fasting on behalf of the deceased. If it is established, then one should fast on his behalf, just as Hajj is performed on his behalf."" And regarding al-Jadid, he was asked about himself and said: ""If it is said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded someone to fast on behalf of another — it is said: Yes, Ibn 'Abbas narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). If it is said: Why do you not take it? It is said: Al-Zuhri narrated from 'Ubaydullah from Ibn 'Abbas from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) a vow, and he did not name it, despite al-Zuhri's long companionship of 'Ubaydullah and his prolonged sitting with Ibn 'Abbas. So when someone other than him narrated from a man from Ibn 'Abbas something other than what is in the hadith of 'Ubaydullah, it is more likely that it is not preserved."" He meant by that the hadith which. [Sahih; narrated by Muslim]"
"8235","(8232) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and others said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us, al-Rabi' informed us, al-Shafi'i informed us, Malik informed us from Ibn Shihab from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""My mother has died and she owed a vow."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Fulfill it on her behalf.""

The Sheikh said: This is a confirmed hadith, which al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated in their two Sahihs from the hadith of Malik and others from al-Zuhri, except that in the narration of Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas: that a woman asked.

And similarly al-Hakam ibn 'Utbah and Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated it from Mujahid from Ibn 'Abbas.

And in a narration from Mujahid and 'Ata' and Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas.

And 'Ikramah narrated it from Ibn 'Abbas, then Buraydah ibn Husayb narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). It is more likely that this is the story in which the question about fasting occurred explicitly, other than the story of Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah in which the question about the vow occurred in general.

How so, when it has been narrated from 'A'ishah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) with a sound chain of narration the explicit text regarding the permissibility of fasting on behalf of the deceased? And I have seen some of our companions weaken the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas with what has been narrated from Yazid ibn Zurai' from al-Hajjaj al-Ahwal from Ayyub ibn Musa from 'Ata' from Ibn 'Abbas that he said: ""No one should fast on behalf of another, and feed on his behalf.""

And with what has been narrated to us from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban from Ibn 'Abbas regarding feeding on behalf of one who died owing the fast of the month of Ramadan and the fast of a vow.

And in the narration of Maymun ibn Mihran from Ibn 'Abbas, and the narration of Abu Husayn from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas, that he said regarding the fast of the month of Ramadan: ""Feed on his behalf,"" and regarding the vow: ""His guardian should fulfill it on his behalf.""

The narrations of Maymun and Sa'id agree with the narration from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding the vow, except that the first two narrations contradict it.

And I have seen some of them weaken the hadith of 'A'ishah with what has been narrated from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayer from a woman from 'A'ishah regarding a woman who died and upon whom there was a fast. She said: ""Feed on her behalf.""

And it has been narrated from another direction from 'A'ishah that she said: ""Do not fast on behalf of your dead, and feed on their behalf.""

There is nothing in what they mentioned that necessitates weakness of the hadith. Whoever permits fasting on behalf of the deceased also permits feeding on his behalf. And in what has been narrated from them regarding the prohibition of fasting on behalf of the deceased there is consideration. And the marfu' hadiths are more sound in chain of narration and more famous in narrators, and the authors of the two Sahihs have deposited them in their two books. Had al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) paused at all their routes and their mutual corroboration, he would not have contradicted them, Allah willing. And with Allah is the success.

Among those who held the view of the permissibility of fasting on behalf of the deceased were Tawus, al-Hasan al-Basri, al-Zuhri, and Qatadah. [Sahih; narrated by al-Bukhari]"
"8236","(8233) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr said: Abu al-'Abbas — that is, al-Asamm — narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' said: Sa'id — meaning Ibn 'Arubah — was asked about a man who died owing two Ramadans without having been healthy between them. He informed us from Abu Yazid al-Madani that a man died owing two Ramadans, and he left a will that the jurists should be asked what would expiate them, and that his debt should be paid on his behalf, and that the debt of Allah should be given priority. He mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: They came to Ibn 'Abbas, and he said: ""Upon him is feeding sixty poor persons."" They returned to Ibn 'Umar and informed him, and he said: ""He has spoken the truth; do accordingly."" [Da'if]"
"8237","(8234) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi informed us — Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Faris al-Naysaburi said — and among what Abd al-Razzaq mentioned from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: ""The verse {a number of other days consecutively} was revealed, and the word 'consecutively' was abrogated."" Her statement ""was abrogated"" means it was abrogated (naskh); no other interpretation is valid. [Sahih. Reported by al-Daraqutni.]"
"8238","(8235) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us — Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz informed us — Abd al-Malik ibn Ahmad al-Daqqaq informed us — Bahr ibn Nasr informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Mu'awiyah ibn Salih informed us — Azhar ibn Sa'id informed me — he heard Abu Amir al-Hawzani saying: I heard Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah (may Allah be pleased with him) being asked about making up (fasts of) Ramadan, and he said: ""Allah has not granted you permission to break the fast while He intends to make it difficult for you in making it up. Count the number (of days) and do as you wish."" [Hasan.]"
"8239","(8236) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Ali ibn Umar informed us — Ibn Mani' informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us — Zayd ibn al-Hubab informed us — Mu'awiyah ibn Salih informed us — Musa ibn Yazid ibn Muhab informed us — from his father — from Malik ibn Yakhmir — from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that he was asked about making up (fasts of) Ramadan, and he said: ""Count the number (of days) and fast however you wish."" [Da'if. Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
"8240","(8237) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr informed us — they both said: Abu al-Abbas, that is, al-Asamm, informed us — Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us — Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us — Sa'id, that is, Ibn Abi Arubah, informed us — Ali ibn al-Hakam informed us — Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed us — Uqbah ibn al-Harith informed him that Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saw no harm in making it up (i.e., the fasts) separately — meaning the making up of the fasts of Ramadan. [Da'if.]"
"8241","(8238) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us — Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us — Ubayd ibn Abd al-Wahid ibn Sharik informed us — Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam informed us — Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me — Ibn Jurayj informed me — from Ata' — from Ibn Abbas, that he used to say regarding the making up of Ramadan: ""Whoever has any of it (to make up) should separate it (i.e., make it up promptly)."" [Hasan. Yahya ibn Ayyubi is Egyptian.]"
"8242","(8239) And Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sa'id al-Isfara'ini informed us with it — Abu Sahl, that is, Bishr ibn Ahmad, informed us — Hamzah ibn Muhammad al-Katib informed us — Nu'aym ibn Hammad informed us — Ibn al-Mubarak informed us — from Ma'mar — from al-Zuhri — from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah — from Ibn Abbas, regarding the one upon whom making up the month of Ramadan is obligatory, he said: ""He should make it up separately, for Allah said: {a number of other days}."" [Hasan for other than that.]"
"8243","(8240) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr informed us — they both said: Abu al-Abbas, that is, al-Asamm, informed us — Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us — Abd al-Wahhab informed us — Sulayman al-Taymi informed us — Bakr ibn Abd Allah informed us — from Anas ibn Malik, that he saw no harm in it, and he would say: ""Allah only said: {a number of other days}."" [Hasan. Its chain is connected.]"
"8244","(8241) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz informed us — Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us — Ibn Idris informed us — from Shu'bah — from Abd al-Hamid ibn Rafi' — from his grandmother — that Rafi' ibn Khadij used to say: ""Count the number (of days) and fast however you wish."" And it has been reported in this regard from the Prophet ﷺ with a mursal chain of narration."
"8245","(8242) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us — they both said: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Bahr ibn Nasr informed us — he said: It was recited to Abd Allah ibn Wahb — ""Abu Husayn, a man from the people of Makkah, informed you, he said: I heard Musa ibn Uqbah narrating from Salih ibn Kaysan, he said: It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a man upon whom making up (fasts) of Ramadan is obligatory — he makes up one day or two separate days — will that suffice him?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Tell me, if a man had a debt and paid one dirham or two dirhams until he fulfills his debt, would you consider his obligation discharged?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then it will be discharged for him.'"" And it is also said from Musa ibn Uqbah from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir from the Prophet ﷺ, mursal. [Da'if.]"
"8246","(8243) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz informed us — Ibn Mani' informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah informed us — Yahya ibn Sulaym al-Ta'ifi informed us — from Musa ibn Uqbah — from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: ""It reached me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about making up the fasts of the month of Ramadan separately, and he said: 'That is up to you. Tell me, if one of you had a debt and paid one dirham or two dirhams, would that not be a fulfillment? For Allah is more deserving to pardon or to forgive.'"" Ali said: ""Its chain is hasan, except that it is mursal, and someone other than Abu Bakr connected it from Yahya ibn Sulaym, and it is not established as muttasil."" The Shaykh said: ""And it has been reported from another weak source from Ibn Umar as marfu', and in contrast to it from Abu Hurayrah regarding the prohibition of separation as marfu'. How can that be correct when the madhhab of Abu Hurayrah is the permissibility of separation, and the madhhab of Ibn Umar is consecutiveness? And it has been reported from another source from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As as marfu' regarding the permissibility of separation, and none of that is correct."" [Munkar. Ibn Shabih.]"
"8247","(8244) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd ibn Ṣakhr al-Dārimī narrated to us; Ḥabbān ibn Hilāl narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ibrāhīm narrated from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from his father from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever has the fasts of Ramadan outstanding, let him observe them consecutively and not break them up."" ʿAlī said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ibrāhīm is weak (ḍaʿīf). The Shaykh said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ibrāhīm is a Madinan whom Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Nasāʾī, and al-Dāraquṭnī have declared weak. [Weak — al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"8248","(8245) ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; al-Thawrī narrated from Abū Isḥāq from al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the making up of Ramadan fasts, he said: ""Consecutively (tatanāpuʿan)."" [Weak]"
"8249","(8245) He said: And al-Thawrī informed us from ʿAbd Allāh from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Consecutively."" And it was narrated by ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd from Zuhayr from Abū Isḥāq from al-Ḥārith from ʿAlī: ""That he did not see any harm in making them up separately."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8250","(8247) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us; Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: ""That he would not separate the making up of Ramadan fasts."" Thus said Ibn ʿUmar. And there is a difference of opinion regarding this from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, and the narrator is al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar. And al-Ḥārith is weak. [Ṣaḥīḥ — its narrators are reliable]"
"8251","(8248) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from Abī ʿUbayd, the freedman of Ibn Azhar, who said: ""I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). He came and prayed, then turned and addressed the people, saying: 'These are two days regarding which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade fasting: the day of your breaking fast from your fasting, and the other is the day on which you eat from your sacrifice.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8252","(8249) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb — and this is his hadith — both said: Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abī ʿUbayd, who said: ""I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). He began with the prayer before the sermon, then said: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade fasting on these two days: as for the Day of Sacrifice (al-Aḍḥā), you eat from your sacrifices, and as for the Day of Breaking Fast (al-Fiṭr), it is your breaking of the fast from your fasting.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8253","(8250) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us; Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated to me; Ḥakīm ibn Abī Ḥurrah narrated: ""That he heard a man asking ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar about a man who vowed that no day would pass by which he named except that he would be fasting on it, and that coincided with the Day of Sacrifice (al-Aḍḥā) or the Day of Breaking Fast (al-Fiṭr). Ibn ʿUmar said: 'Indeed, in the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) you have an excellent example.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not fast on the Day of Sacrifice nor on the Day of Breaking Fast, nor did he command fasting on them."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr al-Muqaddamī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8254","(8251) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Faqīh in Ṭabarān informed us; Abū ʿAlī Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ṣawwāf informed us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; both said: Muḥammad ibn Sābiq narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from Abī al-Zubayr from Ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik from his father, who narrated to him: ""That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent him and Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān during the Days of Tashrīq, and he proclaimed: 'None shall enter Paradise except a believer, and the Days of Minā are days of eating and drinking.'"" Their wording is the same. Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Muḥammad ibn Sābiq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Muslim]"
"8255","(8252) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīb ibn Milḥān informed us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Ibn al-Hādī from Abī Murrah, the freedman of ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib: ""That he and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr entered upon ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and that was the day after or two days after the Day of Sacrifice. ʿAmr presented food to them, and ʿAbd Allāh said: 'I am fasting.' ʿAmr said to him: 'Break your fast, for these are the days in which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us to break the fast and forbade us from fasting.' So ʿAbd Allāh broke his fast and ate, and we ate with him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"8256","(8253) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Zāhid informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Wakīʿ narrated from al-Aʿmash from Abī Ẓabyān from Ibn ʿAbbās: ""That ablution (wuḍūʾ) from food and cupping for the fasting person were mentioned in his presence."" Al-Aʿmash said once: ""He said: 'Ablution is only from what exits, not from what enters, and the breaking of the fast is only from what enters, not from what exits.'"" And it has been narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said to Laqīt ibn Ṣabirah: ""Be thorough in sniffing water into the nose, unless you are fasting."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr]"
"8257","(8254) Al-Faqīh Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shurayḥī informed us; Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us; Sharīk reported from Sulaymān, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""There is no harm in the fasting person tasting something — meaning broth and the like."" [Weak. Reported by Ibn al-Jaʿd.]"
"8258","(8255) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us; al-Layth reported from Bukayr, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāllāh — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""One day I felt desire, so I kissed while I was fasting. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'Today I have done a grave thing — I kissed while I was fasting.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'What do you think — if you were to rinse your mouth with water while you are fasting?' I said: 'There is no harm in that.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Then what is the problem?'"" [Sound. Its meaning has been extracted elsewhere.]"
"8259","(8256) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Maryam informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Sufyān reported from Ismāʿīl ibn Kathīr, from ʿĀṣim ibn Laqīṭ ibn Ṣabrah, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: 'Interlace your fingers, perform the wuḍūʾ thoroughly, and when you sniff water into the nose, do so vigorously — unless you are fasting.'"" [Sound. Its meaning has been extracted elsewhere.]"
"8260","(8257) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; ʿAbbād — meaning Ibn Manṣūr — reported from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Use kohl (ithmid), for it sharpens the vision and promotes hair growth."" He (the narrator) claimed that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a kohl container from which he applied kohl every night — three applications in this eye and three in that. This is the most authentic narration reported regarding the Prophet's ﷺ use of kohl. [Weak. Reported by al-Tirmidhī.]"
"8261","(8258) It has been narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ — who is not strong — from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet ﷺ used kohl (ithmid) while he was fasting. Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī reported it to us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṭākī informed us; Luwayn informed us; Ḥibbān ibn ʿAlī reported from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ. Likewise, Maʿmar narrated it from Muḥammad from his father with the same meaning. [Denounced (Munkar). Reported by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
"8262","(8259) Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Zubaydī, the companion of Baqiyyah, narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: ""Sometimes the Prophet ﷺ applied kohl while he was fasting."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr reported it to us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Ṭayyib informed us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd reported from Saʿīd al-Zubaydī, and he mentioned it. Saʿīd al-Zubaydī is among the unknown shaykhs of Baqiyyah, narrating unique reports that are not corroborated. It has also been narrated from Anas ibn Mālik in a marfūʿ chain with a weak isnād, that he saw no harm in it. A hadith prohibiting it during the daytime while fasting has also been reported, which al-Bukhārī extracted in al-Tārīkh. [Denounced (Munkar). Reported by Abū Yaʿlā.]"
"8263","(8260) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Abū al-Nuʿmān al-Anṣārī informed me; my father reported from my grandfather — and his grandfather had come to the Prophet ﷺ, who placed his hand upon his head and said: ""Do not apply kohl during the daytime while you are fasting; apply it at night. Kohl (ithmid) sharpens the vision and promotes hair growth."" The Shaykh said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān is Ibn al-Nuʿmān ibn Maʿbad ibn Hawdhah, Abū al-Nuʿmān, and Maʿbad ibn Hawdhah al-Anṣārī is the one who had this companionship. [Denounced (Munkar). Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8264","(8261) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Naṣr al-Marwazī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us; al-Qaʿnabī informed us — in what he recited to Mālik — from Sumayy, the freedman of Abū Bakr, from Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command the people to break their fast during the year of the Conquest and he said: 'Strengthen yourselves against your enemy.' And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted."" Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said, and the one who narrated to me said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-ʿArj pouring water over his head while he was fasting, due to thirst — or he said, due to the heat."" [Sound. Its meaning has been extracted elsewhere.]"
"8265","(8262) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb reported from al-Mundhir ibn Abī al-Mundhir, who said: ""I saw Ibn ʿAbbās — may Allah be pleased with him — splashing water into the basins of Zamzam while he was fasting."" [Weak. al-Jannah ibn al-Mundhir.]"
"8266","(8263) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Tamtām, Ibn Abī Qammāsh, and Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mukharramī said: Abū Maʿmar informed us; ʿAbd al-Wārith reported from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ underwent cupping (ḥijāmah) while he was fasting. In the narration of Tamtām: ""Ayyūb informed us,"" and the rest are the same. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Maʿmar. [Sound. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8267","(8264) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us that Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us that al-Firyābī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Yazīd, meaning Ibn Abī Ziyād, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) performed cupping (ḥijāmah) between Makkah and Madīnah while he was fasting and in the state of iḥrām.""

It was also narrated by Maymūn ibn Mihrān from Ibn ʿAbbās. [Weak. Reported by Aḥmad.]"
"8268","(8265) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed me that Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us that Ādam narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Ḥumayd, who said: ""I heard Thābit al-Bunānī asking Anas ibn Mālik: 'Did you used to dislike cupping for one who is fasting?' He said: 'No, except on account of weakness.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Shuʿbah, who said: ""I heard Thābit al-Bunānī say: 'Anas was asked.'""

The authentic version is what we have narrated from Ādam, for Abū al-Naḍr narrated it from Shuʿbah from Ḥumayd, as we have narrated it. [Authentic. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8269","(8266) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā from a man among the Companions of Muḥammad (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prohibited continuous fasting (al-wiṣāl) and cupping for the one who is fasting, out of consideration for his Companions, and he did not declare them forbidden. It was said to him: 'You yourself fast continuously.' He said: 'Indeed, I am shaded, and my Lord feeds me and gives me drink.'"" [Authentic. Reported by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8270","(8267) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, Abū al-Shaykh, narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us that Bundār narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah from Abī al-Mutawakkil al-Nājī from Abī Saʿīd, who said: ""Cupping was disliked for the one who is fasting only out of fear of weakness."" [Authentic. Reported by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
"8271","(8268) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Aḥmad al-Daqqāq and Abū ʿUbayd ibn al-Maḥāmilī both narrated to us that Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī narrated to us that al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated from Ḥumayd from Abī al-Mutawakkil from Abī Saʿīd, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) granted permission for the fasting person regarding kissing and cupping.""

ʿAlī said: ""All of them are trustworthy (thiqāt), but other than al-Muʿtamir narrate it as a mawqūf (stopped) narration.""

The Shaykh said: ""It has also been narrated through another chain from Abī al-Mutawakkil as a marfūʿ (elevated) narration."" [Authentic. Reported by Ibn Khuzaymah and al-Nasāʾī.]"
"8272","(8269) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-ʿAṭṭār al-Ḥīrī narrated it to us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Dāwūd al-Simnānī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿīd al-Jawharī narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated from Sufyān from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ from Abī al-Mutawakkil from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) granted permission for cupping for the fasting person."
"8273","(8270) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Muʿaddal, Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿUthmān, in Wāsiṭ, narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn Khalaf al-Bazzāz narrated to us that Isḥāq al-Azraq narrated to us that Sufyān narrated, mentioning it with the same wording.

ʿAlī said: ""All of them are trustworthy (thiqāt)."" And al-Ashjaʿī also narrated it, and he is among the trustworthy narrators.

The Shaykh said: ""However, al-Ashjaʿī said in his narration: 'Permission was granted (rukhiṣa).'"" [Authentic. See the preceding narration.]"
"8274","(8271) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to me that ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhṭawayh narrated to us that Yazīd ibn al-Haytham narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth narrated to us that al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Sufyān from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ from Abī al-Mutawakkil from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Permission was granted for the fasting person regarding cupping and kissing.""

It has also been narrated through another chain from Abī Saʿīd. [Authentic. See the preceding narration.]"
"8275","(8272) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī in al-Kūfah informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghurzah narrated to us that Yaḥyā, meaning al-Ḥimmānī, narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from his father from ʿAṭāʾ from Abī Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The one who vomits does not break his fast, nor does the one who performs cupping, nor does the one who experiences a wet dream."" [Denounced (Munkar). Reported by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
"8276","(8273) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Ḥajabī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, Abū Zayd, narrated to us that my father narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Three things do not break the fast of the fasting person: vomiting, cupping, and the wet dream.""

This is how ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd narrated it, and he is not strong (laysa bi-l-qawī). [Denounced (Munkar). See the preceding narration.]"
"8277","(8274) The authentic narration is that of Sufyān al-Thawrī and others from Zayd ibn Aslam, from one of his companions, from one of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The one who vomits does not break his fast, nor does the one who performs cupping (ḥijāmah), nor the one who experiences a wet dream.""

It was narrated to us by ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān, who was informed by Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad, who narrated to us Isḥāq al-Dabarī from ʿAbd al-Razzāq from al-Thawrī, and he mentioned it. A similar narration has been reported from al-Thawrī as that of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd, but it is not authentic.

We have also been narrated regarding the permissibility of cupping from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, Zayd ibn Arqam, ʿĀʾishah bint al-Ṣiddīq, and Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with them all).

[Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8278","(8275) Abū Bakr al-Fārisī informed us, who was informed by Abū Isḥāq al-Aṣbahānī, who narrated to us Abū Aḥmad ibn Fāris, who narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī, who narrated to me ʿAyyāsh, who narrated to me ʿAbd al-Aʿlā from Yūnus from al-Ḥasan from the Prophet (ﷺ).

[Authentic in its meaning through other chains]"
"8279","(8276) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, who was informed by Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Isfarāʾinī, who narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Barāʾ, who narrated to us ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, who narrated to us al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān from his father from al-Ḥasan from more than one of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

ʿAlī said: Yūnus narrated it from al-Ḥasan from Abū Hurayrah.

Qatādah narrated it from al-Ḥasan from Thawbān. ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib narrated it from al-Ḥasan from Maʿqil ibn Yasār. Maṭar narrated it from al-Ḥasan from ʿAlī from the Prophet (ﷺ).

Imām Aḥmad said: It was also narrated by al-Ashʿath from al-Ḥasan from Usāmah ibn Zayd from the Prophet (ﷺ), and ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī also mentioned it.

[Authentic in its meaning through other chains]"
"8280","(8277) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī, who was informed by Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Qalānisī, who narrated to us ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsā, who narrated to us Abū ʿĀṣim al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad, who narrated to us al-Ashʿath from al-Ḥasan from Usāmah ibn Zayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

[Authentic in its meaning through other chains]"
"8281","(8278) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us, who was informed by Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, who narrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf, who narrated to us Abū al-Mughīrah, who informed us al-Awzāʿī.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Sūsī informed us, who were informed by Abū al-ʿAbbās, who informed us al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd, who informed us my father, who said: I heard al-Awzāʿī narrate to me Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, who narrated to me Abū Qilābah al-Jarmī, who narrated to me Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī, who narrated to me Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the eighteenth night of Ramaḍān. Suddenly, a man was performing cupping at al-Baqīʿ. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

Similarly, it was narrated by Shaybān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Naḥwī and Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbd Allāh al-Dastuwāʾī from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr. However, Maʿmir ibn Rāshid contradicted them and narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qāriẓ from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd from Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8282","(8279) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him), who was informed by Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī, who narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr and Abū al-Azhar and Ḥamdān al-Sulamī, who said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, who informed us Maʿmir from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qāriẓ from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd from Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

[Authentic in its meaning through other chains. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī]"
"8283","(8280) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us, who was informed by Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār, who narrated to us Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr, who narrated to us ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and he mentioned it with the same wording.

Similarly, it was narrated by Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām from Yaḥyā.

[Authentic in its meaning through other chains]"
"8284","(8281) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, who narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh, who narrated to us ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk, who narrated to us al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ, who narrated to us Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, and he mentioned it with a similar wording. It is as though Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated the ḥadīth through both chains of narration together.

It has also been said that Abū Qilābah narrated it from Abū al-Ashʿath from Shaddād ibn Aws.

[Authentic in its meaning through other chains. See the previous]"
"8285","(8282) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, who narrated to us Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh, who narrated to us ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, who was informed by Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah, who narrated to us Abū Dāwūd, who said: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, who narrated to us Wuhibb, who narrated to us Ayyūb from Abū Qilābah from Abū al-Ashʿath from Shaddād ibn Aws: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came upon a man at al-Baqīʿ who was performing cupping, while he (the Prophet) was holding my hand, on the eighteenth night of Ramaḍān. He said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

Abū Dāwūd said: Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ narrated it from Abū Qilābah with the same chain as Ayyūb.

Imām Aḥmad said: It was also narrated by ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from Abū Qilābah from Abū al-Ashʿath from Shaddād. It is as though Abū Qilābah heard the ḥadīth from both men together. It has also been said that ʿĀṣim narrated from Abū Qilābah from Abū al-Ashʿath from Abū Asmāʾ from Shaddād.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8286","(8283) It was narrated to us by ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān, who was informed by Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd, who narrated to us al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah, who narrated to us Yazīd ibn Hārūn, who informed us ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd, who is Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Ashʿath al-Ṣanʿānī from Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī from Shaddād ibn Aws, who said: I passed by with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the eighteenth night of the month of Ramaḍān. He saw a man performing cupping and said: ""The one who performs cupping (al-ḥājim) and the one upon whom cupping is performed (al-maḥjūm) have both broken their fast.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"8287","(8284) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Muḥammad al-Azharī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Barāʾ narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī said: ""I do not consider the two hadiths except to be sound. It is possible that Abū Asmāʾ heard it from both of them.""

Imām Aḥmad said: ""It has been narrated from another route from Abū Asmāʾ, from Thawbān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8288","(8285) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī by dictation, and Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh by recitation; they both said: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; Marwān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Yaḥyya ibn Ḥamzah narrated to me; Abū al-Muhallab Rāshid ibn Dāwūd al-Ṣanʿānī narrated to us; Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī narrated to us, from Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a man in al-Baqīʿ who was being cupped on the eighteenth or nineteenth of Ramadan, and he said: ""The cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast.""

It was narrated by al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥārith and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, from Makhūl, from Abū Asmāʾ, from Thawbān.

It was narrated by Ibn Jurayj, from Makhūl, that a shaykh from the tribe informed him that Thawbān, the freedman of the Prophet ﷺ, informed him that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: ""The cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
"8289","(8286) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr and ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ""Makhūl informed me,"" and he mentioned it.

It was narrated from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, from the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8290","(8287) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Azhar and Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Marwazī Zāj both narrated to us; Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated to us, from Maṭar al-Warrāq, from Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī, from Abū Rāfiʿ, who said: ""I entered upon Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī while he was being cupped at night during Ramadan. I said: 'Would this not have been better during the day?' He said: 'Do you command me to spill my blood while I am fasting? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast.'""

Thus narrated Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. And it was narrated by ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, from Saʿīd, from one of his companions, from Ibn Buraydah, from Abū Mūsā, as a marfūʿ hadith. And it was narrated by Shuʿbah, from Maṭar, from Bakr, from Abū Rāfiʿ, from Abū Mūsā, as a mawqūf hadith.

Similarly, it was narrated by Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl, from Bakr, as mawqūf and not marfūʿ. It was narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
"8291","(8288) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā.]"
"8292","(8289) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Dāwūd al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: ""Abū Hurayrah said,"" and he said ""al-mustahjim"" (the one seeking cupping) instead of ""al-maḥjūm"" (the one being cupped).

It was narrated by Qabīṣah, from Fiṭr ibn Khalīfah, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭaḥāwī.]"
"8293","(8290) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr; they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated to us; Fiṭr narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast.""

Thus narrated a group from Qabīṣah. And it was narrated by Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān, from Qabīṣah, that he narrated to him from his book, from Fiṭr, from ʿAṭāʾ, from the Prophet ﷺ, as a mursal hadith. And this is the preserved (maḥfūẓ) version, and the mention of Ibn ʿAbbās in it is an error (wahm).

It was narrated by ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah, as a mawqūf hadith. And it was narrated by Layth ibn Abī Sulaym, from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet ﷺ. And Allah knows best. [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
"8294","(8291) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us; I heard Abū Ḥāmid al-Sharqī say: I heard ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd al-Nasawī say: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal being asked: ""Which hadith is most authentic in your view regarding 'the cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast'?"" He said: ""The hadith of Thawbān, from the hadith of Yaḥyya ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū Asmāʾ, from Thawbān."" It was said to Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal: ""Then what about the hadith of Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj?"" He said: ""That one was narrated uniquely by Maʿmar."" Abū Ḥāmid said: ""And it was narrated by Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām, from Yaḥyya ibn Abī Kathīr."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. This chain is sound.]"
"8295","(8292) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Tamīmī informed us; I heard Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah say: I heard al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAẓīm al-ʿAnbarī say: I heard ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh say: ""I do not know of any hadith more authentic than this regarding 'the cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast'—meaning the hadith of Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8296","(8293) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: I heard Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī say: I heard ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī say: ""The hadith 'the cupper and the one being cupped have broken their fast' has been authenticated for me through the hadith of Thawbān and Shaddād ibn Aws, and I act upon it. And I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal act upon it and mention that the hadith of Thawbān and Shaddād was authenticated for him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8297","(8294) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Ibn Abī ʿIṣmah narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Abī Yaḥyá narrated to us — I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: ""The hadiths 'the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast' and 'there is no marriage without a guardian' are hadiths that mutually reinforce one another, and I hold to them."" [Weak. Narrated by Ibn ʿUrsī.]"
"8298","(8295) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; I heard Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāniʾ say: I heard Aḥmad ibn Salamah say: I heard Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm say regarding the hadith of Shaddād ibn Aws: ""This is a sound isnād by which the proof is established, and this hadith is sound through its chains of narration, and we hold to it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"8299","(8296) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Isfarāyinī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Barrāʾ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī said: The hadith of Shaddād ibn Aws from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — that he saw a man being cupped in Ramadan — was narrated by ʀāsim al-Aḥwal from Abū Qilābah from Abī al-Ashʿath from Shaddād. And it was narrated by Yaḥyá ibn Abī Kathīr from Abū Qilābah from Abī Asmāʾ from Thawbān. I do not regard the two hadiths except as sound, for it is possible that he (Abū Qilābah) heard it from both of them together. [Ṣaḥīḥ. This isnād.]"
"8300","(8297) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd said: I said to Aḥmad, meaning Ibn Ḥanbal: ""Which hadith is most sound regarding 'the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast'?"" He said: ""The hadith of Ibn Jurayj from Makḥūl from a shaykh of the tribe from Thawbān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8301","(8298) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; I heard Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ say: I said to ʿAbdān al-Ahwāzī: ""Is it established that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while fasting?"" He said: I heard ʿAbbās al-ʿAnbarī say: I heard ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī say: ""The hadith of Abū Rāfiʿ from Abū Mūsá is sound — that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.'""

And it reached me from Abū ʿĪsá al-Tirmidhī that he said: I asked Abū Zurʿah about the hadīth of ʀāṭāʾ from Abī Hurayrah, marfūʿ (elevated to the Prophet), and he said: ""It is a ḥasan hadith."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. This isnād is sound.]"
"8302","(8299) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn narrated from Hushaym, from Manṣūr, from Abū Qilābah, from Abī al-Ashʿath, from Shaddād ibn Aws, who said: ""I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of the Conquest (of Makkah). He passed by a man being cupped on the eighteenth or seventeenth of Ramadan, and he said: 'The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Meaning extracted.]"
"8303","(8300) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated from Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ, from Abū Qilābah, from Abī al-Ashʿath, from Shaddād ibn Aws, who said: ""We were with the Prophet ﷺ at the time of the Conquest. He saw a man being cupped on the eighteenth night that had elapsed of Ramadan, and he said — while he was holding my hand: 'The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it.]"
"8304","(8301) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped while in a state of iḥrām and while fasting.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Ibn ʿAbbās's hearing from the Prophet ﷺ was in the year of the Conquest, and he was not in a state of iḥrām at that time, and he did not accompany anyone in iḥrām before the Farewell Pilgrimage. So Ibn ʿAbbās mentioned the cupping of the Prophet ﷺ in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage, in the tenth year, and the hadith 'the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast' was in the eighth year, two years before the Farewell Pilgrimage. If both are established, then the hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās is the nāskh (abrogating) and the hadith 'the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast' is the mansūkh (abrogated).""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The isnād of both hadiths is similar, but the hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās has the better isnād. If a man avoids cupping, it is more beloved to me as a precaution, lest he expose his fast to weakness and thus break it. But if he is cupped, cupping does not invalidate his fast. Alongside the hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās, there is the qiyās (analogical reasoning) which I have preserved from some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Tābiʿīn and the generality of the Madinans — that no one's fast is invalidated by cupping."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it.]"
"8305","(8302) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Muthanná narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""The first time cupping was disliked for the fasting person was when Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — was cupped while fasting. The Prophet ﷺ passed by him and said: 'These two have broken their fast.' Then the Prophet ﷺ thereafter granted permission for cupping for the fasting person, and Anas used to be cupped while fasting."" ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Dāraquṭnī said: ""All of them are trustworthy (thiqāt), and I know of no defect in it.""

Al-Shaykh said: ""The hadith of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī with the wording of permissibility (al-tarkhīṣ) indicates this, for the most probable case is that permissibility comes after prohibition. And Allah knows best."" [Weak. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8306","(8303) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Rabīʿah narrated to us; Abū al-Ashʿath narrated from Thawbān, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a man who was being cupped by a cupper while he was reciting (Qurʾān) for a man, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.'"" His words ""while he was reciting for a man"" — I did not record it except in this hadith. Others narrated it from Abū al-Ashʿath from Shaddād ibn Aws without this wording, and Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī narrated it from Thawbān without this wording. And Allah knows best. [Very weak. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr.]"
"8307","Nāfiʿ reported: Ibn ʿUmar used to perform cupping (ḥijāmah) while he was fasting, then he abandoned it afterwards and would perform cupping at night. I do not know whether he mentioned something he had heard or something he had stated."
"8308","Nāfiʿ reported from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to perform cupping during the month of Ramaḍān at the time of breaking the fast (iftār)."
"8309","Saʿīd ibn ʿĪsá reported from his grandmother that she heard Umm Ḥabībah, the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him), say: ""The fasting person should not chew [any] chewing gum."" The Sheikh said: His grandmother was Umm al-Rabīʿ, and the hadith is mawqūf (a statement stopping at the Companion)."
"8310","Ibn ʿAbbās reported: ʿAlī passed by a madwoman from the Banū Fulān who had committed adultery and was being stoned. ʿAlī said to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both): ""O Commander of the Faithful, did you order the stoning of so-and-so?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Do you not remember the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him): 'The pen has been lifted from three: from the sleeper until he awakens, from the child until he reaches puberty, and from the insane until he regains sanity'?"" He said: ""Yes."" So he ordered that she be released."
"8311","Sufyān ibn ʿAṭiyyah ibn Rabīʿah al-Thaqafī reported: Our delegation from Thaqīf came to the Prophet (peace be upon him), and he pitched a tent for them. They embraced Islam in the middle of Ramaḍān. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) commanded them, so they fasted the remaining days thereof, and he did not command them to make up what had passed."
"8312","Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Fasting is a shield (junnah). When any one of you is fasting, let him not indulge in obscene speech nor act ignorantly. If someone fights him or insults him, let him say: 'I am fasting.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah from Abū al-Zinād."
"8313","Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Every deed of the son of Adam is for him except fasting, for it is for Me and I shall reward for it. Fasting is a shield. When it is the day of fasting for any one of you, let him not indulge in obscene speech that day, nor raise his voice. If someone reviles him or fights him, let him say: 'I am a fasting person.' By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, the smell of the mouth of the fasting person is more pleasant to Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the fragrance of musk. The fasting person has two occasions of joy in which he rejoices: when he breaks his fast, he rejoices at his breaking of the fast, and when he meets his Lord, he rejoices at his fast."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Yūsuf from Ibn Jurayj, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Ibn Jurayj. In the ḥadīth of Hishām at the beginning of the hadith, it is: ""Allah said: 'Every deed of the son of Adam is for him.'"""
"8314","Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Whoever does not give up false speech, acting upon it, and ignorant behaviour, Allah has no need for him to give up his food and drink."" Aḥmad said: I understood its chain of narration from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, and a man beside him—I think he was his brother—explained the hadith to me. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus and Ādam ibn Abī Iyās."
"8315","Abū Hurayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Fasting is not merely abstaining from food and drink; rather, fasting is abstaining from vain talk and obscene speech. If someone reviles you or acts ignorantly toward you, say: 'I am fasting.'"""
"8316","Abū Hurayrah reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""How many a person who stands in prayer gains from his standing nothing but sleeplessness, and how many a fasting person gains from his fasting nothing but hunger and thirst."""
"8317","(8314) Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Bahr ibn Nasser narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to me: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Ibn Abi Sayf, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Iyadh ibn Ghutaif, from Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""Fasting is a shield, so long as it is not torn."" [Da'if. Narrated by al-Nasa'i]"
"8318","(8315) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Rawh narrated — (H) — and Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to us: Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bilal al-Bazzaz narrated to us: Abu al-Azhar narrated to us: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to me, from Ata', that he heard Ibn Abbas reciting: {And upon those who are able to fast [but with difficulty] is a ransom of feeding a poor person} [al-Baqarah:184]. Ibn Abbas said: ""It is not abrogated. It applies to the elderly man and the elderly woman who are unable to fast; so let each of them feed one poor person in place of every day."" This is the wording of the hadith of al-Saghani. [Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Ishaq ibn Mansur from Rawh]. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari]"
"8319","(8316) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us: Warqa' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas, regarding His statement {And upon those who are able to fast} — meaning, those who endure difficulty but are not truly able {is a ransom of feeding a poor person, but whoever volunteers it is better for him} — so he feeds another poor person {and that is better for him}. It is not abrogated. Ibn Abbas said: ""No concession was granted in this except for the elderly man who is unable to fast and the sick person who knows that he will not recover."" [Hasan. Narrated by al-Hakim]"
"8320","(8317) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Abbas, that is al-Asamm, narrated to us: Usayd ibn Asim narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to recite it: {And upon those who are able to fast}. He said: ""That is the elderly man who is unable to fast, so he breaks his fast and feeds half a sa' in place of each day."" In this narration it is specified: half a sa' of wheat. It is also reported from him that he said: ""A mudd of food and a mudd of condiment."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tabrani]"
"8321","(8318) Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami and Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih narrated to us: Ali ibn Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Mansur ibn Abi al-Jahm al-Shaybani narrated to us: Nasser ibn Ali narrated to us: Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us: Khalid al-Hadha' narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: ""When the elderly man becomes unable to fast, let him feed one mudd for every day, one mudd."" And Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Faqih in Rayy narrated to us: Abu Hatim Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Raqashi narrated to us: Wuhaib narrated to us: Khalid al-Hadha' narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: ""The elderly man has been granted a concession to break his fast and feed one poor person for every day, and there is no making up [of missed fasts] upon him."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Daraqutni]"
"8322","(8319) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Abu Salih narrated to me: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me that Abu Hamzah narrated to him, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: ""Whoever is overtaken by old age and is unable to fast the month of Ramadan, upon him is one mudd of wheat for every day."" [Da'if. Narrated by al-Daraqutni]"
"8323","(8320) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih narrated to us: Ali ibn Umar narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wakil narrated to us: Ibn Arafah narrated to us: Rawh narrated to us: Sa'id and Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, that Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, grew weak a year before his death, so he broke his fast and instructed his family to feed one poor person in place of every day. Hisham said in his hadith: ""So he fed thirty poor persons."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tabrani]"
"8324","(8321) Abu Tahir al-Faqih narrated to him: Abdus ibn al-Husayn ibn Mansur narrated to us: Abu Hatim al-Razi narrated to us: al-Ansari said: Humayd narrated to me, saying: ""Anas was unable to fast Ramadan in the year in which he passed away, and he knew that he would not be able to make it up. So I asked his son Umar ibn Anas: 'What did Abu Hamzah do?' He said: 'We prepared for him large bowls of bread and meat, and we fed the number — or more — meaning thirty men, one man for every day.'"" [Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat)]"
"8325","(8322) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith narrated to us: Ali ibn Umar narrated to us: Abu Salih al-Isbahani narrated to us: Abu Mas'ud narrated to us: Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maisarah, from Mujahid, who said: I heard Qays ibn al-Sa'ib saying: ""Indeed, a man may ransom the month of Ramadan by having a poor person fed on his behalf for every day. So feed on my behalf two poor persons."" [Hasan. Narrated by al-Tabrani]"
"8326","(8323) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah narrated to us: Abu Mansur al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us: Ya'qub narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, regarding His statement {And upon those who are able to fast is a ransom of feeding a poor person} [al-Baqarah:184], he said: ""That refers to the elderly man who used to fast but then became unable, and the pregnant woman for whom fasting is difficult — upon both of them is the feeding of one poor person for every day, until the month of Ramadan comes to an end."" [Hasan]"
"8327","(8324) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar informed me from Abū ʿAmr, the freedman of ʿĀʾishah, that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to recite: {And upon those who are able to fast [but do not], a ransom [as compensation]}. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī]"
"8328","(8325) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, saying: It was read to ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb: Sufyān al-Thawrī informed you that ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated to him from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah al-ʿAdawī, from his father, who said: ""I cannot count nor enumerate how many times I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) using the siwāk while he was fasting."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8329","(8326) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muʿādh ibn al-Muthannā informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn informed us: Abū Ismāʿīl al-Muʾaddib narrated from Mujāld, from al-Shaʿbī, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The best characteristic of one who fasts is the siwāk."" Mujāld is not as reliable as others, and ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbaydullāh is not strong. And Allah knows best. As for the hadith which… [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Mājah]"
"8330","(8327) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fihr informed us in Makkah: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Jaʿfar ibn Maymūn al-Balkhī informed us in Makkah: Ibrāhīm ibn Yūsuf ibn Maymūn al-Balkhī informed us: Abū Isḥāq al-Khwārizmī, the judge of Khwārizm, informed us — he came to us during the days of ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā — saying: I asked ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, ""Does one who fasts use the siwāk?"" He said, ""Yes."" I said, ""With a moist siwāk and a dry one?"" He said, ""Yes."" I said, ""At the beginning of the day and at the end of it?"" He said, ""Yes."" I said, ""On whose authority?"" He said, ""From Anas ibn Mālik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."" This is narrated uniquely by Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Biṭār — also called Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the judge of Khwārizm — who narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal with objectionable content; he cannot be used as a proof. It has also been narrated from him through another chain, which does not contain the mention of the beginning and the end of the day. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"8331","(8328) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Mazdak al-Bukhārī informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Wāṣil informed us: Muḥammad ibn Sallām informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I asked ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal about the siwāk for one who is fasting, and he said, ""There is no harm in it."" I said, ""With a moist siwāk and a dry one?"" He said, ""Do you consider it to have more moisture than water?"" I said, ""On whose authority?"" He said, ""From Anas ibn Mālik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."" Abū Aḥmad said: The majority of this Ibrāhīm's narrations are not preserved. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn ʿAdī]"
"8332","(8329) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Miṣʿar narrated from Abū Nahīk al-Asadī, from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr, who said: ""I have not seen anyone more diligent in using the siwāk while fasting than ʿUmar."" He (the narrator) said: ""I think he said, 'with a dried piece of wood.'"" Abū ʿUbayd said: ""Meaning, with dry wood."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"8333","(8330) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Wakīʿ narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ, the freedman of Ibn ʿUmar, from his father, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he used to use the siwāk while he was fasting. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8334","(8331) Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Qurashī al-Harawī narrated in al-Rawḍah, saying: I read to Abū ʿAlī al-Raffāʾ, and I said to him: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed you: Abū Nuʿaym informed us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Allah says: 'Fasting is for Me, and I shall reward it. He abandons his desire, his eating, and his drinking for My sake. Fasting is a shield. The fasting person has two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord. And the changed smell of the fasting person's mouth is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Close in meaning]"
"8335","(8332) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Hāshim al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥushaysh al-Tamīmī al-Muqriʾ in Kufah informed us — al-ʿAlawī said ""informed us"" and al-Muqriʾ said ""narrated to us"" — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh informed us: Wakīʿ narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Every deed of the son of Adam is multiplied: a good deed is rewarded ten times its like up to seven hundred times. Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, said: 'Except fasting, for it is for Me, and I shall reward it. He abandons his food and his desire for My sake.' The fasting person has two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord. And the changed smell of the fasting person's mouth is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk. Fasting is a shield. Fasting is a shield."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Saʿid al-Ashajj from Wakīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8336","(8333) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad informed us: Abū al-Muthannā (ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muʿādh ibn al-Muthannā informed us: Isḥāq ibn ʿUmar ibn Salīṭ informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīm ibn Muslim informed us: Ḍirār ibn Murrah narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd, who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, says: 'Fasting is for Me, and I shall reward it. The fasting person has two joys: when he breaks his fast, he rejoices; and when he meets his Lord and He rewards him, he rejoices. And the changed smell of the fasting person's mouth is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn ʿUmar ibn Salīṭ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8337","(8334) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us. He said: I heard al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad saying: I heard Yahya ibn Ma'in saying: 'Ali ibn Thabit narrated to us, from Kaysan Abu 'Umar, from Yazid ibn Bilal, his freedman — and he had witnessed Siffin with 'Ali — from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The fasting person should not use the miswak in the afternoon, but rather at night. Indeed, the dryness of the fasting person's lips shall be a light between his eyes on the Day of Resurrection."" [Weak. Al-Tabarani]"
"8338","(8335) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us: Abu al-Tayyib al-Muzaffar ibn Sahl al-Khalil narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ayyub ibn Hassan al-Wasiti narrated to us, from his father, who said: I heard a man ask Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, saying: ""O Abu Muhammad, regarding what the Prophet (ﷺ) narrates from his Lord, exalted and glorified: 'Every deed of the son of Adam is for himself except fasting, for it is for Me and I shall reward for it' — what is its meaning?"" Ibn 'Uyaynah said: ""This is among the finest and most precise of hadiths. When the Day of Resurrection comes, Allah, exalted and glorified, will hold His servant accountable and will have him repay all injustices owed from the rest of his deeds, until nothing remains except fasting. Then Allah will bear upon Himself whatever injustices remain against him, and will admit him into Paradise by virtue of his fasting."" [Weak. Al-Zaffar ibn Sahl]"
"8339","(8336) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Abu Khurasan Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us: 'Abd al-Samad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us: Abu 'Umar al-Qassab, i.e., Kaysan, narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Bilal, from 'Ali, who said: ""When you fast, use the miswak in the morning and do not use it in the afternoon. For there is no fasting person whose lips dry up in the afternoon except that they shall be a light between his eyes on the Day of Resurrection."" [Weak. Its chain of narration has already been discussed.]"
"8340","(8337) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar narrated to us: Abu 'Ubayd narrated to us: Abu Khurasan narrated to us: 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us: Kaysan Abu 'Umar narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Khabbab, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar narration. 'Ali said: ""Kaysan Abu 'Umar is not strong, and the narrators between him and 'Ali are not well known."" [Denounced. Reported by al-Daraqutni]"
"8341","(8338) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Khayyat narrated to us: Abu Mansur narrated to us: 'Umar ibn Qays narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Abu Hurayrah, who said: ""The miswak is for you up until 'Asr. When you have prayed 'Asr, discard it, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'The changed odour of the mouth of the fasting person is more pleasing to Allah than the fragrance of musk.'"" [Weak. Al-Daraqutni]"
"8342","(8339) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed me: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Imam narrated to us: Abu Kamil narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us. (H) He also said: Abu 'Amr informed me: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mu'adh al-'Aqadi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Talhah ibn Yahya ibn Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah narrated to us. 'A'ishah bint Talhah narrated to me, from 'A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me one day: ""O 'A'ishah, do you have anything?"" She said: I said: ""No, by Allah, we have nothing."" He said: ""I am fasting."" She said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out, and then a gift was given to us, or visitors came to us. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) returned, I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a gift was given to us, or visitors came to us, and I have set something aside for you."" He said: ""What is it?"" I said: ""Hays."" He said: ""Bring it."" So I brought it and he ate, then he said: ""I had intended to fast this morning."" Abu 'Abdullah said: The wording is that of al-'Aqadi. Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Abu Kamil al-Jahdari, and added therein that Talhah said: ""I narrated this hadith to Mujahid and he said: 'That is like a man who gives charity from his wealth — if he wishes, he may give it, and if he wishes, he may withhold it.'"" [Sahih. Reported by Muslim]"
"8343","(8340) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from his paternal aunt 'A'ishah bint Talhah, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to me and I said: ""We have set aside some hays for you."" He said: ""I had intended to fast, but bring it near."" Thus did a group narrate it from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, and likewise a group narrated it from Talhah ibn Yahya — none of them mentioned the making up (of the fast) in this hadith. [Sahih. See the preceding.]"
"8344","(8341) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isbahani narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Jamil narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn al-'Abbas narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from his paternal aunt, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) came to me and I said: ""We have set aside some hays for you."" He said: ""I had intended to fast, but bring it near and I shall make up a day in its place."" Abu al-Hasan al-Daraqutni (may Allah, exalted, have mercy on him) used to attribute this wording to Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn al-'Abbas al-Bahili, and claimed that no one else narrated it with this wording and that he was not followed up on it. But that is not the case, for Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated it at the end of his life, and among the scholars of hadith it is not preserved. [See the preceding.]"
"8345","(8342) Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Urmawi informed us of that: Shafi' ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Abu Ja'far ibn Salamah narrated to us: al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, mentioning this hadith with the wording narrated by al-Rabi', and added at its end: ""I shall fast a day in its place."" Al-Muzani said: I heard al-Shafi'i saying: I heard Sufyan during the generality of his sittings not mention ""I shall fast a day in its place."" Then I presented it to him before his death by a year, and he responded with it: ""I shall fast a day in its place."" The Sheikh said: His narration throughout his lifetime of this hadith does not include this wording, along with the narration of the group from Talhah ibn Yahya — none of them mention it, including Sufyan al-Thawri, Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, Waki' ibn al-Jarrah, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan, Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd, and others — all of which indicate the error of this wording. And Allah knows best. It has also been narrated through another chain from 'A'ishah, and it does not contain this wording. [Weak. See the preceding.]"
"8346","(8343) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak narrated it to us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, from Simak, from 'Ikrimah, from 'A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to me one day and said: ""Do you have anything?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Then I shall fast."" She said: He came to me another day and said: ""Do you have anything?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then I shall break my fast, even though I had made the fast obligatory upon myself."" And this is a sound chain of narration. [Sahih. Reported by al-Tayalisi]"
"8347","(8344) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us. Abū ʿAbd Allāh said: ""He informed me,"" and al-Qāḍī said: ""Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us."" He said: ""Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us."" He said: ""Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us."" He said: ""Abū ʿUmays informed us from ʿAwn ibn Abī Juḥayfah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established a bond of brotherhood between Salmān and Abū al-Dardāʾ.""

He said: ""Salmān came to visit him and found Umm al-Dardāʾ in a state of dishevelment. He said: 'What is the matter with you, O Umm al-Dardāʾ?' She said: 'Your brother Abū al-Dardāʾ stands in prayer at night and fasts during the day, and he has no need of anything from the worldly life.'""

Abū al-Dardāʾ then came, and Salmān welcomed him and brought food close to him. Salmān said to him: 'Eat.' He said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'I adjure you by Allah, you must break your fast.' He said: 'I will not eat until you eat.' So he ate with him, and then he spent the night at his place.

When it was during the night, Abū al-Dardāʾ intended to stand in prayer, but Salmān prevented him and said: 'O Abū al-Dardāʾ, indeed your body has a right over you, and your Lord has a right over you, and your family has a right over you. Fast and break your fast, pray and attend to your family, and give every rightful person their right.'""

""When it was near the break of dawn, he said: 'Now rise if you wish.'"" He said: ""So they both rose, performed ablution, prayed two rakʿahs, and then went out for the prayer. Abū al-Dardāʾ drew near to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to inform him of what Salmān had instructed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'O Abū al-Dardāʾ, indeed your body has a right over you, just as Salmān told you.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bundār, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8348","(8345) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us. He said: ""Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us."" He said: ""al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us."" He said: ""ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us."" He said: ""Shuʿbah informed us from Qatādah, from Abū Ayyūb, from Juwayriyyah bint al-Ḥārith, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:""

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me on a Friday while I was fasting. He said: 'Did you fast yesterday?' She said: 'No.' He said: 'Will you fast tomorrow?' She said: 'No.' He said: 'Then break your fast.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8349","(8346) Abū Dharr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Qāsim al-Mudhakkir informed us. He said: ""Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us."" He said: ""Abū ʿAmr Aḥmad ibn al-Mubārak al-Mustamlī narrated to us."" He said: ""Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us."" He said: ""Yaḥyā ibn Abī al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us."" He said: ""Ḥātim ibn Abī Ṣaghīrah informed us from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Umm Hānīʾ, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:""

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me and asked for water. He drank and then gave me his remaining water to drink, and I was fasting. I drank the leftover of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have done something I do not know whether I was right or wrong — you gave me your leftover water while I was fasting, and I disliked returning the leftover of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said: 'Was it a voluntary fast or a make-up fast from Ramaḍān?' I said: 'A voluntary fast.' He said: 'The one who fasts voluntarily has the choice; if he wishes, he may fast, and if he wishes, he may break his fast.'""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
"8350","(8347) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. He said: ""Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us."" He said: ""Bakkār ibn Qutaybah al-Qāḍī narrated to us."" He said: ""Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā al-Qāḍī narrated to us."" He said: ""Abū Yūnus Ḥātim ibn Abī Ṣaghīrah informed us from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Umm Hānīʾ, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say:""

""'The one who fasts voluntarily is the master of himself; if he wishes, he may fast, and if he wishes, he may break his fast.'""

[Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding.]"
"8351","(8348) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us. He said: ""Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us."" He said: ""Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us."" He said: ""Abū al-Walīd narrated to us."" He said: ""Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us.""

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us. He said: ""Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us."" He said: ""Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us."" He said: ""Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us."" He said: ""Abū ʿAwanah informed us from Simāk, from the son of the son of Umm Hānīʾ, from his grandmother, that he heard from her. She said:""

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought a drink on the day of the Conquest of Makkah, and he drank. Then he gave it to me and I drank, and I was fasting. I disliked returning the leftover of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting and I disliked returning the leftover of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said to her: 'Were you making up something on your behalf?' I said: 'No.' He said: 'Then it will not harm you.'""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibrāhīm. In the narration of Abū al-Walīd, Hārūn said: ""The son of the son of Umm Hānīʾ, from Umm Hānīʾ, claiming that he heard from her, that the Prophet ﷺ said to her: 'Were you making up something on your behalf?' She said: 'No.' He said: 'Then it will not harm you.'"" Abū al-Walīd said: ""He narrated to us the ḥadīth of Simāk from his book.""

[Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding.]"
"8352","(8349) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us. He said: ""ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us."" He said: ""Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us."" He said: ""Abū Dāwūd narrated to us."" He said: ""Shuʿbah informed us."" He said: ""Jaʿdah, a man from Quraysh — and he was the son of Umm Hānīʾ — informed us, and Simāk used to narrate to him, and he would say: 'The two sons of Umm Hānīʾ informed me.' Shuʿbah said: 'Then I met the better of the two, Jaʿdah, and he narrated to me from Umm Hānīʾ that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her and gave her a drink, and he drank. Then he gave it to her and she drank. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The one who fasts voluntarily is the guardian — or the master — of himself; if he wishes, he may fast, and if he wishes, he may break his fast.'""

Shuʿbah said: ""I said to Jaʿdah: 'Did you hear it yourself from Umm Hānīʾ?' He said: 'Our family and Abū Ṣāliḥ, the freed slave of Umm Hānīʾ, informed me from Umm Hānīʾ.'""

[Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding.]"
"8353","(8350) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us. He said: ""Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us."" He said: ""Abū Dāwūd narrated to us."" He said: ""ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us."" He said: ""Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd informed us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith, from Umm Hānīʾ, who said:""

""On the day of the Conquest of Makkah, Fāṭimah came and sat to the left of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Umm Hānīʾ was to his right. A young girl came with a vessel containing a drink and gave it to him, and he drank. Then he gave it to Umm Hānīʾ and she drank from it. She said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have broken my fast and I was fasting.' He said to her: 'Were you making up something?' She said: 'No.' He said: 'Then it will not harm you, if it was a voluntary fast.'""

[Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding.]"
"8354","(8351) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Shādhān in Baghdād informed us. He said: ""Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās informed us."" He said: ""ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us."" He said: ""ʿUbayd Allāh, meaning Ibn Mūsā, informed us."" He said: ""Isrāʾīl informed us from Abū Isḥāq, from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said:""

""When you wake up in the morning having intended to fast, then you have the choice between the two options: if you wish, you may fast, and if you wish, you may break your fast.""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"8355","(8352) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us. He said: ""Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us."" He said: ""al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us."" He said: ""al-Shāfiʿī informed us."" He said: ""Muslim ibn Khālid and ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Rāwād informed us from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, that Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, saw no harm in a person breaking a voluntary fast.""

He would give examples for that: ""A man who performed ṭawāf for seven rounds but did not complete it — he has the reward of what he intended. Or a person who prayed one rakʿah but did not pray a second — he has the reward of what he accounted for himself.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
"8356","(8353) Abū Zakariyyā informed us. He said: ""Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us."" He said: ""al-Rabīʿ informed us."" He said: ""al-Shāfiʿī informed us."" He said: ""Muslim and ʿAbd al-Majīd informed us from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who said:""

""Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, saw no harm in breaking a voluntary fast.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
"8357","(8354) And Abū Zakariyyā narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us — al-Rabīʿ narrated to us — al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated from Ibn Jurayj — from Abī al-Zubayr — from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him: He used to see no harm in breaking a voluntary fast.

[Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī]"
"8358","(8355) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān narrated to us — Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us — Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Abī Mālik al-Ashjaʿī — from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah — from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The fasting person has the choice between that and half of the day.

And this has been reported from other routes as a raised (marfūʿ) hadith, but its being raised is not authentic. [Authentic. Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
"8359","(8356) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī narrated to us — Abū al-Faḍl al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Qūhyār narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Saʿdī narrated to us — ʿAwn ibn ʿUmārah narrated to us — Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl Abū ʿUbaydah narrated from Anas ibn Mālik that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The fasting person has the choice between that and half of the day.""

[Munkar. Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"8360","(8357) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī narrated to us — Abū al-Faḍl narrated to us — Ibrāhīm narrated to us — ʿAwn ibn ʿUmārah narrated to us — Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr narrated from al-Qāsim — from Abī Umāmah — from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, the like of it.

ʿAwn ibn ʿUmārah al-ʿAnbarī is alone in reporting it, and he is weak. [Munkar. See the preceding]"
"8361","(8358) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz in Baghdad narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Ghaylān narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Muzāḥim narrated to us — Sarīʿ ibn Nabḥān said: I heard Abū Dharr saying: I heard my intimate friend Abū al-Qāsim, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""The one fasting voluntarily has the choice up to half of the day.""

[Munkar]"
"8362","(8359) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated it with its chain, the like of it. Ibrāhīm ibn Muzāḥim and Sarīʿ ibn Nabḥān are both unknown (majhūlān). [Munkar. See the preceding]"
"8363","(8360) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb — from Hārūn ibn Umm Hānī — from Umm Hānī bint Abī Ṭālib, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came to me and I called for a drink for him, so he drank — or she said, he called for a drink and drank — then he offered it to me, so I drank it. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting, but I disliked returning your leftover drink. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""If it was a make-up fast from Ramaḍān, then fast a day in its place. And if it was voluntary, then if you wish, make it up, and if you wish, do not make it up.""

[Weak. Meaning of the words]"
"8364","(8361) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAwn narrated to us — Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Bartī narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb — from Hārūn ibn the daughter of Umm Hānī — or the son of the son of Umm Hānī — who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, came to me on the Day of the Conquest of Makkah and offered me the remainder of his drink, so I drank it. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting, and I disliked returning your leftover drink. He said: ""If it was a make-up fast from Ramaḍān, then fast a day in its place. And if it was voluntary, then if you wish, make it up, and if you wish, do not make it up.""

[Weak]"
"8365","(8362) And Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī narrated to us — Abū Ḥātim ibn Abī al-Faḍl al-Harawī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sāmī narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us — Abū Uways narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir — from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: I prepared food for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he came to me along with his companions. When the food was set down, a man from the people said: I am fasting. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Your brother has invited you and has gone to effort for you."" Then he said to him: ""Break your fast and fast a day in its place, if you wish."" And that has been reported by another chain from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, which we have cited in the chapter on differences of opinion. [Weak. Al-Ṭabarānī reported it in al-Awsaṭ]"
"8366","(8363) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Baḥr ibn Naṣr said: It was read to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and Mālik ibn Anas and Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you from Ibn Shihāb, who said: It reached me that ʿĀʾishah and Ḥafṣah, may Allah be pleased with them both, woke up in the morning fasting voluntarily, and food was presented to them, so they broke their fast upon it. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, entered upon them. ʿĀʾishah said — and Ḥafṣah preceded me with the speech, and she was her father's daughter: O Messenger of Allah, I and ʿĀʾishah woke up fasting voluntarily, and food was presented to us, so we broke our fast upon it. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Make it up on another day in its place.""

This is a hadith reported by trustworthy, precise memorizers from the companions of al-Zuhrī from him, as an interrupted (munqaṭiʿ) report: Mālik ibn Anas, Yūnus ibn Yazīd, Maʿmar ibn Rāshid, Ibn Jurayj, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, Sufyān ibn ʿUaynah, Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd al-Zubaydī, Bakr ibn Wāʾil, and others besides them. [Weak. Mālik reported it]"
"8367","(8364) And it has been narrated to us by Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī, by dictation, who informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī informed us that ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us that Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated on the authority of al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀ'ishah, who said: ""Ḥafṣah and I were fasting, and food was presented to us which we desired, so we ate it. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us, and Ḥafṣah preceded me — for she was her father's daughter — and she recounted the matter to him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Make up another day in its place.'""

Thus did Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrate it, as did Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar and Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, and they erred therein regarding al-Zuhrī. [Weak. Reported by al-Tirmidhī]"
"8368","(8365) And it has been informed to us by Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād, who informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad informed us that Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us that Ibn Jurayj narrated on the authority of Ibn Shihāb. I said to him: ""Did ʿUrwah narrate to you from ʿĀ'ishah that she said: 'Ḥafṣah and I began the morning fasting'?"" He said: ""I did not hear anything from ʿUrwah regarding this, but people narrated to me during the caliphate of Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, from among those who used to visit ʿĀ'ishah, that she said: 'Ḥafṣah and I began the morning fasting, and a gift was presented to us, which we ate. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us, and Ḥafṣah preceded me — for she was her father's daughter — and she mentioned that to him, and he said: 'Make up a day in its place.'""

And thus did ʿAbd al-Razzāq ibn Hammām and Muslim ibn Khālid narrate it on the authority of Ibn Jurayj. [Weak. Reported by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"8369","(8366) And it has been informed to us by Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, who informed us that Aḥmad ibn Salmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Faqīh informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Abbār informed us that Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr al-Jawwāz informed us that Sufyān said: We heard it from Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀ'ishah, who said: ""Ḥafṣah and I began the morning fasting, and food was presented to us — and the food was something coveted — so we ate from it. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) came to us, and Ḥafṣah preceded me — for she was her father's daughter — and she said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we began the morning fasting, and food was presented to us, so we ate from it.' The Prophet (ﷺ) smiled and said: 'Fast a day in its place.'""

Sufyān said: ""So they asked al-Zuhrī while I was present, and they said: 'Is it from ʿUrwah?' He said: 'No.'"" [Weak. Reported by al-Nasā'ī]"
"8370","(8367) And it has been informed to us by Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān, who informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī informed us that Sufyān said: ""I heard al-Zuhrī narrate from ʿĀ'ishah, and he mentioned this hadith as a mursal narration.""

Sufyān said: ""It was said to al-Zuhrī: 'Is it from ʿUrwah?' He said: 'No.' And that was when he had risen from the assembly and the prayer had been commenced."" Sufyān said: ""And I had heard Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar narrate it from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah."" Al-Zuhrī said: ""It is not from ʿUrwah."" So I thought that Ṣāliḥ was affected by the manner of oral transmission (al-ʿarḍ). Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī said: ""Someone other than one narrator informed me from Maʿmar that he said regarding this hadith: 'Had it been from the hadith of ʿUrwah, I would not have forgotten it.'"" So these two — Ibn Jurayj and Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah — bore witness against al-Zuhrī, and they are upright witnesses, that he did not hear it from ʿUrwah. So how can the connection of the one who connected it be valid?

Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said: ""I asked Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī about this hadith, and he said: 'The hadith of al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah from ʿĀ'ishah is not authentic.' And likewise said Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhalī, and he cited as evidence the account of Ibn Jurayj and Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, and the mursal narration of those from among the imams who narrated the hadith as mursal from al-Zuhrī.""

And it has been narrated from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀ'ishah.

And Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim, although he is among the trustworthy narrators, was mistaken (wāhim) therein, and Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī erred him in that. And the preserved narration from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd is from al-Zuhrī, from ʿĀ'ishah, as a mursal hadith. [Weak. Reported by al-Ḍuway']"
"8371","(8368) It has been informed to us by Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, who informed us that ʿAlī ibn Bundār al-Ṣayrafī said: I heard ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Bujayr say: I heard Abū Bakr al-Athram say: I said to Abū ʿAbdillāh — meaning Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal — ""Do you know it from Yaḥyā, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀ'ishah: 'Ḥafṣah and I began the morning fasting'?"" He rejected it and said: ""Who narrated it?"" I said: ""Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim."" He said: ""Jarīr used to narrate by conjecture (al-tawahhum)."" [Authentic. Ibn Jarīr]"
"8372","(8369) And it has been informed to us by Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, who narrated to me Muḥammad ibn Muẓaffar al-Ḥāfiẓ, who informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Khallāl informed us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī said: I said to ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī: ""O Abū al-Ḥasan, do you know from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀ'ishah, that she said: 'Ḥafṣah and I began the morning fasting'?"" He said to me: ""Who is this?"" I said: ""Ibn Wahb, from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd."" He laughed and then said: ""Someone like you says such a thing! Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from al-Zuhrī, that ʿĀ'ishah and Ḥafṣah began the morning fasting.""

And it has been narrated from another chain from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀ'ishah. [Authentic]"
"8373","(8370) It has been informed to us by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb informed me that Ḥaywah and ʿUmar ibn Mālik informed me, from Ibn al-Hād, who said: ""Zumayl, the freed slave of ʿUrwah, narrated to me, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀ'ishah, that she said: 'A gift of food was presented to me and Ḥafṣah while we were fasting. One of them said to her companion: ""Do you wish to break your fast?"" She said: ""Yes."" So we both broke our fast. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came, and they said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, a gift was presented to us and we desired it, so we broke our fast."" He said: ""There is no blame upon you; fast another day in its place.'""

A group established its chain from Ibn Wahb, and some of them said: ""From Abū Zumayl,"" and some of them did not mention ʿUrwah in its chain. [Weak. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8374","(8371) It has been informed to us by Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī, who informed us that Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ said: ""Zumayl ibn ʿAbbās narrated from ʿUrwah; Ibn al-Hād narrated from him. No hearing (samāʿ) of Zumayl from ʿUrwah is known, nor of Ibn al-Hād from Zumayl, and no proof is established by it. I heard Ibn Ḥammād mention it from al-Bukhārī.""

The Sheikh said: ""And it has been narrated from other routes from ʿĀ'ishah, none of which is authentic. I have clarified their weakness in the chapter on differing."" [Authentic. Mentioned by Ibn ʿAdī]"
"8375","(8372) It has been informed to us by Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, who informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us that Misʿar narrated from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from ʬā'iʿ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""When one of you begins the morning fasting and it occurs to him to break his fast, let him fast a day in its place"" — or he said: ""in its place, a day"" — Misʿar was uncertain. [Weak]"
"8376","(8373) It has been informed to us by Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Faqīh in Ṭabarān, who informed us that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us that al-Qaʿnabī narrated from what he read to Mālik.

(Ḥ) And it has been informed to us by Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, who informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: ""I read to Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade al-wiṣāl (continuous fasting). They said: 'But you practice al-wiṣāl!' He said: 'I am not like you; indeed, I am fed and given to drink.'"" The wording is that of the hadith of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Authentic. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8377","(8374) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ and Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to us, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) observed *wiṣāl* (continuous fasting) during Ramaḍān and forbade them from it. It was said to him: ""You observe *wiṣāl*!"" He said: **""I am not like you. I am fed and given to drink.""**

Muslim narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from the ḥadīth of ʿUbaydullāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8378","(8375) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated to us by dictation. ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us. ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us. Maʿmar narrated to us, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: ""This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to me."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: **""Beware of *wiṣāl* (continuous fasting)!""** They said: ""But you observe *wiṣāl*, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said: **""I am not in that regard like you. Indeed, I spend the night and my Lord feeds me and gives me to drink. So undertake of deeds what you have the strength for.""**

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Yaḥyā, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Zurʿah and al-Aʿraj and Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8379","(8376) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī narrated to me. ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā, narrated to us. Abū al-Yamān narrated to us. Shuʿayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī. Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me that Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade *wiṣāl* (continuous fasting). A man from among the Muslims said to him: ""But you observe *wiṣāl*, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said: **""And which one of you is like me? Indeed, I spend the night and my Lord feeds me and gives me to drink.""** When they refused to desist from *wiṣāl*, he observed *wiṣāl* with them for one day, then another day. Then they saw the new moon, and he said: **""Had it been delayed, I would have continued further,""** as a form of reproach to them for their refusal to desist.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd, from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"8380","(8377) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbaydillāh al-Narsī narrated to us. Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us. Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated to us, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas ibn Mālik, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) observed *wiṣāl* (continuous fasting) at the end of the month, and the people observed *wiṣāl*. This reached the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: **""Had the month been extended for me, I would have observed such *wiṣāl* that those who go to extremes would abandon their extremism. You are not like me. Indeed, I spend the night and my Lord feeds me and gives me to drink.""**

Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith, from Ḥumayd, and he said: ""At the end of the month of Ramaḍān."" And he said: **""Indeed, I remain and my Lord feeds me and gives me to drink.""** Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Qatādah, from Anas. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8381","(8378) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn Salamah and Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī narrated to us, saying: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) forbade them from *wiṣāl* (continuous fasting) out of mercy for them. They said: ""But you observe *wiṣāl*!"" He said: **""I am not like you. Indeed, my Lord feeds me and gives me to drink.""**

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from ʿAbdah, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and ʿUthmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8382","(8379) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us. Ibn Milḥān narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us. Al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn al-Hād, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Khabbāb, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: **""Do not observe *wiṣāl* (continuous fasting). Whoever among you wishes to observe *wiṣāl*, let him continue it until *suḥūr* (pre-dawn).""** They said: ""But you observe *wiṣāl*, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said: **""I am not like you. Indeed, I have a feeder who feeds me and a giver of drink who gives me to drink.""**

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, from al-Layth."
"8383","(8380) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Bakkār ibn Qutaybah narrated to us. Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah and ʿAmr ibn Ḥakkām narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated to us. He said: I heard Ghaylān ibn Jarīr narrating from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿbad al-Zimmānī, from Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked about fasting on the day of ʿArafah. He said: **""It expiates the sins of the past year and the remaining year.""**

Muslim narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8384","(8381) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād. Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us. ʿAbdullāh ibn Ayyūb al-Makhramī narrated to us. Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from Dāwūd ibn Shābūr, from Abū Qazʿah, from Abū al-Khalīl, from Abū Ḥarmalah, from Abū Qatādah, reaching the Prophet (ﷺ): **""Fasting on the day of ʿArafah is an expiation for one year, and the one that follows it. And fasting on the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ is an expiation for one year.""** [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
"8385","(8382) Mujāhid narrated it from Ḥarmalah ibn Iyāsh al-Shaybānī, from Abū Qatādah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked about fasting on the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ, and he said: **""It expiates the year.""** And he was asked about fasting on the day of ʿArafah, and he said: **""It expiates two years: a past year and a coming year.""**

Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā al-Sukkarī narrated it to us in Baghdād. Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us. ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us. Al-Thawrī narrated to us. Manṣūr narrated to me, from Mujāhid, and he mentioned it. And Jarīr narrated it from Manṣūr, from Abū al-Khalīl al-Baṣrī, from Ḥarmalah ibn Iyāsh al-Shaybānī, from Abū Qatādah, or from a client of Abū Qatādah, from Abū Qatādah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: **""Fasting on ʿArafah is an expiation for two years: a year before it and a year after it. And fasting on ʿĀshūrāʾ is an expiation for one year.""**

Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ narrated it to us. Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us. Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us. Jarīr narrated, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. See the preceding.]"
"8386","(8383) Al-Thawrī narrated it from Manṣūr, from Abū al-Khalīl, from Ḥarmalah al-Shaybānī, from a client of Abū Qatādah, from Abū Qatādah.

Abū Ḥāmid al-Mihrajānī narrated to us. Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us. Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī narrated to us, from Sufyān, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain.]"
"8387","(8384) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; he said: Mālik narrated from Abū al-Naḍr, the freed slave of ʿUmar ibn ʿUbaydillāh, from ʿUmayr, the freed slave of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith, that a group of people disputed in her presence on the Day of ʿArafah concerning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). Some of them said, ""He is fasting,"" while others said, ""He is not fasting."" So Umm al-Faḍl (may Allah be pleased with her) sent a cup of milk to him while he was standing at ʿArafah on his camel, and he drank it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8388","(8385) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūh al-Shaybānī informed us; he said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām and Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad both said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: I recited it before Mālik, and he mentioned it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

Likewise, Sufyān al-Thawrī, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, and others narrated it from Sālim Abī al-Naḍr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith]"
"8389","(8386) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; he said: Ḥusayn ibn Ḥasan ibn Muhājir and Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān both said: Hārūn ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to me; ʿAmr, meaning Ibn al-Ḥārith, informed me from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj from Kurayb, the freed slave of Ibn ʿAbbās, from Maymūnah, that she said: ""Indeed, the people were in doubt concerning the fasting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the Day of ʿArafah. So Maymūnah sent a vessel of milk to him while he was standing at the Mawqif (place of standing), and he drank from it while the people were watching.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān from Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Hārūn ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8390","(8387) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed me; he said: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; he said: Sahl, meaning Ibn Bakkār, narrated to us; he said: Wuhaib narrated to us; he said: Ayyūb narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: ""I came upon Ibn ʿAbbāh while he was eating pomegranates at ʿArafah, and he narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) broke his fast at ʿArafah."" Likewise, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it from Ayyūb. And Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated it as well. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Nasāʾī]"
"8391","(8388) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; he said: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Abū al-Rabīʿ both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyūb from ʿIkrimah, that Ibn ʿAbbāh broke his fast at ʿArafah. He was brought pomegranates, which he ate, and he said: ""Umm al-Faḍl narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) broke his fast at ʿArafah. Umm al-Faḍl brought him some milk, and he drank it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Aḥmad]"
"8392","(8389) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā both informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: Abū Usāmah al-Kalbī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; he said: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; they both said: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; he said: Ḥawshab ibn ʿAqīl narrated from Mahdī al-Hajarī; he said: ʿIkrimah narrated to us, saying: ""We were at Abū Hurayrah's house, and he narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prohibited fasting on the Day of ʿArafah at ʿArafah."" [Ḍaʿīf. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8393","(8390) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us; he said: Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zibriqān informed us; he said: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; he said: Ḥawshab ibn ʿAqīl narrated to us; he said: Mahdī ibn Ḥassān al-ʿAbdī narrated from ʿIkrimah from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prohibited fasting on the Day of ʿArafah at ʿArafāt."" [Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding hadith]"
"8394","(8391) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī informed us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us; he said: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us; he said: Mālik narrated from Ziyād, the freed slave of Ibn ʿAyyāsh, from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn Kurayz, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The best of supplications is the supplication of the Day of ʿArafah, and the best of what I and the Prophets before me have said is: 'There is no deity except Allah alone, without any partner.'"" This is a mursal hadith. [Ḥasan due to corroborating narrations. Reported by Mālik]"
"8395","(8392) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us; he said: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Muslim al-Baṭīn.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; he said: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; he said: Abū Dāwūd informed us; he said: Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aʿmash. He said: I heard Muslim al-Baṭīn narrating from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no deed performed in any days that is more excellent than in these ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, not even jihād in the cause of Allah?"" He said: ""Not even jihād in the cause of Allah, except for a man who goes out with his life and his wealth in the cause of Allah and returns with nothing from it."" This is the wording of Shuʿbah's narration. In the narration of Abū Muʿāwiyah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There are no days in which righteous deeds are more beloved to Allah than these days,"" meaning the ten days, and the remainder carries the same meaning. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿArʿarah from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8396","(8393) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; he said: Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl informed us; he said: Musaddad informed us; he said: Abū ʿAwānah narrated from al-Ḥurr ibn al-Ṣayyāḥ from Hunaydah ibn Khālid from his wife, from some of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to fast the nine days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah, the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ, and three days of every month: the first Monday of the month, Thursday, and another day."" Abū Dāwūd narrated it from Musaddad. [Ḍaʿīf. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8397","(8394) Abu Muhammad — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn al-Mu'mmal — narrated to us: Abu 'Uthman 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr al-Basri narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: al-A'mash — (h) — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — and the wording is his — informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us: al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) fasting during the ten days (ashr) at all.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and others.

The one who affirms is more deserving of precedence than the one who negates, in addition to what has preceded from the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8398","(8395) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu Nasr — Ahmad ibn 'Amr al-'Iraqi — narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Sufyan, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from his father, that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""There are no days more beloved to me to perform righteous deeds therein than the days of the ten (ashr).""

He said: And Sufyan narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Mawhab narrated to me — he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, and a man asked him: ""I owe the fasting of Ramadan, and I wish to volunteer fasting during the ten (days)."" He said: ""No, rather begin with the right of Allah, then fulfill it, then volunteer thereafter as you wish."" [Sahih.]"
"8399","(8396) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu 'Uthman al-Basri informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us: Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, who said: 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Do not make up Ramadan in Dhu al-Hijjah, and do not fast Friday alone. I think he was alone in this. And do not cup yourself while you are fasting.""

It was also narrated from al-Hasan from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the dislike of making up (fasts) during the ten (days).

This is because he used to see the making up of it as continuous in one of the two narrations from him. So when what is obligatory upon him of making up exceeds nine days, his continuity is severed by the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Nahr) and the Days of Tashriq. [Da'if.]"
"8400","(8397) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: Ibn Abi 'Umar narrated to us: Sufyan, from Ayyub, from 'Abd Allah ibn Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) arrived in Madinah and found the Jews fasting on the Day of 'Ashura'. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to them: ""What is this day that you fast?"" They said: ""This is a great day — Allah, exalted is He, saved Musa and his people therein and drowned Pharaoh and his people therein. Musa fasted it out of gratitude, and we fast it."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Then we have more right and are closer to Musa than you."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) fasted it and commanded its fasting.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ali al-Madini from Sufyan, and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Abi 'Umar. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8401","(8398) And Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — in Baghdad — informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid — that he heard Ibn 'Abbas saying: ""I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) seek out the virtue of any day to fast it above another, except this day — the Day of 'Ashura' — and the month of Ramadan.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi' from 'Abd al-Razzaq, and both (al-Bukhari and Muslim) extracted it from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyaynah from 'Ubayd Allah. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
"8402","(8399) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd, Hisham, and Mahdi — Hammad and Mahdi said: from Ghaylan ibn Jarir, and Hisham said: from Qatadah, from Ghaylan ibn Jarir, from 'Abd Allah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani, from Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him), that a Bedouin asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about his fasting, and he became angry until that was visible in his face. So 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and said: ""We are pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with you as Prophet. I seek refuge in Allah from the wrath of Allah and the wrath of His Messenger."" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) did not cease repeating this until he calmed down. Then he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about a man who fasts the entire year?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""He neither fasted nor broke fast"" — or he said: ""He did not fast and did not break fast."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about one who fasts two days and breaks fast one day?"" He said: ""And who can bear that?"" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about one who breaks fast two days and fasts one day?"" He said: ""I wish I could bear that."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the fasting of Monday?"" He said: ""That is the day I was born in and the day revelation was sent down to me."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about a man who fasts one day and breaks fast one day?"" He said: ""That is the fasting of my brother Dawud (peace be upon him)."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the fasting of the Day of 'Ashura'?"" He said: ""I hope upon Allah, exalted is He, that it will expiate for the year."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about one who fasts the Day of 'Arafah?"" He said: ""I hope upon Allah that it will expiate for the year before it and the year after it.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and others from Hammad ibn Zayd, and from another direction from Mahdi ibn Maymun. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8403","(8400) Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — in Baghdad — informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, who said: ""I never saw anyone more commanding of the fasting of the Day of 'Ashura' than 'Ali and Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with them both)."" [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn Abi Shaybah.]"
"8404","(8401) Abu al-Hasan — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi — informed us in dictation: Abu Nasr — Muhammad ibn Hamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Mutawwi'i — narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Hammad al-Amuli narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us — (h) — and Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani — and the wording is his — informed us: Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Qattan — informed us: Muhammad ibn Hayyawayh informed us: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me: Isma'il ibn Umayyah — that he heard Abu Ghatafan ibn Tarif saying: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas saying, when the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) fasted the Day of 'Ashura' and commanded its fasting, they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, it is a day that the Jews and Christians venerate."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Then if the coming year arrives, we shall fast the ninth, if Allah wills."" He (the narrator) said: ""The coming year did not arrive before the Prophet (peace be upon him) passed away.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Halwani from Ibn Abi Maryam. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8405","(8402) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Bakkar ibn Qutaybah, judge of Egypt, narrated to us: Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhub narrated to us: al-Qasim ibn 'Abbas, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""If I remain alive until the coming year, I shall certainly fast the ninth.""

Muslim extracted it in his Sahih from the hadith of Waki' from Ibn Abi Dhub.

And Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated it from Ibn Abi Dhub, and said in its text: ""If I live, if Allah wills, I shall fast the ninth,"" out of fear that the Day of 'Ashura' might be missed. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8406","(8403) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Abu Bakrah — Bakkar ibn Qutaybah, judge of Egypt — narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and Ruh ibn 'Ubadah — both said — narrated to us: Hajjib ibn 'Umar said: I heard al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj say: I came upon Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), who was resting with his cloak as a pillow at Zamzam. So I sat with him, and he was an excellent companion. I said: ""Inform me about the Day of 'Ashura'."" He sat up and said: ""About which aspect of it do you ask?"" I said: ""About its fasting — which day should we fast?"" He said: ""When you see the new moon of al-Muharram, then count, and when you wake on the ninth, wake up fasting."" I said: ""Thus did Muhammad (peace be upon him) use to fast?"" He said: ""Yes.""

Muslim extracted it in his Sahih from the hadith of Waki' from Hajjib, and it is as though he (may Allah be pleased with him) intended to fast it along with the tenth, and he intended by his answer ""yes"" what has been narrated of his (peace be upon him) resolve to fast it. And that which clarifies this — [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8407","(8404) Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār in Baghdad — informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: ʬAṭāʾ informed me that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ""Fast the ninth and the tenth, and thereby oppose the Jews.""

It was also narrated by ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Yazīd from Ibn ʿAbbās, likewise, as a mawqūf narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
"8408","(8405) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Dāwūd ibn ʿAlī, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""If I remain alive, I shall certainly command fasting a day before it or a day after it"" — referring to the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ḥumayrī.]"
"8409","(8406) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Ibn Abī Laylā narrated from Dāwūd ibn ʿAlī, from his father, from his grandfather Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fast the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ and oppose the Jews regarding it. Fast a day before it or a day after it.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Muqriʾ. And in the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān it reads: ""Fast a day before it and a day after it.""

Abū Shihāb also narrated something similar from Ibn Abī Laylā — ""before it and after it."" [Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding.]"
"8410","(8407) Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdad informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Kharqī narrated to us: Abū Qilābah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and the wording is his — Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī narrated to us: Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd narrated from Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ that the Prophet (ﷺ) sent a man from the tribe of Aslam on the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ to his people, commanding them to fast that day. The man said: ""I think I will not reach them until they have already eaten."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever among them has eaten, let him fast the remainder of his day.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8411","(8408) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal narrated to us: Khālid ibn Dhakkwān narrated from al-Rabīʿ bint Muʿawwidh ibn ʿAfrāʾ (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent [messengers] on the morning of ʿĀshūrāʾ to the villages of the Anṣār around Madīnah: ""Whoever began the day fasting, let him complete his fast, and whoever began the day not fasting, let him complete the remainder of his day."" She said: ""After that we used to fast it, and we would have our young children fast, and we would make for them toys of wool, and take them to the mosque. If any of them cried for food, we would give them that until the time of ifṭār came.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Nāfiʿ from Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8412","(8409) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ was a day that the Quraysh used to fast during the Jāhiliyyah, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast it during the Jāhiliyyah. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived in Madīnah, he fasted it and commanded [the people] to fast it. When Ramaḍān was made obligatory, it became the obligation, and the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ was left aside. So whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished abandoned it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī — ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah — and Muslim narrated it from other routes from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8413","(8410) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān informed us: Shuʿayb informed us from al-Zuhrī. ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command fasting the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ before Ramaḍān was made obligatory. When fasting the month of Ramaḍān was made obligatory, whoever wished fasted ʿĀshūrāʾ, and whoever wished broke his fast.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah and Yūnus ibn Yazīd from al-Zuhrī. They both also narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿIrāk ibn Mālik from ʿUrwah, but in their narration from ʿUrwah there is not what is in the ḥadīth of Hishām regarding the wording of ""leaving it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"8414","(8411) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal al-Māsarjisī narrated to us: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, who said: al-Ashʿath ibn Qays entered upon ʿAbdullāh on the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ while he was having his midday meal. He said: ""O Abū Muḥammad, come near to the meal!"" He said: ""Is this not the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ?"" He said: ""Do you know what the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ is? It was merely a day that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast before Ramaḍān was revealed. When Ramaḍān was revealed, it was abandoned.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah and Jarīr from al-Aʿmash.

Zubayd also narrated it from ʿUmārah, from Qays ibn al-Sakan, from ʿAbdullāh, and it was said from Zubayd, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Qays ibn al-Sakan. And ʿAlqamah narrated it from ʿAbdullāh."
"8415","(8412) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib and Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl both said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the people of the Jāhiliyyah used to fast the Day of ʿĀshūrāʾ, and that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fasted it, and the Muslims [fasted it] before Ramaḍān was made obligatory. When Ramaḍān was made obligatory, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, ʿĀshūrāʾ is a day among the days of Allah. Whoever wishes may fast it, and whoever wishes may leave it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr and others from ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr, and they both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān from ʿUbaydullāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8416","(8413) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥbūr al-Dahhān narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Hārūn al-Faqīh narrated to us: Bishr ibn Mūsā al-Asadī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Qāsim Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣaqar and Abū al-Qāsim Ghaylān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz in Baghdad both said: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us. They both said: al-Ḥasan ibn Mūsā al-Ashyab narrated to us: Shaybān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Shaybān informed us from al-Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, from Jaʿfar ibn Abī Thawr, from Jābir ibn Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command us to fast ʿĀshūrāʾ, encourage us regarding it, and remind us of it. When Ramaḍān was made obligatory, he neither commanded us, nor forbade us, nor reminded us of it.

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿUbaydullāh. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from ʿUbaydullāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8417","(8414) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Makhlad ibn Jaʿfar al-Bāqarḥiyy informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ṭāhir ibn Abī al-Dumayk informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Madīniyy informed us; Ḥammād ibn Usāmah narrated from Abī al-ʿUmays, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, who said: Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī said: ""The day of ʿĀshūrāʾ was a day which the Jews venerated and took as a festival."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""So observe its fast.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīniyy, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Abī Usāmah Ḥammād ibn Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8418","(8415) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Faqīh in Ṭabarān informed us; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us; Rūḥ informed us; Shuʿbah informed us; Abū Bishr narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived in Madīnah, he found the Jews fasting on ʿĀshūrāʾ. He asked them, and they said: 'This is the day on which Mūsā (peace be upon him) prevailed over Pharaoh.' He said: 'You have more right to Mūsā than they do, so fast it.'""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbadah, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghandar from Shuʿbah, and they both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī regarding the command to fast it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8419","(8416) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakkī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated, in what he read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, that he heard Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān on the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ during the year of the Pilgrimage, while he was on the pulpit, saying: ""O people of Madīnah, where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'This is the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ. Allah has not made its fast obligatory upon you. Whoever wishes, let him fast, and whoever wishes, let him break his fast.'""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Qaʿnabī. Al-Shāfiʿī added in his narration: ""And I am fasting, so whoever wishes, let him fast."" The rest carries the same meaning.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from Mālik, and from other chains from al-Zuhrī.

His statement: ""Allah has not made its fast obligatory upon you"" indicates that it was never obligatory, because ""lam"" ("
"8420","(8417) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī informed us; Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥumayd, who said: Muʿāwiyah said upon the pulpit of Madīnah: ""Where are your scholars, O people of Madīnah? The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to forbid the like of this."" He produced a lock of hair from the fold of his sleeve and said: ""The Children of Israel were only destroyed when their women adopted the like of this. Where are your scholars, O people of Madīnah? I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on this day, the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ, saying: 'I am fasting, so whoever among you wishes to fast, let him fast.'""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous one.]"
"8421","(8418) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawiyy informed us orally; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Dallawayh al-Daqqāq informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from Nāfiʿ, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said — and the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ was mentioned in his presence —

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us; al-Ṭayālisī Abū al-Walīd informed us; Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: ""The day of ʿĀshūrāʾ is a day which the people of the Jāhiliyyah used to fast. So whoever among you wishes to fast it, let him fast it, and whoever dislikes it, let him leave it.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8422","(8419) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithiyy informed us; Abī Usāmah narrated from al-Walīd ibn Kathīr, who said: Nāfiʿ informed me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to them that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say on the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ: ""This is a day which the people of the Jāhiliyyah used to fast. So whoever wishes to fast it, let him fast it, and whoever wishes to leave it, let him leave it.""

And ʿAbd Allāh would not fast it unless it coincided with his [usual] fast.

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb from Abī Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8423","(8420) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: ""The day of ʿĀshūrāʾ was a day which Quraysh used to fast during the Jāhiliyyah. When Islam came, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever wishes, let him fast it, and whoever wishes, let him leave it.'""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8424","(8421) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Musaddad and Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd said: Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Abī Bishr, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥimyārī, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The best fasting after the month of Ramaḍān is the month of Allāh, al-Muḥarram, and the best prayer after the obligatory one is prayer during the night."" Qutaybah did not say ""month""; he said ""Ramaḍān.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8425","(8422) Abū Naṣr al-Fāmiyy informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; al-Ḥajabiyy and Musaddad said: Abū ʿAwanah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl informed us; Musaddad informed us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥimyārī, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The best fasting after the month of Ramaḍān is the month of Allāh which you call al-Muḥarram, and the best prayer after the obligatory one is prayer in the depths of the night.""

Likewise, Zāʾidah and Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr. As for the ḥadīth of Zāʾidah, it has already been mentioned in the book of Ṣalāh. And as for the ḥadīth of Jarīr— [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous one.]"
"8426","(8423) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Jarīr narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked: ""Which prayer is best after the obligatory prayer, and which fasting is best after the month of Ramaḍān?"" He said: ""The best prayer after the obligatory prayer is prayer in the depths of the night, and the best fasting after the month of Ramaḍān is the fast of al-Muḥarram.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr. And ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqqīy differed from them in its chain, so he narrated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous one.]"
"8427","(8424) Abū Aḥmad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānī al-ʿAdl narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs al-ʿAnazī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʾ, Abū Tawbah al-Ḥalabī, narrated to us, that ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated to them from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr from Jundub ibn Sufyān al-Bajalī, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) used to say: ""Indeed, the most virtuous prayer after the obligatory prayer is the prayer in the depths of the night, and indeed, the most virtuous fasting after the month of Ramadan is the month of Allah which you call al-Muḥarram."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
"8428","(8425) Abū Aḥmad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānī and Abū Naṣr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī both said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm said: I asked Saʿīd ibn Jubayr about fasting in Rajab — what do you think of it?

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us; ʿĪsā, meaning Ibn Yūnus, narrated to us; ʿUthmān, meaning Ibn Ḥakīm, said: I asked Saʿīd ibn Jubayr about fasting in Rajab, and he said: Ibn ʿAbbās informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast so much that we would say he would not break his fast, and he would break his fast so much that we would say he would not fast.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8429","(8426) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād —

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī from Abī al-Salīl from Mujībah al-Bāhilīyah from her father or her uncle, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then departed, and returned to him after a year.

In the narration of Abī Mūsā: He came to him after a year, and his condition and appearance had changed. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you not recognize me?"" He said: ""Who are you?"" He said: ""I am the Bāhilī who came to you a year ago."" He said: ""What has changed you, when you were of good appearance?"" He said: ""I have not eaten any food since I left you except at night."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Why did you torment yourself? Fast the month of patience, and one day from every month."" He said: ""Increase for me, for I have strength."" He said: ""Fast two days from every month."" He said: ""Increase for me, for I have strength."" He said: ""Fast three days."" ʿAbd al-Wāḥid added: ""From every month."" He said: ""Increase for me, for I have strength."" He said: ""Fast from the sacred months and leave."" He said it three times. In the narration of Mūsā, he said: ""Increase for me."" He said: ""Fast from the sacred months and leave. Fast from the sacred months and leave. Fast from the sacred months and leave."" And he indicated with his three fingers, then closed them, then opened them. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8430","(8427) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn, and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us; they said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: I read to Mālik from Abī al-Naḍr, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn ʿUbaydullāh, from Abī Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast so much that we would say he would not break his fast, and he would break his fast so much that we would say he would not fast. I never saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) complete the fasting of an entire month except Ramadan, and I never saw him fast more in any month than he did in Shaʿbān.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8431","(8428) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Labīd from Abī Salamah, who said: I asked ʿĀʾishah about the fasting of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and she said: He used to fast so much that we would say he has certainly fasted, and he would break his fast so much that we would say he has certainly broken his fast. I never saw him fasting in any month more than his fasting in Shaʿbān. He would fast all of Shaʿbān. He would fast all of Shaʿbān except a little.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8432","(8429) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us by dictation in Naysābūr, and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān recited to him in Baghdad; they said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī narrated to us in Makkah; Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-Jārī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Yazīd ibn al-Hād from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm from Abī Salamah,

from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: One of us would break her fast in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and would not be able to make it up along with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until Shaʿbān came. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not fast in any month as much as he fasted in Shaʿbān. He would fast all of it except a little; rather, he would fast all of it. [Ḥasan. The wording is that of al-Nasāʾī.]"
"8433","(8430) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb informed me; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me that ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qays narrated to him that he heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: The most beloved of months to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to fast in was Shaʿbān, and then he would connect it with Ramadan. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8434","(8431) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Muāḍir ibn al-Mūriʿ narrated to us; Saʿd ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī informed me; ʿUmar ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī said: I heard Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six days from Shawwāl, that is like fasting the entire year.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar, ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr, and ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak from Saʿd ibn Saʿīd, the brother of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8435","(8432) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr and al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah both said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Ibn Lahīʿah, Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb, and Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated from ʿAmr ibn Jābir al-Ḥaḍramī, who said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Whoever fasts Ramadan and six days from Shawwāl, it is as if he fasted the entire year.""

In the narration of al-Faqīh, he said, from Jābir, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8436","(8433) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me that he heard Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī narrating from Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fasting one month is equal to ten months, and six days after it is equal to two months — that is the completion of the year."" Meaning Ramadan and six days after it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
"8437","(8434) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh al-Naḥwī informed us: Abū Yūsuf Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū al-Nuʿmān Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl and al-Ḥajjāj both said: Mahdī ibn Maymūn informed us: Ghaylān ibn Jarīr informed us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿbad al-Zimmānī, from Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī, from the Prophet ﷺ, that a man said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about fasting on Monday?"" He said: ""On that day I was born, and on that day the Qurʾān was revealed to me.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Mahdī ibn Maymūn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8438","(8435) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Hishām informed us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, that ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥakim ibn Thawbān informed him that the mawlā of Qudāmah ibn Maẓʿūn informed him that the mawlā of Usāmah ibn Zayd informed him that Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) used to ride to his property in Wādī al-Qurā and used to fast on Monday and Thursday. So I said to him: ""Do you fast when you have grown old and frail?"" He said: ""Indeed, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasting on Monday and Thursday."" So I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you fast on Monday and Thursday?"" He said: ""Indeed, deeds are presented on Monday and Thursday.""

And similarly it was narrated by Abān ibn Yazīd al-Aṭṭār and Ḥarb ibn Shaddād, from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī.]"
"8439","(8436) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from al-ʿAbbās al-Jurayrī, from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī informed us in Marw: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from Abū Shimr, from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""My beloved ﷺ advised me to three things: sleeping after Witr, fasting three days of every month, and the two rakʿahs of Ḍuḥā.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbdullāh. In the narration of Ibn Fūrak it is: ""Witr before sleeping,"" and he said: ""the prayer of Ḍuḥā.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, from Shuʿbah, from al-Jurayrī, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah, from al-Jurayrī and Abū Shimr al-Ḍubaʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8440","(8437) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī informed us: ʿAffān informed us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us: Thābit informed us, from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī, that Abū Hurayrah was on a journey. When they halted, they sent a message to him while he was praying, inviting him to eat. He said to the messenger: ""I am fasting."" When the food was placed and they were about to finish, he came and began to eat. The people looked at their messenger, and he said: ""What are you looking at? He has informed me that he is fasting."" Abū Hurayrah said: ""He spoke the truth. Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Fasting the month of patience and fasting three days of every month is fasting the entire lifetime.' So I have fasted three days of the month; thus I am breaking my fast by the concession of Allah, and I am fasting by the multiplication of Allah's reward.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8441","(8438) And Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī informed us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us: al-Aʿmash informed us, from Jaʿfar ibn Iyās, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq al-ʿUqaylī, who said: ""I came to Madīnah and there was a tall, black man. I said: 'Who is this?' They said: 'Abū Dharr.' I said: 'I must see what state he is in today.' I said: 'Are you fasting?' He said: 'Yes.' And they were waiting for permission to enter upon ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). So they entered, and they brought dishes, and he ate. I nudged him with my hand to remind him, and he said: 'I have not forgotten what I told you. I informed you that I am fasting. Indeed, I fast three days of every month; rather, I am always fasting.'""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
"8442","(8439) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us: Shaybān informed us, from ʿĀṣim, from Zirr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast three days of the whiteness (ayyām al-bīḍ) of every month.

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8443","(8440) And with his chain, from ʿAbdullāh, who said: ""I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ breaking his fast on Friday.""

[Ḥasan. See the preceding.]"
"8444","(8441) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān, and Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Subʿī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Shaqīq informed us: Abū Ḥamzah al-Sukkarī informed us: ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah informed us, mentioning it with its meaning, and he said: ""It was rare that he would miss fasting on Friday.""

[Ḥasan. See the preceding.]"
"8445","(8442) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: Hammām informed us, from Anas ibn Sīrīn, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Qatādah ibn Milḥān al-Qaysī, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to fast the ayyām al-bīḍ: the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth, and he said: 'They are like fasting the entire lifetime.'""

[Ḥasan in other chains. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8446","(8443) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: Shuʿbah informed us. He said: ""I heard Anas ibn Sīrīn say: 'I heard ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Minhāl, from his father, say: ""And he was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ."" He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to fast the three ayyām al-bīḍ, and he would say: 'They are fasting the entire lifetime.'"" Al-ʿAbbās said: ""Thus said Rūḥ in the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Minhāl.""

The Shaykh said: ""And it has been narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn that he said: 'This is an error; rather, it is ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Qatādah ibn Milḥān al-Qaysī.'""

[Ḥasan in other chains. See the preceding.]"
"8447","(8444) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us; Hammām narrated from Anas from Ibn Milḥān.

And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Ibn Numayr narrated from Fiṭr ibn Khalīfah from Yaḥyā ibn Sām from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah from Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us to fast the three days of al-Bīḍ: the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth."
"8448","(8445) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from al-Aʿmash from Yaḥyā ibn Sām, who said: I heard Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah say: I heard Abū Dharr at al-Rabadhah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O Abū Dharr, when you fast three days of the month, then fast the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth.""

And others narrated it from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah from Abū Dharr, and it was also said from Mūsā from Abū Hurayrah.

[Ḥasan. See its predecessor.]"
"8449","(8446) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah from Sawāʾ al-Khuzāʿī from Ḥafṣah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast three days of the month: Monday, Thursday, and the following Monday.

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8450","(8447) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Ibn Fuḍayl narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUbaydullāh from Hunaydah al-Khuzāʿī from his mother from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command me to fast three days of the month: Monday, Thursday, and Friday.

[Ḍaʿīf. Meaning of its narration.]"
"8451","(8448) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Dāwūd al-Munādī narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Yazīd al-Rishk from Muʿādhah al-ʿAdawiyyah: that she asked ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her): ""Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fast three days of every month?"" She said: ""Yes."" I said: ""Which days of the month did he fast?"" She said: ""He did not mind which days of the month he fasted.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh from ʿAbd al-Wārith.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8452","(8449) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī informed us; Abū ʿAmr Aḥmad ibn al-Mubārak al-Mustamlī informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed me; ʿAbdullāh ibn Wāqid informed me; Ayyūb ibn Nahīk, the freedman of Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, narrated from ʿAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿUmar from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever fasts Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday and gives in charity, be it little or much, Allah will forgive his sins and he will emerge from his sins as on the day his mother gave birth to him.""

Ayyūb ibn Nahīk said: And Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me from his father from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he used to recommend fasting Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday, and he used to report that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command fasting them and giving in charity, be it little or much, for Allah has abundant bounty.

ʿAbdullāh ibn Wāqid is not strong; some of the ḥuffāẓ declared him reliable and some declared him weak. Yaḥyā al-Bāblutṭī narrated it from Ayyūb ibn Nahīk from Muḥammad ibn Qays from Abū Ḥāzim from Ibn ʿUmar.

And al-Bāblutṭī is weak. The fasting of Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday has been narrated through other chains, weaker than this, from Anas.

[Very weak. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
"8453","(8450) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Rūḥ narrated; Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from ʿAmr ibn Aws from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The most beloved prayer to Allah, the Most High, is the prayer of Dāwūd: he would sleep half the night, then stand for a third of it after the half, then sleep the last of it. And the most beloved fasting to Allah, the Most High, is the fasting of Dāwūd: he would fast one day and break fast one day.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Ibn Jurayj, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8454","(8451) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh informed us; Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated from Shuʿbah. And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muthannā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Ziyād ibn Fayyāḍ, who said: I heard Abū ʿIyāḍ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fast one day and you will have the reward of what remains."" He said: ""I can do more than that."" He said: ""Fast two days and you will have the reward of what remains."" He said: ""I can do more than that."" He said: ""Fast three days and you will have the reward of what remains."" He said: ""I can do more than that."" He said: ""Fast four days and you will have the reward of what remains."" He said: ""I can do more than that."" He said: ""Then fast the best of fasting before Allah: the fasting of Dāwūd. He would fast one day and break fast one day.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muthannā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8455","(8452) Abū ʿAbdullāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us; Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ narrated from al-Nuʿmān ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever fasts one day in the cause of Allah, Allah will distance his face from the Fire by that day for seventy autumns.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yazīd ibn al-Hād and ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī from Suhayl, and both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and Suhayl from al-Nuʿmān.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8456","(8453) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; al-Aʿmash narrated from ʿUmārah ibn ʿUmayr from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as young men who had nothing.

He said: ""O company of young men, whoever among you can afford marriage, let him marry, for it is more effective in lowering the gaze and guarding chastity. And whoever cannot afford it, then upon him is fasting, for it is a shield for him.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Aʿmash.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8457","(8454) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Rajāʾ al-Adīb informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us: Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Sabīʿī from Numayr ibn ʿUrayb from ʿĀmir ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fasting in winter is the coolly-gained bounty."" This is a mursal hadith. [Weak. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī]"
"8458","(8455) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us: Hammām narrated from Qatāda from Anas.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: ʿAffān narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us: Qatāda narrated to us: Anas, who said: Abū Hurayra said: ""Shall I not direct you to the coolly-gained bounty?"" He said: We said: ""What is that, O Abū Hurayra?"" He said: ""Fasting in winter."" This is a mawqūf hadith. [Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
"8459","(8456) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-Aswad narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿa narrated from Darrāj Abī al-Samḥ from Abī al-Haytham from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Winter is the spring of the believer: its day is shortened so he fasts, and its night is lengthened so he stands in prayer."" [Munkar. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8460","(8457) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abī ʿUbayd, the freed slave of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf: that he witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him), and he prayed before delivering the sermon, without an adhān or an iqāma. Then he addressed the people and said: ""O people, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade you from fasting on these two days. As for one of them, it is the day of your breaking of the fast from your fasting — your ʿĪd; and as for the other, it is the day on which you eat from your sacrificial offerings.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhrī. [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8461","(8458) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Salām al-Warrāq narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read to Mālik from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān from al-Aʿraj from Abī Hurayra: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade fasting on two days — the day of al-Aḍḥā and the day of al-Fiṭr.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ismāʿīl from Mālik, with a more complete chain than that. [Sound. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8462","(8459) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Wuhib narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā narrated from his father from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade fasting on two days — the day of al-Fiṭr and the day of al-Aḍḥā — and he forbade two types of garments: al-ṣammāʾ (wrapping oneself in a garment so that no part of the hand can emerge), and a man wrapping himself in a single garment; and he forbade prayer at two times: after the morning prayer and after the ʿAṣr prayer.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from ʿAmr. [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8463","(8460) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim — meaning al-Baghawī — narrated to us: Surayj ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Khālid informed us from Abī al-Malīḥ from Nubaysha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The days of al-Tashrīq are days of eating and drinking.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Surayj ibn Yūnus. [Sound. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8464","(8461) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in the book al-Mustadrak: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Abī Naṣr al-Marwazī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Qāḍī narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated, reciting to Mālik, from Yazīd ibn al-Hād from Abī Murra, the freed slave of Umm Hānīʾ: that he entered with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr upon his father, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and he brought food close to them and said: ""Eat."" He said: ""I am fasting."" So ʿAmr said: ""Eat, for these are the days in which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command us to break our fast and forbid us from fasting."" Mālik said: ""They are the days of al-Tashrīq."" [Sound. Narrated by Mālik.]"
"8465","(8462) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Isḥāq al-Bazzāz in Baghdād informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī in Makka narrated to us: Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarra narrated to us: al-Muqrīʾ and ʿUthmān ibn al-Yamān said: Mūsā ibn ʿAlī narrated to us. He said: I heard my father narrating from ʿUqba ibn ʿĀmir.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Bāghandī narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Mūsā ibn ʿAlī ibn Rabāḥ al-Lakhmī narrated to us. He said: I heard my father say: I heard ʿUqba ibn ʿĀmir say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The day of ʿArafa, the day of al-Naḥr, and the days of al-Tashrīq are our festival, the people of Islam, and they are days of eating and drinking."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
"8466","(8463) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Muʾadhdhin informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Tirmidhī narrated to us: Ayyūb ibn Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Uways narrated to me: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd that he heard Yūsuf ibn Masʿūd ibn al-Ḥakam al-Anṣārī, then al-Zuraqī, narrate: that his grandmother narrated to him: that she saw, while she was in Minā during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), a rider calling out, saying: ""O people, they are days of eating and drinking, and of intimacy with wives, and of the remembrance of Allah, the Most High."" She said: I said: ""Who is this?"" They said: ""ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him)."" [Sound through other chains. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8467","(8464) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, from Bishr ibn Suḥaym, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him during the days of Tashrīq to proclaim that they were days of eating and drinking. And he said: ""None shall enter Paradise except a believer."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
"8468","(8465) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb narrated to us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, Ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm narrated to us, Bundār narrated to us, Muḥammad — meaning Ghundar — narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, he said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĪsā ibn Abī Laylā narrating from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, and from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that they both said: ""Fasting was not permitted during the days of Tashrīq except for one who could not find a sacrificial animal."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8469","(8466) Abū ʿAmr narrated to us, Abū Bakr narrated to us, Ibn Nājiyyah narrated to us, Abū Mūsā narrated to us, Ghundar narrated to us, and he mentioned it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bundār Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8470","(8467) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from Mālik, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: ""The fasting of the one performing Tamattuʿ (Ḥajj) is from the time he enters into the state of Iḥrām for Ḥajj until the Day of ʿArafah. If he misses it, he should fast the days of Minā."""
"8471","(8468) And with his chain of narration, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, a similar narration. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik. Al-Bukhārī said: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd corroborated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8472","(8469) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm, and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, regarding the one performing Tamattuʿ: if he does not find a sacrificial animal and has not fasted before ʿArafah, he should fast the days of Minā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
"8473","(8470) And with his chain of narration, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from his father, a similar narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
"8474","(8471) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, from what he read to Mālik ibn Anas. (Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, dictating, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Muḥammad ibn Naṣr said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I read to Mālik, from Abū al-Naḍr, the freed slave of ʿUmar ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast so continuously that we would say: 'He will not break his fast,' and he would break his fast so much that we would say: 'He will not fast.' I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complete the fasting of an entire month except Ramaḍān, and I never saw him fast more in any month than he did in Shaʿbān."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8475","(8472) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us, Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Sufyān, Manṣūr narrated to me, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah, who said: I said to ʿĀʾishah: ""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ single out any particular days?"" She said: ""No. His deeds were consistent. And which of you has the capacity that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had?"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Jarīr, from Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8476","(8473) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, Ādam narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, who said: I heard Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Makkī — and he was a poet and was not accused regarding ḥadīth — say: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""Do you fast perpetually and stand in prayer all night?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""If you do that, your eyes will become sunken and your soul will become exhausted. There is no fast for the one who fasts perpetually. Fast three days of each month — that is equal to fasting the entire year."" I said: ""I am capable of more than that."" He said: ""Then fast the fast of Dāwūd: he would fast one day and break fast one day, and he would not flee when he encountered the enemy."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8477","(8474) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsūs al-Sūsī and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd narrated to me, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Awzāʿī narrate to me, Yaḥyā narrated to me, Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: ""Shall I not inform you that you fast during the day and stand in prayer at night?"" He said: I said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Do not do so. Sleep, and stand in prayer, and fast, and break your fast. For your body has a right over you, and your eyes have a right over you, and your wife has a right over you, and your guest has a right over you. It is sufficient for you to fast three days of every month, for every good deed is rewarded tenfold, and that is the fasting of the entire lifetime."" He said: ""I pressed further, and it was made more difficult upon me. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I find strength.' He said: 'Then fast three days of every week.' He said: 'I pressed further, and it was made more difficult upon me. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I find strength.' He said: 'Then fast the fast of the Prophet of Allah, Dāwūd (peace be upon him), and do not exceed that.' He said: 'I said: What was the fast of the Prophet of Allah, Dāwūd?' He said: 'Half of the lifetime.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8478","(8475) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Ḥabbān narrated from Ibn al-Mubārak: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: ""And do not increase upon it."" And he added at the end: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr used to say, after old age had overtaken him: ""Would that I had accepted the concession of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Muqātil from Ibn al-Mubārak, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār and Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it]"
"8479","(8476) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Abān ibn Yazīd narrated to us: Ghaylān ibn Jarīr al-Maʿwalī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿbad al-Zimmānī from Abū Qatādah that a Bedouin came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said to him: ""O Prophet of Allah, how is your fast, or how do you fast?"" The Prophet (ﷺ) remained silent and did not reply to him with anything. When his anger had subsided, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb asked him: ""O Prophet of Allah, how is your fast, or how do you fast? What do you say about one who fasts the entire lifetime?"" He said: ""He has neither fasted nor broken his fast,"" or he said: ""He has not fasted and has not broken his fast."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about one who fasts two days and breaks one day?"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""And who has the strength for that, O ʿUmar? I wish I could do that."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about one who fasts one day and breaks one day?"" He said: ""That is the fast of Dāwūd (peace be upon him)."" He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what do you say about one who fasts the day of ʿArafah?"" He said: ""It expiates the sins of the year and the year before it."" He said: ""What do you say about one who fasts three days of every month?"" He said: ""That is the fast of the entire lifetime."" He said: ""What do you say about one who fasts the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ?"" He said: ""It expiates the year."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about one who fasts the day of Monday?"" He said: ""That is the day on which I was born and the day on which Prophethood was revealed to me."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ḥabbān ibn Hilāl from Abān ibn Yazīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8480","(8477) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Yasār narrated from Abū Tamīmah — (ḥ) and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Rajāʾ al-Adīb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, dictating: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to me: Abū al-Walīd narrated from al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Yasār al-Yashkurī: Abū Tamīmah al-Hujaymī narrated to us from Abū Mūsā (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever fasts the entire lifetime, Hellfire will be narrowed for him thus."" And he gestured with ninety. The wording is that of Abū Dāwūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī]"
"8481","(8478) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah from Abū Tamīmah from Abū Mūsā, who said: ""Whoever fasts the entire lifetime, Hellfire will be narrowed for him thus."" And he gestured with ninety. Shuʿbah did not raise it (to the Prophet). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī]"
"8482","(8479) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us: Maʿmar narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Ibn Muʿāniq or Abī Muʿāniq from Abī Mālik al-Ashʿarī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, in Paradise there is a chamber whose exterior is visible from its interior and whose interior is visible from its exterior. Allah has prepared it for the one who softens his speech, feeds others, maintains continuous fasting, and prays at night while the people are asleep."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than the previous chain. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"8483","(8480) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Yaʿqūb al-Ḍabbī narrated to him from Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah — I think — from Abī Umāmah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent us on a military expedition, and he mentioned the ḥadīth. Then I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, command me with a matter by which Allah will benefit me."" He said: ""Upon you is fasting, for there is nothing like it."" He said: Abū Umāmah would not be found except in a state of fasting — he, his wife, and his servant. If smoke was seen in his house during the day, it would be said: ""A guest has come to them."" Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you commanded me with a matter, and I hope that Allah has blessed me in it. So command me with another matter."" He said: ""Know that you do not prostrate a single prostration to Allah except that Allah raises you by it a degree, or records for you by it a good deed, or erases from you by it a sin."" Mahdī ibn Maymūn followed him (in narration) from Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaʿqūb. And Shuʿbah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaʿqūb from Abī Naṣr al-Hilālī from Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah from Abī Umāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī]"
"8484","(8481) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) used to fast continuously before his death. Nāfiʿ said: And ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar also fasted continuously in the latter part of his life. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8485","(8482) Abū Zakariyyā and Abū Bakr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Baḥr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿĀmir ibn Jashīb that he heard Zurʿah ibn Thawb say: ""I asked ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar about fasting the entire lifetime. He said: 'We used to count them among the foremost among us.' He said: 'And I asked him about fasting one day and breaking one day. He said: 'A fasting person has not left any room for fasting beyond that.' He said: 'And I asked him about fasting three days of every month. He said: 'He has fasted the entire lifetime and broken it.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"8486","(8483) Abū Zakariyyā and Abū Bakr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Baḥr narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: Ibn Lahīʿah and Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ narrated from Abū al-Aswad from ʿUrwah that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to fast the entire lifetime, both while traveling and while resident. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī]"
"8487","(8484) Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us — Bahr narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to me — 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me — that Bukayr narrated to him — that he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad saying: I saw 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, fasting during a journey. She stood up to mount after the 'Asr prayer, but a hot wind (samum) overcame her until she could not bear to mount. [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat).]"
"8488","(8485) Abu 'Abd Allah informed us: Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi narrated to us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Baghdadi informed us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub ibn Isma'il narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from Thabit and Humayd, from Anas, who said: Abu Talhah used not to fast during the era of the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, on account of military expeditions (ghazw). After the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, passed away, I never saw him in a state of breaking fast (muftir) except on the Day of Fitr or the Day of al-Nahr. [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
"8489","(8486) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi in Hamadan narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us — Adam narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — Thabit al-Bunani said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: Abu Talhah used not to fast during the era of the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him. When the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, was taken in death, I never saw him in a state of breaking fast except on a day of Fitr or a day of Adha. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"8490","(8487) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Hazim al-Hafiz, and Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us — they said: Abu 'Amr Isma'il ibn Nujayd al-Sulami informed us — Abu Muslim Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Basri narrated to us — Abu 'Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated — from Ibn Jurayj — from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr — from Muhammad ibn 'Abbad, who said: I said to Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah: Did the Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, forbid fasting on Friday? He said: Yes, by the Lord of this House. Al-Bukhari narrated it from Abu 'Asim, and Muslim reported it from the hadith of 'Abd al-Razzaq from Ibn Jurayj. Al-Bukhari said: Someone other than Abu 'Asim added: ""…that it be singled out with fasting."" The Sheikh said: This addition was mentioned by Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan from Ibn Jurayj, except that he shortened his isnad and did not mention 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Jubayr in it. This addition has also been reported in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah and others. [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
"8491","(8488) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""None of you should fast on Friday unless he fasts a day before it or a day after it."" [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
"8492","(8489) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Nsar al-Imam narrated to us — Yahya ibn Yahya and Ishaq said — Abu Mu'awiyah informed us, mentioning it with his isnad, except that he said: ""None of you shall fast on Friday unless he fasts before it or fasts after it."" Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya from Abu Mu'awiyah, and both al-Bukhari and Muslim reported it from the hadith of Hafs ibn Ghiyath from al-A'mash. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"8493","(8490) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim al-Muzakki narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us — al-Husayn ibn 'Ali narrated from Zaidah, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: ""Do not single out the night of Friday for standing in prayer (qiyam) among all the nights, and do not single out the day of Friday for fasting among all the days, unless it falls on a day that one of you is already fasting as part of his habitual fast."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Karib from al-Husayn ibn 'Ali. [Sahih. Reported by Muslim.]"
"8494","(8491) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Marzuq informed us — Shu'bah narrated from Qatadah, from Abu Ayyub, from Juwayriyah, that the Prophet, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon her on a Friday while she was fasting and said: ""Did you fast yesterday?"" She said: ""No."" He said: ""Will you fast tomorrow?"" She said: ""No."" He said: ""Then break your fast."" [Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
"8495","(8492) And with his isnad, he said: Yusuf narrated — Musaddad narrated — Yahya narrated from Shu'bah, with his isnad, something similar to it. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"8496","(8493) Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Tahir al-Daqqaq in Baghdad informed us — Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih informed us — 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Abu 'Asim informed us — Thawr ibn Yazid narrated — (h) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — al-Baghandi narrated to us — Abu 'Asim narrated from Thawr, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from 'Abd Allah ibn Busr, from his sister al-Samma', who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be blessings of Allah be upon him, said: ""Do not fast on Saturday, and if any of you finds nothing but a stick, let him chew it."" This is the wording of the hadith of al-Daqqaq. In the narration of Ibn 'Abdān: ""None of you shall fast on Saturday except what has been made obligatory upon him, and if he finds nothing but the bark of a tree, let him chew it."" Al-Walid ibn Muslim and others also narrated it from Thawr. Abu Dawud reported it in his Sunan. [Conflicting (mudtarib). Reported by Abu Dawud.]"
"8497","(8494) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us — al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us — Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us — al-Layth narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr, from his father, from his paternal aunt al-Ṣammāʾ, that she used to say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on the day of Saturday. And he would say: 'If any of you finds nothing but a green stick, let him break his fast upon it.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8498","(8495) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānʾ narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated, he said: ""I heard al-Layth narrating from Ibn Shihāb: that whenever it was mentioned to him that the Prophet ﷺ had been forbidden from fasting on the day of Saturday, he would say: 'This is a Ḥimṣī hadith.'"" [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8499","(8496) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ ibn Sufyān narrated to us — al-Walīd narrated from al-Awzāʿī, who said: ""I kept concealing it, but then I saw it had become widespread — meaning this hadith of Ibn Busr regarding fasting on the day of Saturday. And there had already preceded in the hadith of Juwayriyyat al-Ḥārith, may Allah be pleased with her, in the chapter before it, that which indicated the permissibility of fasting on the day of Saturday, as though the prohibition was intended to specify fasting on that day as a form of veneration. And Allah knows best."" [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8500","(8497) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us — Abū al-Muwajjih narrated to us — ʿAbdān informed us — ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn al-Mubārak, narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated from his father: that Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, informed him that Ibn ʿAbbās and a number of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, to ask her which days the Prophet ﷺ fasted most frequently. She said: ""Saturday and Sunday."" So I returned to them and informed them, and it was as though they found it strange. So they all went to her and said: ""We only sent you to her regarding such-and-such, and you are saying such-and-such."" She said: ""You have spoken the truth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted most frequently on Saturday and Sunday, and he used to say: 'They are days of celebration for the polytheists, and I wish to oppose them.'"" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān]"
"8501","(8498) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Bakr, Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān, narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: ""This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us,"" and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""A woman must not fast while her husband is present except with his permission."" Muslim transmitted it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8502","(8499) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me — ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsá narrated to us — Musaddad ibn Qaṭan narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ while we were with him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my husband Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal beats me when I pray, and he breaks my fast when I fast, and he does not pray Fajr until the sun rises.' And Ṣafwān was present, so he asked him about what she had said. He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, as for her saying that I beat her when I pray — she recites two sūrahs, and I have forbidden her from them. I said: if she were to recite a single sūrah, it would suffice people. And as for her saying that I break her fast when she fasts — she goes off and fasts, and I am a young man, and I cannot bear it.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on that day: 'A woman must not fast except with her husband's permission.' And as for her saying that I do not pray until the sun rises — we are a people for whom that is known; we hardly ever wake up until the sun rises. So when you wake up, pray."" [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8503","(8500) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said — Abū al-ʿAbbās, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Yūnus narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Ibn Abī Unas: that his father narrated to him that he heard Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When Ramaḍān comes, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of Hellfire are closed, and the devils are chained."" Muslim transmitted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyá from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī transmitted it from another route from Ibn Shihāb. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8504","(8501) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr, ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Simāk, in Baghdad, narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyāsh narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the first night of Ramaḍān arrives, the rebellious jinn are chained, the gates of the Fire are closed and none of them is opened, the gates of the Gardens are opened and none of them is closed, and a caller calls out: 'O seeker of good, come forth! O seeker of evil, desist!' And Allah will emancipate [souls] from the Fire."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī]"
"8505","(8502) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī al-Faqīh informed us — Abū ʿAlī, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār, narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, narrated to us — and the wording is his — Abū Saʿīd, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī, in Makkah, dictating, narrated to us — Yaḥyá ibn Jaʿfar ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayrān narrated to us — both of them said: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us — Kathīr ibn Zayd narrated from ʿAmr ibn Tamīm, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The month of Ramaḍān has come upon you — by the oath of the Messenger of Allah, no month has passed over the Muslims better for them than it, and by the oath of the Messenger of Allah, no month has passed over the hypocrites worse for them than it. Verily, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, records his reward and his supererogatory deeds before he enters it, and records his sin and his wretchedness before he enters it. That is because the believer prepares provisions of expenditure for worship, and the hypocrite prepares therein heedlessness of the Muslims and pursuit of their faults. So it is a gain for the believer which the wicked man squanders."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"8506","(8503) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — Abū Bakr, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Warrāq, informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Ḥibbān ibn Mūsá narrated from Ibn al-Mubārak, from Kathīr ibn Zayd, who narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn Tamīm narrated from his father that he heard Abū Hurayrah mentioning the hadith in a similar manner, except that he said: ""And a loss for the wicked man."" [Ḍaʿīf. See the preceding hadith]"
"8507","(8504) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Sulaymān — meaning Ibn Bilāl — from Kathīr ibn Zayd.

(Ḥ) And al-Qāḍī Abū ʿUmar — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Haytham al-Bisṭāmī — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūd ibn Kharzādh al-Qāḍī in al-Ahwāz informed us: Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Anṣārī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah al-Zubayrī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated to us, from Kathīr ibn Zayd, from al-Walīd ibn Rabāḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the minbar and said: ""Āmīn, āmīn, āmīn."" It was said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, you do not usually do this."" He said: ""Jibrīl, peace be upon him, said to me: 'May the nose be soiled of the servant upon whom Ramadān enters and he is not forgiven' — so I said Āmīn. Then he said: 'May the nose be soiled of the servant in whose presence you are mentioned and he does not send blessings upon you' — so I said Āmīn. Then he said: 'May the nose be soiled of the servant who finds his parents, or one of them, and they do not cause him to enter Paradise' — so I said Āmīn.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. And in the narration of Sulaymān: ""He ascended the minbar"" and he said: ""May the nose be soiled of a servant"" or ""may he be far removed.""

[Ṣaḥīgh for other than him. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
"8508","(8505) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad — al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm ibn Muḥammad al-Dihqān — in Marw informed us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Qurṭ narrated to me that ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār narrated to him that he heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Whoever fasts Ramadān, knowing its limits and observing what he ought to observe therein, it will expiate what preceded it of sins.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
"8509","(8506) Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī — narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī — in Makkah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever fasts Ramadān out of faith and seeking reward, he will be forgiven whatever preceded of his sins.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī, from Sufyān.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8510","(8507) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān al-Murādī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb ibn Muslim al-Qurashī narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Yazīd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib informed me that he heard Abū Hurayrah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Every deed of the son of Ādam is for him except fasting, for it is for Me and I shall reward for it. By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the changed breath of the mouth of the fasting person is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah, from Ibn Wahb, and he said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: 'Every deed of the son of Ādam…'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8511","(8508) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs — narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, from what he read to Mālik, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""By the One in whose hand is my soul, the changed breath of the mouth of the fasting person is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk. He leaves his desire, his food, and his drink for My sake. Fasting is for Me and I shall reward for it. Every good deed is multiplied ten times up to seven hundred times, except fasting, for it is for Me and I shall reward for it.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8512","(8509) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both said: al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb — Abū al-Faḍl — narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Every deed of the son of Ādam is multiplied. A good deed is multiplied ten times up to seven hundred times. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: 'Except fasting, for it is for Me and I shall reward for it. He leaves his food and his desire for My sake.' The fasting person has two joys: a joy at the time of breaking his fast, and a joy at the time of meeting his Lord. And indeed, the changed breath of his mouth is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk. Fasting is a shield. Fasting is a shield.""

Muslim narrated it from Abū Saʿīd al-Ashajj, from Wakīʿ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
"8513","(8510) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Abū al-Ṭayyib al-Muẓaffar ibn Sahl al-Khalīlī narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb ibn Ḥassān al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, from his father, who said: I heard a man ask Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, saying: ""O Abū Muḥammad, what do you say regarding what the Prophet ﷺ narrates from his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic: 'Every deed of the son of Ādam is for him except fasting, for it is for Me and I shall reward for it'?""

Ibn ʿUyaynah said: ""This is among the finest and most firmly established of aḥādīth. When the Day of Resurrection comes, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will take account of His servant and will have him repay whatever injustices are due from him regarding all of his deeds, until nothing remains except fasting. Then Allah will bear whatever injustices remain upon him and will admit him into Paradise through his fasting.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by the author.]"
"8514","(8511) Abū Naṣr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī, the righteous shaykh — narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to me: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me, from Sahl ibn Saʿd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, in Paradise there is a gate called al-Rayyān. The fasting people will enter through it on the Day of Resurrection, and none will enter with them. It will be said: 'Where are the fasting people?' So they will enter through it. When the last of them has entered, it will be closed, and none will enter after them.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Khālid ibn Makhlad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Khālid.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8515","(8512) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī in Ṭūs informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Abū Ghassān narrated to me: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me, from Sahl ibn Saʿd, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Paradise has eight gates, among them a gate called al-Rayyān. None will enter it except the fasting people.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it.]"
"8516","(8513) Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf — narrated to us by dictation, and Abū Ṭāhir al-Imām narrated to us by reading over him, both saying: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān — informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Baghdādī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Ḥabīb ibn Zayd al-Anṣārī, who said: I heard a freedwoman of ours, called Laylā, narrate from my grandmother, Umm ʿUmārah bint Kaʿb, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to her and she offered him food. He said to her: ""Eat."" She said: ""I am fasting."" He ﷺ said: ""When food is eaten in the presence of the fasting person, the angels send blessings upon him until they finish"" — or he said: ""until they complete their eating.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
"8517","(8514) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us — Sufyān said: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār said: I heard ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about ""al-sāʾiḥīn"" (the travellers/devotees). He ﷺ said: ""They are the fasting ones."" [Weak. Reported by Ibn Mughīrah]"
"8518","(8515) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Awīsī narrated to us — (ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us — both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the most generous of people with goodness, and he was at his most generous in Ramaḍān when he would meet Jibrīl (peace be upon him). Jibrīl would meet him every night in Ramaḍān until it would depart, and the Prophet ﷺ would recite the Qurʾān to him. When Jibrīl met him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more generous with goodness than the unleashed wind. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8519","(8516) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbdullāh informed me — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Manṣūr ibn Abī Muzāḥim narrated to us — (ḥ) And al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dārimī narrated to me — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Warkānī narrated to us — both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned it similarly. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl and others from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd, and Muslim narrated it from Manṣūr ibn Abī Muzāḥim and Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous entry]"
"8520","(8517) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us by dictation — Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī in Makkah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Daqīqī narrated to us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us — Ṣadaqah ibn Mūsā narrated to us — Thābit narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: It was said, ""O Messenger of Allah, which fasting is most excellent?"" He ﷺ said: ""The fasting of Shaʿbān, out of reverence for Ramaḍān."" He said, ""Then which charity is most excellent?"" He ﷺ said: ""Charity given in Ramaḍān."" [Weak. Reported by al-Tirmidhī]"
"8521","(8518) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibnbishrān in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us — Maʿmar informed me — a man from Banī Ghaṭfān informed me: He heard Saʿīd al-Maqburī narrating from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The one who eats and gives thanks is like the one who fasts and is patient."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. Reported by al-Tirmidhī]"
"8522","(8519) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us — ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī narrated from Maʿn ibn Muḥammad al-Ghifārī, from Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿAlī, who said: I was with Abū Hurayrah at al-Baqīʿ, and I heard Abū Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The one who eats and gives thanks is like the one who fasts and is patient."" And it was also narrated from ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī from Maʿn from al-Maqburī and Ḥanẓalah from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. See the previous entry]"
"8523","(8520) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ḥurrah narrated from his paternal uncle, Ḥakīm ibn Abī Ḥurrah, from Salmān al-Agharr, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: I do not know it except from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he ﷺ said: ""Indeed, the one who eats and gives thanks shall have a reward like that of the one who fasts and is patient."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. See the previous entry]"
"8524","(8521) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-ḤāfiẾ informed us by dictation — Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {Indeed, We sent it down on the Night of Decree} — he said: The Qurʾān was sent down in the Night of Decree as a single whole to the lowest heaven, and it was at the position of the stars. And Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, would send it down upon His Messenger ﷺ, some parts following others. And Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {And they say, ""Why was the Qurʾān not sent down to him all at once?"" Thus [it is] that We may strengthen your heart thereby, and We have rehearsed it in careful order.} [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Ḥākim]"
"8525","(8522) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Saqqāʾ al-Isfarāʾinī in Naysābūr informed us — he said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Baṭṭah al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Zakariyyā al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā al-Umawī narrated to us — Muslim ibn Khālid al-Zanjī narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned a man from the Children of Israel who wore his weapon in the cause of Allah for a thousand months. The Muslims were amazed at that. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent down: {Indeed, We sent it down on the Night of Decree. And what can make you know what the Night of Decree is? The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months} — during which that man wore his weapon in the cause of Allah for a thousand months. And this is a mursal [hadīth]. [Weak. Reported by Ibn Abī Ḥātim]"
"8526","(8523) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Abū Muslim narrated to us — Muslim narrated to us — Hishām narrated from Yaḥyā, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever stands [in prayer] on the Night of Decree out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins shall be forgiven; and whoever fasts during Ramaḍān out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins shall be forgiven."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, and Muslim reported it from the ḥadīth of Muʿādh ibn Hishām al-Dastūwāʾī from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8527","(8524) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyawayh al-Isfarāʾinī informed us in the year two hundred and fifty-eight; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shuʿayb informed us; Abū al-Zinād informed us, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever stands (in prayer) on the Night of Decree, encountering it with faith and seeking reward, his preceding sins will be forgiven.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Warqāʾ from Abū al-Zinād.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
"8528","(8525) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī al-Ḥarbī informed us in the Jāmiʿ of al-Ḥarbiyyah in Baghdad; Abū Aḥmad Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ghālib informed us; Mūsā ibn Masʿūd informed us; ʿIkrimah informed us, from Abū Zumayl, from Mālik ibn Murthad, from his father, who said: I said to Abū Dharrr: ""Did you ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the Night of Decree?"" He said: ""I was the one who asked about it most""—meaning, more than the people. So I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, tell me about the Night of Decree—is it in Ramadan, or in other than it?"" He said: ""No, rather it is in the month of Ramadan."" I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, does it accompany the Prophets for as long as they are alive, and when the Prophets are taken and raised, it is raised with them, or does it remain until the Day of Resurrection?"" He said: ""No, rather it remains until the Day of Resurrection."" He said: I said: ""Then tell me, in which part of the month of Ramadan is it?"" He said: ""Seek it in the last ten and the first ten."" Then the Prophet of Allah ﷺ spoke and narrated, and I seized upon his moment of inattention, so I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, tell me in which ten it is."" He said: ""Seek it in the last ten, and do not ask me about anything after this."" Then he narrated and narrated, and I seized upon his moment of inattention, so I said: ""I adjure you, O Messenger of Allah, by my right over you, tell me in which of the ten it is."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry with me—an anger he had not shown before nor after—then he said: ""Seek it in the last seven, and do not ask me about anything after this.""
[Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad extracted it.]"
"8529","(8526) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Naysābūrī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Muslim ibn Wārah informed us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Abū Isḥāq al-Hamdānī, from Saʿīb ibn Jubayr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked—and I was listening—about the Night of Decree, and he said: ""It is in every Ramadan.""

Sufyān and Shuʿbah narrated it from Abū Isḥāq, Mawqūf (stopped) at Ibn ʿUmar; they did not raise it to the Prophet ﷺ.
[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd extracted it.]"
"8530","(8527) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: ""Seek the Night of Decree in the last ten (nights) of Ramadan.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
"8531","(8528) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us; it was recited to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you, from Ibn Shihāb, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I was shown the Night of Decree, then one of my family woke me, and I forgot it. So seek it in the last ten (nights).""

Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir and Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb.

And we have narrated in that regard from Jabalah ibn Suḥaym and Muḥārib ibn Dithār from Ibn ʿUmar.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim extracted it.]"
"8532","(8529) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us. Both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us; Sufyān informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, reaching the Prophet ﷺ: A man saw the Night of Decree in the last ten (nights). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Your dream has been shown to me, and it has coincided upon this. So seek it in the last ten (nights).""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
"8533","(8530) From Sufyān, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, who said: A man saw the Night of Decree on the night of the twenty-seventh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Your dream has been shown to me in the last ten (nights). So seek it among the odd nights thereof.""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; Abū ʿAmr informed me; Abū Yaʿlā informed us; ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb informed us; both said: Sufyān informed us, and he mentioned it.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
"8534","(8531) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym informed us; Qutaybah informed us. Both said: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar informed us, from Abū Suhayl Nāfiʿ ibn Mālik, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Seek the Night of Decree among the odd nights of the last ten (nights) of Ramadan.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd.

And we have narrated it also from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī and others from the Prophet ﷺ.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
"8535","(8532) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Abū Masʿūd—meaning al-Jurayrī—informed us, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed iʿtikāf during the middle ten (nights) of the month of Ramadan, seeking the Night of Decree before it was made clear to him. When they were completed, he ordered the structure to be dismantled, so it was taken down and removed. Then it was made clear to him in the last ten (nights), so he ordered the structure to be re-erected in its place, and he performed iʿtikāf during the last ten (nights). He came out to us and said: ""O people, I was informed of the Night of Decree, so that I might inform you of it or tell you about it. But two men quarreled, disputing, and the Shayṭān was with them, so I forgot it. Seek it in the ninth, the seventh, and the fifth."" Abū Naḍrah said: I said to Abū Saʿīd: ""You are the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; you are more knowledgeable of numbers than us. How do we count them?"" He said: ""Indeed, we are more entitled to that than you. When twenty-one has passed, the one following it is the ninth. When the one following it has passed, the one following it is the seventh. When the one following it has passed, the one following it is the fifth.""

Abū Masʿūd said: And Abū al-ʿAlāʾ informed me, from Muṭarrif, from Muʿāwiyah, that he said: ""And in the third.""

Muslim extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muthannā and others from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī with its meaning, except that he said: ""When one and twenty has passed, the one following it is two and twenty, and it is the ninth."" And he did not mention the ḥadīth of Muʿāwiyah.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim extracted it.]"
"8536","(8533) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū Salamah informed us; Wuhaib informed us; Ayyūb informed us, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Seek it—the Night of Decree—in the last ten (nights) of Ramadan: in the ninth that remains, in the seventh that remains, and in the fifth that remains.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Salamah Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. Al-Bukhārī said: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb followed him, from Ayyūb.

And from Khālid, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: ""Seek it in the twenty-fourth.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
"8537","(8534) Abū ʿAmr — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb — informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān — informed me; Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Sadūsī — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād — narrated to us; ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal — narrated from; Lāḥiq ibn Ḥumayd and ʿIkrimah, who both said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Who knows when Laylat al-Qadr is?"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""It is in the ten, and it is in the nine that pass, or in the seven that remain.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī al-Aswad from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8538","(8535) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh — informed me; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī — narrated from what he read to Mālik, from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith al-Taymī, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Saʿīd that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform iʿtikāf during the middle ten days of Ramadan. He performed iʿtikāf one year until, when it was the night of the twenty-first — which was the night on which he would emerge from his iʿtikāf — he said: ""Whoever performed iʿtikāf with me, let him perform iʿtikāf during the last ten. I was shown this night, then I was made to forget it. And I saw myself prostrating in water and mud the following morning. So seek it in the last ten, and seek it in every odd night."" Abū Saʿīd said: It rained that night, and the mosque was on a trellis roof, and the mosque leaked. Abū Saʿīd said: My eyes saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and upon his forehead and his nose were traces of water and mud, on the morning of the twenty-first.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Dārawardī from Yazīd ibn al-Hād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8539","(8536) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī — both said: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Shādhān al-Abnandarqī — narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Khashram — narrated to us; Abū Ḍamrah — narrated from; al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān — from; Abū al-Naḍr, the freed slave of ʿUmar ibn ʿUbayd Allāh — from; Busr ibn Saʿīd — from; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I was shown Laylat al-Qadr, then I was made to forget it, and I was shown that I would be prostrating in water and mud the following morning."" He said: We were rained upon on the night of the twenty-third, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led us in prayer, then he turned away, and indeed the traces of water and mud were upon his nose and his forehead. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays used to say: ""The twenty-third."" The wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Khashram. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8540","(8537) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Faqīh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ṭabarānī — informed us therein; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh — narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī — narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb — narrated to us; Yazīd ibn al-Hād — informed me that Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm informed him from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays, who said: We were in the desert, and we said: If we come with our families it will be difficult for us, and if we leave them behind they will face hardship. He said: So they sent me, and I was the youngest of them, to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I mentioned their words to him. He commanded us regarding the night of the twenty-third. Ibn al-Hād said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm used to exert great effort during that night. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8541","(8538) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus — narrated to us; Zuhayr — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm — narrated from; Ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays al-Juhanī — from his father, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have a desert dwelling in which I reside, and I praise Allah and pray therein. So command me with a night that I may come down to this mosque."" He said: ""Come down on the night of the twenty-third."" I said to his son: ""How would your father act?"" He said: ""He would enter the mosque after praying ʿAṣr and would not leave it for any need until he prayed Fajr. When he prayed Fajr, he would find his mount at the door of the mosque, and he would sit upon it and depart to his desert dwelling."" [Ṣaḥīḥ ghayrihi. See the one before it]"
"8542","(8539) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān — informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār — narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah — narrated from; al-Aʿmash — from; Abū Ṣāliḥ — from; Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""How much of the month has passed?"" They said: ""Twenty-two have passed and eight remain."" He said: ""Rather, twenty-two have passed and seven remain. Seek it this night."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Mājah]"
"8543","(8540) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan — informed us; Abū Muḥammad — Daʿlaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijistānī — narrated to us in the City of Peace; Mūsā ibn Hārūn — narrated to us. He said: I said to Abū Nuʿaym: ""Did Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī narrate to you from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah — and I think he mentioned Ibn ʿUmar — who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and they mentioned Laylat al-Qadr, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'How much of the month has passed?' They said: 'Twenty-two and eight remain.' He said: 'Twenty-two have passed and seven remain. The month is twenty-nine. So seek it this night.'"" Abū Nuʿaym said: ""Yes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it]"
"8544","(8541) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā — informed me; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr — narrated to us; Khallād al-Juʿfī — narrated to us; Abū Muslim — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿīd, the commander of al-Aʿmash — narrated from; al-Aʿmash — from; Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ — from his father — from Abū Hurayrah, who said: We mentioned Laylat al-Qadr, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""How much of the month has passed?"" We said: ""Twenty-two have passed and eight remain."" He said: ""Twenty-two have passed and seven remain. Seek it this night. The month is twenty-nine."" [Munkar al-isnād]"
"8545","(8542) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim — narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ — narrated from; Sufyān — from; al-Aʿmash — from; Ibrāhīm — from; al-Aswad, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: ""Seek Laylat al-Qadr on the night of the seventeenth, the morning of Badr, or the twenty-first, or the twenty-third."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī]"
"8546","(8543) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd — narrated to us; Ḥakīm ibn Sayf al-Raqqī — narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh, meaning Ibn ʿAmr — narrated from; Zayd, meaning Ibn Abī Unaysah — from; Abū Isḥāq — from; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad — from his father — from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to us: ""Seek it on the night of the seventeenth of Ramadan, and the night of the twenty-first, and the night of the twenty-third."" Then he fell silent. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
"8547","(8544) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: al-Layth, Yūnus, and Mālik ibn Anas informed me from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ were shown in a dream that Laylat al-Qadr was in the last seven nights of Ramaḍān. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I see that your dreams have agreed upon it being in the last seven. So whoever seeks it, let him seek it in the last seven.'"" [Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8548","(8545) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad and Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read before Mālik, and he mentioned the hadith with its meaning. al-Bukhārī reported it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim reported it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"8549","(8546) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from Sālim ibn ʿAbdillāh from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Some of you were shown Laylat al-Qadr in the first seven, and some of you were shown it in the last seven."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Seek it in the last seven."" al-Bukhārī reported it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim reported it from the hadith of Yūnus from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī]"
"8550","(8547) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī, and Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I read before Mālik from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last seven."" Muslim reported it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"8551","(8548) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām informed us: Aswad ibn ʿĀmir Shādhān informed us: Shuʿbah narrated to me. ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār informed me, saying: ""I heard Ibn ʿUmar narrate from the Prophet ﷺ regarding Laylat al-Qadr: 'Whoever seeks it, let him seek it on the twenty-seventh night.'"" Shuʿbah said: ""A trustworthy man mentioned to me from Sufyān that he used to say: 'He said: ""Whoever seeks it, let him seek it in the remaining seven.""'"" I do not know which of the two is correct; Shuʿbah was uncertain. The authentic narration is that of the group, not the narration of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous one]"
"8552","(8549) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿUqbah ibn Ḥurayth, who heard Ibn ʿUmar narrate from the Prophet ﷺ regarding Laylat al-Qadr: ""Seek it in the last ten. If any one of you is weak or unable, then let him not be overcome regarding the remaining seven."" Muslim reported it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ghandar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim]"
"8553","(8550) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Saʿdī informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Anas ibn Mālik from ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us intending to inform us about Laylat al-Qadr, but two men from among the Muslims quarreled. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I came out to you intending to inform you about Laylat al-Qadr, but there was a dispute between so-and-so and so-and-so, and it was taken away. Perhaps it is for the best. Seek it in the last ten: in the fifth, the seventh, and the ninth.'"" al-Bukhārī reported it from the hadith of Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
"8554","(8551) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz and Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAbadah ibn Abī Lubābah and ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd from Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh, who said: ""I asked Ubayy ibn Kaʿb about Laylat al-Qadr, and he swore without making any exception that it was the twenty-seventh night."" I said: ""What makes you say that, Abā al-Mundhir?"" He said: ""By the sign or the indication that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned: 'On that morning, the sun rises without any rays.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim]"
"8555","(8552) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us: al-Ḥumaydī informed us: Sufyān said. (Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar informed us: Sufyān said. al-Ḥumaydī said: ʿAbadah ibn Abī Lubābah and ʿĀṣim ibn Bahdalah narrated that they both heard Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh say: ""I said to Ubayy ibn Kaʿb: 'O Abā al-Mundhir, your brother Ibn Masʿud says: ""Whoever stands [in prayer] throughout the year will attain Laylat al-Qadr.""'"" He said: 'May Allah have mercy on him. He intended that you should not rely [on that]. He knew that it is in the month of Ramaḍān, and that it is in the last ten, and that it is the twenty-seventh night.' We said: 'O Abā al-Mundhir, by what do you recognize that?' He said: 'By the sign or the indication that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed us of: the sun rises on that day without any rays.'"" Muslim reported it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous one]"
"8556","(8553) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī informed us. Abū ʿAbdillāh said: informed us, and Abū Naṣr said: narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to me: my father narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dimashqī said: Marwān narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī informed us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated from Yazīd ibn Kaysān from Abī Ḥāzim from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""We mentioned Laylat al-Qadr in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'Which one of you remembers when the moon rose and it was like the split of a bowl?'"" Muslim reported it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād and others. It has been said that this occurs on the twenty-third. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
"8557","(8554) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Khalīl narrated to us; Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us; al-Masʿūdī narrated from Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr ibn Jaʿdah, from Abū ʿUbaydah, from ʿAbd Allāh, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and asked him about Laylat al-Qadr. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Which one of you remembers the night of al-Ṣahbāwāt?"" ʿAbd Allāh said: ""By Allah, I do remember it, O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you. I had in my hand some dates with which I was taking the pre-dawn meal (suḥūr), concealing myself at the back of a mount at the time of Fajr, and that was when the moon had risen."" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā]"
"8558","(8555) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from Muṭarrif, from Muʿāwiyah, who said: ""Laylat al-Qadr is the night of the twenty-seventh."" Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī stopped it (as a mawqūf narration), and Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh raised it (to the Prophet). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8559","(8556) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, who heard Muṭarrif, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding Laylat al-Qadr, that he said: ""It is the night of the twenty-seventh."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8560","(8557) Abū Saʿd — Yaḥyā ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ṣāʾigh — narrated to us in al-Rayy; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī al-Khuzaʿī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥanbil ibn Hilāl ibn Asad al-Shaybānī and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar both said: Muʿādh narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Harawī — informed us; Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd — ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī ʿUthmān al-Zāhid — informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Manīʿī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us; my father narrated to me from Qatādah, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am an old, sick man, and standing in prayer is difficult for me. So command me of a night in which perhaps Allah will grant me the opportunity of Laylat al-Qadr."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Take it to be the seventh (night)."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"8561","(8558) The jurist Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī informed us in Baghdād, in the Mosque of al-Ruṣāfah; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar informed us from Qatādah and ʀāṣim, that they both heard ʿIkrimah saying: Ibn ʿAbbās said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) gathered the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and asked them about Laylat al-Qadr. They agreed that it was in the last ten (nights). I said to ʿUmar: ""Indeed I know — and I think I know — which night it is."" He said: ""Which night is it?"" I said: ""The seventh that has passed, or the seventh that remains, of the last ten."" He said: ""How do you know that?"" I said: ""Allah created seven heavens, and seven earths, and seven days, and indeed time revolves in seven. And the human being was created, and he eats and prostrates upon seven limbs. And the Ṭawāf is seven (circuits), and the mountains are seven."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""You have discerned a matter that we did not discern."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
"8562","(8559) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us; Ibn Fuḍayl narrated from ʀāṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: I was with ʿUmar, and his Companions were with him, and he asked them: ""What do you say about the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding Laylat al-Qadr: 'Seek it in the last ten, in the odd (nights)' — which night do you think it is?"" Some of them said: ""The night of the first (of the odd nights),"" and some said: ""The night of the third,"" and some said: ""The night of the fifth,"" and some said: ""The night of the seventh."" They said, ""And I was silent."" He said: ""Why do you not speak?"" I said: ""You commanded me not to speak until they had spoken."" He said: ""I only sent for you so that you would speak."" I said: ""Indeed, I have heard Allah mention the seven, and He mentioned seven heavens, and of the earth the like thereof, and the human being was created from seven, and the vegetation of the earth is seven."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""This is what you have told me that I did not know. Tell me about what I do not know — your statement: 'The vegetation of the earth is seven.'"" He said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {Indeed, We have split the earth with a splitting, and We have caused to grow therein grain, and grapes, and herbage, and olives, and palm gardens, and dense orchards} — the dense orchards (ḥadāʾiq ghlubā) are the gardens of palm trees and trees. {And fruits and grass (abā)} — al-Abb is what the earth produces of that which the beasts and cattle eat, and which the people do not eat."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) then said to his Companions: ""Have you been unable to say what this boy has said, whose head's veins have not yet fully formed? By Allah, I indeed hold the same opinion as he has expressed."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
"8563","(8560) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, and both said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to them; both said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Abū Yaʿqūb al-ʿAbdī, from Muslim, from Masrūq, who said: I heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) saying: ""When the last ten (nights) of Ramaḍān entered, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would enliven the night, awaken his family, and tighten his waist-wrapper (mizar)."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Ibn Abī ʿUmar, all of them from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8564","(8561) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us; Abū ʿAmr al-Mustamlī narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, who said: I heard Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd saying: I heard al-Aswad ibn Yazīd saying: ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would strive (exert himself) in the last ten (nights) as he would not strive in any other (nights)."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and Abū Kāmil. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8565","(8562) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʀāṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""When the last ten (nights) of Ramaḍān arrived, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would tighten his waist-wrapper and withdraw from his wives."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8566","(8563) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us; Abū Bakr Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from Abū Ḥuṣayn, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would perform iʿtikāf for ten days every Ramaḍān. Then in the year in which he passed away, he performed iʿtikāf for twenty days."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8567","(8564) It was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak, it was narrated to us by ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, it was narrated to us by Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb, it was narrated to us by Abū Dāwūd, it was narrated to us by Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Thābit, from Abū Rāfiʿ, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days of Ramadan. One year he travelled and did not perform iʿtikāf; so the following year he performed iʿtikāf for twenty days.

And something similar has been narrated from Anas ibn Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8568","(8565) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ, it was narrated to us by al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, it was narrated to us by Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb, it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr, it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAdīy, from Ḥumayd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was resident, he would perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days, and when he travelled, he would perform iʿtikāf the following year for twenty days. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
"8569","(8566) It was narrated to us by Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, it was narrated to us by Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, it was narrated to us by Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār, it was narrated to us by Abū Muʿāwiyah.

(Ḥ) And it was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, it was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, it was narrated to us by Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn, it was narrated to us by Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, it was narrated to us by Abū Muʿāwiyah, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days of Ramadan. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā.

It was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah, and both of them narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8570","(8567) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, it was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Shaybānī, it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym al-Madanī and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq, who both said: it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, it was narrated to us by al-Muʿtamir, it was narrated to me by ʿImārah ibn Ghaziyyah al-Anṣārī, I heard Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrating from Abū Salamah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed iʿtikāf during the first ten days of Ramadan, then he performed iʿtikāf during the middle ten days in a Turkish tent, at the entrance of which there was a mat. He said: He took the mat with his hand and moved it to one side of the tent, then he raised his head and spoke to the people, and they drew close to him. He said: ""I performed iʿtikāf during the first ten days seeking this night, then I performed iʿtikāf during the middle ten days, then I was told that it is in the last ten days. So whoever among you wishes to perform iʿtikāf, let him do so."" So the people performed iʿtikāf with him. He said: ""And I was shown it on a night of odd-numbered date, and I was shown that I would prostrate in the morning in mud and water."" So when it was the morning of the twenty-first, he had risen for Fajr, and the sky rained, and the mosque was flooded with water. I saw the mud and water. When he finished the Ṣalāh of Fajr, his forehead and the tip of his nose had mud and water on them, and it was the night of the twenty-first from the last ten days.

It was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8571","(8568) And it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, it was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, it was narrated to us by Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn, it was narrated to us by Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, it was narrated to us by Abū Muʿāwiyah, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) intended to perform iʿtikāf, he would pray Fajr, then enter his place of iʿtikāf. He had ordered for his tent to be pitched, as he intended to perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days of Ramadan. So Zaynab (may Allah be pleased with her) ordered for her tent to be pitched, and it was pitched. And other wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered for tents to be pitched, and they were pitched. When he prayed Fajr, he looked and saw the tents. He said: ""They intend righteousness (al-birr)."" So he ordered for his tent to be dismantled, and he abandoned the iʿtikāf in the month of Ramadan until he performed iʿtikāf in the first ten days of Shawwāl.

It was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from other routes from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8572","(8569) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāh al-Muzakkī, who said: it was narrated to us by Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, it was narrated to us by Baḥr ibn Naṣr, who said: it was recited to Ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed you that Nāfiʿ narrated to him from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days of Ramadan. He said: And Nāfiʿ said: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar showed me the place in the mosque where the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform iʿtikāf.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir, both of them from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8573","(8570) It was narrated to us by Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, who said: it was narrated to us by Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, it was narrated to us by Baḥr ibn Naṣr, who said: it was recited to ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb: Yūnus ibn Yazīd, Mālik ibn Anas, and al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed you, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr and ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said.

(Ḥ) And it was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, it was narrated to us by ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā, it was narrated to us by Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlīy and Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī, who both said: it was narrated to us by Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, who said: I read to Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) performed iʿtikāf, he would bring his head close to me and I would comb it, and he would not enter the house except for the need of human necessity.

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And in the narration of Ibn Wahb from the group: The Prophet (ﷺ) would not enter the house except for the need of human necessity.

And ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: He would bring his head to me while he was in the mosque, and I would comb it. And he narrated from ʿUrwah and ʿAmrah, as though he reconciled the narration of Mālik with the narration of al-Layth and Yūnus. As for Mālik, he says in it: from ʿUrwah, from ʿAmrah.

It was narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā thus, and both of them narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Layth ibn Saʿd from Ibn Shihāb from ʿUrwah and ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8574","(8571) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān, it was narrated to us by Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār, it was narrated to us by ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk, it was narrated to us by Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Bukayr, it was narrated to us by al-Layth, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days of Ramadan until Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, took him in death. Then his wives performed iʿtikāf after him.

And the Sunnah regarding the muʿtikaf is that he should not exit except for a need that is unavoidable, and he should not visit the sick, and he should not touch a woman or have intimate contact with her, and there is no iʿtikāf except in a mosque where congregational prayer is held. And the Sunnah for the one who performs iʿtikāf is that he fasts. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī]"
"8575","(8572) It was narrated to us by Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūh al-Faqīh, it was narrated to us by Abū Saʿīd ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī, it was narrated to us by Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb, it was narrated to us by Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, it was narrated to us by Hishām, it was narrated to us by Qatādah, that Ibn ʿAbbās and al-Ḥasan both said: There is no iʿtikāf except in a mosque in which the prayer is established. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqāt)]"
"8576","(8573) It was narrated to us by Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Sudarī in Khusrawjird, it was narrated to us by Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Khusrawjirdī, it was narrated to us by Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn, it was narrated to us by Ḥumayd ibn Zanjawayh, it was narrated to us by Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, it was narrated to us by Sharīk, from Layth, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from ʿAlī al-Azdī, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The most hateful of matters to Allah is innovation (bidʿah), and among innovations is performing iʿtikāf in the mosques that are in the residential quarters (of houses). [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8577","(8574) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdūyah ibn Sahl al-Ghāzī informed us; Maḥmūd ibn Ādam al-Marwazī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Jāmiʿ ibn Abī Rāshid, from Abū Wāʾil, who said:

Ḥudhayfah said to ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him — while he was standing between his house and the house of Abū Mūsā: ""Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'There is no iʿtikāf in the Masjid al-Ḥarām'?"" — or he said: ""in the three mosques."" ʿAbdullah said: ""Perhaps you have forgotten and they have remembered, and you have erred and they have been correct."" (The doubt is from me.) [Ṣaḥīḥ. The chain is sound.]"
"8578","(8575) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd informed us; Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would extend his head out of the mosque while he was in iʿtikāf, and I would wash it while I was menstruating.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Firyābī from Sufyān. And Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Zāʾidah from Manṣūr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
"8579","(8576) Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz informed us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd al-Ḥanafī informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Badīl narrated to me; ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated from Ibn ʿUmar, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to the Prophet ﷺ on the day of al-Jiʿrānah: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have a day of iʿtikāf that I owe."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Go and perform your iʿtikāf and fast it."""
"8580","(8577) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Ibn Badīl is alone in narrating it from ʿAmr, and he is weak in ḥadīth.

ʿAlī said: I heard Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī say: ""This is a munkar ḥadīth, because the trustworthy narrators from the companions of ʿAmr ibn Dīnār did not mention it — among them Ibn Jurayj, Ibn ʿUyaynah, Ḥammād ibn Salamah, Ḥammād ibn Zayd, and others — and Ibn Badīl is weak in ḥadīth."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8581","(8578) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū ʿĀmir Mūsā ibn ʿĀmir informed us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed us; Saʿīd — meaning Ibn Bashīr — informed me; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, had vowed to perform iʿtikāf during the period of disbelief and to fast. He asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ after his acceptance of Islam, and he commanded him to fulfill his vow.

The mention of the vow of fasting along with iʿtikāf is gharīb; Saʿīd ibn Bashīr is alone in narrating it from ʿUbaydullāh. And Allah knows best. [Munkar.]"
"8582","(8579) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Saʿīd — meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: ""There is no iʿtikāf except with fasting.""

Thus Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated it from his father from ʿĀʾishah.

And al-Zuhrī narrated it from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah in a ḥadīth he mentioned, at the end of which is: ""And the Sunnah for the one who performs iʿtikāf is that he fasts."" Its mention has preceded in this section. Thus more than one narrated it from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it.]"
"8583","(8580) It was narrated from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no iʿtikāf except with fasting.""

Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUmayr al-Dimashqī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Hāshim informed us; Suwayd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn narrated it.

This is a mistake from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn or from Suwayd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and Suwayd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dimashqī is utterly weak; what he narrates alone is not accepted.

It was narrated from ʿAṭāʾ from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in a mawqūf narration: ""Whoever performs iʿtikāf, upon him is fasting."" [Munkar. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
"8584","(8581) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us; Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Sufyān, from Ḥabīb, from ʿAṭāʾ, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"8585","(8582) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl in Baghdād informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustūyah informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Abī Fākhtah, who said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ""The mujāwar (one who resides) fasts."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
"8586","(8583) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī informed us; Sufyān informed us; ʿAmr narrated: I heard Abā Fākhtah Saʿīd ibn ʿIlāqah say: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ""The mujāwar fasts, and the mujāwar is the muʿtakif (one in iʿtikāf)."" It was mentioned to Sufyān that Hushaym says it from ʿAmr from Abī Fākhtah that Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""There is no iʿtikāf except with fasting."" Sufyān said: ""Hushaym has erred; it is as I told you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"8587","(8584) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us that a man said to ʿAmr ibn Dīnār: ""O Abū Muḥammad, what is the statement of Ibn ʿAbbās regarding fasting being obligatory upon the muʿtakif (one in Iʿtikāf)?"" ʿAmr said: ""Ibn ʿAbbās did not say that. Rather, he said: 'The muʿtakif fasts (i.e., fasting is part of his practice).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8588","(8585) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn ʿUmar, that they both said: ""The muʿtakif fasts."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8589","(8586) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us, Abū al-Muwajjih informed us, ʿAbdān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar informed us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that ʿUmar — raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu — said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, I made a vow during the Jāhiliyyah that I would perform Iʿtikāf for one night in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām."" The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: ""Fulfil your vow.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muqātil from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak. (T) Likewise, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān, Abū Usāmah, and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated it from ʿUbaydullāh, and they said in it: ""a night."" Likewise, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated it from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar. And Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim and Maʿmar said from Ayyūb: ""a day"" instead of ""a night."" Likewise, Shuʿbah narrated it from ʿUbaydullāh. The narration of the group from ʿUbaydullāh is more appropriate, and Ḥammād ibn Zayd is more knowledgeable about Ayyūb than others. And we narrated in the ḥadīth of Abī Muʿāwiya, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā — that the Prophet ﷺ performed Iʿtikāf in the first ten days of Shawwāl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8590","(8587) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Maḥbūb al-Ramlī informed us in Makkah, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ramlī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyya ibn Abī ʿAmr al-ʿAdanī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, from Abū Suhayl, the uncle of Mālik, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""There is no fasting obligatory upon the muʿtakif unless he makes it obligatory upon himself."" ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ramlī is alone in narrating this.

(T) Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad, from Abū Suhayl ibn Mālik, who said: ""I and Ibn Shihāb gathered before ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and his wife was under an obligation of three days of Iʿtikāf in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām. Ibn Shihāb said: 'There is no Iʿtikāf except with fasting.' ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: 'Is this from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Then from Abū Bakr?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Then from ʿUmar?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Then from ʿUthmān?' He said: 'No.' Abū Suhayl said: 'So I departed and found Ṭāwūs and ʿAṭāʾ and asked them about that. Ṭāwūs said: Ibn ʿAbbās did not consider fasting obligatory upon the muʿtakif unless he made it obligatory upon himself. And ʿAṭāʾ said: That is his personal opinion (raʾy).' This is the correct version, and it is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). Its raising (to the Prophet) is a mistake (wahm).""

Likewise, ʿAmr ibn Zurārah narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz as a mawqūf narration, and it is in what was mentioned. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
"8591","(8588) Abū ʿAbdillāh informed me by way of ijāzah that Abū al-Walīd informed them, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shīrūyah narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Zurārah narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, and he mentioned it as a mawqūf narration in an abbreviated form. He said: ""Ibn ʿAbbās did not consider fasting obligatory upon the muʿtakif, and ʿAṭāʾ said: That is his personal opinion (raʾy)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8592","(8589) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Bakr — who is ibn Muḍar — narrated to us, from Ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī — raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu — who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to perform Iʿtikāf (mujāwarah) during the middle ten days of the month. When twenty nights had passed and the twenty-first approached, he would return to his dwelling, and those who had been performing Iʿtikāf with him would return. Then he performed Iʿtikāf in a month during which he observed Iʿtikāf on the night on which he would normally return. He addressed the people and commanded them with what Allāh willed, then he said: 'I had intended to perform Iʿtikāf during these ten days, but it occurred to me that I should perform Iʿtikāf during these last ten days. So whoever performed Iʿtikāf with me, let him remain in his place of Iʿtikāf.' And he said: 'I was shown this night, but I was made to forget it, so seek it in the last ten days, in the odd-numbered nights. And I was shown that I was prostrating in water and mud.'"" Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: ""We were rained upon on the twenty-first night, and the mosque leaked at the place of prayer of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. I looked at him after he had finished the Ṣubḥ prayer, and his face was wet with mud and water.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8593","(8590) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me, Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us, al-Dārawardī narrated to us, from Yazīd ibn al-Hād, and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its meaning.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it]"
"8594","(8591) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us.
(H) And Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf narrated to us, Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us, al-Awzāʿī narrated to us, Yaḥyya narrated to us, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ""We discussed Laylat al-Qadr among a group of Quraysh, so I stood until I came to Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī and said: 'O Abū Saʿīd, will you not come out with us to the palm trees?' He said: 'Yes.' So he called for a wrap and put it on, and we went out. I said: 'Did you hear the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ mention Laylat al-Qadr?' He said: 'Yes. We performed Iʿtikāf with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ during the middle ten days of Ramaḍān. When the morning of the twentieth of Ramaḍān came, he stood among us and said: ""Whoever has gone out, let him return, for I was shown Laylat al-Qadr but I was made to forget it, so seek it in the last ten days, in the odd-numbered nights. And I was shown that I am prostrating in water and mud.""' We did not see any cloud in the sky, but then the prayer was established and a cloud rose, and we were rained upon until the roof of the mosque flowed with water. The roof at that time was made of palm fronds. I saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prostrating in the mud and water until I saw the trace of mud on his forehead and the bridge of his nose.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū al-Mughīrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
"8595","(8592) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk and Ibn Milḥān both narrated to us, Yaḥyya — who is ibn Bukayr — narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us.
(H) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Firyābī narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr and ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that ʿĀʾishah — raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā — the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: ""I used to enter the house for a need while a sick person was in it, and I would not ask about him except while passing. And the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to place his head in to me while he was in the mosque, and I would comb his hair. And he would not enter the house except for a need when he was in Iʿtikāf.""

In the narration of Ibn Bukayr: ""When they were in Iʿtikāf.""

al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd, except that al-Bukhārī did not mention her statement about the sick person. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8596","(8593) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid informed us, Yaḥyya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that the Prophet ﷺ used to perform Iʿtikāf during the last ten days of Ramaḍān until Allāh took him (in death). Then his wives performed Iʿtikāf after him.

And the Sunnah regarding the muʿtakif is that he should not go out except for a need that is unavoidable, and he should not visit the sick, and he should not touch his wife, and he should not have sexual intercourse with her, and there is no Iʿtikāf except in a mosque where congregational prayer is held. And the Sunnah for the one who performs Iʿtikāf is that he fasts. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Meaning as previously stated]"
"8597","(8594) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us that Khālid narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — meaning Ibn Isḥāq — from al-Zuhrī from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah that she said: ""The Sunnah for the one performing iʿtikāf is that he should not visit the sick, nor attend a funeral, nor touch a woman nor have intercourse with her, nor go out for any need except for that which is absolutely necessary. There is no iʿtikāf without fasting, and there is no iʿtikāf except in a congregational mosque.""

The Shaykh said: ""Many of the ḥuffāẓ have held that this statement is from someone other than ʿĀʾishah, and that whoever incorporated it into the ḥadīth was mistaken in doing so. Indeed, Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from ʿUrwah, who said: 'The muʿtikaf should not attend a funeral, nor visit the sick, nor accept an invitation. There is no iʿtikāf without fasting, and there is no iʿtikāf except in a mosque where the congregational prayer is held.' And from Ibn Jurayj from al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab that he said: 'The muʿtikaf should not visit the sick nor attend a funeral.'""

[Ṣaḥīgh for its narrators. Abū Dāwūd]"
"8598","(8595) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī and Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá both said: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated to us that Layth ibn Abī Sulaym narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim from his father from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. Al-Nufaylī said she said: ""The Prophet ﷺ would pass by a sick person while he was in iʿtikāf and would pass on as he was, without stopping to ask about him.""

And Ibn ʿĪsá said she said: ""Indeed, the Prophet ﷺ would visit the sick while he was in iʿtikāf.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
"8599","(8596) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us that Ādam narrated to us that Warqāʾ narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid that Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""When one performs iʿtikāf, he should not have intercourse with women.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"8600","(8597) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn al-Fuḍayl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us that Ādam narrated to us that Hushaym narrated from Ḥuṣayn from Jaʿfar ibn Iyās from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding His statement, ""And do not have intercourse with them while you are in iʿtikāf in the mosques"" (al-Baqarah 2:187). He said: ""Al-mubāsharah (direct contact), al-mulāmasah (touching), and al-mass (physical contact) — all of it refers to sexual intercourse. However, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, employs euphemisms as He wills.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"8601","(8598) Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī informed us in Naysābūr that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb narrated to us that ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Sharīk narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us that Ibn ʿUfayr narrated to us that Layth narrated from Ibn Musāfir — meaning ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khālid ibn Musāfir — from Ibn Shihāb from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn that Ṣafiyyah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, informed him: ""She came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in iʿtikāf in the mosque during the last ten days of Ramadan. Then she stood to leave, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood with her to escort her, until when he reached near the door of the mosque, at the door of Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, two men from the Anṣār passed by. They greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and then went on. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: 'Take it easy! It is only Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy.' They both said: 'Glory be to Allah, O Messenger of Allah!' And that weighed heavily upon them. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Indeed, Satan reaches in a human being the same extent as the blood reaches, and I feared that he might cast something into your hearts.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Maʿmar and Shuʿayb from al-Zuhrī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8602","(8599) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us that Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to us that Khālid ibn Dīnār narrated from Abū al-ʿĀliyah from one who served the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ performed wuḍūʾ in the mosque with a light ablution.""

[Ḥasan]"
"8603","(8600) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf al-Ṭāʾī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd narrated to us that my father narrated to me; he said: I heard al-Awzāʿī say.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf al-Ṭāʾī narrated to us that Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us that al-Awzāʿī narrated to me that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī narrated to me that ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned that he intended to perform iʿtikāf during the last ten days of the month of Ramadan. So ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, sought his permission, and he granted it to her. Then Ḥafṣah asked ʿĀʾishah to seek permission for her as well, and she did so. When Zaynab bint Jaḥsh saw this, she ordered a structure to be erected for her, and it was built. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he had prayed, would turn toward his structure. He saw the structures and said: 'What are these structures?' They said: 'The structures of ʿĀʾishah, Ḥafṣah, and Zaynab.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Do they intend righteousness by this? I am not in iʿtikāf.' So he returned, and when he broke his fast, he performed iʿtikāf for ten days in Shawwāl.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Salamah ibn Shabīb from Abū al-Mughīrah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Yaḥyā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8604","(8601) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnbarī informed us. Both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to perform iʿtikāf. When he turned to the place where he intended to perform iʿtikāf, he saw structures — the tent of ʿĀʾishah, the tent of Ḥafṣah, and the tent of Zaynab, may Allah be pleased with them all. When he saw them, he inquired about them, and it was said to him: ""This is the tent of ʿĀʾishah, the tent of Ḥafṣah, and the tent of Zaynab."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do you say they intend righteousness by them?"" Then he turned away and performed iʿtikāf for ten days in Shawwāl.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. This chain from Mākal is mursal. However, al-Awzāʿī, Ḥammād ibn Zayd, ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, Abū Muʿāwiyah al-Ḍarīr, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd, and ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith all connected it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
"8605","(8602) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us that Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ narrated from ʿIkrimah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""A woman from among his wives performed iʿtikāf with the Prophet ﷺ while she was experiencing istihāḍah (irregular bleeding). She would see redness and yellowness. She said: 'Sometimes we would place a basin beneath her while she was praying.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
"8606","(8603) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed it to us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá and Qutaybah both said: Yazīd narrated from Khālid, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he said: ""A woman from among his wives.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding ḥadīth]"
"8607","(8604) Abū Naṣr ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb al-Ṣibghī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād informed us; Ibn Abī Uways informed me; Ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to me from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Abī al-Zubayr, who said: I asked Jābir about a divorced woman performing iʿtikāf. He said: ""No, nor a woman whose husband has passed away, until she becomes ḥalāl (i.e., until her waiting period ends)."" [Weak]"
"8608","(8605) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham informed us; Abū al-Yamān informed us.
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shuʿayb narrated to us from al-Zuhrī; ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn informed me that Ṣafīyah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, informed him: She came to visit the Prophet ﷺ while he was in iʿtikāf in the mosque during the last ten days of Ramaḍān. She spoke with him for an hour, then she stood up to leave, and the Prophet ﷺ stood up with her to see her off, until she reached the door of the mosque near the door of Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. Two men from the Anṣār passed by them and greeted the Prophet ﷺ, then they went on their way. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: ""Take it easy — she is Ṣafīyah bint Ḥuyayy."" They both said: ""Glory be to Allah, O Messenger of Allah!"" and this weighed heavily upon them. The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Indeed, Satan reaches in the son of Ādam the same extent as the blood reaches. I feared that he might cast something into your hearts.""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8609","(8606) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs al-Ṭarāʾifī informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding His statement {And whoever disbelieves, then indeed, Allah is Free from need of the worlds}: He said: ""Whoever disbelieves in the Ḥajj and does not consider performing it an act of righteousness nor consider abandoning it a sin."" [Weak. Tafsīr al-Ṭabarī: 3/357, Tafsīr Ibn Abī Ḥātim: 3/3922]"
"8610","(8607) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us; Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrawī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated from Sufyān from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from ʿIkrimah, who said: When the verse {And whoever seeks a religion other than Islam, it will never be accepted from him} was revealed, the Jews said: ""We are Muslims."" Allah, Mighty and Majestic, silenced them with their own argument — that is, the Prophet ﷺ said to them: ""Indeed, Allah has made obligatory upon the Muslims the pilgrimage to the House for whoever is able to find a way."" They said: ""It has not been prescribed for us,"" and they refused to perform Ḥajj. Then Allah said: {And whoever disbelieves, then indeed, Allah is Free from need of the worlds}. ʿIkrimah said: ""And whoever disbelieves among the People of the Scriptures, then indeed, Allah is Free from need of the worlds."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Durr al-Manthūr: 2/276]"
"8611","(8608) Abū Naṣr informed us; Abū Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad informed us; Saʿīd informed us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid regarding His statement {And whoever disbelieves, then indeed, Allah is Free from need of the worlds}: ""He who, if he performs Ḥajj, does not consider it an act of righteousness, and if he abandons it, does not consider it a sin."" We have also narrated from Mujāhid something similar to what ʿIkrimah said. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Tafsīr al-Ṭabarī: 3/357]"
"8612","(8609) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us; Warqāʾ narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid regarding His statement {And whoever seeks a religion other than Islam}: He said: ""When this verse was revealed, all the People of the Scriptures said: 'We are Muslims.' Then Allah revealed {And to Allah belongs the pilgrimage of the House for mankind} — meaning, upon the people. So the Muslims performed Ḥajj and the polytheists did not."" [Weak]"
"8613","(8610) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw informed us; ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ informed us; Kahmas ibn al-Ḥasan informed us. He said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah narrating from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me and said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day, a man appeared — intensely white in his garments, intensely black in his hair, with no trace of travel visible upon him, and we did not recognize him — until he sat before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, placed his knee against his knee, and put his hands upon his thighs. Then he said: ""O Muḥammad, tell me about Islam — what is Islam?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Islam is that you bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger, that you establish the Ṣalāh, give the Zakāh, fast Ramaḍān, and perform the pilgrimage to the House if you are able to find a way."" The man said: ""You have spoken the truth."" Then he mentioned the hadith in its entirety. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""O ʿUmar, do you know who the questioner was?"" I said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: ""That was Jibrīl — he came to you to teach you your religion.""
Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from two routes from Kahmas. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 8, al-Nasāʾī 4990]"
"8614","(8611) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in the book Maʿrifat al-Ḥadīth; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Abū al-Naḍr narrated from Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah from Thābit from Anas, who said: We were forbidden from asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about anything, and it pleased us that a man from the desert folk would come and ask him while we listened. A man from among them came and said: ""O Muḥammad, your messenger came to us and claimed that you claim that Allah has sent you."" He said: ""He has spoken the truth."" He said: ""Who created the heavens?"" He said: ""Allah."" He said: ""Who created the earth?"" He said: ""Allah."" He said: ""Who set up these mountains?"" He said: ""Allah."" He said: ""Who placed in them these benefits?"" He said: ""Allah."" He said: ""Then, by the One who created the heavens and the earth, set up the mountains, and placed in them these benefits — has Allah sent you?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Your messenger claimed that upon us are five prayers in our day and night."" He said: ""He has spoken the truth."" He said: ""Then, by the One who sent you — has Allah commanded you with this?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Your messenger claimed that upon us is charity from our wealth."" He said: ""He has spoken the truth."" He said: ""Then, by the One who sent you — has Allah commanded you with this?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Your messenger claimed that upon us is fasting one month in our year."" He said: ""He has spoken the truth."" He said: ""Then, by the One who sent you — has Allah commanded you with this?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Your messenger claimed that upon us is the pilgrimage to the House for whoever is able to find a way."" He said: ""He has spoken the truth."" He said: ""Then, by the One who sent you — has Allah commanded you with this?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""By the One who sent you with the truth, I shall neither add to them nor subtract from them."" When he departed, he (the Prophet ﷺ) said: ""If he has spoken the truth, he shall surely enter Paradise.""
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid from Abū al-Naḍr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim. Al-Bukhārī said: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl and ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it from Sulaymān from Thābit from Anas. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 12]"
"8615","(8612) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ informed us; Ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated from Qatādah from al-Ḥasan from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The pen has been lifted from three: from the sleeper until he awakens, from the minor until he reaches puberty (al-ḥinth), and from the insane until he regains his senses.""
We have narrated it from the hadith of Abī Ẓabyān and Abī al-Ḍuḥā from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain]"
"8616","(8613) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl informed us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from Sulaymān al-Aʿmash from Abī Ẓabyān from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Any child who performs Ḥajj and then reaches the age of puberty must perform another Ḥajj. Any Bedouin who performs Ḥajj and then emigrates must perform another Ḥajj. Any slave who performs Ḥajj and then is freed must perform another Ḥajj.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Khuzaymah 3050. al-Ḥākim 1/655]"
"8617","(8614) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us: Shu'bah informed us from al-A'mash, from Abu Dhubyan, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, that he said: ""When a Bedouin performs Hajj and then emigrates, the Hajj of Islam is obligatory upon him, and likewise the case of the slave and the young boy."" Thus it was narrated as a mawquf hadith. [Sahih. Ibn Abi Shaybah 4875]"
"8618","(8615) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi in Marw narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mas'ud narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said — (h) — and Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i informed us, the wording being his: 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Muslim al-Qurashi, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: ""Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) addressed us and said: 'O people, Hajj has been prescribed upon you, so perform Hajj.' A man said: 'Every year, O Rasulullah?' He remained silent until the man said it three times. Then Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) said: 'Had I said yes, it would have become obligatory, and you would not have been able to bear it.' Then he said: 'Leave me as I leave you. Verily, those before you were destroyed by the abundance of their questions and their disagreement with their prophets. When I command you with something, do of it as much as you are able, and when I forbid you from something, abandon it.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb, from Yazid ibn Harun. [Sahih. Muslim 1337]"
"8619","(8616) Abu al-Qasim 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar ibn 'Ali al-Fami in Baghdad, in the Mosque of al-Rusafah, narrated to us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman al-Najjad narrated to us: al-Harith ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Ruh narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us: Ata' informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah saying: ""We, the companions of Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam), entered into ihram for Hajj exclusively (ifrad)."" He mentioned the hadith and said that therein Suraqah ibn Malik said: ""Is this tamattu' of ours, O Rasulullah, for this year only or for all time?"" He (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) said: 'No, rather for all time.'"" Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in their Sahihs from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Bukhari 6933, Muslim 1213]"
"8620","(8617) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Bajali narrated to us: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us: I heard Ibn Shihab narrating from Abu Sinan, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) addressed us and said: 'O people, verily Allah has prescribed Hajj upon you.' Then al-Aqra' ibn Habis stood and said: 'Is it every year, O Rasulullah?' He said: 'Had I said it, it would have become obligatory, and had it become obligatory, you would not have acted upon it, nor would you have been able to act upon it. Hajj is once; whoever does more, then it is voluntary (tatawwu')."" Sufyan ibn Husayn and Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah followed him up from al-Zuhri, from Abu Sinan, and 'Uqayl also said from al-Zuhri, from Sinan — and he is Abu Sinan al-Du'ali. And in the hadith of Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, that Suraqah ibn Malik said: ""Is this tamattu' of ours, O Rasulullah, for this year only or for all time?"" He said: 'No, rather for all time.'"" [Sahih. Sunan Abi Dawud 1721. Nasa'i 2620]"
"8621","(8618) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Habib ibn Abi 'Amrah narrated to us: 'A'ishah bint Talhah narrated to us from 'A'ishah Umm al-Mu'minin (radiyallahu 'anha), she said: ""I said to the Prophet (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam): 'We go out to fight and strive alongside you.' Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) said: 'But the best and most excellent jihad is a Hajj Mabrur (an accepted Hajj).'' 'A'ishah said: 'So I never abandoned Hajj ever after I heard this from Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam).'"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad. [Sahih. Bukhari 1762]"
"8622","(8619) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki said: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Kamil al-Qadi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Jamal narrated to us: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Mu'awiyah ibn Ishaq, from 'A'ishah bint Talhah, from 'A'ishah Umm al-Mu'minin (radiyallahu 'anha), she said: ""His wives sought his permission for jihad, and he (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) said: 'Hajj is sufficient for you, or he said: Your jihad is Hajj.'"" And al-Firyabi said from Sufyan: ""We sought the Prophet's (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) permission for jihad, and he said: 'Hajj is sufficient for you, or he said: Your jihad is Hajj.'"" [Sahih. Bukhari 2720]"
"8623","(8620) Abu 'Abdillah and Abu Zakariyya said: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Kamil narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Qabisah narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Habib ibn Abi 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah bint Talhah, from 'A'ishah Umm al-Mu'minin, from the Prophet (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam), something similar. Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their Sahihs from Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah."
"8624","(8621) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: al-Hasan ibn Halim al-Marwazi narrated to us: Abu al-Muwajjih informed us: 'Abdan informed us: Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Sa'd, from his father, from his grandfather, said: ""Verily, 'Umar (radiyallahu 'anhu) gave permission to the wives of the Prophet (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) for Hajj, and sent with them 'Uthman ibn 'Affan and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf. 'Uthman called out to the people: 'Let no one approach them or look upon them, except with a gaze extended,' while they were in the howdahs on the camels. He set them down at the beginning of the valley, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf and 'Uthman (radiyallahu 'anhuma) dismounted at its rear, and no one sat near them."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Ahmad ibn Muhammad, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, in an abridged form. [Sahih. Bukhari 1761]"
"8625","(8622) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur and Sa'id ibn Sulayman together said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us from Zayd ibn Aslam — Sa'id ibn Mansur said — from Waqid ibn Abi Waqid al-Laythi, from Abi Waqid, who said: ""I heard Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) say to his wives during the Farewell Hajj: 'This, then [remain upon] the backs of the cushions [i.e., stay at home].'"" The Shaykh said: ""The Hajj of 'A'ishah (radiyallahu 'anha) and other Ummahat al-Mu'minin (radiyallahu 'unna) after Rasulullah (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) indicates that the intent of this report is the obligation of Hajj upon them once, just as its obligation upon men has been clarified, and it does not mean prohibition from doing more. And Allah knows best."""
"8626","(8623) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Abu al-Qasim Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Tabarani informed us: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyabi said — and Sulayman narrated to us: Hafs narrated to us: Qabisah and Abu Hudhayfah said: Sufyan narrated to us from Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Muhammad ibn 'Abbad ibn Ja'far, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""It was said: 'O Rasulullah, what is the path (as-sabil) to Hajj?' He said: 'The path is provisions (az-zad) and a mount (ar-rahilah).'"" This has also been narrated from the hadith of al-Hasan al-Basri from the Prophet (sallallahu 'alayhi wa sallam) as a mursal hadith. [Da'if. Tirmidhi 813. Dar al-Qutni 2/217]"
"8627","(8624) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Shawdhab al-Muqri' in Wasit narrated to us — Shu'ayb ibn Ayyub narrated to me — Abu Dawud, meaning al-Hafri, narrated to us — from Sufyan — from Yunus — from al-Hasan, who said: The Prophet ﷺ was asked about al-sabil (the means to perform Hajj). He ﷺ said: ""Al-zad wa al-rahila (provisions and a mount)."" This is a corroborating witness for the hadith of Ibrahim ibn Yazid al-Khuzi, and it has also been reported from Ibn 'Abbas as his own statement, in a mawquf form. [Da'if]"
"8628","(8625) Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Muzakki narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — Malik informed us.

(And) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — al-Qa'nabi narrated to us — from Malik.

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us — Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik — from Ibn Shihab — from Sulayman ibn Yasar — from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, that he said: Al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a woman from Khath'am came to seek a fatwa. Al-Fadl began looking at her, and she began looking at him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned al-Fadl's face to the other side. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the obligation of Allah upon His servants regarding Hajj has overtaken my father, and he is an elderly man who is unable to remain firm upon the mount. May I perform Hajj on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes."" And this was during the Farewell Pilgrimage.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Bukhari 5848. Muslim 1334]"
"8629","(8626) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — 'Uthman ibn 'Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us.

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Ibrahim ibn Masiyy informed us — Abu Muslim — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us, they both said: 'Abd Allah ibn Raja' narrated to us — 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Salama al-Majishun informed us — from al-Zuhri — from Sulayman ibn Yasar — from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A woman from Khath'am came to seek a fatwa from the Prophet ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage. She said: ""O Prophet of Allah, the obligation of Allah upon His servants regarding Hajj has overtaken my father, and he is an elderly man who is unable to remain firm upon the mount. Will it suffice that I perform Hajj on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes."" [Sahih. Bukhari 1442]"
"8630","(8627) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us — Hisham ibn 'Ali narrated to us — Ibn Raja' and Abu Salama narrated to us, they both said: 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us — he mentioned it with the same meaning.

Al-Bukhari narrated it from Abu Salama Musa ibn Isma'il."
"8631","(8628) Abu al-Husayn — 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us — Abu Ja'far — Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah narrated to us — Ishaq ibn Yusuf al-Azraq narrated to us — Ibn Jurayj narrated to us.

(And) Abu Hazim — 'Umar ibn Ahmad al-Hafiz informed us through dictation — Abu 'Amr — Isma'il ibn Nujayd informed us — Abu Muslim narrated to us — Abu Asim narrated to us — from Ibn Jurayj — from Ibn Shihab — from Sulayman ibn Yasar — from Ibn 'Abbas — from al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas, that: A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""My father has been overtaken by Hajj, and he is an elderly man who is unable to ride the camel. May I perform Hajj on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Perform Hajj on his behalf.""

The wording is that of the hadith of Abu Asim. In the narration of al-Azraq: ""The obligation of Allah regarding Hajj has overtaken my father.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it from Abu Asim, and Muslim reported it through another chain from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Bukhari 1755. Muslim 1335]"
"8632","(8629) Abu al-Husayn — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us — Abu Bakr al-Humaydi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri more than once say: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar say: I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: A woman from Khath'am asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Sacrifice, while al-Fadl was riding behind him. She said: ""The obligation of Allah regarding Hajj upon His servants has overtaken my father, and he is an elderly man who is unable to hold firm upon the mount. Do you think that Hajj may be performed on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes.""

Sufyan said: This is what I have memorized from her — that she said: ""Do you think that Hajj may be performed?"" And others narrate in this hadith: ""Do you think that I may perform Hajj on his behalf?"" Sufyan said: And 'Amr ibn Dinar first narrated it to us from al-Zuhri from Sulayman ibn Yasar from Ibn 'Abbas, and he said in it: ""Will that benefit him, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes, just as if one of you had a debt and he fulfilled it."" Then when al-Zuhri came to us and narrated it, I examined it, and he did not mention this statement which 'Amr had narrated from him. [Sahih. Al-Ma'rifa lil-Ansawi 1/354]"
"8633","(8630) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Susi and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated to us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas, that is, al-Asamm, narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid ibn Mazyid informed me — my father narrated to me — al-Awza'i narrated to me — al-Zuhri narrated to me — from Sulayman ibn Yasar — that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas informed him: that a woman from Khath'am sought a fatwa from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage, while al-Fadl was riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the obligation of Allah regarding Hajj upon His servants has overtaken my father, and he is an elderly man who is unable to remain firm upon the mount. Will it suffice that I perform Hajj on his behalf?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: ""Yes.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it from al-Firyabi from al-Awza'i. [Sahih. Bukhari 4138]"
"8634","(8631) Abu Tahir al-Faqih and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr and others narrated to us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us — Zakariyya ibn 'Adi narrated to us — Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn 'Ayyash al-Makhzumi — from Zayd ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn — from his father — from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi' — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: that a young woman from Khath'am said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my father is an elderly man upon whom the obligation of Allah upon His servants regarding Hajj has been imposed, and he is unable to fulfill it. Will it suffice me that I fulfill it on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes.""

Al-Darawardi narrated it from 'Uthman ibn 'Umar, and he said in it: ""Will it suffice him that I fulfill it on his behalf?"" [Hasan. Tirmidhi 885]"
"8635","(8632) And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm narrated to us — al-Rabi' informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — 'Amr ibn Abi Salama narrated to us — from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad — from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith al-Makhzumi — he mentioned it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: ""My father is an elderly man who has become feeble,"" and he said: ""Will it suffice him that I fulfill it on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes."" And he did not mention her statement ""a young woman."" [Hasan. Tirmidhi 885]"
"8636","(8633) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — al-Nu'man ibn Salim informed me — from 'Amr ibn Awf al-Thaqafi — from Abi Razin al-'Uqayli, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my father is an elderly man who is unable to perform Hajj, nor 'Umrah, nor to travel."" He ﷺ said: ""Perform Hajj on behalf of your father and perform 'Umrah."" [Hasan. Tirmidhi 930. Nasa'i 2637]"
"8637","(8634) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥmmad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, from Yūsuf ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, who said:

A man from the tribe of Khathʿam came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my father reached Islam as an elderly man who is unable to mount a riding animal, and the Ḥajj is obligatory upon him. May I perform Ḥajj on his behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Are you his eldest son?"" He said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Tell me, if your father had a debt and you paid it off, would that suffice?"" He said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Then perform Ḥajj on his behalf.""

A difference of opinion has been recorded regarding this narration from Manṣūr. Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it in this manner. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad also narrated it from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, from a freed slave of Ibn al-Zubayr known as Yūsuf ibn al-Zubayr or al-Zubayr ibn Yūsuf, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from Sawdah bint Zamʿah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'My father is an elderly man who is unable to perform Ḥajj.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'If your father had a debt and you paid it on his behalf, would it be accepted from you?' He said: 'Yes.' He ﷺ said: 'Then Allah is more merciful. Perform Ḥajj on behalf of your father.'""

[Ṣaḥīh in its routes other than this one. Aḥmad 6/429. Dārimī 1837.]"
"8638","(8635) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated it.

Isrāʾīl also narrated it from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, from a freed slave of the family of Ibn al-Zubayr, from Ibn al-Zubayr, that Sawdah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""O Messenger of Allah…"" and he mentioned the hadith. Al-Thawrī sent it as a mursal narration from Manṣūr, saying: from Yūsuf ibn al-Zubayr, from the Prophet ﷺ. The correct narration is from Mujāhid, from Yūsuf ibn al-Zubayr, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from the Prophet ﷺ. This is what al-Bukhārī also stated. [Ṣaḥīh in its routes other than this one. Aḥmad 6/429.]"
"8639","(8636) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Mālik ibn Anas informed me from Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), that:

A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""My mother is an elderly woman. We are unable to place her upon a camel, for she cannot hold on, and if we tie her, I fear she may die. May I perform Ḥajj on her behalf?"" He ﷺ said: ""Yes.""

The narrations of Ibn Sīrīn from Ibn ʿAbbās are considered mursal. It has also been narrated from ʿAwf ibn Abī Jamīlah, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, but the narration of Ayyūb is more authentic, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīh in its routes other than this one. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 1051.]"
"8640","(8637) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Saʿīd ibn Sālim informed us, from Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar, who said:

We were sitting with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and I heard him say: ""A man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: 'What is a Ḥājj (pilgrim)?' He ﷺ said: 'One whose hair is dishevelled and covered in dust.' Then another stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, which Ḥajj is most excellent?' He ﷺ said: 'That in which there is great exertion and sacrifice (al-ʿajj wa al-thajj).' Then another stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is the sabīl (way)?' He ﷺ said: 'Provisions and a riding animal (zādun wa rāḥilah).'""

This is what al-Shāfiʿī intended by his statement from it that which the people of knowledge do not hesitate to authenticate. However, they refrained from it because the hadith is known through Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Khūzī, and the scholars of hadith have declared him weak. [Weak.]"
"8641","(8638) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us. He said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn say: ""Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Khūzī narrated this hadith of Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād, and he is not trustworthy (thiqah).""

The Shaykh said: It has also been narrated by Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād, but he is weaker than Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd. It has also been narrated by Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥajjāj from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād, and Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥajjāj is abandoned (matrūk). It has also been narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah and Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Qatādah, from Anas, from the Prophet ﷺ, regarding provisions and the riding animal, but I consider it to be an error (wahm)."
"8642","(8639) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated to us from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, who said:

He was asked about the statement of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And [due] to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House—for whoever is able to find thereto a way} (Āl ʿImrān 3:97). He said: ""It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is the sabīl (way)?' He ﷺ said: 'Whoever finds provisions and a riding animal.'""

This is the preserved narration from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from the Prophet ﷺ, as a mursal hadith. Likewise, Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd narrated it from al-Ḥasan. Al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb from Yūnus. [Weak.]"
"8643","(8640) ʿAttāb ibn Aʿyan narrated it from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd, from al-Ḥasan, from his mother, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ was asked: 'What is the sabīl (way) to Ḥajj?' He ﷺ said: 'Provisions and a riding animal (al-zād wa al-rāḥilah).'""

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥātim narrated to us, saying: ""I found it in the book of ʿAttāb ibn Aʿyan,"" and he mentioned it. It has also been narrated through another route from ʿAttāb, and other narrations have been reported through it, none of which are authentic. The hadith of Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd is the most well-known among them, and we have corroborated it with what al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī narrated, even though it is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). [Weak. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/254. Al-ʿUqaylī 3/332.]"
"8644","(8641) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the statement of Allah, the Exalted: {And [due] to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House—for whoever is able to find thereto a way} (Āl ʿImrān 3:97). He said: ""The sabīl (way) means that the person's body is sound and he has the means for provisions and a riding animal without being caused undue hardship thereby."" [Weak. Tafsīr al-Ṭabarī 3/357.]"
"8645","(8642) Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us; Abū ʿUbaydullāh al-Makhzūmī narrated to us; Hishām ibn Sulaymān and ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj. He said: ʿUmar ibn ʬāṭāʾ informed me from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, with the same statement as that of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him): ""The sabīl (way) is provisions and a riding animal."" [Weak. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/218.]"
"8646","(8643) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us; Ibn ʿAwn narrated to us from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad and from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, who both said: ʿĀʾishah said:

""O Messenger of Allah, will the people depart having performed two acts of worship while I depart having performed only one?"" He ﷺ said to her: ""Wait, and when you become pure, go out to al-Tanʿīm and enter into iḥrām from there, then come to us at such and such a place. But [the reward] will be in proportion to your effort and hardship.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it through another route from Ibn ʿAwn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1695. Muslim 1211.]"
"8647","(8644) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj from 'Ata' from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""I do not regret anything as much as I regret that I did not perform Hajj on foot."" [Sahih. Al-Kamil by Ibn 'Adi 4/258]"
"8648","(8645) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mahdi narrated to us, 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, 'Ubayd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr narrated to them, saying: Ibn 'Abbas said: ""I do not regret anything that escaped me in my youth except that I did not perform Hajj on foot. And al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, performed Hajj twenty-five times on foot, while the finest riding camels were led alongside him. And he divided his wealth with Allah three times, to the extent that he would give away the leather sock and keep only the sandal.""

Ibn 'Umayr stated that this was narrated on the authority of al-Hasan ibn 'Ali. A marfu' hadith has also been narrated concerning this from Ibn 'Abbas, but it contains weakness. [Da'if. Al-Hakim 3/185]"
"8649","(8646) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq informed us, Bishr ibn Musa al-Asadi narrated to us, Farwah ibn Abi al-Maghra al-Kindi narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Sawadah narrated to us from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid from Zadhan, who said: ""Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, fell ill, so he gathered his children and his family and said to them: 'O my children, I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: ""Whoever performs Hajj from Makkah on foot until he returns to it, for every step he takes, seven hundred good deeds of the Haram are written for him.""' Some of them asked: 'What are the good deeds of the Haram?' He said: 'Every good deed is equal to a hundred thousand good deeds.'""

'Isa ibn Sawadah is the sole narrator of this, and he is unknown (majhul). [Munkar. Ibn Khuzaymah 2791. Al-Hakim 1/631]"
"8650","(8647) Abu Tahir al-Ziyadi informed us, Abu 'Uthman al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us, Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, who said: ""Ibrahim and Isma'il, peace and blessings be upon them both, performed Hajj on foot."" [Da'if. Tarikh Dimashq by Ibn 'Asakir 6/211]"
"8651","(8648) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us, Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' informed us, Ibn 'Awn narrated to us from Ibrahim from al-Aswad and Ibn 'Awn from al-Qasim, that they both said: Umm al-Minin said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him—

And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me, Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed us, al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us from Ibn 'Awn from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from Umm al-Mu'minin, and from al-Qasim from Umm al-Mu'minin, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, the people will depart with two acts of worship, and I will depart with only one.' He said: 'Wait. When you become pure, go out to al-Tan'im and enter into Ihram from there, then meet us at such and such a place.' He said: 'I think he said: 'Tomorrow, but it will be according to your exertion'—or he said: 'your expenditure'—or as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih]"
"8652","(8649) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani narrated to us verbally, Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri narrated to us in Makkah, al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu 'Awanah narrated to us from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib from Abu Zuhayr al-Du'bi from 'Abd Allah ibn Buraydah from his father Buraydah al-Aslami, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Spending in Hajj is like spending in the cause of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, seventy times over."" [Da'if. Musnad Ahmad 5/354. Al-Tarikh al-Kabir by al-Bukhari 3/63]"
"8653","(8650) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad, and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki informed us in Nishapur, saying: Abu Sahl Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ruh al-Madani narrated to us, Shababah narrated to us, Warqa' narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Dinar from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""The people of Yemen used to perform Hajj without carrying provisions, saying: 'We are those who put their trust in Allah.' So they would come to Makkah for Hajj and beg from the people. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And take provisions, for indeed the best provision is taqwa (piety)}.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Bishr from Shababah. [Sahih. Bukhari 1401. Abu Dawud 1730]"
"8654","(8651) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Muqri' informed us, al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi informed us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr informed us, Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us, 'Azrah ibn Thabit narrated to us from Thumamah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Anas, that Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, used to perform Hajj on a mount and was not miserly, and he narrated that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, performed Hajj on a mount and it was his pack animal.

Al-Bukhari extracted it in his Sahih, saying: ""And Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr said."" [Sahih. Bukhari 1445]"
"8655","(8652) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Hamid ibn Abi Hamid al-Muqri', Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi, and Abu Sadiq ibn Abi al-Fawaris narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Mukram narrated to us, Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sa'id narrated to us from his father, who said: ""I departed with Ibn 'Umar on the day of departure, and a Yemeni caravan passed by us whose saddles were of leather and whose camels' nose-rings were of rope. 'Abd Allah said: 'Whoever wishes to see the caravan most resembling the caravan that came for Hajj this year with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his Companions when they arrived for the Farewell Pilgrimage, let him look at this caravan.'"" [Sahih. Abu Dawud 4144. Ibn Abi Shaybah 15802]"
"8656","(8653) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Tahir al-Faqih, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas, who is al-Asamm, informed us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us, Sa'id ibn Bashir al-Qurashi informed me, 'Abd Allah ibn Hakim al-Kinani, a man from the people of Yemen from among their freedmen, narrated to us from Bishr ibn Qudamah al-Dabbabi, who said: ""My eyes beheld my beloved, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, standing at 'Arafat with the people on a red, cropped-eared she-camel of his, beneath which was a Bawlanian velvet cloth, and he was saying: 'O Allah, make it a Hajj free of showing off, and of vanity, and of seeking fame.' And the people were saying: 'This is the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him.'"" Sa'id ibn Bashir said: ""I asked 'Abd Allah ibn Hakim: 'O Abu Hakim, what is al-Qaswa'?' He said: 'I think it is the one whose ears are cropped, for the ears of she-camels are cropped so that they may hear better.'"" [Da'if. Ibn Khuzaymah 2836]"
"8657","(8654) Abū ʿUmar al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Ḥaḍramī informed us; Hārūn ibn Isḥāq informed us from his book; Wakīʿ narrated from Ṭāriq, who said: I heard Ibn Abī Awfā being asked about a man who takes a loan in order to perform Ḥajj. He said: ""He should seek provision from Allah and should not take a loan."" He added: ""We used to say: one should not take a loan unless he has the means to repay it."""
"8658","(8655) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Muslim and Saʿīd narrated from Ibn Jurayj from ʿAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man asked him: ""Shall I hire myself out to these people and perform the rites of Ḥajj with them in exchange for a fee?"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Yes. 'For them is a share of what they earned, and Allah is swift in reckoning.'"""
"8659","(8656) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us from the original of his book; Abū Manṣūr Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-ʿAtakī al-Ṣabbāghī informed us by dictation; al-Labbād, i.e. Aḥmad ibn Naṣr, informed us; Abū Nuʿaym informed us; al-Aʿmash narrated from Muslim al-Baṭīn from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: A man came to Ibn ʿAbbās and said: ""I hire myself out for Ḥajj and stipulate upon them that I will perform Ḥajj. Will that suffice me?"" He said: ""You are among those about whom Allah said: 'For them is a share of what they earned, and Allah is swift in reckoning.'"" ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Jazarī also narrated it from Saʿīd."
"8660","(8657) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Muthannā informed us; Musaddad informed us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād informed us; al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Musayyab informed us; Abū Umāmah al-Taymī said: I used to hire myself out for this purpose, and there were people who would say: ""You have no Ḥajj."" So I met Ibn ʿUmar and said: ""O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, I am a man who hires himself out for these purposes, and people say to me: 'You have no Ḥajj.'"" He said: ""Do you not enter iḥrām, recite the talbiyyah, perform ṭawāf of the House, depart from ʿArafāt, and stone the jamarāt?"" I said: ""Yes, indeed."" He said: ""Then you have a Ḥajj. A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and asked him the same question that you have asked me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) remained silent and did not answer him until this verse was revealed: 'There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) then sent for him, recited this verse to him, and said: 'You have a Ḥajj.'"""
"8661","(8658) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ʿUkāẓ, Majannah, and Dhū al-Majāz were marketplaces during the Jāhiliyyah. When Islam came, they felt uneasy about trading therein. So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: ""There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord"" during the seasons of Ḥajj. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh and others."
"8662","(8659) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Ḥamzah ibn al-ʿAbbās al-ʿAqabī informed us in Baghdad; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us in Hamadhān; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) that in the early days of Ḥajj, the people used to trade at Minā, ʿArafah, the market of Dhū al-Majāz, and during the seasons of Ḥajj. They became apprehensive about buying and selling while in the state of iḥrām. So Allah, the Blessed and Most High, revealed: ""There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord"" during the seasons of Ḥajj. Ādam added in his narration: ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr told me that he used to read it thus in the muṣḥaf."
"8663","(8660) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Shādhān informed us; Sharīk narrated from Layth from Ibn Sābit from Abū Umāmah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""Whoever is not prevented by illness, a manifest need, or a tyrannical ruler, and yet does not perform Ḥajj, let him die, if he wishes, as a Jew or a Christian."" Although its chain of narration is not strong, it has a corroborating witness from the statement of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"8664","(8661) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-Ṣaydalānī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ḥajjāj said: Ibn Jurayj informed me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nuʿaym informed him that al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ashʿarī informed him that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm informed him that he heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say — repeating it three times: ""Let him die as a Jew or a Christian"" — referring to a man who died without performing Ḥajj despite having ample means and an open path. He said: ""A single Ḥajj that I perform is more beloved to me than six or seven military campaigns."" Ibn Nuʿaym was uncertain. And a military campaign that I undertake after having performed Ḥajj is more beloved to me than six or seven Ḥajj pilgrimages. Ibn Nuʿaym was uncertain regarding both."
"8665","(8662) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sahl ibn Sakhtawayh informed us; Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyāʾ and Ṣāliḥ ibn ʿUmar narrated from Muṭarrif ibn Ṭarīf from Bashīr ibn Muslim from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""No one should travel by sea except one who is a warrior in the cause of Allah (ghāzī), one performing ʿUmrah, or one performing Ḥajj. Indeed, beneath the sea there is fire, and beneath the fire there is a sea."""
"8666","(8663) It was also narrated from Muṭarrif from Bishr Abū ʿAbd Allāh from Bashīr ibn Muslim from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr. Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyāʾ narrated from Muṭarrif, and he mentioned it, saying: ""He should not travel by sea."""
"8667","(8664) And Abū Bakr al-Fārisī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn Fāris informed us, and Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī said: ""His hadith is not authentic"" — meaning this hadith of Bashīr ibn Muslim."
"8668","(8665) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī informed us, Abū al-Muwajjih informed us, Maḥmūd ibn Ghaylān informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us from Shuʿbah and Hammām, from Qatādah, from Abū Ayyūb, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, that he said: ""The water of the sea does not suffice for wuḍūʾ nor for ghusl from janābah. Indeed, beneath the sea is fire, then water, then fire,"" until he counted seven seas and seven fires. Thus it was narrated as a mawqūf (stopped) narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 1394]"
"8669","(8666) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū ʿĀṣim — al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ḥuṣayn informed me from Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā, from Yaʿlā, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The sea is Jahannam."" Then he recited: {Fire will surround them with its walls} [Al-Kahf:29]. Yaʿlā said: ""By Allah, I shall never enter it, and by Allah, not a single drop of it shall ever touch me."" [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 4/224. Ḥākim 4/638]"
"8670","(8667) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed us from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Saʿīd ibn Yasār, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A single ḥajj for one who has not yet performed ḥajj is better than ten military expeditions; a single military expedition for one who has already performed ḥajj is better than ten ḥajjs; and a military expedition at sea is better than ten military expeditions on land. Whoever crosses the sea is as though he has crossed all the valleys, and the one who suffers seasickness therein is as though he were drenched in his own blood.""

Thus Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd with this isnād from him.

And Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, who said: A narrator informed me from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: ""A military expedition at sea is equal to ten military expeditions on land, and whoever crosses the sea is as though he has crossed all the valleys, and the one who suffers seasickness on a ship is as though he were drenched in his own blood."" Thus it was narrated as a mawqūf (stopped) narration. [Munkar. Ḥākim 2/155]"
"8671","(8668) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakkār al-ʿAyshī said — and Abū Dāwūd also narrated from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm al-Dimashqī, with the same meaning — Marwān informed us, Hilāl ibn Maymūn al-Ramlī informed us from Yaʿlā ibn Shaddād, from Umm Ḥarām (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said: ""The one who suffers seasickness at sea has the reward of a martyr, and the one who drowns has the reward of two martyrs."" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 2494. Ḥumaydī 349]"
"8672","(8669) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed us from al-Zubayr ibn Khirrīt, from al-Ḥasan ibn Hādiyah, who said: I met Ibn ʿUmar and he said: ""Where are you from?"" I said: ""From the people of ʿUmān."" He said: ""From the people of ʿUmān?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Shall I narrate to you what I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say?"" I said: ""Yes, indeed."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Indeed, I know a land called ʿUmān, the sea laps at its sides; a single ḥajj from it is better than two ḥajjs from elsewhere.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 2/30]"
"8673","(8670) Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Shabīb al-Fāmī informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us, Zuhayr informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah, from his father Buraydah ibn al-Ḥuṣayb, that a woman came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had given a slave-girl in charity on behalf of my mother, and she has passed away, leaving behind the slave-girl."" He said: ""Your reward is confirmed, and it has returned to you as inheritance."" She said: ""She has passed away and she owed fasts — will it suffice that I fast on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes."" She said: ""And she did not perform ḥajj — will it suffice that I perform ḥajj on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1149. Abū Dāwūd 2877]"
"8674","(8671) Abū Naṣr al-Fāmī informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us, Muḥammad ibn Shādhān informed us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr informed us, ʿAlī ibn Mushhir informed us from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAṭāʾ with this same isnād, a similar narration.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr."
"8675","(8672) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us, Musaddad informed us, Abū ʿAwānah informed us from Abū Bishr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""My mother vowed to perform ḥajj but passed away before performing it — shall I perform ḥajj on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes, perform ḥajj on her behalf. Tell me, if your mother had a debt, would you repay it?"" She said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then fulfill [the right of] Allah, for Allah is most deserving of fulfillment."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 6885]"
"8676","(8673) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk informed us, Ṣafwān informed us, al-Walīd — meaning Ibn Muslim — informed us, Shuʿayb ibn Ruzayq informed us, and I heard ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī narrate from Abū al-Ghawth ibn al-Ḥuṣayn al-Khathʿamī, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my father has reached the age where Allah's obligation of ḥajj has become incumbent upon him, but he is an elderly man who cannot mount a riding animal. What do you think — shall I perform ḥajj on his behalf?"" He said: ""Yes, perform ḥajj on his behalf."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, likewise, if any of our family members pass away without having made a will for ḥajj, shall ḥajj be performed on their behalf?"" He said: ""Yes, and you will be rewarded."" He said: ""And shall charity be given on their behalf and fasts be observed on their behalf?"" He said: ""Yes."" And charity is more excellent. And likewise regarding vows and walking to the mosque. Its isnād is weak. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8677","(8674) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, that Abū al-'Abbāṣ al-Aṣamm informed him, that al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed him, that al-Shāfi'ī informed him, that Muslim ibn Khālid informed him, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Aṭā' and Ṭāwūs, that both of them said: The obligatory Ḥajj is to be performed from one's own wealth."
"8678","(8675) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, that Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj informed him, that Muṭayyin informed him, that Ibn Numayr informed him, that 'Abadah ibn Sulaymān informed him — (Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Sijzī informed him through dictation, that 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Ḥanẓalī informed him, that Hārūn ibn Isḥāq al-Hamdānī informed him, that 'Abadah ibn Sulaymān al-Kilābī informed him, from Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Arūbah, from Qatādah, from 'Azrah, from Sa'īd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbās, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man saying: ""Labbayka on behalf of Shubrumah."" He ﷺ asked: ""Who is Shubrumah?"" The man mentioned a brother or a relative. He ﷺ then asked: ""Have you ever performed Ḥajj?"" He replied: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then perform this Ḥajj on your own behalf, and then perform Ḥajj on behalf of Shubrumah."""
"8679","(8676) Abū al-Ḥusayn 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdad informed us, that Ismā'īl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed him, that Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik informed him, that 'Amr ibn 'Awn informed him, that Abū Yūsuf informed him, from Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Arūbah, from Qatādah, from 'Azrah, from Sa'īd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbās, that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man performing talbiyah on behalf of Shubrumah. He ﷺ said: ""Who is Shubrumah?"" He replied: ""My brother or a relative of mine."" He ﷺ asked: ""Have you performed Ḥajj?"" He replied: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then perform this Ḥajj on your own behalf, and then perform Ḥajj on his behalf."""
"8680","(8677) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: I heard Abū 'Alī al-Ḥāfiẓ saying that. He said: And Qatādah also narrated from 'Azrah ibn Tamīm and from 'Azrah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān."
"8681","(8678) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, that Abū al-'Abbāṣ al-Aṣamm informed him, that al-Rabī' informed him, that al-Shāfi'ī informed him, that Muslim, meaning Ibn Khālid, informed him, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Aṭā', who said: The Prophet ﷺ heard a man saying: ""Labbayka on behalf of so-and-so."" The Prophet ﷺ said to him: ""If you have already performed Ḥajj, then perform talbiyah on his behalf; otherwise, perform Ḥajj on your own behalf first, then perform Ḥajj on his behalf."""
"8682","(8679) And Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Abī Khalaf al-Ṣūfī al-Mihrajānī therein informed us, that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Yazdād ibn Mas'ūd informed him, that Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Sulaymān informed him, that Ḥasan ibn Khalaf al-Wāsiṭī informed him, that Isḥāq al-Azraq informed him, from Sharīk, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from 'Aṭā', from Ibn 'Abbās, that the Prophet ﷺ saw a man performing talbiyah on behalf of another man. He ﷺ said to him: ""Have you performed talbiyah on your own behalf?"" He replied: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then perform talbiyah on your own behalf, then perform talbiyah on behalf of so-and-so."""
"8683","(8680) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, that 'Alī ibn 'Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him, that al-Ḥasan ibn Sa'īd ibn Yūsuf al-Marwarūdhī informed him, that Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Zanjawayh informed him, that al-Firyābī Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed him, that Abū Bakr ibn 'Ayyāsh informed him, from Ibn 'Aṭā', from 'Aṭā', from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: The Prophet ﷺ heard a man saying: ""Labbayka on behalf of Shubrumah."" He ﷺ asked: ""Have you performed Ḥajj on your own behalf?"" He replied: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then perform talbiyah on your own behalf."""
"8684","(8681) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥasan al-Dārimī informed me, that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, meaning Ibn Khuzaymah, informed him, that Muḥammad ibn 'Alī ibn Ḥamzah informed him, that 'Abd al-Wārith ibn 'Ubayd Allāh informed him, that Khālid ibn Ṣubayḥ informed him, that al-Ḥasan ibn 'Umarāh informed him, that 'Amr ibn Dīnār informed him, that 'Aṭā' informed him, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man performing talbiyah on behalf of Shubrumah. He ﷺ called him and said to him: ""Have you performed Ḥajj?"" He replied: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then this Ḥajj is on your own behalf, and perform Ḥajj on behalf of Shubrumah."""
"8685","(8682) Abū Sa'īd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, that Abū al-'Abbāṣ al-Aṣamm informed him, that al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed him, that al-Shāfi'ī informed him, that 'Abd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī informed him, from Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah and Khālid al-Ḥadhā', from Abī Qilābah, from Ibn 'Abbās, that he heard a man saying: ""Labbayka on behalf of Shubrumah."" He ﷺ said: ""Woe to you! What is Shubrumah?"" One of them said: ""My brother."" The other said: ""He mentioned a relative."" He ﷺ asked: ""Have you performed Ḥajj on your own behalf?"" He replied: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then perform this Ḥajj on your own behalf, then perform Ḥajj on behalf of Shubrumah."""
"8686","(8683) And Mu'āwiyah ibn Hishām narrated from Sufyān, from Khālid al-Ḥadhā', from Abī Qilābah, from Ibn 'Abbās, that a man vowed to perform Ḥajj but had not yet performed the Ḥajj of Islam. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Perform the Ḥajj of Islam first, then perform Ḥajj for your vow afterwards.""

'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, that Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭabarānī informed him, that al-Ḥaḍramī informed him, that Abū Kurayb informed him, that Mu'āwiyah ibn Hishām informed him, that Sufyān informed him. Sulaymān said: No one narrated this from Sufyān except Mu'āwiyah."
"8687","(8684) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us — 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Mubashshir informed us — 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Bayan informed us — Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Umara from 'Abd al-Malik from Tawus from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet ﷺ heard a man performing the talbiyah on behalf of Nabisha, so he said: ""O you who are performing the talbiyah on behalf of Nabisha — this is on behalf of Nabisha, so perform Hajj for yourself."" [Munkar. Al-Daraqutni 2/268]"
"8688","(8685) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us — 'Ali informed us — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id informed us — al-Hasan ibn Ja'far ibn Midrar informed us — my father, Tahir ibn Midrar, informed us — al-Hasan ibn 'Umara narrated from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maisara from Tawus from Ibn 'Abbas that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man saying: ""Labbayka on behalf of Shubruma."" The Prophet ﷺ said to him: ""Who is Shubruma?"" He said: ""A brother of mine."" He said: ""Have you performed Hajj?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Perform Hajj for yourself, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubruma.""

'Ali said: This is the correct narration from Ibn 'Abbas, and the one before it is erroneous. It is said that al-Hasan ibn 'Umara used to narrate it, then he retracted it and narrated it correctly, in agreement with the narration of others from Ibn 'Abbas, but his hadith is abandoned (matruk) in any case. [Sahih by other chains. Al-Daraqutni 3/268]"
"8689","(8686) Abu al-Qasim 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn 'Ali al-Fami al-Faqih in Baghdad informed us — Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman al-Najjad informed us — al-Harith ibn Muhammad informed us — Ruh informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed us — 'Ata' informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, among a group of people with me, say: We, the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, entered ihram for Hajj exclusively, with nothing else alongside it, purely and solely. 'Ata' said: Jabir said: The Prophet ﷺ arrived in Mecca on the morning of the fourth of Dhu al-Hijja. When we arrived, the Prophet ﷺ commanded us, saying: ""Exit your ihram and have relations with your wives."" 'Ata' said: He did not compel them to have relations with their wives, but rather he made it permissible for them. It reached him that we were saying: ""When there remained only five days between us and 'Arafat, he permitted us to go to our wives, and we would come to 'Arafat with our private parts dripping with semen."" And Jabir would say, moving his hand as if I were looking at his hand moving — then the Prophet ﷺ stood and said: ""Do you know that I am the most fearful of Allah among you, the most truthful, and the most righteous? Were it not for the sacrificial animal, I would have exited my ihram as you have exited yours. And had I known beforehand what I came to know later, I would not have brought the sacrificial animal."" So we exited our ihram, heard, and obeyed. Jabir said: Then 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, arrived from his duties, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: ""With what did you enter ihram, O 'Ali?"" He said: ""With that with which the Prophet ﷺ entered ihram."" He said: ""Then offer a sacrifice and remain in ihram as you are."" So 'Ali offered a sacrifice for him. Then Suraqah ibn Malik said: ""Is this mut'ah of ours, O Messenger of Allah, for this year only, or forever?"" He said: ""No, rather forever.""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it as sahih from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 6933. Muslim 1213]"
"8690","(8687) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us — Ja'far ibn Awn informed us — Abu 'Umayr informed me, he said: I heard Qays ibn Muslim narrating from Tariq ibn Shihab from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Yemen. I met him in the year in which he performed Hajj. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""O Abu Musa, what did you say when you entered ihram?"" He said: I said: ""Ihram like the ihram of the Prophet ﷺ."" He said: ""Did you bring a sacrificial animal?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Then go and perform tawaf of the House, and between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then exit your ihram."" So I went and performed tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa. Then I went to some women of the tribe of Qays — meaning his aunts — and they combed my head with washing. Then, during the caliphate of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, I came as a pilgrim, and while I was narrating to the people near the House what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded me, a man came and said: ""Beware, O man, with your narration, for you do not know what the Commander of the Faithful has introduced regarding the rites."" I said: ""O people, whoever has heard something, let him not act upon it until the Commander of the Faithful arrives — follow him."" When 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, arrived, I said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, has something new been introduced in the rites?"" 'Umar became angry at that, then said: ""Yes! If we take the Book of Allah, Allah has commanded perfection; and if we take the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among us, he did not exit his ihram until the sacrificial animal reached its place of slaughter."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd ibn Humayd from Ja'far ibn Awn. [Sahih. Muslim 1221]"
"8691","(8688) Abu Bakr ibn Fawak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Yunus ibn Habib informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Shu'ba narrated from Qays ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab narrating from Abu Musa, who said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was encamped in al-Battha', and he said to me: ""How did you enter ihram?"" He said: I said: ""Labbayka with an ihram like the ihram of the Prophet ﷺ."" He said: ""You have done well. Perform tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then exit your ihram."" And he mentioned the hadith.

Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in their Sahihs from the hadith of Shu'ba. In the narration of Tawus: that the Prophet ﷺ set out from Medina without designating it as Hajj or 'Umrah, awaiting the divine decree. The divine decree came to him while he was between al-Safa and al-Marwa, and he commanded those who had entered ihram and had no sacrificial animal to make it an 'Umrah.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, corroborated this mursal narration with musnad hadiths narrated regarding their ihram, which attest to the correctness of Tawus's narration. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 1484. Muslim 1221]"
"8692","(8689) Among them is what Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Isma'il al-Tabaranī narrated to us in it — 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Tusi informed us — Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'igh informed us — Ruh informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed me — Mansur ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from his mother, Safiyya bint Shayba, from Asma' bint Abi Bakr, who said: We set out in ihram, and the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever has a sacrificial animal with him, let him remain in his ihram; and whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him exit his ihram."" I did not have a sacrificial animal with me, so I exited my ihram, and al-Zubayr had a sacrificial animal with him, so he did not exit his ihram. She said: I put on my garments, then went out and sat near al-Zubayr. He said: ""Get away from me."" I said: ""Are you afraid that I might jump on you?"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb from Ruh ibn 'Ubada.

Al-Shafi'i mentioned alongside this the hadith of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and 'Amra from 'A'isha, may Allah be pleased with her, then he distinguished between ihram for Hajj or 'Umrah and ihram for Salah. [Sahih. Muslim 1236]"
"8693","(8690) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — Muslim ibn Khalid narrated from Ibn Jurayj from 'Ata', that he said regarding a man who has not performed Hajj and then performs Hajj intending a voluntary act, or performs Hajj on behalf of another person, or performs Hajj to fulfill a vow: ""This is the Hajj of Islam. Then let him perform Hajj on behalf of the man afterwards, if he wishes, and for his vow."" [Da'if. Musnad al-Shafi'i 507]"
"8694","(8691) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — al-Qaddah narrated from al-Thawri from Zayd ibn Jubayr, who said: I was with 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar when he was asked about this, and he said: ""This is the Hajj of Islam. Let him seek to fulfill his vow"" — meaning one upon whom Hajj is obligatory and who has vowed a Hajj. [Sahih. Ibn Abi Shayba 12738]"
"8695","(8692) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Amr ibn Matar informed us — Yahya ibn Muhammad informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh informed us — my father informed us — Shu'ba narrated from Zayd ibn Jubayr, who said: I heard a woman asking Ibn 'Umar. She said: ""I vowed to perform Hajj but I have not performed it."" He said: ""Begin with the Hajj of Islam."" She said: ""I am poor and destitute; pray to Allah for me."" So he prayed to Allah to make it easy for her. [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"8696","(8693) Abu 'Abd Allah informed us — Abu 'Amr ibn Matar informed us — Yahya ibn Muhammad informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh informed us — my father informed us — Shu'ba narrated from Sulayman, or Abu Sulayman, who heard Anas ibn Malik saying regarding one who vowed to perform Hajj and has never performed Hajj at all: He said: Let him begin with the obligatory (farida). [Da'if]"
"8697","(8694) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us, saying: Abū Muʿāwiya narrated to us, from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAmr, from Mihrān Abī Ṣafwān, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever intends to perform Hajj, let him hasten to do so."""
"8698","(8695) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us, saying: Abū Ḥudhayfa narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Ismāʿīl al-Kūfī, from Fuḍayl ibn ʿAmr al-Fuqaymī, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Hasten to set out towards Makkah, for one of you does not know what may befall him of illness or need."" Abū Isrāʾīl al-Malāʾī also narrated it from Fuḍayl."
"8699","(8696) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, saying: Abū Isrāʾīl al-Malāʾī narrated to us, from Fuḍayl ibn ʿAmr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, from al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever intends to perform Hajj, let him hasten, for the one who is ill may fall sick, the mount may go astray, and a need may arise."""
"8700","(8697) And al-Qāḍī Abū ʿUmar Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Mughīra narrated to us in Tustar, saying: Abū al-Haytham Sayyār ibn al-Ḥasan al-Tustarī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Walīd Hishām ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us, and he mentioned it with a similar meaning. Except that he said: from Ibn ʿAbbās, from al-Faḍl, or from one of them both. And likewise, ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī said from Abū al-Walīd, with doubt."
"8701","(8698) Abū Saʿd ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī ʿUthmān al-Zāhid narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Najda ibn al-ʿUrayyān narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥimmānī narrated to us, saying: Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿUmar al-Aḥmasī narrated to us, saying: al-Aʿmash narrated to us, from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd, who said: I heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""Perform Hajj before you are no longer able to perform Hajj."" It is as though I am looking at a small-limbed, flat-nosed Abyssinian with a pickaxe in his hand, demolishing it stone by stone. I said to him: ""Is this something you say from your own opinion, or did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?"" He said: ""No, by the One who split the seed and created the soul, but I heard it from your Prophet (ﷺ)."""
"8702","(8699) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyayna narrated to us, and Ziyād ibn Saʿd narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The Kaʿba will be destroyed by Dhū al-Suwayqatayn (the man with thin legs) from among the Abyssinians."""
"8703","(8700) And Abū ʿAbdillāh narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr narrated to us, saying: Mūsā ibn Isḥāq al-Anṣārī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Shayba narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated with a similar version. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Shayba."
"8704","(8701) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī narrated to us, saying: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn al-Akhnas, who narrated to me: Ibn Abī Mulayka narrated to me, that Ibn ʿAbbās informed him from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""It is as though I am looking at a black, knock-kneed man, pulling it down stone by stone"" — meaning the Kaʿba. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī, from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān."
"8705","(8702) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Mubashshir narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĪsā ibn Baḥīr narrated to me, saying: Muḥammad ibn Abī Muḥammad narrated to me, from his father, from Abū Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Perform Hajj before you are no longer able to perform Hajj."" It was said: ""What is the matter with Hajj?"" He said: ""The Bedouins of the land will sit at the mouths of its valleys, and no one will be able to reach Hajj."""
"8706","(8703) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī said: The obligation of Hajj was revealed to the Prophet (ﷺ) after the Hijra. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) conquered Makkah in the month of Ramaḍān and departed from it in Shawwāl, appointing ʿAttāb ibn Asīd as his deputy over it. He then performed Hajj on behalf of the Muslims by the command of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in Madīnah, fully capable of performing Hajj, along with his wives and the generality of his Companions. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) returned from Tabūk and sent Abū Bakr, who performed Hajj for the people in the ninth year, while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in Madīnah, fully capable of performing Hajj, yet he did not perform Hajj, nor did his wives, nor any of his Companions, until he performed Hajj in the tenth year. Thus, we deduce from this that the obligation of Hajj is once in a lifetime, the first instance being upon reaching puberty, and the last being that one performs it before one's death.

The Sheikh said: That which al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) mentioned is established in the narrations and the histories. As for what he mentioned regarding the revelation of the obligation of Hajj after the Hijra, it is as he stated, and our scholars have drawn evidence from the ḥadīth of Kaʿb ibn ʿUjra to show that it was revealed at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyya."
"8707","(8704) And it is that which Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Sayf narrated to us; Mujāhid narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā narrated to me that Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stopped by me at al-Ḥudaybiyyah while my head was shedding lice. He said: ""Are your vermin harming you?"" I said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Then shave your head,"" or he said, ""Shave it."" He said: ""Then this verse was revealed concerning me: {And whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of the head, then a ransom of fasting, or charity, or sacrifice} [al-Baqarah:196] to its end."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fast three days, or give a ṣāʿ of food among six persons, or offer whatever sacrifice is easy.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from another chain. Through this, it is established that the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: {And complete the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah for Allah} [al-Baqarah:196] to its end was revealed at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah.

And it has been narrated to us from Ibn Masʿūd and others that he said regarding His statement {And complete the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah for Allah}: ""Perform the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah for Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""

And from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him: The perfection of Ḥajj is that you assume iḥrām from the dwelling place of your family. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1720. Muslim 1201]"
"8708","(8705) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaffār informed us; Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Labbād narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to us; Asbāṭ ibn Naṣr narrated to us, from al-Suddī, from Abī Mālik and Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, and from Murrah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, and from people among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), regarding His statement: {And complete the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah for Allah}, he says: ""Perform the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8709","(8706) Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū al-Jawwāb narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Shuʿbah, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salamah, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him: that he was asked about the perfection of Ḥajj, and he said: ""The perfection of Ḥajj is that you assume iḥrām from the dwelling place of your family.""

The Sheikh said: The time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah was in the year six of the Hijrah, in Dhū al-Qaʿdah. [Ḍaʿīf. Ḥākim 2/303]"
"8710","(8707) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us; Nāfiʿ ibn Abī Nuʿaym narrated to me, from Nāfiʿ, the freed slave of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, saying: Al-Ḥudaybiyyah occurred in the year six after the arrival of the Prophet (ﷺ) in Madīnah, in Dhū al-Qaʿdah. The ʿUmrah was performed in Dhū al-Qaʿdah in the year seven. The Conquest (of Makkah) took place in Ramaḍān in the year eight. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) departed immediately for Ḥunayn and al-Ṭāʾif. When he returned in Shawwāl, he performed ʿUmrah from al-Jiʿrānah. Then ʿAttāb ibn Asīd performed Ḥajj and led the people in Ḥajj; the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had appointed him over the Ḥajj. Then Abū Bakr performed Ḥajj in the year nine; the Prophet (ﷺ) had appointed him. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) performed Ḥajj in the year ten after his arrival in Madīnah, and that was the Farewell Pilgrimage (Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ). In this there is evidence that the matter of the Conquest, the appointment of ʿAttāb ibn Asīd, then the appointment of Abū Bakr in the year nine, then his own Ḥajj in the year ten, is as al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, stated, and it is well-known among the people of the Maghāzī, and it is mentioned in the connected hadiths separately. [Ḥasan. Al-Tārīkh al-Ṣaghīr of al-Bukhārī 1/33]"
"8711","(8708) And Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Hammām narrated to us, from Qatādah, who said: I said to Anas ibn Mālik: How many Ḥajj did the Prophet (ﷺ) perform? He said: One Ḥajj, and he performed four ʿUmrahs: the ʿUmrah from which the polytheists turned him away from the House; the second ʿUmrah when they made peace with him and he returned the following year; an ʿUmrah from al-Jiʿrānah when he distributed the spoils of Ḥunayn in Dhū al-Qaʿdah; and a Ḥajj along with his ʿUmrah.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abū al-Walīd, and he said: ""And an ʿUmrah along with his Ḥajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3917]"
"8712","(8709) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Khālid narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, who said: Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) undertook nineteen military expeditions, and that he performed Ḥajj after his migration only one Ḥajj, after which he performed no Ḥajj except the Farewell Pilgrimage. Abū Isḥāq said: ""And another one in Makkah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Khālid, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from Zuhayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4142. Muslim 1254]"
"8713","(8710) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Ibn Jurayj, from Mujāhid, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed three Ḥajjs: two Ḥajjs while he was in Makkah before the Hijrah, and the Farewell Pilgrimage.

The Sheikh said: His Ḥajj before the Hijrah would have been before the obligation of Ḥajj was revealed, so it is not counted as fulfilling the obligation that was revealed thereafter. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"8714","(8711) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding {The Ḥajj is [during] well-known months}: He said: ""Shawwāl, Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah.""

And something to that effect has been narrated from Ibn ʿUmar from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, as a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mustadrak of Ḥākim 2/303. Dāraquṭnī 2/226]"
"8715","(8712) And Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us; Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrawī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Sharīk narrated to us, from Abī Isḥāq, from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His statement {The Ḥajj is [during] well-known months}: He said: ""Shawwāl, Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah."" [Ḍaʿīf. Dāraquṭnī 2/226]"
"8716","(8713) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr al-Ṣayrafī informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Khuṣayf, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās: {The Ḥajj is [during] well-known months}: He said: ""Shawwāl, Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah.""

And that has been confirmed from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, and that will come, if Allah wills. [Ḍaʿīf. Ṭabarānī in al-Awsaṭ 5/5043]"
"8717","(8714) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated from Abū Saʿd, from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Thaqafī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, who said: ""The months of Ḥajj are Shawwāl, Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah."" [Weak. Dāraquṭnī 2/226]"
"8718","(8715) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from Warqāʾ ibn ʿUmar, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, regarding His statement {and whoever obligates upon himself the Hajj therein} — he said: ""He means the pronouncement of the talbiyah."" [Ḥasan]"
"8719","(8716) He said, and ʿUthmān narrated to us; Sharīk narrated from Abū Isḥāq. ʿUthmān said: ""Our companions said it is from Abū al-Aḥwaṣ."" ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — said: ""The obligation of Ḥajj is the Ihrām."" [Weak. Dāraquṭnī 2/227]"
"8720","(8717) He said, and ʿUthmān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā narrated from Saʿīd, Abū Saʿd, from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh, who said: ""I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr saying: 'The obligation of Ḥajj is the Ihrām.'"" [Weak. Dāraquṭnī 2/227. al-Dur al-Manthūr 1/525]"
"8721","(8718) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʾAṭāʾ informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us from Abū al-Zubayr, who said: ""I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, being asked: 'Is the talbiyah for Ḥajj pronounced outside the months of Ḥajj?' He said: 'No.'"" [Weak. Dāraquṭnī 2/234. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14618]"
"8722","(8719) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl and Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Baḥīrī narrated to us by dictation, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Abū Kurayb narrated to us; Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar narrated from Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""One does not enter Ihrām for Ḥajj except in the months of Ḥajj. Indeed, it is from the Sunnah of Ḥajj that one enters Ihrām for Ḥajj in the months of Ḥajj."" [Weak. Dāraquṭnī 2/234. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14617. Ibn Khuzaymah 2569]"
"8723","(8720) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Dalaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijzī informed us in Baghdad; Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sahl narrated to us; Muṣʿab ibn Salām narrated from Ḥamzah al-Zayyāt, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding a man who enters Ihrām for Ḥajj outside the months of Ḥajj — he said: ""That is not from the Sunnah."" [Weak. See the preceding]"
"8724","(8721) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Ḥakam, from Abū al-Qāsim, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Indeed, it is from the Sunnah of Ḥajj that one does not enter Ihrām for Ḥajj except in the months of Ḥajj."" ʿAlī said: ""Abū al-Qāsim is Miqsam, the freedman of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal."" [Weak. See the preceding]"
"8725","(8722) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us; ʿAbdullāh narrated to us; ʿUthmān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʾAṭāʾ, who said: ""Allah, the Most High, said {Ḥajj is [during] well-known months} so that Ḥajj would not be made obligatory in other than them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Dāraquṭnī 2/234]"
"8726","(8723) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Aḥmasī narrated to us; al-Muḥāribī narrated from Sufyān, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʾAṭāʾ, who said: ""Whoever enters Ihrām for Ḥajj outside the months of Ḥajj, he should make it an ʿUmrah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1683. Muslim 8349]"
"8727","(8724) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr ibn al-Ḥakam narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh; Sumayy narrated from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""An ʿUmrah to another ʿUmrah is an expiation for the sins committed between them, and the accepted Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-mabrūr) has no reward except Paradise.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Sumayy, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Mālik from Sumayy. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1683. Muslim 8349]"
"8728","(8725) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me that Abū al-Zubayr informed him from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) set out intending ʿUmrah until, when she reached Sarif, she began menstruating. The Prophet (ﷺ) came to her and found her weeping. He said: ""What makes you weep?"" She said: ""I have menstruated and have not yet exited the state of iḥrām, nor have I performed ṭawāf of the House, and the people are now departing for Ḥajj."" He said: ""This is a matter that Allah has decreed upon the daughters of Ādam. Perform ghusl and then enter iḥrām for Ḥajj."" She did so and stood at the stations (of ʿArafah, Muzdalifah, and Minā) until, when she became pure, she performed ṭawāf of the Kaʿbah and of al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. Then he said: ""You have exited the state of iḥrām from both your Ḥajj and your ʿUmrah together."" She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I feel in my heart that I did not perform ṭawāf of the House until I had performed Ḥajj."" He said: ""Go with her, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and have her perform ʿUmrah from al-Tanʿīm."" That was on the night of al-Ḥaṣbah.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others from al-Layth.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: Her ʿUmrah was in Dhū al-Ḥijjah, and then she asked him to have her perform another ʿUmrah, so he had her perform ʿUmrah in Dhū al-Ḥijjah. Thus these were two ʿUmrahs in one month. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1213. al-Nasāʾī 2763]"
"8729","(8726) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and others narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to perform ʿUmrah at the end of Dhū al-Ḥijjah from al-Juḥfah, and she used to perform ʿUmrah in Rajab from Madīnah, and she would enter iḥrām from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah. [Ḥasan]"
"8730","(8727) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; Saʿdān narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated; Sufyān narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated; Sufyān narrated from Ṣadaqah ibn Yasār, from al-Qāsim, from ʿĀʾishah, that she performed ʿUmrah three times in one year. I said: ""Did anyone censure her for that?"" He said: ""Glory be to Allah! She is the Mother of the Believers."" Saʿdān said in his narration: ""So I fell silent and was subdued."" And Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ said: Sufyān said: ""Who would censure the Mother of the Believers?"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8731","(8728) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""There is an ʿUmrah in every month."" [Ḍaʿīf. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 515]"
"8732","(8729) And Abū Zakariyyā informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Anas, who is Ibn ʿIyāḍ, narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) performed two ʿUmrahs every year for several years during the era of Ibn al-Zubayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 518]"
"8733","(8730) And Abū Zakariyyā informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ibn Abī Ḥusayn, from one of the children of Anas ibn Mālik, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""We were with Anas ibn Mālik in Makkah, and whenever he washed his head (from dye), he would go out and perform ʿUmrah."" [Ḍaʿīf. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 514. Ibn Abī Shaybah 12727]"
"8734","(8731) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Saʿīd al-Jurayrī narrated from Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr, from Muṭarrif, who said: ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn said: ""I shall narrate to you a ḥadīth; perhaps Allah, the Most High, will benefit you by it after this day. Know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had a group of his family perform ʿUmrah in the ten days of Dhū al-Ḥijjah, and no Qurʾān was revealed to abrogate it. A man may thereafter hold whatever opinion he wishes.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Jurayrī, and he added: ""And he did not forbid it until he passed away."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1226. Ibn Mājah 2978]"
"8735","(8732) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Hannād narrated from Ibn Abī Zāʾidah; Ibn Jurayj and Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṭāwūs, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) only had ʿĀʾishah perform ʿUmrah in Dhū al-Ḥijjah in order to sever the practice of the people of shirk. For this clan of Quraysh and those who followed their religion used to say: 'When the wounds on the camel's back have healed, and the sores have cleared, and Ṣafar has entered, then ʿUmrah is permissible for whoever wishes to perform it.' Thus they used to prohibit ʿUmrah until Dhū al-Ḥijjah and al-Muḥarram had passed."" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1987. Ibn Ḥibbān 1987]"
"8736","(8733) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed me; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl Abū Salamah informed us; Wuhayb narrated to me; Ibn Ṭāwūs narrated from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""They used to consider ʿUmrah during the months of Ḥajj to be among the most grievous acts of disobedience on earth. They would say: 'When the camel's back has healed, and the traces have faded, and Ṣafar has elapsed, then ʿUmrah is permissible for whoever wishes to perform it.' They used to call al-Muḥarram 'Ṣafar.' The Prophet (ﷺ) and his Companions arrived on the morning of the fourth of Dhū al-Ḥijjah, pronouncing the talbiyah for Ḥajj. The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded them to make it an ʿUmrah, and this seemed grave to them. They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what constitutes exiting iḥrām?' He said: 'Exiting iḥrām in its entirety'—meaning they should exit iḥrām from everything (that had been prohibited).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Wuhayb. He clarified in the ḥadīth of ʿľʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that he only commanded those who had not brought the sacrificial animal to do so, and that is subject to the will of Allah, the Most High. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1489. Muslim 1240]"
"8737","(8734) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Abū Muʿāwiya narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Murqaʿ al-Asadī from Abū Dhar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: It was not permissible for anyone to dissolve his Ḥajj into an ʿUmrah, except specifically for the caravan of the Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ).
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1224. Nasāʾī 2812]"
"8738","(8735) Abū Ḥāzim ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbda al-Tamīmī informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Turk informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā said: I read to Mālik from Abū al-Aswad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Nawfal from ʿUrwa from ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage. Among us were those who assumed iḥrām for ʿUmrah, and among us were those who assumed iḥrām for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah together, and among us were those who assumed iḥrām for Ḥajj alone. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) assumed iḥrām for Ḥajj. As for those who assumed iḥrām for ʿUmrah alone, they exited the state of iḥrām. But as for those who assumed iḥrām for Ḥajj alone or combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, they did not exit the state of iḥrām until the Day of Sacrifice.
Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Ibn Yūsuf from Mālik.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1487. Muslim 1211]"
"8739","(8736) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ informed us: Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿIkrima ibn Khālid, who said: I asked Ibn ʿUmar about performing ʿUmrah before Ḥajj, and he said: There is no harm in anyone performing ʿUmrah before Ḥajj.
He said: And ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: The Prophet (ﷺ) performed ʿUmrah before Ḥajj. Al-Bukhārī extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Mubārak and Abū ʿĀṣim from Ibn Jurayj.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1684. Musnad Aḥmad 2/46]"
"8740","(8737) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated from Ṣadaqa ibn Yasār from Ibn ʿUmar that he said: That I perform ʿUmrah before Ḥajj and bring a sacrificial animal is more beloved to me than that I perform ʿUmrah after Ḥajj in Dhū al-Ḥijjah.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muwaṭṭaʾ Mālik 764]"
"8741","(8738) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Hudba informed us: Hammām informed us: Qatāda informed us that Anas informed him: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed four ʿUmrahs, all of them in Dhū al-Qaʿda, except the one that was with his Ḥajj. An ʿUmrah from al-Ḥudaybiya or the time of al-Ḥudaybiya in Dhū al-Qaʿda; an ʿUmrah in the following year in Dhū al-Qaʿda; an ʿUmrah from al-Jiʿrāna, where he distributed the spoils of Ḥunayn, in Dhū al-Qaʿda; and an ʿUmrah with his Ḥajj.
Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from Hudba.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3917. Muslim 1253]"
"8742","(8739) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī informed us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr informed us: ʿUmar ibn Dharr narrated from Mujāhid from Abū Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed three ʿUmrahs, all of them in Dhū al-Qaʿda.
[Ḥasan]"
"8743","(8740) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Isḥāq al-Bazzāz informed us in Baghdād: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī informed us in Makka: Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarra informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad informed us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwa narrated from his father from ʿĀʾisha that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed three ʿUmrahs: one in Shawwāl and two in Dhū al-Qaʿda.
[Ḥasan]"
"8744","(8741) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Sufyān narrated from Shuʿba from Yazīd al-Rishk from Muʿādha al-ʿAdawiyya from ʿĀʾisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ʿUmrah is permissible throughout the entire year except on four days: the Day of ʿArafa, the Day of Sacrifice, and two days after that.
This is a mawqūf narration, and according to us it is understood to refer to one who is occupied with Ḥajj — he should not introduce ʿUmrah upon it, nor perform ʿUmrah until he completes all the rites of Ḥajj. For ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) commanded Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī and Habbār ibn al-Aswad, when each of them missed Ḥajj, to exit the state of iḥrām by performing the rites of an ʿUmrah.
Al-Shāfiʿī said: The greatest of days in sanctity is the first of them, in which a rite is performed for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.
[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8745","(8742) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us: Musaddad informed us: Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Aṭāʾ, who said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās narrating to us, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to a woman from the Anṣār — Ibn ʿAbbās named her but I have forgotten her name: ""What prevented you from performing Ḥajj with us this year?"" She said: ""O Prophet of Allah, we had two camels. So-and-so, her husband and his son, rode one camel, and left the other camel for us to draw water with."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""When Ramaḍān comes, then perform ʿUmrah, for an ʿUmrah in Ramaḍān is equivalent to a Ḥajj.""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1690. Muslim 1256]"
"8746","(8743) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Sūsī and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī from the original of his hearing said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī informed us: Bishr ibn Bakr informed us: al-Awzāʿī informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr informed me: Abū Salama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me: Ibn Umm Maʿqil al-Asadiyya informed me. He said: My mother said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I wish to perform Ḥajj, but my camel is emaciated. What do you command me?"" He said: ""Perform ʿUmrah in Ramaḍān, for an ʿUmrah in Ramaḍān is equal to a Ḥajj.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad Aḥmad 4/210. Muʿjam al-Kabīr al-Ṭabarānī 20/272]"
"8747","(8744) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Mu'adhdhin narrated, saying: Bakr ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Dāwūd ibn Yazīd al-Awdī narrated from ʿĀmir, from Harim ibn Khanbash, who said: I was in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ when a woman came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, in which of the months should I perform ʿUmrah?"" He said: ""Perform ʿUmrah in Ramadan, for an ʿUmrah in Ramadan is equivalent to a Ḥajj.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh.

And Ibn ʿUyaynah similarly narrated it from Dāwūd al-Awdī. In the narration of ʿAbd al-Khāliq, it is Wahb ibn Khanbash, and the narration of Bayān from al-Shaʿbī from Wahb ibn Khanbash. Al-Bukhārī said: ""Wahb is more correct.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Mājah 2992. Musnad Aḥmad 4/177]"
"8748","(8745) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed me, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever has brought a sacrificial animal with him, let him enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj along with the ʿUmrah, and let him not exit from iḥrām until he exits from both of them together.""

She said: I arrived in Makkah while I was menstruating, and I did not perform ṭawāf of the House nor between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. I complained of this to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: ""Undo your hair, comb it, enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj, and leave the ʿUmrah.""

She said: I did so. When we had completed the Ḥajj, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr to al-Tanʿīm, and I performed ʿUmrah. He said: ""This is in place of your ʿUmrah.""

She said: Those who had entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah performed ṭawāf of the House and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, then exited iḥrām, then performed another ṭawāf after they returned from Minā for their Ḥajj. As for those who had combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, they performed only one ṭawāf.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik. And Maʿmar similarly narrated it from al-Zuhrī: ""Whoever has brought a sacrificial animal with him, let him enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj along with his ʿUmrah, and let him not exit from iḥrām until he exits from both of them together.""

And ʿUqayl narrated it from al-Zuhrī, and he said: ""Whoever enters into iḥrām for ʿUmrah and does not bring a sacrificial animal, let him exit iḥrām."" And with the same meaning, ʿAmrah narrated it from ʿĀʾishah, and al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad confirmed her in that. And upon the same meaning, the narration of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and his statement: ""Enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj and leave the ʿUmrah"" — he means by it: refrain from its rites and incorporate Ḥajj upon it, and this is clear in the narration of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, in the story of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1481. Muslim 211]"
"8749","(8746) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth narrated —

And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us, ʿĪsā ibn Ḥammād narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us, Abū al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Jābir, that he said: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, entering into iḥrām for Ḥajj alone, and ʿĀʾishah arrived entering into iḥrām for ʿUmrah. When they reached Sarif, she began to menstruate. When we arrived, we performed ṭawāf of the Kaʿbah and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded those of us who did not have a sacrificial animal with them to exit iḥrām. We said: ""What is this exiting?"" He said: ""Complete exiting."" So we were intimate with our wives, applied perfume, and wore our garments, and there were only four nights between us and ʿArafah. Then we entered into iḥrām on the Day of Tarwiyah. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to ʿĀʾishah and found her weeping. He said: ""What is the matter with you?"" She said: ""The matter is that I have menstruated, and the people have exited iḥrām, and I have not exited, and I have not performed ṭawāf of the House, and the people are now going to Ḥajj."" He said: ""This is a matter that Allah has decreed upon the daughters of Ādam. Perform ghusl, then enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj."" She did so and stood at the stations until, when she became pure, she performed ṭawāf of the Kaʿbah and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. Then he said: ""You have now exited from your Ḥajj and your ʿUmrah together."" She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I feel in my soul that I did not perform ṭawāf of the House until I performed Ḥajj."" He said: ""Go with her, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and have her perform ʿUmrah from al-Tanʿīm."" And that was on the night of al-Ḥaṣbah.

Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from al-Layth.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1213]"
"8750","(8747) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Mālik ibn Anas and others informed me, that Nāfiʿ informed them that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar went out during the Fitnah intending to perform ʿUmrah, and he said: ""If I am prevented from the House, we shall do as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did."" So he went out, entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah, and traveled until, when he ascended the heights of al-Baydāʾ, he turned to his companions and said: ""The matter of both is but one. I bear witness that I have made Ḥajj obligatory along with ʿUmrah."" He went out until he reached the House and performed ṭawāf of it, and performed ṭawāf between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah seven times, and did not add anything to that, and he considered that sufficient for him, and he offered a sacrificial animal.

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥayn from the ḥadīth of Mālik. And ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and others narrated it from Nāfiʿ, and they added in it: ""He did not exit from iḥrām from either of them until he exited from both of them together with Ḥajj on the Day of Sacrifice."" His statement ""he did not add anything to that"" means: he did not perform saʿy between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah more than once. And if he had entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj and then wished to incorporate ʿUmrah upon it, al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: ""The majority of those I met and from whom I memorized say: That is not permissible for him."" And it is narrated from some of the Tābiʿīn, but I do not know whether anything is established from any of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ in this regard or not. It has been narrated from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, but it is not established. He only intended that.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1712. Muslim 1230]"
"8751","(8748) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥayy narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ḥayyān al-Madāʾinī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from Mālik ibn al-Ḥārith, from Abū Naṣr, who said: I entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj, and I came upon ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and I said: ""I have entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj — can I add ʿUmrah to it?"" He said: ""No. Had you entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah and then wished to add Ḥajj to it, you would have done so. But when you begin with Ḥajj, do not add ʿUmrah to it."" He said: ""What shall I do if I wish to do that?"" He said: ""Pour a vessel of water over yourself, then enter into iḥrām for both of them together, and perform two ṭawāfs.""

Thus narrated Ibn ʿUyaynah from Manṣūr. And this Abū Naṣr is not well-known.

[Ḍaʿīf. Dāraquṭnī 2/265]"
"8752","(8749) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr, who heard Mālik ibn al-Ḥārith, from Abū Naṣr al-Sulamī, that he met ʿAlī, and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, had entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and he himself had entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj. He said: I said to ʿAlī: ""Shall I enter into iḥrām for both of them together?"" ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""That would only be if, when you began, you had called for your vessel and performed ghusl, then entered into iḥrām for both of them together, then performed two ṭawāfs — one ṭawāf for your Ḥajj and one ṭawāf for your ʿUmrah — and nothing would become permissible for you until the Day of Sacrifice.""

And al-Thawrī narrated it from Manṣūr: ""Ibrāhīm narrated to me, from Mālik ibn al-Ḥārith, or Mālik narrated it to me."" And he said: ""That would only be had you begun with ʿUmrah."" ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""When you combine them, do such and such,"" and he mentioned it with its meaning. And Manṣūr used to have doubt as to whether he heard it directly from Mālik himself or from Ibrāhīm from him."
"8753","(8750) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — he is al-Aṣamm — narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī said: Saʿīd ibn Sālim said it, and he cited as evidence that Sufyān al-Thawrī informed him from Muʿāwiyah ibn Isḥāq, from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Ḥanafī, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ḥajj is jihād, and ʿUmrah is a voluntary act.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said in the book: I said to him — meaning one of the people of the East — ""Do you establish such a narration from the Prophet ﷺ?"" He said: ""It is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected).""

The Shaykh said: And it has been narrated from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Isḥāq, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, but the chain to Shuʿbah is a weak chain.

And Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn ʿAṭiyyah narrated it from Sālim al-Afṭas, from Ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, as a marfūʿ narration. And this Muḥammad is matrūk (abandoned).

[Ḍaʿīf. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 805. Ibn Abī Shaybah 1347]"
"8754","(8751) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdūyah ibn Sahl al-Marwazī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥammād al-Āmulī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr al-Anṣārī al-Miṣrī narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to me, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, that he said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is ʿUmrah obligatory, and is its obligation like the obligation of Ḥajj?"" He said: ""No, but if you perform ʿUmrah, it is better for you."" Thus narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh, and he is ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah.

It is uniquely narrated from Abū al-Zubayr. Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm al-Barqī and others mentioned it from Ibn ʿUfayr, from Yaḥyā, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah. And al-Bāghandī narrated it from Jaʿfar ibn Musāfir, from Ibn ʿUfayr, saying: from Yaḥyā, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar — and this is an error on the part of al-Bāghandī.

And Ibn Abī Dāwūd narrated it from Jaʿfar as the people narrated it. And this text is only known from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir.

[Munkar al-Isnād. Muʿjam al-Ṣaghīr al-Ṭabarānī 2/1015]"
"8755","(8752) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated, from al-Ḥajjāj, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man said to the Prophet ﷺ: ""Is ʿUmrah obligatory?"" He said: ""No, but if you perform ʿUmrah, it is better for you."" Thus narrated al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh as a marfūʿ narration.

[Ḍaʿīf. Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī 931. Aḥmad 3/316]"
"8756","(8753) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Abū Ismāʿīl Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me, Ibn Jurayj informed me, and al-Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh informed me, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was asked about ʿUmrah: ""Is it obligatory, is its obligation like the obligation of Ḥajj?"" He said: ""No, but if you perform ʿUmrah, it is better for you."" This is the preserved narration from Jābir, as a mawqūf (stopped) narration, not marfūʿ (raised). It has been narrated from Jābir as a marfūʿ narration contrary to that, and both of them are weak.

[Ḍaʿīf]"
"8757","(8754) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us: Wuḥayb narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwn, that he used to recite {Wa atimmū al-ḥajja wa al-ʿumrata lillāh} (""And complete the Hajj and the ʿUmrah for Allah""), and he would say: ""It is obligatory (wājibah)."" And al-Shaʿbī used to recite it {Wa atimmū al-ḥajja wa al-ʿumratu lillāh} and would say: ""It is supererogatory (taṭawwuʿ)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8758","(8755) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Muʿtamir — Ibn Sulaymān — narrated to us, from his father, from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar, who said: I said to Ibn ʿUmar, ""O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, there are people who claim that there is no divine decree (qadar)."" He said, ""Is there any one of them with us?"" I said, ""No."" He said, ""Then convey to them from me, when you meet them, that Ibn ʿUmar is free of you before Allah, and you are free of him. I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) say: 'While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), a man came to him wearing the garments of travel, not being from among the people of the town. He came forward until he sat before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as one of us sits in prayer, and he placed his hand upon the knees of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, ""O Muḥammad, what is Islam?"" He said: (""That you bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, that you establish the prayer, give the zakāh, perform Hajj to the House, perform ʿUmrah, perform the bath from janābah, perfect the wuḍūʾ, and fast Ramaḍān."" He said, ""If I do this, am I a Muslim?"" He said, ""Yes."" He said, ""You have spoken truly."" And he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth.)'""
Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir from Yūnus ibn Muḥammad, except that he did not cite its full text (matn). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 8. Abū Dāwūd 4696]"
"8759","(8756) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar and Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, with the same meaning. They both said: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Sālim, from ʿAmr ibn Aws, from Abī Razīn — Ḥafṣ said in his ḥadīth: ""A man from Banī ʀmir"" — that he said, ""O Messenger of Allah, my father is an old, elderly man who is unable to perform Hajj or ʿUmrah, nor can he travel."" He said, ""Perform Hajj on behalf of your father and perform ʿUmrah."" [Ḥasan. Previously referenced as 8633]"
"8760","(8757) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, saying: I asked Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj about this ḥadīth — meaning the ḥadīth of Abī Razīn — and he said: ""I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: 'I do not know of any ḥadīth regarding the obligation (ījāb) of ʿUmrah that is more excellent (ajwad) than this, nor more authentic (aṣaḥḥ), and no one has transmitted it with as excellent a narration as Shuʿbah has narrated it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8761","(8758) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Fahd al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ḥumayd ibn Mihrān al-Kindī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn narrated to us, from Ibn Ḥiṭṭān, from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā), that she said, ""O Messenger of Allah, is there jihād upon women?"" He said: (""Yes, a jihād in which there is no fighting: the Hajj and the ʿUmrah are their jihād."")
And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī similarly narrated it from Ḥumayd ibn Mihrān with the same meaning. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Mājah 290. Ibn Khuzaymah 3074]"
"8762","(8759) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ Muḥammad ibn al-Haytham ibn Ḥammād narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us, from Khālid ibn Yazīd, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from Yazīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Hād, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abī Salamah, from Abī Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: (""The jihād of the elderly, the weak, and the woman is the Hajj and the ʿUmrah."") [Ḍaʿīf. Nasāʾī 3626. Sunan Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr 2344]"
"8763","(8760) And ʿAbdullāh ibn Lahīʿah narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: (""The Hajj and the ʿUmrah are two obligatory duties (farīḍatān wājibatān)."")
Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid narrated it to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ḍarīr narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us: Qutaybah narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated it. (j) And Ibn Lahīʿah is not used as a proof (muḥtāj bih). (t) And in the ḥadīth of al-Ṣubayy ibn Maʿbad, he said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu): ""I found the Hajj and the ʿUmrah written upon me as obligatory, and so I entered iḥrām for both."" He said, ""You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet (ﷺ)."" And this, Allah willing, pertains to the chapter of the Qārin who sheds blood (as a sacrifice). [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Kāmil of Ibn ʿAdī: 4/150]"
"8764","(8761) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal al-Māsarjisī narrated to us: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Hajj and the ʿUmrah are two obligatory duties (farīḍatān)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8765","(8762) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā and ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj: Nāfiʿ, the freed slave of Ibn ʿUmar, informed me that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: ""There is no one among the creation of Allah except that upon him are an obligatory Hajj and an obligatory ʿUmrah — for whoever has the means to do so. And whoever does anything beyond that, it is good and supererogatory.""
Ibn Jurayj said: ""And I was informed from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) that he said: 'The ʿUmrah is obligatory, like the obligation of the Hajj, for whoever has the means to it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mustadrak of al-Ḥākim 1/644]"
"8766","(8763) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith both narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us: Abū ʿUbaydillāh al-Makhzūmī narrated to us: Hishām ibn Sulaymān and ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned it with the same wording, except that he said: ""Ibn Jurayj said: 'And I was informed from ʿIkrimah that Ibn ʿAbbās said,' and he mentioned it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Dāraquṭnī 2/288]"
"8767","(8764) Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī al-Faqīh informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Firās informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Daybulī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""By Allah, its counterpart is indeed in the Book of Allah: {And complete the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah for Allah}."" Al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Umm of al-Shāfiʿī 2/187]"
"8768","(8765) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik narrated to us; Yazīd, meaning Ibn Hārūn, informed us; Sulaymān, meaning al-Taymī, narrated from Ḥayyān ibn ʿUmayr Abī al-ʿAlāʾ, that a man asked Ibn ʿAbbās about a man who is a ṣarūrah (one who performs ʿUmrah before Ḥajj). He said: ""They are two acts of worship for Allah; it does not harm you whichever of them you begin with."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8769","(8766) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb al-Maqābirī narrated to us; ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād al-Muhallabī narrated to us; Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, that Zayd ibn Thābit was asked about performing ʿUmrah before Ḥajj. He said: ""They are two acts of worship; it does not harm you whichever of them you begin with."" Ismāʿīl ibn Sālim narrated it from Ibn Sīrīn as a marfūʿ (elevated) narration, but the correct version is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 1/643. Ibn Abī Shaybah 13660]"
"8770","(8767) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Warrāq narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Thawrī, from his father, who said: I heard Ibn Masʿūd saying: ""And establish the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah to the House."" Then he said: ""By Allah, were it not for the scruple that I did not hear anything from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding this, I would say that ʿUmrah is obligatory just like Ḥajj."" [Very Weak. Tafsīr al-Ṭabarī 2/212]"
"8771","(8768) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Baḥr narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated from Ashʿath, from Abū Isḥāq, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: ""You have been commanded to establish four things: establish the prayer, give the zakāh, establish the Ḥajj and the ʿUmrah to the House, and the Ḥajj is the greater Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-Akbar) and the ʿUmrah is the lesser Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-Aṣghar)."" [Munkar. al-Ṭabarānī 10/10298]"
"8772","(8769) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us; ʿAbbād ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā narrated from Dāwūd, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The ʿUmrah is obligatory, like the obligation of Ḥajj, and it is the lesser Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-Aṣghar)."" [Weak. al-Dāraquṭnī 2/285]"
"8773","(8770) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Maḥmūd al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us; Warqāʾ narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaddād, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The greater Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-Akbar) is the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr), and the lesser Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-Aṣghar) is the ʿUmrah."" And this has also been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Dāraquṭnī 2/285, Ibn Abī Shaybah 13659]"
"8774","(8771) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī informed us; Abū Saʿīd al-Khilālī informed us; Abū Yaʿlā al-Mawṣilī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz both said: al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Zuhrī narrated from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote a letter to the people of Yemen containing the obligatory duties, the Sunnah practices, and the blood-money compensations. He sent it with ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, and in it was: ""The ʿUmrah is the lesser Ḥajj (al-Ḥajj al-Aṣghar)."" [Weak. Ibn Ḥibbān 6009]"
"8775","(8772) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Muʾadhdhin informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān al-Marwazī informed us; ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Qādim narrated to us; Miṣʿar narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Mayṣarah, from Ṭāwūs, from Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Jaʿsham, who said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing in the valley, delivering a sermon, and he was saying: 'The ʿUmrah has been incorporated into the Ḥajj until the Day of Resurrection.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1217. Abū Dāwūd 179]"
"8776","(8773) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from al-Ḥakam, from ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, from Marwān al-Ḥakam, who said: ""I witnessed ʿUthmān and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with both of them, between Makkah, and ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, was prohibiting people from mutʿah (combining ʿUmrah with Ḥajj) and from combining the two. When ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, saw this, he entered into iḥrām for both simultaneously, saying: 'Labbayka bi-ʿumratin wa-ḥajjatin maʿan (Here I am for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj together).' ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Do you see me prohibiting the people from something while you do it?' He said: 'I would not abandon the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the statement of anyone among the people.'""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1577]"
"8777","(8774) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us — from al-Aʿmash.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — by dictation and by recitation — Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us — from al-Aʿmash, from Shaqīq ibn Salamah, from al-Ṣubyy ibn Maʿbad, who said:

""I was a man newly removed from the days of Jāhiliyyah and Christianity. I embraced Islam and strove, so I entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah. I set out making the talbiyah for both, and I passed by Zayd ibn Ṣūḥān and Salmān ibn Rabīʿah at al-Udhayb while I was in iḥrām for both. One of them said: 'This one is more astray than the camel of his family.' The other said: 'Both of them together?' I departed as though I were carrying both of them on my back, until I came to ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — and mentioned to him what they had said. He said: 'They have said nothing. You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ.'"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1799]"
"8778","(8775) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf said: Mālik informed us — from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — that she said:

""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever has brought a sacrificial animal with him, let him enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj along with the ʿUmrah, and let him not exit from iḥrām until he exits from both of them together.'"" And he mentioned the remainder of the ḥadīth.

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ collections from the ḥadīth of Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 2793. Muslim 1218]"
"8779","(8776) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad narrated to me: my father —

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. Isḥāq said: ""informed us,"" and the others said: ""narrated to us"" — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated to us — from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — that she said:

""We set out with the Prophet ﷺ in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage. I entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah, and I had not brought a sacrificial animal with me. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Whoever has a sacrificial animal with him, let him enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj along with his ʿUmrah, and let him not exit from iḥrām until he exits from both of them together.'""

She said: ""I began menstruating, and when the night of ʿArafah arrived, I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I had entered into iḥrām for ʿUmrah. What shall I do regarding my Ḥajj?' He said: 'Undo your hair, comb it, abandon the ʿUmrah, and enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj.'""

She said: ""When I had completed my Ḥajj, he ordered ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, and he performed an ʿUmrah for me from al-Tanʿīm in place of the ʿUmrah from which I had desisted.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq.

And in it is evidence that the Prophet ﷺ only commanded those who had a sacrificial animal with them to enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj along with the ʿUmrah. He commanded ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — to do so even though she did not have a sacrificial animal with her, out of fear that her Ḥajj might be missed. Then the Prophet ﷺ sacrificed a cow on behalf of his wives. And the ḥadīth of Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir is decisive that she was a qārinah (one who combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah), and its mention has passed above. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 8211]"
"8780","(8777) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — that she said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed a cow on behalf of his wives.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah.

And some of them said in the ḥadīth ""dhabaḥa"" (slaughtered). And ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Mājishūn said from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim in this ḥadīth: ""He offered a hady (sacrificial animal) on behalf of his wives — a cow."" And ʿAmrah said from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered a cow on behalf of his wives."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 290. Muslim 1211]"
"8781","(8778) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd al-Bayrūtī informed us: ʿUqbah ibn ʿAlqamah informed us: Yūnus ibn Yazīd al-Aylī narrated to us — from al-Zuhrī, who said:

""It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed one cow on behalf of the family of Muḥammad ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage."" ʿAmrah used to narrate it from ʿĀʾishah.

And Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated it from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered a cow on behalf of his wives."" And that is confirmed, if Allah the Most High wills. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1750. Ibn Mājah 3135]"
"8782","(8779) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq informed us: Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir say:

""The Prophet ﷺ sacrificed a cow on behalf of his wives during his Ḥajj.""

Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Muḥammad ibn Bakr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1751]"
"8783","(8780) And Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us: Abū al-Walīd Ḥassān ibn Muḥammad al-Qurashī informed us: Musaddad ibn Qaṭan informed us: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd informed us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us — from al-Awzāʿī, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said:

""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered a cow on behalf of those of his wives who had performed ʿUmrah.""

Al-Walīd ibn Muslim was alone in narrating it, and his hearing of it from al-Awzāʿī was not mentioned. Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī used to fear that he may have taken it from Yūsuf ibn al-Safar. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1751]"
"8784","(8781) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to me: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Aḥmad ibn Shuʿayb al-Faqīh in Miṣr informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maymūn al-Iskandarānī informed us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed us: al-Awzāʿī narrated to me: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me, and he mentioned it. And he said: ""In the Farewell Pilgrimage."" If his statement ""al-Awzāʿī narrated to us"" is preserved, then the ḥadīth becomes sound. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the one before it.]"
"8785","(8782) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, Abū al-Azhar, and Ḥamdān al-Sulamī said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Rāwād narrated to us — from Nāfiʿ, who said:

""Ibn ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — set out intending Ḥajj at the time when al-Ḥajjāj descended upon Ibn al-Zubayr. It was said to him: 'There is likely to be fighting among the people, and we fear that they may bar you from [reaching the House].' He said: 'Indeed, in the Messenger of Allah there is an excellent example for you.' Then he said: 'I shall do as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did. I bear witness to you that I have made ʿUmrah obligatory upon myself.' Then he set out until, when he reached the open desert (ẓahr al-Baydāʾ), he said: 'The matter of Ḥajj and ʿUmrah is but one. I bear witness to you that I have made Ḥajj obligatory upon myself along with my ʿUmrah, and I shall offer a hady (sacrificial animal) which I purchased at Qudayd.' He then proceeded until he arrived at Makkah. He performed ṭawāf of the House and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, and he did not add anything to that. He did not sacrifice, nor shave, nor shorten [his hair], nor exit from iḥrām from anything from which he had been forbidden. When the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr) arrived, he sacrificed and shaved [his head]. Then he considered that he had fulfilled his ṭawāf for both Ḥajj and ʿUmrah with his first ṭawāf. Then he said: 'Thus did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ collections from the ḥadīth of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1713. Muslim 1230]"
"8786","(8783) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Qudāmah ibn Aʿyan and ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah al-Maʿnā said: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us — from Manṣūr, from Abū Wāʾil, who said:

Al-Ṣubyy ibn Maʿbad said: ""I was a Bedouin, a Christian. I embraced Islam, and I came to a man from my clan called Hudhaym ibn Thurmulah. I said: 'O so-and-so, I am eager for jihād, and I have found Ḥajj and ʿUmrah to be incumbent upon me. How can I combine them?' He said: 'Combine them, and slaughter whatever is easy of the hady.' So I entered into iḥrām for both of them. When I reached al-Udhayb, Salmān ibn Rabīʿah and Zayd ibn Ṣūḥān met me while I was making the talbiyah for both of them together. One of them said to the other: 'This one is no more understanding than that camel of his.' It was as though a mountain had been cast upon me, until I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — and said to him: 'O Commander of the Faithful, I was a Bedouin, a Christian, and I embraced Islam. I am eager for jihād, and I have found Ḥajj and ʿUmrah to be incumbent upon me. I came to a man from my people, and he told me to combine them and to slaughter whatever is easy of the hady, and I entered into iḥrām for both of them together.' ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — said: 'You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8787","(8784) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm al-Ṣāniʿ informed us; Abū al-Muwajjih informed us; ʿAbdān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us that ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid asked Ibn ʿUmar about performing ʿUmrah before Ḥajj. He said: ""There is no harm upon anyone to perform ʿUmrah before performing Ḥajj."" ʿIkrimah said: Ibn ʿUmar said: ""The Prophet ﷺ performed ʿUmrah before performing Ḥajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 684]"
"8788","(8785) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāāq al-Muzakkī both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""We set out at the time of the new moon of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever among you wishes to assume iḥrām for ʿUmrah, let him assume iḥrām for ʿUmrah, for had I not brought the hady (sacrificial animal) with me, I would have assumed iḥrām for ʿUmrah.' Among the people were those who assumed iḥrām for ʿUmrah and those who assumed iḥrām for Ḥajj. I was among those who assumed iḥrām for ʿUmrah. I arrived in Makkah while I was menstruating. The day of ʿArafah overtook me, so I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: 'Leave your ʿUmrah, undo your hair, comb it, and assume iḥrām for Ḥajj.' When she had departed and Allah had fulfilled her Ḥajj, the Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr with her on the night of al-Ḥaṣbah. He placed her behind him on his mount, and she assumed iḥrām for ʿUmrah from al-Tanʿīm in place of her (original) ʿUmrah. Thus Allah fulfilled her ʿUmrah, and there was no hady, no fasting, and no ṣadaqah required in that regard.""

His statement ""Allah fulfilled her ʿUmrah"" is from the words of ʿUrwah. The reason no hady was required is that the Prophet ﷺ had offered the hady on her behalf and on behalf of those of his wives who performed ʿUmrah, a cow shared among them, as has been mentioned previously.

This ḥadīth was narrated by both (Bukhārī and Muslim) in the two Ṣaḥīḥs through various routes from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad, from Abū Muʿāwiyah, from Hishām.

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Abū Muʿāwiyah said — and Hannād narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah informed us — from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""We set out with the Prophet ﷺ at the time of the new moon of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us: 'Whoever among you wishes to assume iḥrām for Ḥajj, let him assume iḥrām for Ḥajj, and whoever among you wishes to assume iḥrām for ʿUmrah, let him assume iḥrām for ʿUmrah.'"" Then he mentioned the meaning of the first narration and appended the words of ʿUrwah to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1691. Muslim 1211]"
"8789","(8786) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Asad ibn Mūsá narrated to us; al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb informed us, from Abī ʿImrān, who said: ""I performed Ḥajj with my patron, so I entered upon Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: 'Shall I perform ʿUmrah before I perform Ḥajj?' She said: 'If you wish, perform ʿUmrah before you perform Ḥajj, and if you wish, after you perform Ḥajj.' I said: 'They say that whoever has never performed Ḥajj before (ṣarūrah) should not perform ʿUmrah before Ḥajj.' So I asked the Mothers of the Believers, and they said the same as she had said. I returned to her and informed her, and she said: 'Yes — and I will give you the full report: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Assume iḥrām, O Family of Muḥammad, for ʿUmrah within Ḥajj.""'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Ḥibbān 3920. Aḥmad 6/298]"
"8790","(8787) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Ḥajjāj narrated to us. Ibn Jurayj said: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrating about the Ḥajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: ""The Prophet ﷺ commanded us, after we had performed ṭawāf, to exit iḥrām."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""When you wish to depart for Minā, assume iḥrām (again)."" He said: ""So we assumed iḥrām from al-Baṭḥāʾ."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān, from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1214. Aḥmad 3/378]"
"8791","(8788) Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī al-Faqīh informed us in Baghdād, in the Mosque of al-Ruṣāfah; Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Najjād narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Abū Shihāb Mūsá ibn Nāfiʿ al-Asadī said: ""I arrived in Makkah as a mutaʿammiʿ (one performing Tamattuʿ) with ʿUmrah, three days before Tarwiyah. Some people of Makkah said to me: 'Your Ḥajj will now become Makkan (i.e., a Ḥajj of Makkah).' So I entered upon ʬAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ to seek his verdict. He said: Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me that he performed Ḥajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day he brought the sacrificial camels, and they had assumed iḥrām for Ḥajj alone (ifrād). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: 'Exit your iḥrām by performing ṭawāf of the House and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, and shorten your hair, while you are in a state of ḥalāl. When the day of Tarwiyah arrives, assume iḥrām for Ḥajj, and make that with which you have come (i.e., your ʿUmrah) a mutʿah (Tamattuʿ).' They said: 'How can we make it Tamattuʿ when we have named it Ḥajj?' He said: 'Do as I have commanded you. Had I not brought the hady, I would have done the same as I have commanded you, but nothing that is ḥarām for me will become ḥalāl until the hady reaches its place.' So they did that."" Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr, from Abī Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1493. Muslim 1216]"
"8792","(8789) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr al-Mukharramī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik informed us, from ʬAṭāʾ, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived four nights before Dhū al-Ḥijjah, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to make it an ʿUmrah. Our chests felt constrained by that and it weighed heavily upon us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'O people, exit your iḥrām. Were it not for the hady that is with me, I would do the same as you are doing.'"" He said: ""So we exited iḥrām until we were intimate with our wives and did all that a ḥalāl person does, until when it was the evening of Tarwiyah and we had placed Makkah behind us, we pronounced talbiyah for Ḥajj."" Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān, and he said ""ahlalnā"" (we assumed iḥrām). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1216. Nasāʾī 2994]"
"8793","(8790) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Saʿīd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ""The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ used to perform Tamattuʿ in the months of Ḥajj. If they did not perform Ḥajj that year, they would offer no hady at all."" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 13012]"
"8794","(8791) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Khālid ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Sulaymān al-Rāzī narrated to us; Aflaḥ ibn Ḥumayd informed us, from al-Qāsim, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ assuming iḥrām for Ḥajj in the months of Ḥajj, within the sacred precincts of Ḥajj, and on the nights of Ḥajj, until we stopped at Sarif. He went out to his Companions and said: 'Whoever among you does not have a hady with him and wishes to make it an ʿUmrah, let him do so. And whoever has a hady, he may not.' Among them were those who accepted it and those who did not, among those who did not have a hady. As for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he had a hady, as did certain of his Companions who were strong."" She said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me while I was weeping. He said: 'What is the matter with you?' I said: 'I heard your words to your Companions about ʿUmrah.' He said: 'What is wrong with you?' I said: 'I cannot pray.' He said: 'That will not harm you. Continue with your Ḥajj, and perhaps Allah will provide you with (the opportunity for) ʿUmrah. You are only a daughter of Ādam, and Allah has decreed for you what He has decreed for them (women).'"" She said: ""So I went out on my Ḥajj until we stopped at Minā, and I became pure. I performed ṭawāf of the House. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped at al-Muḥaṣṣab and called ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr and said: 'Take your sister out of the Ḥaram so that she may assume iḥrām for ʿUmrah, then perform ṭawāf of the House, and complete it, until you both come to me, for I am waiting for you here.'"" She said: ""We went out and assumed iḥrām. Then I performed ṭawāf of the House and of al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at his lodging in the middle of the night. He said: 'Have you completed?' I said: 'Yes.' He announced to his Companions the departure. He went out, passed by the House, and performed ṭawāf of it before the Ṣubḥ prayer. Then he departed for Madīnah."" Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, from Aflaḥ, and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Numayr, from Isḥāq ibn Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1696. Muslim 1211]"
"8795","(8792) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us; Hammām informed us, from Qatādah, from Anas, who informed them that the Prophet ﷺ performed four ʿUmrahs, all of them in Dhū al-Qiʿdah, except the one that was with his Ḥajj: the ʿUmrah of al-Ḥudaybiyah in Dhū al-Qiʿdah, and an ʿUmrah the following year in Dhū al-Qiʿdah, and an ʿUmrah from al-Jiʿrānah when he distributed the spoils of Ḥunayn, in Dhū al-Qiʿdah, and his ʿUmrah that was with his Ḥajj. Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hudbah ibn Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3917. Muslim 1253]"
"8796","(8793) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, from Muzāḥim, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khālid, from Muḥarrish al-Kaʿbī, that the Prophet ﷺ set out from al-Jiʿrānah at night, performed ʿUmrah, and returned by morning. [Ḥasan. Tirmidhī 935. Nasāʾī 286]"
"8797","(8794) And with his chain of narration, al-Shāfiʿī reported to us: Muslim reported to us, from Ibn Jurayj, meaning from Muzāḥim, this hadith with this chain of narration. Then Ibn Jurayj said: ""And he is Mukharriš."" Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""And Ibn Jurayj was correct, because our children say Banū Mukharriš."""
"8798","(8795) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr reported to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: Muzāḥim ibn Abī Muzāḥim informed me, from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Muḥarshi al-Kaʿbī, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) set out from al-Jiʿrānah at night, performing ʿUmrah, and entered Makkah at night, completed his ʿUmrah, and then departed during the night and reached al-Jiʿrānah by morning."" Thus he said ""Muḥarshī"" with the letter ḥāʾ, and it appears that the narration is thus. And Ibn Jurayj held the view that it is with the letter khāʾ (Muḥarrish), as in the narration of Muslim ibn Khālid from him. And Allah knows best. [Ḥasan. See before it.]"
"8799","(8796) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan reported to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah informed us that he heard ʿAmr ibn Dīnār say: ""I heard ʿAmr ibn Aws say: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded him to mount ʿĀʾishah behind him and perform ʿUmrah with her from al-Tanʿīm."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1693. Muslim 1212.]"
"8800","(8797) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed me, saying: I heard ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd say: I heard ʿAlī say, from Sufyān, from ʿAmr, and he mentioned it similarly. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Sufyān."
"8801","(8798) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓafar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī in Kūfah narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharazah narrated to us: Shihāb ibn ʿAbbād al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym, from Yūsuf ibn Māḥik, from Ḥafṣah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, from her father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān: ""Mount your sister behind you — meaning ʿĀʾishah — and perform ʿUmrah with her from al-Tanʿīm. When you descend with her at the hill, let her enter iḥrām, for it is a forthcoming ʿUmrah."" Thus I found it in the original of his book: ""mustaqbalah"" (forthcoming)."
"8802","(8799) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us. They both said: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād narrated to us: Dāwūd al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us, and he mentioned it similarly. And he said: ""For it is an accepted ʿUmrah (mutaqabbalah)."""
"8803","(8800) The eminent shaykh Abū al-Qāsim Manṣūr ibn ʿAbd al-Munʿim ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Farāwī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed me — read to him in Naysābūr, may Allah protect it — and he granted me permission for all his heard and authorized transmissions. He said: Abū al-Maʿālī Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Fārisī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed me and granted me permission for all his heard transmissions. He said: And the ḥāfiẓ Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Bayhaqī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us, and more than one of our shaykhs informed us from Abū al-Qāsim Aḥmad ibn Ṭāhir, who said: Abū Bakr al-Bayhaqī said: [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4146. Muslim 1211.] ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage. Among us were those who pronounced the talbiyah for ʿUmrah, those who pronounced it for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and those who pronounced it for Ḥajj alone. And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the talbiyah for Ḥajj. So those who pronounced the talbiyah for Ḥajj or combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah did not exit iḥrām until the Day of Sacrifice."""
"8804","(8801) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed me: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, from what he read to Mālik, from Abū al-Aswad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Nawfal, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), that she said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage. Among us were those who pronounced the talbiyah for ʿUmrah, among us were those who pronounced it for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and among us were those who pronounced it for Ḥajj. And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the talbiyah for Ḥajj. As for those who pronounced the talbiyah for Ḥajj or combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, they did not exit iḥrām until the Day of Sacrifice."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik."
"8805","(8802) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baghdādī in Naysābūr, and Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad al-Jumaḥī in Makkah, reported to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ziyād Sablān narrated to us: ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, from al-Qāsim, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: ""Among us were those who pronounced the talbiyah for Ḥajj alone, among us were those who combined (Ḥajj and ʿUmrah), and among us were those who performed tamattuʿ."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb from ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād."
"8806","(8803) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū Ṣāliḥ, Ibn Bukayr, and Muḥammad ibn Khallād narrated to us, from al-Layth: Ibn Shihāb informed me that Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿAlī al-Aslamī informed him that he heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the son of Maryam shall certainly pronounce the talbiyah at Fajj al-Rawḥāʾ as a pilgrim performing Ḥajj, or ʿUmrah, or both."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1352.]"
"8807","(8804) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān al-Adamī narrated to us in Baghdād: Muḥammad ibn Māhān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us: Mālik narrated.

And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Mālik narrated. And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Haytham al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me: my maternal uncle Mālik ibn Anas narrated.

And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated — he said: I read before Mālik, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Ḥajj Ifrād (Ḥajj alone without ʿUmrah).

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1211. Abū Dāwūd 777]"
"8808","(8805) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we mentioned nothing but Ḥajj. When we reached Sarif, I entered a state of menstruation. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me while I was weeping. He said: ""What makes you weep?"" She said: I said, ""By Allah, I wish I were not performing Ḥajj this year."" He said: ""Perhaps you have entered the state of menstruation (nufishti)."" She said: I said, ""Yes."" He said: ""Indeed, this is a matter that Allah has decreed upon the daughters of Ādam. So do what the pilgrim does, except that you do not perform ṭawāf around the House until you become pure."" When we arrived in Makkah, the Prophet ﷺ said to his Companions: ""Make it an ʿUmrah."" She said: So the people exited iḥrām, except those who had brought the hady (sacrificial animal) with them. She said: The hady was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with them both — and the one of ease (Dhu al-Yasārah). She said: Then they set out reciting the talbiyah for Ḥajj. She said: On the Day of Naḥr (Sacrifice), I became pure, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me and I performed ṭawāf al-ifāḍah. She said: We were brought the meat of a cow, and I said: ""What is this?"" They said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may Allah bless him and his family and grant them peace, offered cows on behalf of his wives."" She said: On the night of al-Ḥaṣabah, I said to the Prophet ﷺ: ""The people will return with a Ḥajj and an ʿUmrah, and I will return with only a Ḥajj."" She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, and he seated me behind him on his camel. She said: I still remember, while I was a young girl of tender age, my face touching the back of the saddle, until we reached al-Tanʿīm, where I entered iḥrām for an ʿUmrah, as compensation for the second ʿUmrah that they had performed.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah al-Mājishūn.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 299. Muslim 1211]"
"8809","(8806) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: ""Whoever among you wishes to enter iḥrām for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, let him do so, and whoever wishes to enter iḥrām for Ḥajj alone, let him do so."" ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered iḥrām for Ḥajj, and some people entered iḥrām with him for Ḥajj, some entered iḥrām for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj, and some entered iḥrām for ʿUmrah alone. And I was among those who entered iḥrām for ʿUmrah.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, and he added in it: ""And whoever wishes to enter iḥrām for ʿUmrah, let him do so.""

And we have narrated from Abī al-Aswad, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, a similar meaning.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1211]"
"8810","(8807) Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet ﷺ, in the account of the Ḥajj, that he said: ""As for me, I shall enter iḥrām for Ḥajj, for I have the hady (sacrificial animal) with me.""

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ḥammād narrated, and he mentioned it.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 778]"
"8811","(8808) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated to us: Ḥabīb, that is al-Muʿallim, narrated, from ʬAṭāʾ, who narrated to me: Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions entered iḥrām for Ḥajj, and none of them had a hady (sacrificial animal) on that day except the Prophet ￺ and Ṭalḥah, may Allah be pleased with him. ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, had arrived from Yemen and had the hady with him, and he said: ""I have entered iḥrām with the same iḥrām with which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered iḥrām."" The Prophet ﷺ commanded his Companions to make it an ʿUmrah — to perform ṭawāf, then shorten their hair, and exit iḥrām — except those who had the hady with them. They said: ""Are we to proceed to Minā while our private parts are dripping?"" This reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: ""Had I known beforehand what I came to know afterwards, I would not have brought the hady. Were it not that I have the hady with me, I would have exited iḥrām.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1693]"
"8812","(8809) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh, that is Ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, narrated to me: my father narrated, and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its chain, similar to it.

And he added: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, experienced menstruation, so she performed all the rites of Ḥajj except that she did not perform ṭawāf of the House. When she became pure, she performed ṭawāf. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will you depart having performed Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, while I depart having performed only Ḥajj?"" So he commanded ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, may Allah be pleased with him, to accompany her to al-Tanʿīm, and she performed ʿUmrah after the Ḥajj in Dhū al-Ḥijjah. And Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum, may Allah be pleased with him, met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-ʿAqabah while he was stoning it, and he said: ""Is this something specific to you, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Rather, it is for all time.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā, from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, in its full length.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous reference]"
"8813","(8810) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī from his original copy, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Bakr ibn Rajāʾ al-Adīb informed us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū ʿUmar Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated, from al-Aʿmash, from Abī Sufyān, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered iḥrām during his Ḥajj for Ḥajj alone, without ʿUmrah.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad Aḥmad 3/315. Abū Yaʿlā 1944]"
"8814","(8811) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Sarī al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Junayd narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: ʿAbbād narrated.

And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq in Naysābūr, and Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās in Makkah informed us — they said: Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Jumaḥī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ziyād narrated to us: ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād narrated, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We entered iḥrām with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for Ḥajj alone (mufradan).

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, from ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād, with this wording.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1231. Aḥmad 2/97]"
"8815","(8812) The Shaykh Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Shurayḥ narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwn narrated to us: ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād narrated, from ʿAbdullāh — or ʿUbaydullāh — ibn ʿUmar (the doubt is mine), from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered iḥrām for Ḥajj alone (mufradan).

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwn, from ʿAbbād, from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, with this wording.

And al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Tamīmī narrated it from Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī, and he said: from ʿUbaydullāh, without doubt.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous reference]"
"8816","(8813) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Narsī Aḥmad ibn ʿUbaydullāh narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated, from Ayyūb, from Abī al-ʿĀliyah al-Barrāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered iḥrām for Ḥajj, and he arrived on the fourth of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. He led us in the Ṣubḥ prayer at al-Baṭḥāʾ, then said: ""Whoever wishes to make it an ʿUmrah, let him do so.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Dīnār, from Rūḥ.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1240. Abū Dāwūd 1782]"
"8817","(8814) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to me; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī informed me; my father narrated to me; Shuʿbah narrated from Ayyūb, who said: I heard Abū al-ʿĀliyah al-Birrāʾ say that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) entered iḥrām for Ḥajj and arrived on the fourth of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. He prayed the morning prayer (Ṣubḥ) and said after praying the morning prayer: ""Whoever wishes to make it an ʿUmrah, let him make it an ʿUmrah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī.

And Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr, Abū Ghassān, similarly reported it from Shuʿbah: ""He entered iḥrām for Ḥajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the hadith before it.]"
"8818","(8815) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʔbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, who said: I heard Abū Ḥassān al-Aʿraj narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) prayed Ẓuhr at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah. Then his sacrificial camel was brought to him. He marked the right side of its hump and wiped the blood from it. Then he came to his mount and rode it. When it stood upright with him upon al-Baydāʾ, he entered iḥrām for Ḥajj.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1244.]"
"8819","(8816) Abū al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Abū Hishām narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us; Abū Ḥuṣayn narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad from his father, who said: I performed Ḥajj with Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he performed Ifrād (Ḥajj only); and with ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he performed Ifrād; and with ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him), and he performed Ifrād. [Ḍaʿīf. Dāraquṭnī 2/329.]"
"8820","(8817) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Bishrān informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed me; Shuʿayb informed us; Nāfiʿ informed us that Ibn ʿUmar used to say: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: ""If you separate between Ḥajj and ʿUmrah and perform the ʿUmrah outside the months of Ḥajj, it is more complete for your Ḥajj and more complete for your ʿUmrah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 769.]"
"8821","(8818) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū Qutaybah Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Adamī in Makkah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us; Abū Muṣʿab al-Zuhrī, Aḥmad ibn Abī Bakr, narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Rabīʿah ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from his father, from ʿAbdullāh and al-Ḥasan, the two sons of Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from their father, that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O my son, perform Ifrād (Ḥajj only), for it is more virtuous."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
"8822","(8819) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; al-Masʿūdī narrated from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, said: ""Perform Ifrād in Ḥaj"
"8823","(8820) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, who said: al-Shāfiʿī said, from Ibn ʿUlayyah, from Abū Ḥamzah — Maymūn — from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿAbdillāh, that he commanded the practice of Ifrād (performing Ḥajj alone). He said: ""Two separate rites are more beloved to me, that each one of them be accompanied by dishevelment and travel."" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14311]"
"8824","(8821) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad — Jaʿfar ibn Hārūn al-Naḥwī — in Baghdād informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ṣadaqah informed us, Khālid ibn Makhlad informed us, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed me, ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me. She said:

I heard ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with five days remaining in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, intending nothing but Ḥajj. When we drew near to Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded those who did not have a sacrificial animal with them that, upon completing the Ṭawāf of the House and the Saʿī between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, they should exit the state of Iḥrām."" ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""On the Day of Naḥr, beef was brought to us, and I said: 'What is this?' It was said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has sacrificed on behalf of his wives.'""

He said: ""I mentioned this ḥadīth to al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, and he said: 'By Allah, she has narrated the ḥadīth to you in its proper form.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Khālid ibn Makhlad, and Muslim narrated it from al-Qanʿabī from Sulaymān. Likewise, Mālik, Ibn ʿUaynah, and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1633. Muslim 1211.]"
"8825","(8822) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us, Muḥāḍir informed us, al-Aʿmash informed us, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, mentioning neither Ḥajj nor ʿUmrah. When we arrived, he commanded us to exit Iḥrām. On the night of departure, Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy experienced menstruation, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'Ḥalqī ʿAqrī — she will, I think, detain you.' He then asked: 'Did you perform Ṭawāf on the Day of Naḥr?' She said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then depart.' She said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I did not enter Iḥrām for ʿUmrah.' He said: 'Then perform ʿUmrah from al-Tanʿīm.' Her brother went with her, and we met a man at night who said: 'Your appointed place is such and such.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad — said to be Ibn Yaḥyā — from Muḥāḍir, except that he said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioning only Ḥajj."" ʿAlī ibn Mushir narrated it from al-Aʿmash with his chain, and she said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, raising the Talbiyah, mentioning neither Ḥajj nor ʿUmrah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1682.]"
"8826","(8823) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, Abū al-Walīd informed me, Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā al-Muqrīʾ informed us, Suwayd ibn Saʿīd informed us, ʿAlī ibn Mushir informed us, and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Suwayd ibn Saʿīd. And Manṣūr narrated it from Ibrāhīm, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""We saw nothing but that it was Ḥajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1211.]"
"8827","(8824) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated from Manṣūr, and he mentioned it. And al-Bukhārī and Muslim both recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ. All of that refers to a single meaning, and Allāh knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
"8828","(8825) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān informed us, Ibn Ṭāwūs, Ibrāhīh ibn Maysarah, and Hishām ibn Ḥujayr narrated to us — they heard Ṭāwūs saying: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) departed from Madīnah without designating [his intention as] Ḥajj or ʿUmrah, awaiting the divine decree. The revelation of the decree came down upon him while he was between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah. He commanded his Companions — those among them who had entered iḥrām for Ḥajj and did not have a sacrificial animal with them — to convert it into ʿUmrah. And he said: 'Had I known beforehand what I came to know afterward, I would not have brought the sacrificial animal; rather, I would have made it ʿUmrah. But I have applied talbīyah to my head and brought my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit iḥrām except at the place where my sacrificial animal is [to be offered].'"" Then Surāqah ibn Mālik (may Allāh be pleased with him) stood before him and said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, decide for us a decree as though we were born today — is this ʿUmrah of ours for this year alone, or is it forever?"" The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: ""Rather, it is forever. The ʿUmrah has been incorporated into the Ḥajj until the Day of Resurrection."" He said: Then ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) arrived from Yemen, and the Prophet (ﷺ) asked him: ""With what did you enter iḥrām?"" One of them said: ""I responded with the iḥrām of the Prophet (ﷺ),"" and the other said: ""I responded with the Ḥajj of the Prophet (ﷺ)."" [Ḍaʿīf. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 504]"
"8829","(8826) Al-Sayyid Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb al-Bazmahrānī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī, that he said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) resided in Madīnah for nine years of Ḥajj without performing Ḥajj. Then he made the announcement to the people regarding Ḥajj. A great multitude of people gathered in Madīnah. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out when five days remained in Dhū al-Qaʿdah, or four days. When he reached Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, he prayed, then mounted his mount. When it carried him into the open desert (al-Baydāʾ), he recited the talbīyah, and we entered iḥrām with no intention other than Ḥajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8830","(8827) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād and Aḥmad ibn Salamah said: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl informed us. And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — and the wording is his — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, Hishām ibn ʿAmmār, and Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dimashqī narrated — and some of them added a word or a phrase over the others. They said: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from his father, who said: ""We visited Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh. When we reached him, he asked about the people until he came to me, and I said: 'I am Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn.' He reached out with his hand to my head, unfastened my upper button, then unfastened my lower button, then placed his palm between my breasts — and I was at that time a young boy — and said: 'Welcome, O son of my brother! Ask whatever you wish.' So I asked him, while he was blind. The time for prayer came, so he stood up wrapped in a garment — meaning a cloth folded around him — and whenever he placed it upon its shoulder, its ends fell back due to its smallness. He led us in prayer while his cloak was beside him on the peg. I said: 'Inform me about the Ḥajj of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).' He gestured with his hand and counted nine, then said: 'The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) remained for nine years without performing Ḥajj. Then in the tenth year, he announced to the people that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) would perform Ḥajj. A great multitude of people came to Madīnah, all of them seeking to follow the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) and to act according to his practice. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out, and we set out with him, until we reached Dhū al-Ḥulayfah. Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays (may Allāh be pleased with her) gave birth to Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr. She sent word to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ): ""What should I do?"" He said: ""Perform ghusl, wrap yourself with a cloth [as a sanitary protection], and enter iḥrām."" The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) prayed in the mosque, then mounted al-Qaṣwāʾ, until when his she-camel stood upright with him upon the open desert — Jābir said: 'I looked as far as my sight could reach before him, from riders and pedestrians, and to his right likewise, and to his left likewise, and behind him likewise — and the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was among us, upon whom the Qurʾān was being revealed, and he knew its interpretation. Whatever he did in practice, we did likewise. He entered iḥrām with the declaration of Tawḥīd: ""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-ḥamda wa al-niʿmata laka wa al-mulka lā sharīka lak"" (Here I am, O Allāh, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours, and the dominion — You have no partner). And the people entered iḥrām with that which they used to recite, and the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) did not reject any of it from them. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) adhered to his talbīyah.' Jābir said: 'We intended nothing but Ḥajj; we did not know of ʿUmrah. When we reached the House with him, he touched the Corner [al-Rukn], then performed ramal for three circuits and walked for four. Then he advanced to the Station of Ibrāhīm and recited: ""And take the Station of Ibrāhīm as a place of prayer"" [al-Baqarah: 125]. He placed the Station between himself and the House.' He said: 'My father used to say — Ibn Nufayl and ʿUthmān said — and I do not know him as having attributed it to the Prophet (ﷺ) — Sulaymān said — and I do not know except that he said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to recite in the two rakʿahs [Sūrat] Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad and Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn. Then he returned to the House and touched the Corner, then exited through the gate toward al-Ṣafā. When he drew near to al-Ṣafā, he recited: ""Indeed, al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah are among the symbols of Allāh"" [al-Baqarah: 125]. We begin with that with which Allāh began. He began with al-Ṣafā, ascended upon it until he could see the House, then declared the greatness of Allāh alone and said: ""Lā ilāha illā Allāhu waḥdahu lā sharīka lah, lahu al-mulku wa lahu al-ḥamdu yuḥyī wa yumit wa Huwa ʿalā kulli shayʾin qadīr. Lā ilāha illā Allāhu waḥdahu, anjaza waʿdah wa naṣara ʿabdah wa hazama al-aḥzāba waḥdah"" (There is no deity except Allāh alone, He has no partner; to Him belongs the dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and causes death, and He is over all things competent. There is no deity except Allāh alone; He fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone).' Then he supplicated between that and said the like of this three times. Then he descended toward al-Marwah, until when his feet sank [into the ground], he performed ramal in the bottom of the valley, and when he ascended, he walked, until he reached al-Marwah. He did at al-Marwah the same as he had done at al-Ṣafā. When it was the final ṭawāf upon al-Marwah, he said: 'Had I known beforehand what I came to know afterward, I would not have brought the sacrificial animal, and I would have made it ʿUmrah. So whoever among you does not have a sacrificial animal, let him exit iḥrām and make it ʿUmrah.' So all the people exited iḥrām and cut their hair, except the Prophet (ﷺ) and those who had a sacrificial animal with them. Surāqah ibn Juʿshum stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, is this for this year of ours, or forever?' The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) intertwined the fingers of one hand with those of the other and said: ""The ʿUmrah has been incorporated into the Ḥajj"" — thus twice — ""Rather, forever and ever. Rather, forever and ever."" He said: ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) arrived from Yemen with the sacrificial animals of the Prophet (ﷺ). He found Fāṭimah (may Allāh be pleased with her) among those who had exited iḥrām, wearing dyed garments and having applied kohl. ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) disapproved of that and said: ""Who commanded you to do this?"" She said: ""My father."" He said: ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) used to say in Iraq: ""I went to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), angry with Fāṭimah regarding what she had done, seeking a ruling from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) concerning what she had mentioned. I informed him that I had disapproved of it upon her, and she said: 'My father commanded me to do this.' He said: 'She spoke the truth, she spoke the truth. What did you say when you entered iḥrām for Ḥajj?' He said: 'I said: O Allāh, I enter iḥrām with that with which the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) entered iḥrām.' He said: 'Then I have a sacrificial animal with me, so do not exit iḥrām.'"" He said: The total number of sacrificial animals that ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) brought from Yemen and that the Prophet (ﷺ) brought from Madīnah was one hundred. So all the people exited iḥrām and cut their hair, except the Prophet (ﷺ) and those who had a sacrificial animal with them. He said: When the Day of Tarwiyah came and they turned toward Minā, they entered iḥrām for Ḥajj. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) mounted and prayed at Minā the Ẓuhr, ʿAṣr, Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ, and Fajr prayers. Then he remained a little while until the sun rose. He commanded that a tent of hair be pitched for him at Namirah. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out, and Quraysh did not doubt that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) would stop at al-Mashʿar al-Ḥarām at al-Muzdalifah, as Quraysh used to do in the Jāhiliyyah. But the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) passed through until he reached ʿArafah. He found the tent had been pitched for him at Namirah, and he alighted in it. When the sun declined, he commanded al-Qaṣwāʾ, and it was saddled for him. He mounted until he came to the bottom of the valley and addressed the people, saying: 'Indeed, your blood and your wealth are sacred to you, as sacred as this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Behold, everything pertaining to the Jāhiliyyah is placed beneath my feet, abolished. The bloodshed of the Jāhiliyyah is abolished, and the first blood I abolish is our blood' — ʿUthmān said: 'The blood of Ibn Rabīʿah' — Sulaymān said: 'The blood of Rabīʿah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib' — and some of them said: 'He was a foster-child among Banī Saʿd, and Hudhayl killed him.' And the usury of the Jāhiliyyah is abolished, and the first usury I abolish is our usury — the usury of ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib — for it is entirely abolished. Fear Allāh regarding women, for you have taken them as a trust from Allāh, and you have made their private parts lawful by the word of Allāh. It is incumbent upon them that they do not allow anyone onto your beds whom you dislike. If they do so, then strike them without severity. And upon you is their provision and clothing in a good manner. I have left among you that which, if you hold fast to it, you will never go astray: the Book of Allāh. You will be asked about me, so what will you say?' They said: 'We bear witness that you have conveyed, fulfilled, and advised.' Then he raised his index finger, pointing it toward the sky and lowering it toward the people: 'O Allāh, bear witness! O Allāh, bear witness! O Allāh, bear witness!' Then Bilāl gave the adhān, then he gave the iqāmah, and he prayed the Ẓuhr. Then he gave the iqāmah and prayed the ʿAṣr, without praying anything between them. Then he mounted al-Qaṣwāʾ until he reached the standing place. He turned the belly of his she-camel al-Qaṣwāʾ toward the rocks, and he placed the rope of the walking path before him, and he faced the qiblah. He remained standing until the sun set and the yellow light had faded somewhat, when the disk [of the sun] had disappeared. He made Usāmah ride behind him. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out, having pulled the reins of al-Qaṣwāʾ tight so that her head would reach the saddle's wooden support, and he said with his right hand: 'Gently, O people, gently!' Whenever he came to a mountain pass, he loosened the reins a little so that she could ascend, until he reached al-Muzdalifah. There he combined the Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ prayers with one adhān and two iqāmahs — ʿUthmān said: 'And he did not glorify [perform any supererogatory prayer] between them' — then they agreed. Then the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) lay down until Fajr rose. He prayed Fajr when the morning became clear to him — Sulaymān said: 'With an adhān and an iqāmah' — then they agreed. Then he mounted al-Qaṣwāʾ until he reached al-Mashʿar al-Ḥarām. He ascended upon it — ʿUthmān and Sulaymān said: 'He faced the qiblah, praised Allāh, declared His greatness, and declared His oneness.' ʿUthmān added: 'And He declared His unity.' He remained standing until it became very bright. Then the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out before the sun rose, and he made al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās ride behind him. Al-Faḍl was a man of beautiful hair, fair-skinned and handsome. When the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out, the women passed by running, and al-Faḍl began to look at them. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) placed his hand upon al-Faḍl's face, and al-Faḍl turned his face to the other side. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) turned his face to the other side, and al-Faḍl turned his face to the other side, looking, until when he reached Muḥassir, he urged [his mount] a little, then took the middle road that leads out to the Jamrah al-Kubrā, until he reached the Jamrah that is by the tree. He pelted it with seven pebbles, declaring the takbīr with each pebble — pebbles the size of sling-stones. He pelted from the bottom of the valley. Then the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) turned to the place of sacrifice and sacrificed with his own hand sixty-three [camels], and he commanded ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) to sacrifice the remainder — meaning what was left — and he included him in his sacrificial offering. Then he commanded that a piece be taken from each camel, and it was placed in a pot and cooked. The two of them ate from its meat and drank from its broth. Then he set out — Sulaymān said: 'Then he mounted and set out' — the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) set out to the House and prayed the Ẓuhr in Makkah. Then he came to Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib while they were drawing water, and he said: 'Draw water, O Banī ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib! Were it not that the people would overwhelm you in your right of providing water, I would draw with you.' They handed him a bucket, and he drank from it."" Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, and he said: ""The blood of Ibn Rabīʿah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1218]"
"8831","(8828) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. And Abū Ḥāzim ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAbdawī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdah al-Tamīmī informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Turk said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Hushaym ibn Bashīr informed us from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb, and Ḥumayd, that they heard Anas ibn Mālik say: ""I heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) recite the talbīyah for both together: 'Labbayka ʿumratan wa ḥajjā, labbayka ʿumratan wa ḥajjā' (Here I am for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj, here I am for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj)."" Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1251]"
"8832","(8829) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī, who said: I heard Anas ibn Mālik say: ""I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) reciting the talbīyah for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj together."" He said: I mentioned that to Ibn ʿUmar, and Ibn ʿUmar said: ""He only entered iḥrām for Ḥajj alone."" He said: I met Anas and informed him of what Ibn ʿUmar had said, and he said: ""They regard us only as boys! I heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say: 'Labbayka ʿumratan wa ḥajjah.'"" Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Surayj ibn Yūnus from Hushaym, and al-Bukhārī recorded it from the ḥadīth of Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal from Ḥumayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4096. Muslim 1232]"
"8833","(8830) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed us, my father informed me, Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam and others, that a man came to Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) and said: ""With what did the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) enter iḥrām?"" Ibn ʿUmar said: ""He entered iḥrām for Ḥajj."" He departed, then came to him the following year and said: ""With what did the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) enter iḥrām?"" He said: ""Did you not come to me the first year?"" He said: ""Yes, but Anas ibn Mālik claims that he performed qirān [combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah]."" Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""Indeed, Anas ibn Mālik used to enter upon the women while they were with uncovered heads, and I was beneath the she-camel of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), its saliva touching me, and I heard him reciting the talbīyah for Ḥajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭabarānī: 274]"
"8834","(8831) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Abū Salamah Mūsā narrated to us, Wahb narrated to us, Ayyūb narrated from Abū Qilābah, from Anas, that the Prophet (ﷺ) spent the night there — meaning at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah — until morning. Then he mounted, and when his mount stood upright with him upon the open desert, he praised Allāh, glorified Him, and declared His greatness. Then he entered iḥrām for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and the people entered iḥrām for both. When we arrived, he commanded the people, and they exited iḥrām. When the Day of Tarwiyah came, they entered iḥrām for Ḥajj. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) sacrificed seven camels with his own hand, standing. Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. Wahb said this from Ayyūb. And Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, attributing that to other than the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1473]"
"8835","(8832) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us. And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr al-Qaṭrānī narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us. They said: Ḥammād narrated to us from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) prayed the Ẓuhr in Madīnah as four rakʿahs and the ʿAṣr at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah as two rakʿahs."" Anas said: ""And I heard them crying out loudly for both together — Ḥajj and ʿUmrah."" The wording is that of Sulaymān. In the narration of Abū al-Rabīʿ: ""They were crying out loudly for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim recorded it from Abū al-Rabīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1473]"
"8836","(8833) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdad, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated to us, mentioning something similar. Sulaymān said: ""Abū Qilābah heard this from Anas, and he was a faqīh. And Ḥumayd and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated from Anas, saying: 'I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) reciting the talbīyah for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj.' They did not preserve [the full narration]. What is correct is what Abū Qilābah said: that the Prophet (ﷺ) performed ifrād [Ḥajj alone]. Some of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and Anas only heard those who combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah saying this statement or something similar."" The Shaykh said: ""A group narrated it from Anas, as Yaḥyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated it, and Wahb narrated it from Ayyūb. The confusion occurred on the part of Anas, not from those below him. It is possible that he heard him (ﷺ) teaching another person how to enter iḥrām for qirān, not that he himself entered iḥrām for both. And Allāh knows best. It has also been narrated from another chain from Anas."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1473. Muslim 690]"
"8837","(8834) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us that Abu al-Nadhir, the faqih, informed us that 'Uthman bin Sa'id and Hasan bin Sufyan both narrated to us that Hudbah bin Khalid narrated to us. And Abu 'Amr, Muhammad bin 'Abd Allah, the literary scholar, informed us that Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us that Ibrahim bin Hashim informed us, and Abu Ya'la and al-Hasan informed me, all saying: Hudbah bin Khalid narrated to us that Hammam narrated to us that Qatadah narrated to us that Anas bin Malik informed him that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ performed four 'Umrahs, all of them in Dhu al-Qi'dah, except the 'Umrah that was with his Hajj — the 'Umrah of Hudaybiyyah, or at the time of Hudaybiyyah in Dhu al-Qi'dah, and an 'Umrah the following year in Dhu al-Qi'dah, and an 'Umrah from al-Ji'ranah, where he distributed the war gains of Hunayn, also in Dhu al-Qi'dah, and an 'Umrah with his Hajj.

Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their Sahihs from Hudbah bin Khalid. Anas only says this based on what he had, that he had combined [Hajj and 'Umrah].

It has also been narrated from other than Anas bin Malik, but its authenticity is questionable. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 1688. Muslim 1253]"
"8838","(8835) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Muhammad bin Bakr informed us that Abu Dawud informed us that al-Nufayli informed us that Zuhayr informed us that Abu Ishaq narrated from Mujahid, who said: Ibn 'Umar was asked, ""How many times did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform 'Umrah?"" He said, ""Twice."" Then 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""Ibn 'Umar certainly knows that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed three, besides the one he combined with the Farewell Pilgrimage.""

This is how Abu Ishaq narrated it from Mujahid. The second narration, from Mansur from Mujahid, does not contain this. [Munkar. Abu Dawud 1992]"
"8839","(8836) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us that Abu al-Fadl, Muhammad bin Ibrahim al-Muzakki, informed us that Ahmad bin Salamah informed us that Qutaybah bin Sa'id and Ishaq bin Ibrahim informed us — Qutaybah said: narrated to us; and Ishaq said: informed us — that Jarir narrated from Mansur from Mujahid, who said: I, along with 'Urwah bin al-Zubayr, entered the mosque and found 'Abd Allah bin 'Umar sitting by the chamber of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and there were people in the mosque praying the Duha prayer. We asked him about their prayer, and he said, ""An innovation."" Then they said to him, ""How many times did the Prophet ﷺ perform 'Umrah?"" He said, ""Four, one of them in Rajab."" We disliked that we should contradict him and refute him. Then we heard the sound of 'A'ishah, the Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, brushing her teeth behind the chamber. 'Urwah said, ""O Mother, have you not heard what Abu 'Abd al-Rahman is saying?"" She said, ""What is he saying?"" He said, ""He says that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed four 'Umrahs, one of them in Rajab."" She said, ""May Allah have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not perform any 'Umrah except that he was present [i.e., she was with him], and he never performed 'Umrah in Rajab.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah, and Muslim narrated it from Ishaq bin Ibrahim. And 'Urwah bin al-Zubayr similarly narrated it from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in this story, but it does not contain what is in the narration of Abu Ishaq. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 1685. Muslim 1225]"
"8840","(8837) 'Ali bin Muhammad bin 'Abd Allah bin Bishran in Baghdad informed us that Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us that Yahya bin Ja'far informed us that al-Dahhak bin Makhlad informed us that Ibn Jurayj informed us from 'Ata' who narrated to me that 'Urwah said: I and Ibn 'Umar were leaning against the chamber of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and I could hear the sound of the tooth-stick being brushed. I said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed 'Umrah in Rajab."" He said, ""Yes."" I said, ""O Mother, do you not hear what Abu 'Abd al-Rahman is saying? He says the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed 'Umrah in Rajab."" She said, ""May Allah have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not perform any 'Umrah except that Abu 'Abd al-Rahman was with him. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never performed 'Umrah in Rajab.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu 'Asim al-Dahhak, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 4007. Muslim 1255]"
"8841","(8838) And Abu al-Hasan, 'Ali bin Muhammad al-Muqri', informed us that al-Hasan bin Muhammad bin Ishaq informed us that Yusuf bin Ya'qub informed that 'Abd al-A'la bin Hammad informed us that Dawud al-'Attar narrated from Hisham bin 'Urwah from his father from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet ﷺ performed two 'Umrahs in Dhu al-Qi'dah and one 'Umrah in Shawwal. [Munkar. Abu Dawud 1991]"
"8842","(8839) And Malik bin Anas narrated from Hisham bin 'Urwah from his father that the Prophet ﷺ only performed three 'Umrahs: one in Shawwal and two in Dhu al-Qi'dah.

Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us that Muhammad bin Ja'far al-Muzakki informed us that Muhammad bin Ibrahim informed us that Ibn Bukayr informed us that Malik mentioned it.

And it has also been narrated from Abu Ishaq from al-Bara' bin 'Azib, but it is not preserved. [Da'if. Malik 759]"
"8843","(8840) Abu al-Husayn bin Bishran informed us that 'Ali bin Muhammad al-Misri informed us that Abu Ghassan, Malik bin Yahya, informed us that Yazid bin Harun informed us that Zakariyya bin Abu Zaidah informed us that Abu Ishaq narrated from al-Bara' bin 'Azib, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed three 'Umrahs, all of them in Dhu al-Qi'dah. Then 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: ""He certainly knows that he performed four 'Umrahs, including the one with which he performed Hajj.""

And it has been narrated in the hadith of Jabir bin 'Abd Allah, but it is not authentic. [Munkar. Tirmidhi 22/291]"
"8844","(8841) Abu Bakr bin al-Harith al-Faqih al-Asbahani informed us that 'Ali bin 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr bin Abu Dawud, Muhammad bin Ja'far bin Rumays, and al-Qasim bin Isma'il — Abu 'Ubayd — and 'Uthman bin Ja'far al-Laban and others all said: Ahmad bin Yahya al-Sufi narrated to us that Zayd bin al-Hubab narrated to us that Sufyan al-Thawri narrated from Ja'far bin Muhammad from his father from Jabir bin 'Abd Allah, who said: The Prophet ﷺ performed three Hajjs: two Hajjs before he migrated, and one Hajj in which he combined an 'Umrah.

How can this be correct when we have narrated from various sources regarding the Prophet's ﷺ ihram something contrary to this?

Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi said: I asked Muhammad bin Isma'il al-Bukhari about this hadith, and he said: ""This is an erroneous hadith. This was only narrated from al-Thawri as a mursal [report].""

Al-Bukhari said: Zayd bin al-Hubab, when he narrated from memory, would sometimes err in something.

The Sheikh said: And it has been narrated in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas, but it is not preserved. [Sahih. Tirmidhi 810. Ibn Khuzaymah 3056]"
"8845","(8842) 'Ali bin Ahmad bin 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad bin 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us that Isma'il bin Ishaq informed us that Ahmad bin 'Abd Allah bin Yunus informed us that Dawud bin 'Abd al-Rahman al-'Attar —

And Abu Nasr, 'Umar bin 'Abd al-'Aziz bin Qatadah, informed us that Abu 'Ali, Hamid bin Muhammad al-Rafa', informed us that 'Ali bin 'Abd al-'Aziz informed us that al-Hasan bin al-Rabi' and Shihab bin 'Abbad both said: Dawud bin 'Abd al-Rahman narrated from 'Amr bin Dinar from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed four 'Umrahs: the 'Umrah of Hudaybiyyah, the 'Umrah of Qada' the following year, his 'Umrah from al-Ji'ranah, and his fourth 'Umrah that was with his Hajj.

Abu al-Hasan, meaning 'Ali bin 'Abd al-'Aziz, said: No one narrates this hadith from Ibn 'Abbas except Dawud bin 'Abd al-Rahman.

The Sheikh said: Sufyan bin 'Uyunah narrated from 'Amr bin Dinar from 'Ikrimah that the Prophet ﷺ performed 'Umrah — as a mursal [report].

Al-Bukhari said: Dawud bin 'Abd al-Rahman is truthful (saduq), except that he sometimes errs in something. [Munkar. Tirmidhi 816. Abu Dawud 1993]"
"8846","(8843) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, in his last days — they said: Abu al-'Abbas, Muhammad bin Ya'qub, informed us that al-Rabi' bin Sulayman informed us that al-Shafi'i informed us that Malik informed us —

And Muhammad bin 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us that Abu Ahmad, Bakr bin Muhammad bin Hamdan al-Sayrafi in Marw, informed us that 'Abd al-Samad bin al-Fadl informed us that Khalid bin Makhlad informed us that Malik bin Anas narrated from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar from Hafsah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that she said to the Prophet ﷺ: ""What is the matter with the people that they have exited ihram while you have not exited ihram from your 'Umrah?"" He said: ""I have matted my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter [the animal].""

This is the wording of Khalid's hadith. And in the narration of al-Shafi'i from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with the people that they have exited ihram with an 'Umrah while you have not exited ihram from your 'Umrah?""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd Allah bin Yusuf and others from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad bin 'Abd Allah bin Numayr from Khalid bin Makhlad. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 4137]"
"8847","(8844) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-Muthannā informed us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated from ʿUbaydillāh, Nāfiʿ narrated to me from ʿAbdillāh, from Ḥafṣah, who said: I said to the Prophet ﷺ: ""What is the matter with the people that they have exited iḥrām while you have not exited iḥrām from your ʿUmrah?"" He said: ""Indeed, I have garlanded my sacrificial animal and applied talcum to my head, so I shall not exit iḥrām until I exit iḥrām from the Ḥajj.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
"8848","(8845) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ said:

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said regarding Ḥafṣah's statement to the Prophet ﷺ: ""And you have not exited iḥrām from your ʿUmrah"" — she meant your iḥrām with which you began — and they (the people) were with a single intention, and Allah knows best. He said: ""I have applied talcum to my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit iḥrām until I slaughter (my sacrificial animal)."" He meant — and Allah knows best — until the pilgrim exits iḥrām, because the divine decree came down upon him that he should make the iḥrām of the one who has a sacrificial animal with him into Ḥajj. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8849","(8846) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb al-Bazmahrānī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to me, my father narrated to me, Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭaḥmān narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he informed him that Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, informed him: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to exit iḥrām during the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage. So Ḥafṣah said to him: ""What prevents you, O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, from exiting iḥrām?"" He said: ""Indeed, I have applied talcum to my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, and I shall not exit iḥrām until I slaughter my sacrificial animal.""

And likewise Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated it from Nāfiʿ, but he did not mention al-ʿUmrah in it, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It has just been mentioned above.]"
"8850","(8847) Abū al-Ḥasan: ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Muqriʾ in Baghdād informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salmān narrated to us, it was recited to ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad while I was listening, he said: Abū Zayd al-Harawī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me, ʿIkrimah narrated to me, Ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāl, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to me while I was in al-ʿAqīq and said: 'Pray two rakʿahs in this blessed valley and say: ʿUmrah within Ḥajj, for indeed the ʿUmrah has been incorporated into the Ḥajj until the Day of Resurrection.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Zayd al-Harawī — thus ʿAlī ibn al-Mubārak narrated it from Yaḥyā. And al-Awzāʿī contradicted him in most narrations from him, and he said: ""And he said: ʿUmrah within Ḥajj"" — he did not say ""and say."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1461.]"
"8851","(8848) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdullāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to me, al-Awzāʿī narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me, ʿIkrimah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, narrated to me, Ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāl, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to me, saying: ""A visitor came to me from my Lord while I was in al-ʿAqīq, saying: 'Pray in this blessed valley, and say: ʿUmrah within Ḥajj.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
"8852","(8849) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us, Abū Muḥammad Daʿlaj ibn Aḥmad ibn Daʿlaj narrated to us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim and Bishr ibn Bakr said: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me, ʿIkrimah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, narrated to me that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās saying: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāl, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying while he was in the valley of al-ʿAqīq: ""A visitor came to me from my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, and said: 'Pray in this blessed valley, and he said: ʿUmrah within Ḥajj.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī.

And likewise Shuʿayb ibn Isḥāq and Miskīn ibn Bukayr narrated it from al-Awzāʿī, and he said: ""ʿUmrah within Ḥajj.""

So that would be a permission — and Allah knows best — for incorporating the ʿUmrah upon the Ḥajj, not that he, peace be upon him, commanded the Prophet ﷺ to do so in his own case. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
"8853","(8850) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to me, Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl al-ʿAdawī narrated to me: I heard Muṭarrif ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr narrating from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, who said: He said to me: ""Shall I not narrate to you a ḥadīth by which Allah may benefit you? Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah, and he did not forbid it, and no Qurʾān was revealed prohibiting it. And indeed, the angels used to greet me with salām, but when I was cauterized, it ceased from me, and when I abandoned it, it returned to me"" — meaning the angels.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah.

And with this meaning, Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated it from Qatādah from Muṭarrif, and Hammām narrated it from Qatādah regarding al-mutʿah, and likewise Muḥammad ibn Wāsiʿ from Muṭarrif regarding al-mutʿah, and likewise Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī from ʿImrān regarding al-mutʿah. And in the narration of Abū al-ʿAlāʾ from Muṭarrif from ʿImrān, he said: ""Know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ʿUmrah for a group of his family during the last ten days (of Dhū al-Ḥijjah).""

His intention in all of that was to demonstrate the permissibility of ʿUmrah during the months of Ḥajj. And his statement ""he combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah"" — if the narrator preserved it — may mean that his intention thereby is his permission regarding it and his command to some of his Companions to do so, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1226. Aḥmad 4/27.]"
"8854","(8851) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn narrated to us, Ḥajjāj narrated to us, Yūnus narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, who said: I was with ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed him over Yemen. He mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the arrival of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. ʿAlī said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: ""How did you do?"" I said: ""I entered iḥrām with the same iḥrām as the Prophet ﷺ."" He said: ""Indeed, I have brought the sacrificial animal and I have performed Qirān (combined Ḥajj and ʿUmrah).""

Thus it is in this narration: ""and I have performed Qirān"" — and this is not in the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh when he described the arrival of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and his iḥrām. And the ḥadīth of Jābir has a more sound chain and a more excellent context. Along with the ḥadīth of Jābir is the ḥadīth of Anas ibn Mālik. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1797. Nasāʾī 2725.]"
"8855","(8852) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him, Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Maḥmūd al-Saʿdī in Marw narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us, Salīm ibn Ḥayyān narrated to me: I heard Marwān al-Aṣfar narrating from Anas ibn Mālik that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Prophet ﷺ from Yemen. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: ""With what did you enter iḥrām?"" He said: ""I entered iḥrām with the same iḥrām as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Were it not that I have the sacrificial animal with me, I would have exited iḥrām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1483.]

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Khallāl, and Muslim narrated it from al-Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir, both from ʿAbd al-Ṣamad.

And in this ḥadīth and in the ḥadīth of Jābir is the attribution of the reason for his refraining from exiting iḥrām to the presence of the sacrificial animal with him. And the one performing Qirān does not exit iḥrām from his iḥrām until he exits from both of them together, whether he has a sacrificial animal with him or not. And that indicates the error of that wording, and Allah knows best."
"8856","(8853) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī from his original copy informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā ibn Asad narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAbdah, I heard Abū Wāʾil saying: Often would I and Masrūq go to al-Ṣubayy ibn Maʿbad to ask him about this ḥadīth. He was a Christian man from Banī Taghlib who then embraced Islam and entered iḥrām for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah. So Salmān ibn Rabīʿah and Zayyād ibn Ṣūḥān heard him while he was calling the talbiyah for Ḥajj and ʿUmrah in al-Qādisiyyah, and they said: ""This one is more astray than the camel of his family."" He said: It was as though a mountain had been placed upon me because of their words, until I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāl, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned that to him. He turned to them and rebuked them, then turned to me and said: ""You have been guided to the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ.""

And this ḥadīth indicates the permissibility of Qirān and that it is not misguidance, contrary to what Zayyād ibn Ṣūḥān and Salmān ibn Rabīʿah imagined — not that it is superior to other forms. And ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded that Ḥajj and ʿUmrah be separated. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8857","(8854) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us. And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah ibn Qaʿnab, Ibn Bukayr, and ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah — all from Mālik, from Ibn Shihāb, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, that he narrated to him: that he heard Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and al-Ḍaḥḥāq ibn Qays, during the year of the Ḥajj of Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, mentioning tamattuʿ (combining ʿUmrah with Ḥajj). Al-Ḍaḥḥāq said: ""No one does that except one who is ignorant of the command of Allah."" Saʿd said: ""Evil is what you have said, O my nephew."" Al-Ḍaḥḥāq said: ""Indeed, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) used to forbid it."" Saʿd said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did it, and we did it with him."" Thus it is in this narration: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did it.""

And in the established narrations from Ghunaym ibn Qays from Saʿd in this hadith: ""We did it"" — there is no mention of the action of the Prophet (ﷺ). And Allah knows best. [Weak. Tirmidhī 823. Ibn Ḥibbān 3923]"
"8858","(8855) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed him: ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq ibn al-Khurāsānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: Shuʿbah informed us: from Sulaymān al-Taymī, who said: I heard Ghunaym ibn Qays say: I asked Saʿd ibn Mālik about al-mutʿah (tamattuʿ), and he said: ""We did it, and that one (meaning Muʿāwiyah) was at that time a disbeliever in al-ʿUrush."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Khalaf from Rūḥ. Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ intended by what he said Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, and by al-ʿUrush he meant the houses of Makkah, and this is clear in the narration of Marwān al-Fazārī from al-Taymī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1225. Musnad Aḥmad 1/181]"
"8859","(8856) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn al-Taymī (meaning al-Muʿtamir) and Ibn al-Mubārak — both said: Sulaymān al-Taymī informed us: Ghunaym ibn Qays informed me, saying: I asked Saʿd ibn Mālik about tamattuʿ (combining ʿUmrah with Ḥajj), and he said: ""I did it with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and that one was at that time a disbeliever in al-ʿUrush"" — meaning Makkah, and by it he meant Muʿāwiyah."
"8860","(8857) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ibn Milḥān informed us: Yaḥyā informed me: al-Layth informed me from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed tamattuʿ during the Farewell Pilgrimage, combining ʿUmrah with Ḥajj, and he offered a sacrifice, so he brought the hady (sacrificial animal) with him from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) began by entering into iḥrām for ʿUmrah, then entered into iḥrām for Ḥajj. The people performed tamattuʿ with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), combining ʿUmrah with Ḥajj. Among the people were those who offered a sacrifice and brought the hady with them, and among them were those who did not offer a sacrifice. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived in Makkah, he said to the people: ""Whoever has a hady with him, he shall not become permissible (i.e., exit iḥrām) from anything that has been forbidden to him until he completes his Ḥajj. And whoever among you has not offered a sacrifice, let him perform ṭawāf around the Bayt, and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, and let him exit iḥrām, then let him enter into iḥrām for Ḥajj and offer a sacrifice. And whoever cannot find a hady, let him fast three days during the Ḥajj and seven days when he returns to his family.""

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed ṭawāf when he arrived in Makkah. He first touched the Rukn (Black Stone), then jogged three circuits out of the seven, and walked four circuits. Then, when he completed his ṭawāf around the Bayt, he prayed two rakʿahs near the Maqām, then gave salām and departed. He went to al-Ṣafā and performed ṭawāf between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah seven circuits. Then he did not exit iḥrā"
"8861","(8858) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muhājir informed us, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth informed me, my father informed me, my grandfather informed me, ʿUqayl ibn Khālid informed me, and he narrated the ḥadīth similarly, except that he said: ""Let him perform Ṭawāf of the House and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, let him shorten [his hair], and let him exit Iḥrām.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb."
"8862","(8859) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Ibn Bukayr informed me, al-Layth informed me. And Muḥammad informed us, Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyīnī informed us, Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUqayl informed us, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me, my father informed me, my grandfather informed me, ʿUqayl informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, that ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, informed him from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding his Tamattuʿ (combining Ḥajj with ʿUmrah) and the people's Tamattuʿ with him, similar to what Sālim ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to me from ʿAbdillāh from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. This is the wording of Bishr's ḥadīth.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr with the wording that preceded, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik with this wording.

We have also narrated from Ibn ʿUmar and ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the Ifrād (sole Ḥajj) of the Prophet ﷺ something that contradicts this. Since he did not exit Iḥrām until he had completed his Ḥajj in this narration as well, this also indicates that he was not a Mutamattiʿ (one performing Tamattuʿ). And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
"8863","(8860) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh informed us, my father informed us, Shuʿbah informed us, from Muslim al-Qurrīʾ, who heard Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, say: ""The Prophet ﷺ entered Iḥrām for ʿUmrah, and his Companions entered Iḥrām for Ḥajj. The Prophet ﷺ did not exit Iḥrām, nor did those of his Companions who had brought the sacrificial animal. The rest of them exited Iḥrām."" Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, was among those who had brought the sacrificial animal and did not exit Iḥrām.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh, and he also narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār from Ghundar from Shuʿbah, except that Ghundar differed with Muʿādh regarding Ṭalḥah, and said: ""Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh and another man were among those who did not have a sacrificial animal with them, and they both exited Iḥrām."" Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah and Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī also differed from them regarding the Ihlāl. As for the ḥadīth of Rūḥ: [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1239.]"
"8864","(8861) Abū Naṣr — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bazzāz — in Ṭabarān informed us, ʿAbdillāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh informed us, Rūḥ informed us. And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbaydillāh al-Narsī informed us, Rūḥ informed us, Shuʿbah informed us. He said: I heard Muslim al-Qurrīʾ say: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions entered Iḥrām for Ḥajj. Those who did not bring a sacrificial animal exited Iḥrām, and Ṭalḥah and so-and-so did not bring a sacrificial animal, so they exited Iḥrām.""

As for the ḥadīth of Abū Dāwūd: [Musnad Aḥmad 1/240.]"
"8865","(8862) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Muslim al-Qurrī, who said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) entered iḥrām for Ḥajj, and those of his Companions who did not have a sacrificial animal with them exited the state of iḥrām, while those who had a sacrificial animal with them did not exit the state of iḥrām. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and Ṭalḥah were among those who had a sacrificial animal with them.""

And the statement of the one who said that he entered iḥrām for Ḥajj is perhaps more consistent with the narration of Abū al-ʿĀliyah, al-Barāʾ, and Abū Ḥassān al-Aʿraj from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him) iḥrām for Ḥajj. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīh. Musnad Ṭayālisī 3763. Ṭaḥāwī 2/141]"
"8866","(8863) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated — and Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us therein that ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us that Rūḥ narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated that al-Ḥakam narrated from Mujāhid from Ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""This is an ʿUmrah from which we have derived benefit. So whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him exit the state of iḥrām completely, for the ʿUmrah has been incorporated into the Ḥajj until the Day of Resurrection.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar and Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh from Shuʿbah. And it is as though he meant — and Allah knows best — his Companions who exited the state of iḥrām and derived benefit (tamattuʿ). And it is established from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he expressed regret when he brought the sacrificial animal and did not exit the state of iḥrām. Had he been a mutamattiʿ (one performing tamattuʿ) with ʿUmrah leading into Ḥajj, he would not have expressed regret over it. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1241]"
"8867","(8864) Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā — informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf — narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad — narrated to us: Musaddad — narrated to us: Yaḥyā — narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj — informed me: ʿAṭāʾ — I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, among a group of people with me, say: ""We, the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), entered iḥrām for Hajj alone, purely and exclusively. The Prophet (ﷺ) arrived on the morning of the fourth day of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. After his arrival, he commanded us to exit iḥrām. He said: 'Exit iḥrām and be intimate with your women.'"" ʿAṭāʾ said: ""He did not make it obligatory upon them to be intimate with their women; rather, he made it permissible for them."" ʿAṭāʾ said: Jābir said: ""Word reached him (the Prophet ﷺ) from us that we were saying: 'There are only five days between us and ʿArafah, and he has commanded us to exit iḥrām and go to our women — shall we then come to ʿArafah with our private parts dripping with semen?'"" Jābir gestured with his hand, and it was as though I could see him moving his hand. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) stood among us and said: 'You know that I am the most God-fearing among you, the most truthful among you, and the most righteous among you. Had I not brought the sacrificial animal with me, I would have exited iḥrām just as you have exited iḥrām. And had I known beforehand what I came to know later, I would not have brought the sacrificial animal. So exit iḥrām.'"" He said: ""So we exited iḥrām, and we heard and obeyed."" Jābir said: ""Then ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived from his duties, and the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: 'With what did you enter iḥrām?' He said: 'With the same iḥrām with which the Prophet (ﷺ) entered iḥrām.' He said: 'Then offer a sacrificial animal and remain in iḥrām.' So ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) offered a sacrificial animal on his behalf."" Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum said: ""Is this concession of ours, O Messenger of Allah, for this year only, or forever?"" He said: ""Rather, forever.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān, and al-Bukhārī narrated it in abridged form from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj and from the ḥadīth of Ḥabīb al-Muʿallim from ʿAṭāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1216. Bukhārī 6933.]"
"8868","(8865) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us in it: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh — narrated to us: Rūḥ — narrated to us: Shuʿbah — from al-Ḥakam — he said: I heard ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn — from Dhakwān, the freed slave of ʿĀʾishah — from ʿĀʾishah, that she said: ""We arrived with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when four or five nights had passed of Dhū al-Ḥijjah. She said: One day he came to me while he was angry. I said: 'Who has angered you, O Messenger of Allah? May Allah admit him to the Fire.' He said: 'Are you not aware that I commanded the people with a matter, and yet they are hesitating in it?'"" Al-Ḥakam said: ""It is as though they were afraid — I think he said: 'And had I known beforehand what I came to know later, I would not have brought the sacrificial animal until I had purchased it, and then I would have exited iḥrām just as they exited iḥrām.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar and Muʿādh from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1492. Muslim 1242.]"
"8869","(8866) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn — narrated to us: Ādam — narrated to us: Shuʿbah — narrated to us: Abū Jamrah — he said: ""I performed tamattuʿ (Hajj al-Tamattuʿ), and some people forbade me from it, so I asked Ibn ʿAbbās, and he commanded me to do it. Then I saw in a dream as though a man was saying to me: 'An accepted Hajj and an accepted ʿUmrah.' So I informed Ibn ʿAbbās, and he said: 'Allāhu Akbar! This is the Sunnah of the Prophet (ﷺ).'"" Abū Jamrah said: Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""Stay with me, and I will allocate a share of my wealth for you."" Shuʿbah said: ""I asked him: 'Why did he say that to you?' He said: 'Because of the dream that I saw.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 11211.]"
"8870","(8867) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī — narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad — narrated to us: Musaddad — narrated to us: Yaḥyā — from ʿImrān Abī Bakr — narrated to us: Abū Rajāʾ — from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn — he said: ""The verse of mutʿah (Hajj al-Tamattuʿ) was revealed in the Book of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and we performed it with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. ʿImrān is Ibn Muslim al-Qaṣīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4246. Muslim 1226.]"
"8871","(8868) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah — narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd — narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ — narrated to us: Ibn Wahb — informed me: Ḥaywah — informed me: Abū ʿĪsā, a Khurāsānī — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Qāsim al-Khurāsānī — from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib — that a man from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and testified before him that he had heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), during the illness in which he passed away, forbid performing ʿUmrah before Hajj. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd 1763.]"
"8872","(8869) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar — narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb — narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī — narrated to us: Hishām — from Qatādah — from Abī Shaykh al-Hunāʾī, whose name is Ḥaywān ibn Khālid — that Muʿāwiyah said to a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade the wearing of gold except in a cut (fragmented) form."" They said: ""Yes, by Allah."" He said: ""And I bear witness."" He said: ""Do you know that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade wearing gold except in a cut form?"" They said: ""Yes, by Allah."" He said: ""Do you know that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade combining Hajj and ʿUmrah (i.e., performing them together as Qirān)?"" They said: ""No, by Allah."" He said: ""By Allah, it is indeed among those things (that are forbidden).""

Similarly, Ḥammād ibn Salamah and al-Ashʿath ibn Barāz narrated it from Qatādah, and Ḥammād ibn Salamah in his ḥadīth said: ""But you have forgotten."" And Maṭar al-Warrāq narrated it from Abī Shaykh regarding mutʿah of Hajj. [Ḍaʿīf. Musnad Aḥmad 4/92.]"
"8873","(8870) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Marzūq — informed us: Hammām — from Qatādah — from Muṭarrif — from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn — he said: ""We performed tamattuʿ with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the Qurʾān was revealed concerning it. So let a man say what he wills according to his own opinion.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hammām ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1496. Muslim 1126.]"
"8874","(8871) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdad — informed us: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār — narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr — narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq — informed us: al-Thawrī — narrated to us: Qays ibn Muslim — from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb — from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī — he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me to the land of my people. When the time for Hajj arrived, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed Hajj, and I performed Hajj as well. I came to him while he was staying in al-Abṭaḥ. He said to me: 'With what did you enter iḥrām, O ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qays?' I said: 'Here I am, with a Hajj like the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' He said: 'You have done well.' Then he said to me: 'Did you bring a sacrificial animal?' I said: 'No.' He said: 'Then go and perform ṭawāf of the House, and perform saʿī between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, then exit iḥrām.'"" He said: ""So I went and did as he commanded me. Then I came to a woman from my people, who washed my head with sidr (lote tree leaves) and combed it. Then I entered iḥrām for Hajj on the day of Tarwiyah. I continued to give the people the same fatwā that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had commanded me with during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until he passed away, and during the time of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and the early part of the caliphate of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). While I was at the Black Stone or the Station of Ibrāhīm, giving the people the fatwā that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had commanded me with, a man came to me and whispered to me, saying: 'Do not be hasty in your fatwā, for the Commander of the Faithful has introduced something new in the rites' — meaning — so I said: 'O people, whoever we have given a fatwā regarding anything, let him hold back, for the Commander of the Faithful is coming, so follow him.' He said: ""When ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) arrived, I went to him and said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, have you introduced something new in the rites?' He said: 'Yes. If we follow the Sunnah of our Prophet (ﷺ), then he did not exit iḥrām until he sacrificed the animal. And if we follow the Book of our Lord, then it commands us to complete (both Hajj and ʿUmrah).'""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Sufyān al-Thawrī and others from Qays. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4089. Muslim 1221.]"
"8878","(8875) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Sālim, who said:

Ibn ʿUmar was asked about mutʿat al-Ḥajj (the enjoyment of combining ʿUmrah and Ḥajj), and he commanded it. It was said to him: ""You are contradicting your father."" He said: ""My father did not say what you are claiming. He only said: 'Separate the ʿUmrah from the Ḥajj' — meaning that the ʿUmrah is not completed in the months of Ḥajj except with a sacrificial animal (hadī). He desired that the House (al-Bayt) be visited outside the months of Ḥajj. But you have made it unlawful and have punished the people for it, whereas Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has made it lawful, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ practiced it."" When they persisted in arguing with him, he said: ""Is the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, more deserving of being followed, or ʿUmar?""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Amālī al-Ṣaḥābah 142. Tadhkirat al-Fāẓ 3/8005]"
"8879","(8876) And Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭabarānī informed us therein: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us: Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar narrated to us: Ibn Shihāb narrated from Sālim, who said:

ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to issue fatwā based on what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had revealed regarding the permissibility of tamattuʿ (combining ʿUmrah and Ḥajj), and the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding it. People would say to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿU"
"8880","(8877) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓafar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī, Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us that Bishr ibn Bakr narrated from al-Awzāʿī that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr narrated to me from his father, who said: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Have you prohibited mutʿah?"" He said: ""No, but I desired that visits to the House be frequent."" ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) then said: ""Whoever performs Ḥajj alone, it is good, and whoever performs tamattuʿ has indeed adhered to the Book of Allah and the Sunnah of His Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
"8881","(8878) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, who said: I heard Abā Naḍrah say: I said to Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh: ""Indeed Ibn al-Zubayr prohibits mutʿah, while Ibn ʿAbbās commands it."" Jābir said: ""The matter revolves before our eyes — we performed tamattuʿ during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). Then when ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) came, he addressed the people and said: 'Indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, used to make lawful for His Prophet (peace be upon him) whatever He willed, and the Qurʾān has been revealed in its proper manner. So separate your Ḥajj from your ʿUmrah, and be done with the marriage of these women (mutʿah marriage). I shall not be brought a man who has married a woman for a specified period without stoning him.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah. And Hammām narrated it from Qatādah and added in the ḥadīth: ""For that is more complete for your Ḥajj and more complete for your ʿUmrah.""

And in this there is evidence that the prohibition of mutʿah of Ḥajj was for the reason that he clarified in the ḥadīth before it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1217]"
"8882","(8879) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us therein that ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us that Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Muslim al-Qurrī, who said: I asked Ibn ʿAbbās about mutʿah of Ḥajj, and he granted permission regarding it, while Ibn al-Zubayr used to prohibit it. He said: ""This is the mother of Ibn al-Zubayr narrating that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) granted permission regarding it. So go to her and ask her."" He said: ""So we went to her, and she was a large, blind woman, and she said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) indeed granted permission regarding it.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1238]"
"8883","(8880) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us that ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us that Shuʿbah narrated from al-Ḥakam from ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn from Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, who said: I heard ʿUthmān and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) between Makkah and Madīnah, and ʿUthmān was prohibiting mutʿah and combining the two (Ḥajj and ʿUmrah). When ʿAlī saw that, he entered iḥrām for both together, saying: ""Labbayka ʿumratan wa ḥajjatan maʿan (Here I am for ʿUmrah and Ḥajj together)."" ʿUthmān said: ""Do you see me prohibiting the people from something and you do it yourself?"" He said: ""I would not abandon the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) for the statement of any person among the people.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1488]"
"8884","(8881) And Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us that Abū ʿUmar narrated to us that Shuʿbah informed me that ʿAmr ibn Murrah narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ʿAlī and ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them both) met at ʿUsfān, and ʿUthmān was prohibiting mutʿah. So ʿAlī said to him: ""What do you intend by a matter that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did, that you prohibit it?"" He said: ""Leave us alone."" He said: ""I cannot leave you alone."" When ʿAlī saw that, he entered iḥrām for both together.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1488]"
"8885","(8882) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf narrated to me that my father narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated — and Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us that Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated — both saying: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Shiqīq said: ""ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) used to prohibit mutʿah, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to command it. So ʿUthmān said something to ʿAlī, and then ʿAlī said: 'You know well that we performed tamattuʿ with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).' He said: 'Yes, but we were fearful.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1223]"
"8886","(8883) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that Jarīr narrated from Bayān from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, who said: I said to Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī and Ibrāhīm al-Taymī: ""I am resolved to combine ʿUmrah and Ḥajj."" Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī said: ""But your father would not have been pleased with that for him."" And Ibrāhīm al-Taymī narrated from his father that he passed by Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) at al-Rabadhah and mentioned that to him. He said: ""That was exclusively for us, not for you.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1224. Nasāʾī 2812]"
"8887","(8884) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Munādī informed us: Abū Badr Shujaʿ ibn al-Walīd informed us: Sulaymān ibn Mihrān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from his father, from Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Mutʿah in Hajj was exclusively for the Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ).""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah. And in the narration of Abū Bakr, he said: ""Indeed, mutʿah of Hajj was exclusively for us.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.

And what he intended — and Allah knows best — was their annulment of Hajj by combining it with ʿUmrah, which is that some of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) entered iḥrām for Hajj and they did not have a sacrificial animal with them, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded them to make it an ʿUmrah so that it would be dissolved — and Allah knows best regarding that — their practice of prohibiting ʿUmrah during the months of Hajj. And this is not permissible today, as has been indicated in the narration of Ibn ʿAbbās and in the narration of Murriqʿ al-Usayyidī from Abū Dharr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1224. Ibn Mājah 2985]"
"8888","(8885) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Hannād narrated from Ibn Abī Zāʾidah: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad, from Sulaym ibn al-Aswad, that Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say regarding the one who performs Hajj and then annuls it with an ʿUmrah: ""That was only for the caravan that was with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1807]"
"8889","(8886) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Saʿdān informed us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — said regarding the verse {The Hajj is [during] well-known months} [al-Baqarah: 197]: ""There is no ʿUmrah in them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning. al-Ṭabarānī 9703]"
"8890","(8887) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us: Miʿsar narrated from Qays ibn Muslim, from"
"8891","(8888) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to me that al-Layth narrated — and Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Ḥusayn ibn Ḥasan ibn Muhājir narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth narrated to me that my father narrated to me from his father that ʿUqayl ibn Khālid narrated from Ibn Shihāb from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said — and he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the Ḥajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) — he said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) arrived in Makkah, he said to the people: 'Whoever among you has brought a sacrificial animal, then he must not exit the state of iḥrām from anything that is forbidden until he completes his Ḥajj. And whoever among you has not brought a sacrificial animal, let him perform ṭawāf of the House and between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, let him shorten (his hair), and let him exit the state of iḥrām. Then let him enter iḥrām for Ḥajj and let him offer a sacrificial animal. And whoever does not find a sacrificial animal, let him fast three days during the Ḥajj and seven days when he returns to his family…'"" And he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1606]"
"8892","(8889) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Wasil informed me; my father narrated to me; Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to me; he said: Abu Kamil narrated to us; Abu Ma'shar narrated to us from 'Uthman ibn Ghiyath from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas: that he was asked about Mut'ah of the Hajj (Tamattu'), and he said: The Muhajirun, the Ansar, and the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) entered into Ihram during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered into Ihram as well. When we arrived at Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Convert your Ihram for Hajj into an 'Umrah, except for the one who has garlanded the Hady (sacrificial animal)."" So we performed Tawaf around the Bayt (the Ka'bah), and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and we were intimate with our wives and wore garments. And he said: ""Whoever has garlanded the Hady, he shall not exit the state of Ihram until the Hady reaches its place (of sacrifice)."" Then he commanded us on the evening of Tarwiyah that we enter into Ihram for Hajj. When we had completed the rites, we came and performed Tawaf around the Bayt and between al-Safa and al-Marwah. Our Hajj was complete, and upon us was the Hady, as Allah the Most High said: {And whatever you can afford of the Hady; but whoever cannot find one, then a fast of three days during Hajj and seven when you return} [al-Baqarah: 196] — to your cities. A sheep suffices. So they combined two acts of worship in one year — Hajj and 'Umrah. Indeed, Allah revealed it in His Book and in the Sunnah of His Prophet, and permitted it for other than the people of Makkah. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {That is for those whose people are not present at al-Masjid al-Haram} [al-Baqarah: 196]. And the months of Hajj that Allah mentioned are: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and Dhu al-Hijjah. Whoever performs Tamattu' in these months, upon him is a Hady (sacrifice) or fasting. And al-Rafth means sexual intercourse; al-Fusuq means acts of disobedience; and al-Jidal means argumentation.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih thus. [Sahih. Bukhari 1497]"
"8893","(8890) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; al-Qasim al-Mutarrif narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us; Abu Kamil narrated to us; Abu Ma'shar — al-Bara' narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'd narrated to us from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, with the same meaning in its entirety.

The Shaykh Abu Bakr said: Thus said al-Qasim: 'Uthman ibn Sa'd."
"8894","(8891) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Hasan 'Isa ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us; Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us; my father narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq; Ibn Abi Najih narrated to me from Mujahid and 'Ata' from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah regarding the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and his commanding them to exit the Ihram by performing 'Umrah, his sermon, and his saying: ""Had I known beforehand what I came to know afterward, I would not have brought the Hady, and I would have exited the Ihram as they exited. So whoever does not have a Hady with him, let him fast three days and seven when he returns to his family. And whoever finds a Hady, let him sacrifice it.""

He said: We used to sacrifice a camel on behalf of seven persons, and he mentioned the hadith. [Hasan. Ibn Khuzaymah 2936. Hakim 647]"
"8895","(8892) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Malik ibn Anas informed me from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar that he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar say: Whoever performs 'Umrah during the months of Hajj — in Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, or Dhu al-Hijjah — has indeed performed Tamattu', and upon him is the obligation of a Hady (sacrifice) or fasting if he cannot find a Hady.

And it has been narrated to us in the chapter before it from Salim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar from his father that he said regarding al-Mut'ah (Tamattu'): It is not complete except that its performer offers a Hady or, if he cannot find a Hady, fasts three days during Hajj and seven when he returns to his family. And 'Umrah performed outside the months of Hajj is complete without a Hady and without fasting. [Sahih. Malik 450]"
"8896","(8893) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us from Abu Jamrah, who said: I performed Tamattu', and some people forbade me from it. So I asked Ibn 'Abbas, and he commanded me to do it. I returned to my house and slept, and someone came to me in a dream and said: ""An accepted 'Umrah and a blessed Hajj."" So I came to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him. He said: ""Allah is the Greatest! It is the Sunnah of Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings be upon him)"" — or he said: ""the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)."" And he was asked about what is affordable of the Hady, and he said: ""A camel, a cow, a sheep, or a share in a sacrifice.""

Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah, and al-Bukhari mentioned the narration of Wahb. [Sahih. Bukhari 1492. Muslim 1242]"
"8897","(8894) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from al-Qāsim, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding the words {**mā istaysara min al-hadyi**} (whatever is easy of sacrificial animals): it means a sheep. {**Hadyan bāligha al-Kaʿbah**} (a sacrificial animal reaching the Kaʿbah) [al-Māʾidah: 95].

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah: 12785]"
"8898","(8895) With this chain of narration, from al-Qāsim, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: {**Mā istaysara min al-hadyi**} (whatever is easy of sacrificial animals) means a camel or a cow.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8899","(8896) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī al-ʿAdl informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated to us, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father: that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālliib — may Allah be pleased with him — used to say: {**Mā istaysara min al-hadyi**} (whatever is easy of sacrificial animals) means a sheep.

[Ḍaʿīf. Mālik: 861]"
"8900","(8897) With this chain of narration, Mālik narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: {**Mā istaysara min al-hadyi**} (whatever is easy of sacrificial animals) means a badanah (camel) or a cow.

And we hold the view of ʿAlī and Ibn ʿAbbās, because the term *hady* (sacrificial offering) applies to a sheep. This is also the view of ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabbāḥ, al-Ḥasan, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī, and others.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muwaṭṭaʾ Mālik: 863]"
"8901","(8898) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Mālik ibn Anas informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that she said: Fasting for the one who performs *tamattuʿ* (combining ʿUmrah with Hajj) is for the one who cannot find a sacrificial animal, from the time he enters into iḥrām for Hajj until the Day of ʿArafah. And whoever does not fast during those days should fast the days of Minā.

He (Mālik) said: And Ibn Shihāb narrated to me, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from his father, something similar to that.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it with both chains from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik.

He (the author) said: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd followed him (in narrating) from Ibn Shihāb, and this has already been mentioned in the Book of Fasting.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī: 1894. Mālik: 954]"
"8902","(8899) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Muḥammad — meaning Ibn Jaʿfar — narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us. He said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĪsā narrating from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — and from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, that both of them said: No concession was granted for fasting during the days of Tashrīq except for the one who cannot find a sacrificial animal.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār, and this is the correct wording with this expression, along with the wording of the ḥadīth of Mālik that has preceded.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī: 1894]"
"8903","(8900) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Salām al-Baṣrī narrated to me that Shuʿbah narrated to him, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted concession to the one performing *tamattuʿ* who could not find a sacrificial animal and was unable to fast until the days of the ten (Dhū al-Ḥijjah) had passed, that he should fast the days of Tashrīq in their place.

This is how Yaḥyā ibn Sallām narrated it, and it is not strong. This Ibn Abī Laylā is ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĪsā ibn Abī Laylā. As for the reports narrated prohibiting fasting during the days of Tashrīq in general, their mention has already passed in the Book of Fasting.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8904","(8901) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, from his father, from ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — regarding His words {**Fa-ṣiyāmun thalāthati ayyāmin fī al-Ḥajji**} (then fasting for three days during Hajj) [al-Baqarah: 196]. He said: One day before the Day of Tarwiyah, the Day of Tarwiyah, and the Day of ʿArafah.

[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah: 15149]"
"8905","(8902) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Aṣbahānī al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Dārabajirdī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us, from Sufyān, who said: Jaʿfar narrated to me, from his father, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: He should fast after the days of Tashrīq if the fasting has elapsed.

And from Sufyān, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: He should fast the days of Tashrīq if the fasting has elapsed.

And from Sufyān, who said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: He should not fast them except while in the state of iḥrām.

The ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar and ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with them both — is connected (mawṣūl), and they both stated in the narration of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĪsā from al-Zuhrī that which indicates the concession.

The concession comes after the general prohibition. And the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī from ʿAlī is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). And Allah knows best.

[Ḍaʿīf. Muṣannaf Ibn Abī Shaybah: 15149]"
"8906","(8903) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Abū Zayd al-Harawī narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Nawwār, who said: I heard a man from Banī Sulaym called Khifāq say: I asked Ibn ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — about fasting three days during Hajj and seven days when you return. He said: When you return to your family.

The Sheikh (the author) said: They differed regarding the name of this man. It was said it is thus (Khifāq), and it was said Abū al-Khifāq, and it was said Ḥabbān al-Sulamī, the companion of the Dafīnah.

And we have narrated this in the marfūʿ (elevated) ḥadīth from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, and from ʿAṭāʾ and Mujāhid, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās.

[Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Ibn Abī Shaybah: 12786]"
"8907","(8904) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh — meaning al-Bukhārī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Fuḍayl narrated to us; Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us; Kurayb informed me from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man may perform ṭawāf around the House (the Kaʿbah) so long as he is in a state of ḥalāl, until he enters into iḥrām for Ḥajj. When he sets out towards ʿArafah, then whatever hadyah is made available to him from camels, cattle, or sheep — whatever is facilitated for him from that, whichever of these he wishes — (is permissible). But if nothing is made available to him, then upon him are three days of fasting during the Ḥajj, and that is before the Day of ʿArafah. If the last of those three days falls on the Day of ʿArafah, then there is no sin… and he mentioned the ḥadīth.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 4249]"
"8908","(8905) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; Abū ʿUmays narrated to us; ʿAbdah ibn Abī Lubābah narrated to us from Abū Yaḥyā from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A man came to him and said: ""I have combined ʿUmrah with Ḥajj."" He said: ""How much silver do you have?"" He said: ""Forty dirhams."" He said: ""There is no surplus in this. Ten of them will feed your mount, ten you will take as provisions, ten you will spend on clothing, and ten you will use to treat your companions generously.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8909","(8906) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Aḥmad ibn Shaybān narrated to us; Sufyān narrated.
And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us among others; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated.
And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated.
And Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to me; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated — from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The people of al-Madīnah enter into iḥrām from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah; the people of al-Shām from al-Juḥfah; and the people of Najd from Qarn.""
Ibn ʿUmar said: ""And it was mentioned to me — though I did not hear it — that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'And the people of Yemen from Yalamlam.'""
The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿUmar. In the narration of al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him), Ibn ʿUmar said: ""And they claim that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said…"" And in the narration of ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, he said: ""And it was mentioned to me, though I did not hear, that he designated Yalamlam for the people of Yemen."" And similarly in the narration of Ibn Shaybān.
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and Muslim narrated it from Ibn Abī ʿUmar.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1400. Muslim 1182]"
"8910","(8907) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us by dictation; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb al-Bazmahrānī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; my father narrated to me; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭaḥmān narrated to us from Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: A man called out to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was in the mosque and said: ""From where do you command us to enter into iḥrām, O Messenger of Allah?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The people of al-Madīnah enter into iḥrām from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah; the people of al-Shām from al-Juḥfah; and the people of Najd from Qarn."" He said: ""And they say: 'And the people of Yemen from Yalamlam.'""
[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8911","(8908) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us from men among the people of knowledge, among them Mālik ibn Anas, Usāmah ibn Zayd, and al-Layth ibn Saʿd.
(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us from Mālik.
(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: I read to Mālik from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The people of al-Madīnah enter into iḥrām from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah; the people of al-Shām from al-Juḥfah; and the people of Najd from Qarn.""
ʿAbd Allāh said: ""And it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'And the people of Yemen enter into iḥrām from Yalamlam.'""
The wording is that of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh al-Qaʿnabī and Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. In the narration of Ibn Wahb, he said: ""And Ibn ʿUmar said: 'They claim that he said: ""And the people of Yemen enter into iḥrām from Yalamlam.""'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1453]"
"8912","(8909) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Mālik ibn Anas informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār informed me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded the people of al-Madīnah to enter into iḥrām from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, the people of al-Shām from al-Juḥfah, and the people of Najd from Qarn.
ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""As for these three, I heard them from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). And I have been informed that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'As for the people of Yemen, they enter into iḥrām from Yalamlam.'""
Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1182. Mālik 380]"
"8913","(8910) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir and Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us — the wording being that of al-Aswad — they said: Zuhayr narrated to us from Zayd ibn Jubayr, that he came to Ibn ʿUmar at his dwelling, where he had a tent and a canopy. He said: ""I asked him: 'From where is it permissible for me to perform ʿUmrah?' He said: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) designated Qarn for the people of Najd, Dhū al-Ḥulayfah for the people of al-Madīnah, and al-Juḥfah for the people of al-Shām.'""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl from Zuhayr.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1450]"
"8914","(8911) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī — Abū ʿAbd Allāh said ""informed us"" and Abū Naṣr said ""narrated to us"" — both from Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, who said: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Anmāṭī narrated to us; Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to me; Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh being asked about the muhall (place of entering iḥrām). He said: ""I heard"" — then he stopped, and it seemed as though he was referring to the Prophet (ﷺ) — and said: ""The muhall of the people of al-Madīnah is Dhū al-Ḥulayfah; the other route is al-Juḥfah; the muhall of the people of Iraq is Dhāt ʿIrq; the muhall of the people of Najd is Qarn; and the muhall of the people of Yemen is Yalamlam.""
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim and others from Muḥammad ibn Bakr.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1183]"
"8915","(8912) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Ibn Lahīʿah informed me from Abū al-Zubayr al-Makkī from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""And the muhall of the people of Iraq is Dhāt ʿIrq.""
Thus said ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahīʿah. And similarly it was narrated from Ibn Abī al-Zinād from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Abū al-Zubayr. The correct narration is that of Ibn Jurayj. And it is possible that Jābir heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say that regarding the muhall of the people of Iraq.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad Aḥmad 4/159]"
"8916","(8913) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭawābīqī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ḥasanī narrated to me; Ibn Numayr narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When these two cities (Baṣra and Kūfah) were conquered, they came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) designated Qarn for the people of Najd, but it deviates from our route. If we were to go to Qarn, it would be difficult for us."" He said: ""Then look for a point opposite it on their route."" He said: ""So he designated Dhāt ʿIrq for them.""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Muslim from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr.
And Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar.
This was the view of Ṭāwūs, Jābir ibn Zayd Abū al-Shaʿthāʾ, and Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn: that the Prophet (ﷺ) did not designate it; rather, it was designated after him. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) chose this view. And ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ held the view that the Prophet (ﷺ) designated it, though he did not narrate it with a chain in the narration of Ibn Jurayj from him.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1458]"
"8917","(8914) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Saʿīd ibn Sālim informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: ʿAṭāʾ informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) designated Dhū al-Ḥulayfah as the miqāt for the people of Madīnah, al-Juḥfah for the people of the West, Dhāt ʿIrq for the people of the East, and Qarn for the people of Najd. And whoever from the people of Yemen or others traverses the route of Najd, [the miqāt is] Qarn al-Maʿādin, and for the people of Yemen it is Yalamlam. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Al-Shāfiʿī 523]"
"8918","(8915) He said: And al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us: Muslim and Saʿīd informed us from Ibn Jurayj. He said: I went back to ʿAṭāʾ and said to him: ""They claim that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) did not designate Dhāt ʿIrq, and that there were no people of the East at that time."" He said: ""That is what we heard — that he designated Dhāt ʿIrq or al-ʿAqīq for the people of the East."" He said: ""There was no ʿIrāq [as a settled region] at that time, but it was designated for the people of the East."" And he did not attribute it to anyone other than the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), but he would not refrain from [saying] that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) designated it.

This is the authentic narration from ʿAṭāʾ from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), as a mursal hadith. Al-Ḥajjaj ibn Arṭāh narrated it, and his weakness is apparent, from ʿAṭāʾ and others, and he connected it [with a complete chain]. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Al-Shāfiʿī 524]"
"8919","(8916) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Naṣr ibn ʿAlī informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: al-Ḥajjāj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh; and from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh; and from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) designated Dhū al-Ḥulayfah as the miqāt for the people of Madīnah, al-Juḥfah for the people of Shām, Yalamlam for the people of Yemen and the people of Tihāmah, Qarn for the people of al-Ṭāʾif — and it is [in] Najd — and Dhāt ʿIrq for the people of ʿIrāq.

And this has been narrated in narrations other than the hadith of Jābir. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Abū Yaʿlā 222]"
"8920","(8917) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Abū Ghālib, the son of the daughter of Muʿāwiyah, informed us: Hishām ibn Bahrām informed us in al-Madāʾin — and I asked him — he informed us: al-Muʿāfā ibn ʿImrān informed us from Aflaḥ ibn Ḥumayd from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The people of Madīnah enter iḥrām from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah; the people of Shām and Egypt from al-Juḥfah; the people of Yemen from Yalamlam; and the people of ʿIrāq from Dhāt ʿIrq.""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Kitāb al-Sunan from Hishām, in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd 1739. Al-Nasāʾī 2653]"
"8921","(8918) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us; Waki' narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad from Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) designated al-'Aqeeq as the miqat for the people of the East. [Munkar. Abu Dawud 1740. Ahmad 1/344. Tirmidhi 832]"
"8922","(8919) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Abi Qumash narrated to us; Abu Ma'mar 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us; 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Malik al-Sahmi narrated to us; Zurarah ibn Karim ibn al-Harith narrated to us that al-Harith ibn 'Amr narrated to him, saying: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) at 'Arafat — or he said at Mina — and the people had surrounded him. He said: The Bedouins would come, and when they saw him they would say: ""This is a blessed face."" And he mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: He designated Yalamlam as the miqat for the people of Yemen, from which they should enter into Ihram, and Dhu 'Irq for the people of Iraq and for the people of the East. Abu Dawud narrated it from Abu Ma'mar. [Sahih li-ghayrihi. Abu Dawud 1742]"
"8923","(8920) To this conclusion went 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, according to what Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Isfara'ini narrated to us; Abu Muslim narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us from Hisham ibn 'Urwah from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) designated Dhu 'Irq as the miqat for the people of the East. [Sahih li-ghayrihi]"
"8924","(8921) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us; al-Qadi Isma'il narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us from Ibn Tawus; and 'Ali informed us; Ahmad narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to us; Musa, meaning Ibn Isma'il, narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us; Ibn Tawus narrated to us from his father from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) designated Dhu al-Hulayfah as the miqat for the people of Madinah, al-Juhfah for the people of Syria, Qarn al-Manazil for the people of Najd, and Yalamlam for the people of Yemen. And he said: ""They are for them and for whoever comes upon them from other than their people, among those who intend Hajj and 'Umrah. And whoever is within that (closer to Makkah), then his place of Ihram is from where he sets out, until the people of Makkah — from Makkah itself.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Musa ibn Isma'il and others, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Wuhaib. [Sahih. Bukhari 1452. Muslim 1181]"
"8925","(8922) Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Fami narrated to us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad and Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us. Yahya said: narrated to us; and Muhammad said: informed us — Yahya ibn Yahya; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us.

(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Musaddad and Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us; they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Dinar from Tawus from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) designated Dhu al-Hulayfah as the miqat for the people of Madinah, al-Juhfah for the people of Syria, Qarn al-Manazil for the people of Najd, and Yalamlam for the people of Yemen. So they are for them and for whoever comes upon them from other than their people, among those who intend Hajj and 'Umrah. And whoever is within that, his place of Ihram is from his family — and likewise — until the people of Makkah, who enter into Ihram from it (Makkah).

The wording is from the hadith of Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya and Abu al-Rabi'. [Sahih. Bukhari 1454. Muslim 1181]"
"8926","(8923) Abū al-Mihrānī informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik from Nāfi': that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) pronounced the talbiyah from al-Furʿ.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: And this is our position — and Allah knows best — that he passed by his miqāt without intending ḥajj or ʿumrah, then it occurred to him [to assume iḥrām] from al-Furʿ, so he pronounced the talbiyah from there. Or he came to al-Furʿ from Makkah or elsewhere, then the intention to enter iḥrām occurred to him, so he pronounced the talbiyah from there. And he himself narrated the ḥadīth from the Prophet (peace be upon him) regarding the mawāqīt. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 727]"
"8927","Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us; Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Ibn Uyaynah narrated from Amr from Abu al-Sha'tha' that he saw Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, turning back those who passed the miqat points without entering the state of ihram. [Sahih. Al-Shafi'i 531]"
"8928","Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Abdullah ibn Umar and Malik ibn Anas and others informed me that Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah informed them from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Abdullah ibn Abbas that he said: ""Whoever forgets something of his rites or omits it, let him offer a sacrifice (blood)."" [Sahih. Muwatta' Malik 940]"
"8929","Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id al-Sayrafi both said: Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu Utbah — Ahmad ibn al-Faraj al-Hijazi al-Himsi narrated to us; Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us.

(And in another chain:) Abu al-Qasim — Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Ali, known as Ibn Urwah al-Bundar in Baghdad, informed us; Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us; Abu al-Fadl — Salih ibn Muhammad al-Razi narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us; Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us.

(And in another chain:) Abu Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us; Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yahnas from Yahya ibn Abi Sufyan al-Akhnasi from his grandmother Hakimah from Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Whoever enters ihram for a Hajj or an Umrah from al-Masjid al-Aqsa towards al-Masjid al-Haram, his past and future sins will be forgiven, or Paradise will become obligatory for him.""

Abdullah was uncertain which of the two wordings he said. [Da'if. Abu Dawud 1741. Ibn Majah 3001]"
"8930","Abu Tahir al-Faqih and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr both said: Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; Ibn Abi Maryam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us that Yunus informed him from Ibn Shihab from Nafi' from Ibn Umar that he entered the state of ihram from Iliya' in the year of the arbitration (of al-Hakamayn).

Abu Bakr, meaning al-Saghani, said: ""This is among the narrations that it is said Ibn Shihab heard from Nafi'."" [Sahih]"
"8931","Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi Amr both said: Abu al-Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarir narrated from Shu'bah from Amr ibn Murrah from Abdullah ibn Salamah al-Muradi, who said: A man said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: ""What is the meaning of His statement {And complete the Hajj and the Umrah for Allah} [al-Baqarah: 196]?"" He said: ""That you enter ihram from the vicinity of your family."" [Da'if. Mustadrak al-Hakim 2/303]"
"8932","This has also been narrated from the hadith of Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayyah as a marfu' narration, though there is some discussion regarding it. Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Muhammad al-Sha'rani narrated to us; my grandfather narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ja'far in Fayd narrated to us; Jabir ibn Nuh narrated from Muhammad ibn Amr from Abu Salamah from Abu Hurayyah from the Prophet ﷺ regarding the statement of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And complete the Hajj and the Umrah for Allah} [al-Baqarah: 196], he said: ""Among the completions of Hajj is that you enter ihram from the vicinity of your family."" [Munkar. Shu'ab al-Iman 5025. Al-Kamil of Ibn Adi 2/120]"
"8933","Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan informed us; Abu al-Abbas, that is al-Asamm, informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Muslim narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Ata' that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ designated the miqat points, he said: ""Let a man enjoy his family and his garments until he reaches such and such"" — referring to the miqat points. This is a mursal narration. [Very Da'if. Al-Shafi'i 530]"
"8934","Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us; Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Fasawi narrated to us; Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; al-Hayyaj ibn Bistam al-Hanzali narrated from Wasil ibn al-Sa'ib al-Raqashi from Abu Sawrah from his uncle Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Let one of you enjoy his state of halal as much as he is able, for he does not know what may befall him during his ihram."" This chain of narration is weak. Wasil ibn al-Sa'ib is denounced in hadith, as stated by al-Bukhari and others. It has also been narrated on the authority of Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, and it is well-known from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, although the chain is broken. [Munkar. Al-Shashi 1057]"
"8935","Abu al-Hasan ibn Abdan informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Mahmuyah al-Askari narrated to us; Isa ibn Ghaylan narrated to us; Hadir ibn Mutahhar narrated to us; Abu Ubaydah — Muja'ah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from al-Hasan that Imran ibn Husayn entered the state of ihram from Basrah, and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, disapproved of that for him. [Da'if]"
"8936","Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Qadi in Bukhari narrated to us; Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Bistam al-Marwazi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sayyar al-Faqih narrated to us; it was recited to al-Hasan ibn Ishaq from Sulayman ibn Salih, who said: Muslimah ibn Muharib narrated from Dawud ibn Abi Hind that when Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Kurayz conquered Khurasan, he said: ""I shall make my gratitude to Allah that I depart from my place in the state of ihram."" So he entered ihram from Nishapur. When he arrived before Uthman, he rebuked him for what he had done and said: ""Would that you had adhered to the time at which the people entered ihram."" [Da'if. Ibn Asakir 29/363]"
"8937","(8934) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, ʿAmmār ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: Then ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir departed from Naysābūr as a muʿtamir (one performing ʿUmrah), having entered into iḥrām from there, and he appointed al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays as his deputy over Khurāsān. When he completed his ʿUmrah, he came to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān — may Allah be pleased with him — and that was in the year in which ʿUthmān — may Allah be pleased with him — was killed. ʿUthmān — may Allah be pleased with him — said to him: ""You have indeed jeopardized your ʿUmrah when you entered into iḥrām from Naysābūr."" [Weak]"
"8938","(8935) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, and he said: I read to Mālik, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from ʿUbayd ibn Jurayj, that he said to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar: ""O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, I have seen you do four things which I have not seen any of your companions do."" He said: ""What are they, O Ibn Jurayj?"" So he mentioned the hadith. He said in it: ""And I have seen you, when you are in Makkah, the people recite the talbiyah when they see the crescent moon, but you do not recite the talbiyah until the day of Tarwīyah (the 8th of Dhū al-Ḥijjah)."" ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""As for the talbiyah, I did not see the Messenger of Allah — ﷺ — recite the talbiyah until his mount set out with him.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from al-Qaʿnabī from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5513. Muslim 1187]"
"8939","(8936) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Abū Shihāb narrated from Dāwūd, from Abī Naḍrah, from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""We arrived crying out loudly about the Hajj. When we had performed ṭawāf of the House, the Messenger of Allah — ﷺ — said: 'Make it an ʿUmrah.' When the day of Tarwīyah came, we recited the talbiyah for Hajj."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad Aḥmad 3/5. Ibn Ḥibbān 3793]"
"8940","(8937) And in the hadith of ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from Abī Naḍrah, from Abī Saʿīd, who said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah — ﷺ — crying out loudly about the Hajj. When we arrived in Makkah, he commanded us to make it an ʿUmrah, except for the one who had brought the hady (sacrificial animal). When the day of Tarwīyah came and we departed for Minā, we recited the talbiyah for Hajj.""

Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it, Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qawārīrī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us. Muslim narrated it from ʿUbaydullāh al-Qawārīrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith]"
"8941","(8938) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdak al-Qazzāz narrated to us, Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, and he said: Ibn Jurayj said, Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh — may Allah be pleased with him — narrating about the Hajj of the Prophet — ﷺ — and he said: ""He commanded us, after we had performed ṭawāf, to exit the state of iḥrām."" The Prophet — ﷺ — said: ""When you wish to set out for Minā, then recite the talbiyah."" He said: ""So we began reciting the talbiyah from al-Baṭḥāʾ."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1210. Musnad Aḥmad 3/378]"
"8942","(8939) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned it with the same meaning. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1210. Musnad Aḥmad 3/378]"
"8943","(8940) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Abū Ghassān Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr Zunayn narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, in the hadith of Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays — may Allah be pleased with her — when she experienced postpartum bleeding at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah: that the Prophet — ﷺ — commanded Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — to instruct her to perform ghusl and to recite the talbiyah.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ghassān and Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — this Yaḥyā is al-Anṣārī. The hadith of Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad has already been mentioned in full in this and other places. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1209. Abū Dāwūd 1743]"
"8944","(8941) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, ʿAbdah narrated —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ and Ismāʿīl al-Jurjānī informed us, and they said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Zaydān al-Bajalī narrated to us, Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us, ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: ""Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays experienced postpartum bleeding due to Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr, so the Messenger of Allah — ﷺ — commanded Abū Bakr to perform ghusl and to recite the talbiyah.""

The wording is that of al-Rūdhbārī's hadith. And in the hadith of Abū ʿAbdillāh: ""The Messenger of Allah — ﷺ — commanded Abū Bakr — may Allah be pleased with him — to instruct her to perform ghusl and to recite the talbiyah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, Hannād ibn al-Sarī, and Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb.

And Mālik narrated it from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim from his father, as a mursal narration, without mentioning ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her. And Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī narrated it from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq — may Allah be pleased with him — that he set out as a pilgrim, then he mentioned it. And ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar refined it from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and he is a trustworthy ḥāfiẓ. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8945","(8942) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us by dictation, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Hāshim narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays: ""She experienced postpartum bleeding due to Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, so Abū Bakr asked the Prophet — ﷺ — about that, and he commanded him to instruct her to perform ghusl and to recite the talbiyah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. Ṭabarānī al-Kabīr 141]"
"8946","(8943) And Abū Ghazīyah Muḥammad ibn Mūsā — and he is not strong — narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād, from his father, from Khārijah ibn Zayd ibn Thābit, from his father: ""That the Messenger of Allah — ﷺ — performed ghusl for his iḥrām.""

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us of it, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Khālid Abū Sulaymān al-Makhzūmī narrated to us, and Abū Ghazīyah narrated to me, and he mentioned it.

Ibn Ṣāʿid said: ""This is a gharīb hadith; we did not hear it except from him.""

The Shaykh said: ""And it has been narrated from other than Abū Ghazīyah."" [Ḥasan for other than it. Ṭabarānī al-Kabīr 4862. Dāraquṭnī 2/220]"
"8947","(8944) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sukkari narrated to me, and he wrote it down for me in his own hand: Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Dallal narrated to us: Nasr ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Marwan al-Naysaburi narrated to us in Baghdad: al-Aswad ibn 'Amir Shadhan narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated from his father, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ removed his garments for his ihram and performed a ritual bath (ghusl). [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Tirmidhi 830. Ibn Khuzaymah 2590]"
"8948","(8945) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abi al-Tayyib narrated, saying: it was recited to Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash while I was looking at this book, and he affirmed it, from Ya'qub ibn 'Ata', from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed a ritual bath (ghusl), then put on his garments. When he reached Dhu al-Hulayfah, he prayed two rak'ahs, then sat upon his camel. When it stood upright upon the open ground (al-Bayda'), he entered into ihram for Hajj. Ya'qub ibn 'Ata' is not strong (in narration). [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Hakim 1/477. Darqutni 2/219]"
"8949","(8946) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Ali al-Hafiz narrated to us: 'Abdan al-Ahwazi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Sahl ibn Yusuf narrated to us: Humayd narrated from Bakr ibn 'Abd Allah al-Muzani, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: It is from the Sunnah that one should perform a ritual bath (ghusl) when he intends to enter into ihram and when he intends to enter Makkah. [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Mustadrak of Hakim 1/610. Darqutni 2/220]"
"8950","(8947) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik informed us from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he broke his fast from Ramadan and intended Hajj, would not take anything from his hair or his beard until he had performed Hajj. [Sahih. Malik 888]"
"8951","(8948) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Ahmasi narrated to us: al-Muharibi narrated from Ash'ath, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were commanded to let our hair grow long during Hajj and 'Umrah. Al-Muharibi said, meaning on the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Nahr) at the time of shaving. [Da'if. Ibn Abi Shaybah 25504]"
"8952","(8949) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Maqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us: Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us: Kurayb narrated to me, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out from Madinah after having combed his hair, applied oil, and donned his lower garment (izar) and upper garment (rida'), and so did his Companions. He did not prohibit any type of lower garments or wrappings except the saffron-dyed garment that is worn directly against the skin. When he reached Dhu al-Hulayfah, he mounted his riding beast, and when it stood upright upon the open ground (al-Bayda'), he and his Companions pronounced the talbiyah, and he garlanded his sacrificial camel (badanah). This was when five days remained in Dhu al-Qi'dah. He arrived in Makkah on the fourth of Dhu al-Hijjah. He performed tawaf of the House (al-Bayt) and sa'y between al-Safa and al-Marwah. He did not exit the state of ihram on account of his sacrificial camels, because he had garlanded them. He settled in the upper part of Makkah near al-Hajjun, while he was in a state of ihram for Hajj. He did not approach the Ka'bah after his tawaf of it until he returned from 'Arafah. He commanded his Companions to perform tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then to shorten their hair and exit the state of ihram. This was for those who had not brought a sacrificial camel that they had garlanded. And whoever had his wife with her, she was lawful for him, as were perfume and garments. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami. [Sahih. Bukhari 1470]"
"8953","(8950) Abu Sa'd Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Malini informed us: Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us in Makkah: Abu Hamzah narrated to us: Abu Qurratah Musa ibn Tariq narrated to us: 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us: Layth ibn Sa'd narrated from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ entered into ihram wearing two Qatari garments. [Da'if. al-Kamil of Ibn 'Adi 5/345]"
"8954","(8951) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khaytham, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""The best of your garments are the white ones; let your living wear them and shroud your dead in them."" And Abu Ja'far Kamil ibn Ahmad al-Mustamli informed us: Bishr ibn Ahmad al-Isfarayini narrated to us: Dawud ibn al-Husayn narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Uthman ibn Khaytham, and he mentioned something similar to it, except that he said: ""Wear from your garments the white, for they are the best of your garments."" [Sahih]"
"8955","(8952) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik informed us. (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi both informed us, saying: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani narrated to us: al-Sari ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: 'Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Maslamah, narrated from Malik. (H) And Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah informed us: Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated, saying: I recited to Malik, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I used to apply perfume to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for his ihram before he entered into ihram, and for his exiting the state of ihram before he performed tawaf of the House. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih]"
"8956","(8953) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us by dictation: Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri informed us in Makkah: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah al-Za'farani informed us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who spread out her hands and said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with these two hands of mine for his ihram when he entered into ihram, and for his exiting the state of ihram before he performed tawaf of the House. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Ali ibn al-Madini from Ibn 'Uyaynah. [Sahih. Bukhari 1460. Muslim 1189]"
"8957","(8954) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, among the last of his narrations, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) with my two hands for his iḥrām when he entered the state of consecration, and for his ḥill (release from iḥrām) before he performed ṭawāf around the House.""

Al-Ḥumaydī added in his narration: ""It was said to Sufyān: 'Did you hear it from al-Zuhrī?' He said: 'Yes.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 1667]"
"8958","(8955) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, among the last of his narrations, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUrwah, who said: ""I heard my father say: I heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: 'I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) for his iḥrām and for his ḥill.' I said to her: 'With what perfume?' She said: 'With the finest perfume.'""

ʿUthmān said: ""Hishām did not narrate this ḥadīth except from me."" Muslim extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah, and al-Bukhārī extracted it from the ḥadīth of Wahb from Hishām from his brother. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1189, Bukhārī 1667]"
"8959","(8956) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Khurāsānī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed me, ʿUmar ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUrwah informed me that he heard ʿUrwah and al-Qāsim narrating from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: ""I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) with dhariḥah (a type of perfume) during the Farewell Pilgrimage for ḥill and iḥrām.""

Al-Bukhārī extracted it in his Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ""ʿUthmān ibn al-Haytham — or Muḥammad from him — narrated to us; it is said he is Ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī."" And Muslim extracted it from another route from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 8854, Muslim 1189]"
"8960","(8957) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhabārī, the jurist, informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī narrated to us in Baṣrah, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, al-Ḥakam narrated from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""It is as though I am looking at the radiance of perfume in the part of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while he is in the state of iḥrām.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam and others, and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 268, Muslim 1190]"
"8961","(8958) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān, the trustworthy narrator, informed us in Baghdād, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar informed us, al-Ḍaḥḥāk — meaning ibn Makhlad — informed us, Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""It is as though I am looking at the radiance of perfume in the partings of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while he is in the state of iḥrām.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Firyābī from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 1464]"
"8962","(8959) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāniʾ, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār informed us, they said: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Anas narrated to us, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī informed us by dictation, Abū Manṣūr Muḥammad ibn al-Qāsim al-Ṣibghī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Anas narrated to us, Abū ʿĀṣim al-Nabīl narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUbaydullāh, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""It is as though I am looking at the radiance of musk in the partings of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while he is in the state of iḥrām.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Abī ʿĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1190]"
"8963","(8960) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, and from Muslim, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""It is as though I am looking at the radiance of perfume in the partings of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while he is in the state of iḥrām.""

Muslim narrated it from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"8964","(8961) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us, Abū al-Azhar informed us, ʿAbd al-Malik — meaning Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī — narrated from Sufyān and Saʿīd ibn Zayd, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""It is as though I am looking at the radiance of perfume in the part of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) three days after his iḥrām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Nasāʾī 2702]"
"8965","(8962) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Walīd informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Kāmil narrated to us, Abū ʿAwānah narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, who said: ""I asked ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar about a man who perfumes himself and then enters the state of iḥrām in the morning. He said: 'I would not like to enter the state of iḥrām in the morning reeking of perfume, for being anointed with saffron is more beloved to me than doing that.' Then ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: 'I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) at the time of his iḥrām, then he went around to his wives, and then he entered the state of iḥrām.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and Abī Kāmil.

The ḥadīth of Masrūq and al-Aswad from ʿĿʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) indicates that the trace of the perfume remained after his ghusl and iḥrām, so that its radiance could still be seen in his partings. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1192]"
"8966","(8963) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us, Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Ghamr narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""I used to perfume the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) with the finest and most expensive perfume (al-ghāliyah) at the time of his iḥrām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Dāraquṭnī 2/232]"
"8967","(8964) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us, Abu al-Abbas al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabi' — informed us, al-Shafi'i — informed us, Sufyan — from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlān, that he heard 'A'ishah bint Sa'd saying: ""I perfumed my father with musk and dhari'ah at the time of his iḥrām."" [Ḥasan. Al-Shāfi'ī 559]"
"8968","(8965) And Abu Bakr — informed us, Abu al-Abbas — informed us, al-Rabi' — informed us, al-Shafi'i — informed us, Sa'id ibn Sālim al-Qaddāḥ — from al-Hasan ibn Zayd — from his father, who said: ""I saw Ibn 'Abbās in the state of iḥrām, and upon his head was a clotted mass of ghāliyah perfume."" [Ḍa'īf. Al-Shāfi'ī 650]"
"8969","(8966) And Abu 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī — informed us, Abu al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī — informed us, 'Alī ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz — from Abu 'Ubayd — informed us, Hushaym — informed us, 'Uyaynah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān — from his father — from Ibn 'Abbās, that he was asked about perfume at the time of iḥrām, and he said: ""As for me, I dab it into my head and then I love for it to remain."" Abu 'Ubayd said: ""Abu Zayd and al-Aṣma'ī said: 'Al-saghsaghah is al-tarwiyah (moistening/permeation).'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū 'Ubayd in Gharīb al-Ḥadīth 4/221]"
"8970","(8967) Abu Aḥmad — 'Abd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī — informed us, Abu Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī — informed us, Ibn Bukayr — informed us, Mālik — from Nāfī', the mawlā of 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Umar — from Aslam, the mawlā of 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu), that 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) detected the scent of perfume while he was at al-Shajarah and said: ""From whom is this scent of perfume?"" Mu'āwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān said: ""From me, O Commander of the Faithful."" 'Umar said: ""From you, by my life!"" Mu'āwiyah said: ""Umm Ḥabībah perfumed me, O Commander of the Faithful."" 'Umar (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) said: ""I adjure you — go back and wash it off!"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muwaṭṭa' Mālik 721]"
"8971","(8968) Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us, Abu Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān — informed us, Abu Yaḥyā al-Dīrāqūlī — informed us, Abu al-Yamān — informed us, Shu'ayb — from al-Zuhrī, who said: ""'Abd Allāh ibn 'Umar used to narrate from 'Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) that he detected from Mu'āwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān the scent of perfume while he was at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah and they were pilgrims, and 'Umar (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) said: 'From whom is this scent of perfume?' He said: 'Something with which Umm Ḥabībah perfumed me.' 'Umar (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) said: 'By my life, I swear by Allāh, you shall go back and wash it off. By Allāh, if I were to detect the smell of qaṭirān from a muḥrim, I would prefer that to detecting the smell of perfume from him.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]

The Shaykh said: ""It is possible that the ḥadīth of 'Ā'ishah (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhā) had not reached him, and had he received it, he would have retracted. And it is also possible that he disliked it so that the ignorant might not be deceived by it, imagining that the initial application of perfume is permissible for the muḥrim, as he said regarding Ṭalḥah and the torn garment. And Allāh knows best."""
"8972","(8969) Abu 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us, Abu 'Abd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb — informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā — informed us, Musaddad — informed us, 'Abd al-Wārith — from 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Ṣuhayb — from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallā Allāhu 'alayhi wa sallam) forbade that a man should apply saffron (zafarān).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 5508. Muslim 2101]"
"8973","(8970) Abu Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq — both said: Abu al-Abbas, i.e., al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabi' — informed us, al-Shāfi'ī — informed us, Ismā'īl, known as Ibn 'Ulayyah — informed us, 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Ṣuhayb — from Anas ibn Mālik (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu), that the Prophet (ṣallā Allāhu 'alayhi wa sallam) forbade that a man should apply saffron (zafarān).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Ismā'īl."
"8974","(8971) Abu 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah — informed us, Abū Dāwūd — informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismā'īl — informed us, Ḥammād — informed us, 'Aṭā' al-Khurāsānī — from Yaḥyā ibn Ya'mar — from 'Ammār ibn Yāsir (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu), who said: ""I came to my family at night and my hands had become chapped, so they applied saffron (zafarān) to me. In the morning I went to the Prophet (ṣallā Allāhu 'alayhi wa sallam) and offered him salām, but he did not return my salām nor welcome me, and he said: 'Go and wash this off yourself.' So I went and washed it. Then I came back, but a trace of it remained upon me, and I offered salām, but he did not return my salām nor welcome me, and he said: 'Go and wash this off yourself.' I washed it, then came and offered him salām, and he returned my salām and welcomed me, and he said: 'Verily, the angels do not attend the funeral of an unbeliever with any good, nor the one anointed with saffron (al-mutadaṃmakh bi-l-zafarān), nor the one in a state of janābah.'"" And he granted concession to the one in a state of janābah, if he slept, ate, or drank, to perform wuḍū'. [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd 4178. Aḥmad 4/403]"
"8975","(8972) Abu 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah — informed us, Abū Dāwūd — informed us, Mūsā ibn Ismā'īl — informed us, Ḥammād — informed us, 'Aṭā' al-Khurāsānī — from Yaḥyā ibn Ya'mar — from 'Ammār ibn Yāsir (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu), who said: ""I came to my family at night and my hands had become chapped, so they applied saffron (zafarān) to me. In the morning I went to the Prophet (ṣallā Allāhu 'alayhi wa sallam) and offered him salām, but he did not return my salām nor welcome me, and he said: 'Go and wash this off yourself.' So I went and washed it. Then I came back, but a trace of it remained upon me, and I offered salām, but he did not return my salām nor welcome me, and he said: 'Go and wash this off yourself.' I washed it, then came and offered him salām, and he returned my salām and welcomed me, and he said: 'Verily, the angels do not attend the funeral of an unbeliever with any good, nor the one anointed with saffron (al-mutadaṃmakh bi-l-zafarān), nor the one in a state of janābah.'"" And he granted concession to the one in a state of janābah, if he slept, ate, or drank, to perform wuḍū'. [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd 4176. Aḥmad 4/210]"
"8976","(8973) And Abu 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī — informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Dāsah — informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī — informed us, Naṣr ibn 'Alī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr — informed us, Ibn Jurayj — informed me, 'Umar ibn 'Aṭā' ibn Abī al-Khawwār, that he heard Yaḥyā ibn Ya'mar narrating from a man who informed him from 'Ammār ibn Yāsir (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) — 'Umar claimed that Yaḥyā named that man, but 'Umar forgot his name — that 'Ammār said: ""I applied saffron (takhallaqtu) in this manner."" And the first narration is more reliable. He said: ""I said to 'Umar: 'And they are in iḥrām?' He said: 'No, the people are resident (muqīmūn).'"" And it was narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī from 'Ammār ibn Yāsir (raḍiya Allāhu 'anhu) from the Prophet (ṣallā Allāhu 'alayhi wa sallam) in an abridged form. [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd 477]"
"8977","(8974) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Ahmad ibn Sulayman al-Faqih informed us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq informed us — Isma'il ibn Abi Uways informed me — my father narrated to me — from Sulayman ibn Bilal — from Thawr ibn Zayd — from 'Abd al-Rahman al-Saraj — from al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan — from 'Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Three things the angels do not approach with any goodness: the corpse of a disbeliever, one perfumed with Khaluq (a fragrant perfume), and the one in a state of major ritual impurity (junub) — let him not eat or sleep until he performs the ablution (wudu') for the prayer."" [Weak. Abu Dawud 4180]"
"8978","(8975) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Mahri informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Yunus informed me — from Ibn Shihab — from Salim, meaning Ibn 'Abd Allah — from his father, who said: ""I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, reciting the Talbiyah while having his hair matted (mulabbidan)."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Asbagh on the authority of Ibn Wahb, and Muslim narrated it from Harmalah on the authority of Ibn Wahb. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 1466. Muslim 1184]"
"8979","(8976) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz informed us — Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar al-Qawariri informed us — 'Abd al-A'la informed us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, matted his head with water (ghusl). [Weak. Abu Dawud 1778. Al-Hakim 1/619]"
"8980","(8977) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub informed us — Abu al-Rabi' informed us — Fulayh ibn Sulayman informed us — from Nafi', who said: ""When Ibn 'Umar intended to set out for Makkah, he would apply an oil that had no pleasant scent, then he would come to the Mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah and pray two rak'ahs. Then he would mount his riding beast, and when it stood upright with him upon it, he would enter the state of ihram and say: 'Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, do.'"" Al-Bukhari narrated it from Abu al-Rabi'. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 1419]"
"8981","(8978) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Husayn ibn 'Umar ibn Burhan and Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan and Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — they said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah informed us — 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb al-Mula'i informed us — from Khusayf — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, recited the Talbiyah after the prayer. [Weak. Al-Nasa'i 2754. Abu Ya'la 2512]"
"8982","(8979) Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal informed me — my father informed me — Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us — my father informed us — from Ibn Ishaq — Khusayf ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Jazari informed me — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: ""I said to 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas: 'O Abu al-'Abbas, I am amazed at the difference among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, regarding the ihlal of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when he made it obligatory.' He said: 'I am the most knowledgeable of the people about that. It was only one Hajj performed by the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and from that point they differed. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, set out as a pilgrim. When he had prayed his two rak'ahs in his mosque at Dhu al-Hulayfah, he made it obligatory upon himself in his place of prayer and recited the Talbiyah for Hajj when he had finished his two rak'ahs. Some people heard that from him and memorized it. Then he mounted, and when his she-camel stood upright with him, he recited the Talbiyah, and some people caught that from him. That was because the people had come in groups, and they heard him reciting the Talbiyah when his she-camel stood upright with him, and they said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, only began the ihlal when his she-camel stood upright with him."" Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, continued on. When he ascended the elevation of al-Bayda', he recited the Talbiyah, and some people caught that from him, and they said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, only began the ihlal when he ascended the elevation of al-Bayda'."" By Allah, he made it obligatory in his place of prayer, and he recited the Talbiyah when his she-camel stood upright with him, and he recited the Talbiyah when he ascended the elevation of al-Bayda'."" Sa'id ibn Jubayr said: ""So whoever takes the statement of Ibn 'Abbas, he recited the ihlal in his place of prayer when he had finished his two rak'ahs."" Khusayf al-Jazari is not strong. Al-Waqidi narrated it with his chain from Ibn 'Abbas, but the corroboration of al-Waqidi is not beneficial. And the hadiths that have been reported on this matter from Ibn 'Umar and others have strong, well-established chains of narration. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"8983","(8980) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus informed us — 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi informed us — al-Qa'nabi, in what he recited to Malik, may Allah have mercy upon him — (H) And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — al-Qa'nabi informed us — from Malik — from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi — from 'Ubayd ibn Jurayj, that he said to 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar: ""O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, I have seen you practice four things which I have not seen any of your companions practice."" He said: ""What are they, O Ibn Jurayj?"" He said: ""I have seen you touch nothing of the corners except the two Yemeni corners; I have seen you wear sandals made of leather without hair; I have seen you dye with yellow (sufrah); and I have seen you, when you were in Makkah, the people would recite the ihlal when they saw the new moon, but you would not recite the ihlal until the Day of Tarwiyah."" 'Abd Allah said: ""As for the corners, I have not seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, touch anything except the two Yemeni corners. As for the hairless sandals, I have seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, wear sandals that had no hair upon them and perform ablution while wearing them, so I love to wear them. As for the yellow dye, I have seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, dye with it, so I love to dye with it. As for the ihlal, I have not seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, recite the ihlal until his riding beast was sent forth with him."" This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Dawud. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya from Malik. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 5513. Muslim 1187]"
"8984","(8981) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu 'Uthman Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn 'Uthman and Abu Muhammad 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Hamid al-Muqri' and Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Mu'awiyah — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan al-'Amiri informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi informed us — from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when he placed his foot in the stirrup and his she-camel stood upright with him, he recited the ihlal from the Mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah. Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in their Sahih collections from the hadith of 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 2710]"
"8985","(8982) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan informed us — Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us — Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham informed us — Hajjaj said: Ibn Jurayj said: Salih ibn Kaysan informed me — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to narrate: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, recited the ihlal when his riding beast stood upright with him."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu 'Asim from Ibn Jurayj, and Muslim narrated it from Harun al-Hamal from Hajjaj ibn Muhammad. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 1477. Muslim 1187]"
"8986","(8983) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Jurjani informed us — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah informed us — Harmalah informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Yunus informed me — from Ibn Shihab, that Salim ibn 'Abd Allah informed him that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, mount his riding beast at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and then he recited the ihlal when it stood upright with him."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Harmalah ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari extracted it from Ahmad ibn 'Isa from Ibn Wahb. [Weak]"
"8987","(8984) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, that he said: ""It is your Bādiyyah about which you attribute a falsehood to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not begin the talbiyyah except from near the mosque — meaning the mosque of Dhū al-Ḥulayfah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1467]"
"8988","(8985) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated, saying: I read it before Mālik, and he mentioned it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā."
"8989","(8986) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah and Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym both narrated to us, saying: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, that when it was said to him, ""The iḥrām is from al-Baydāʾ,"" he said: ""It is al-Baydāʾ in which you attribute a falsehood to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not begin the talbiyyah except from near the tree, when his camel stood up with him."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1186]"
"8990","(8987) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ḥassān narrated to us; Duḥaym narrated to us; al-Walīd narrated to us; al-Awzāʿī narrated, saying: I heard ʬāʾ narrating from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh that the iḥlāl of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was from Dhū al-Ḥulayfah when his mount stood upright with him. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. And the ḥadīth of Abī al-Zubayr from Jābir regarding their iḥlāl from al-Baṭḥāʾ has already passed. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1444]"
"8991","(8988) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdad informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Kharqī narrated to us; Abū Qilābah narrated to us; Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from Anas ibn Mālik that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr in Madīnah as four rakʿahs and prayed ʿAṣr in Dhū al-Ḥulayfah as two rakʿahs, then spent the night there. When morning came and his mount stood upright with him, he began the talbiyyah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from another route from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1471]"
"8992","(8989) Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us; my father narrated to me; I heard Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrating from Abī al-Zinād from ʿĀʾishah bint Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, who said: Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the route of al-Furʿ, he began the talbiyyah when his mount lifted him up, and when he took the other route, he began the talbiyyah when he ascended the elevation of al-Baydāʾ."" And another said: ""The route of Uḥud."" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1775]"
"8993","(8990) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated, saying: Abū Naṣr — meaning ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬāʾ — said: Saʿīd — meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — was asked about a man who intends to enter iḥrām in his place of prayer or when his mount stands upright with him. He informed us from Maṭar, from Qatādah, from Abī Ḥassān al-Aʿraj, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet ﷺ entered iḥrām at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah when his mount stood upright with him on al-Baydāʾ; he entered iḥrām after Ẓuhr and made the talbiyyah for Ḥajj. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj and Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbd Allāh from Qatādah, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""He mounted his mount, and when it stood upright on al-Baydāʾ, he made the talbiyyah for Ḥajj."" And in the narration of Hishām: ""He entered iḥrām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1243. Nasāʾī 2791]"
"8994","(8991) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to me; my father narrated from his father, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, that when Ibn ʿUmar came to Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, he ordered his mount to be saddled, then he prayed the morning prayer, then he rode until, when it stood upright with him, he faced the qiblah and made the talbiyyah. He said: ""Then he continued the talbiyyah until he reached the Ḥaram, whereupon he desisted until he came to Dhū Ṭuwā, where he spent the night. He said: 'He would pray the morning prayer there, then take a bath, and he asserted that the Prophet ﷺ did that.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Maʿmar from ʿAbd al-Wārith al-Akbar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1478]"
"8995","(8992) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah al-Tamīmī narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, who was informed that he heard ʿAlqama ibn Waqqāṣ saying: He heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Actions are but by intentions, and each person shall have only what he intended. So whoever's migration was to Allah (Mighty and Majestic), his migration was to Allah and His Messenger, and whoever's migration was for worldly gain to be attained or a woman to marry, his migration was to that for which he migrated.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yazīd ibn Hārūn and others, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from various routes from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1. Muslim 1907]"
"8996","(8993) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn al-Khalīl narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Mushir narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, mentioning neither Ḥajj nor ʿUmrah,"" and she mentioned the ḥadīth. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Suwayd ibn Saʿīd from ʿAlī ibn Mushir, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""He made the talbiyyah, mentioning neither Ḥajj nor ʿUmrah."" And al-Bukhārī narrated it from another route from al-Aʿmash, as has preceded. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1683. Muslim 1211]"
"8997","(8994) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Amr al-Muqri' informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Hisham ibn 'Ammar and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah both said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah, in the account of the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""He (the Prophet ﷺ) pronounced the talbiyah of Tawhid, and the people also pronounced the talbiyah that they used to pronounce, and he did not forbid them any part of it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ adhered to his own talbiyah."" Jabir said: ""We did not intend anything other than Hajj; we did not know of 'Umrah.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah. [Sahih. Muslim 1218]"
"8998","(8995) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Asam informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Ibrahim ibn Muhammad informed us from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ruqaysh that Jabir ibn 'Abdillah said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never named Hajj or 'Umrah in his talbiyah at all.""

[Munkar. al-Shafi'i 566]"
"8999","(8996) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us; 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Najih from Nafi' that Ibn 'Umar heard a man saying: ""Labbayka bi-hajjatan"" (Here I am for a Hajj). He struck him on his chest and said: ""Do you inform Allah of what is in your soul?""

[Da'if]"
"9000","(9997) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Sariy ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; al-Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated from Dawud ibn Abi Hind from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who both said: ""We arrived with the Prophet ﷺ, and we were calling out aloud for Hajj.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Hajjaj ibn al-Sha'ir from al-Mu'alla ibn Asad. [Sahih. Muslim 1248]"
"9001","(8998) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Ziyad ibn al-Khalil narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Hammad narrated — and Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Ka'bi informed us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Abu al-Rabi' al-'Ataki informed us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyub, who said: I heard Mujahid say: Jabir ibn 'Abdillah narrated to us, saying: ""We arrived with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we were saying: 'Labbayka bi-l-Hajj' (Here I am for Hajj). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us, so we made it an 'Umrah.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abu al-Rabi'. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 1495. Muslim 1216]"
"9002","(8999) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us; Habib al-Mu'allim narrated from 'Ata', from Jabir, who said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj"" — and he mentioned the hadith.

It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from al-Hasan ibn 'Umar ibn Shaqiq from Yazid. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 6803]"
"9003","(9000) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Bakr ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi in Marw narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from Anas ibn Malik, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Dhuhr in Madinah as four rak'ahs and 'Asr in Dhu al-Hulayfah as two rak'ahs."" Anas said: ""And I heard them calling out for both Hajj and 'Umrah together.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from Sulayman ibn Harb. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 1473]"
"9004","(9001) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Hamid ibn Bilal al-Bazzaz narrated to us; al-Za'farani narrated to us; Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh al-'Anbari narrated to us; Humayd narrated from Anas that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj and 'Umrah, saying: ""Labbayka 'umratan wa-hajjan"" (Here I am for 'Umrah and Hajj).

[Sahih. Muslim 1251]"
"9005","(9002) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn 'Abdillah informed us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Humayd narrated from Bakr from Anas, who said: ""I heard the Prophet ﷺ pronouncing the talbiyah for Hajj and 'Umrah together."" Humayd said: Bakr said: ""I narrated this to Ibn 'Umar, and he said: 'He pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj alone.' So I met Anas and informed him of what Ibn 'Umar had said. Anas said: 'You consider us nothing but children! I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Labbayka 'umratan wa-hajja"" (Here I am for 'Umrah and Hajj).'""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Hisham from Humayd. [Sahih. Muslim 1232]"
"9006","(9003) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal al-Qattan informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us; Ya'la ibn 'Ubayd informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated from Muhammad ibn Shihab from Yahya ibn 'Abbad from 'Abbad, meaning Ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: ""I was informed that when 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, entered Bayt al-Maqdis, he said: 'Labbayka, Allahumma, labbayka' (Here I am, O Allah, here I am).""

[Sahih]"
"9007","(9004) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari al-Faqih informed us; al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Tusi informed us; Abu Yahya ibn Abi Masarra narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mansur and 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr al-Humaydi both said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us. He said: I heard Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman narrating from al-Harith ibn Bilal ibn al-Harith from his father, who said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is the dissolution of Hajj (faskh al-hajj) specific to us, or for those who come after us?"" He said: ""Rather, it is specific to us.""
[Weak. Abu Dawud 1808. al-Nasa'i 2808]"
"9008","(9005) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Ibn Milhan narrated to us; Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us; Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us; al-Murraqi' al-Usaydi, who was a man of good standing, narrated that Abu Dhar, may Allah be pleased with him, the Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, said: ""It was a concession for us specifically, not for anyone after us"" — meaning the dissolution of Hajj by combining it with 'Umrah. Yahya said: ""And this was confirmed among us: that Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, did not dissolve Hajj with 'Umrah, nor did they grant permission for anyone to do so, and they were more knowledgeable about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and about what he did during his Hajj than those who witnessed only part of it.""
[Hasan. al-Humaydi 132]"
"9009","(9006) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Muhammad Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Khalidi informed us; al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah informed us; Rawh informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us. He said: 'Ata' informed me. He said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari say: ""We, the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, entered ihram for Hajj exclusively."" He then mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: ""Then 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, arrived from his errand, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'With what did you enter ihram, O 'Ali?' He said: 'With the same ihram with which the Prophet ﷺ entered ihram.' He said: 'Then offer a sacrifice and remain in the state of ihram as you are.'""
[al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Makki from Ibn Jurayj, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Ibn Jurayj. The hadith of Abu Musa has already passed under this heading.]
[Sahih. al-Bukhari 1482. Muslim 1216]"
"9010","(9007) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us; Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Baghdadi informed us; Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us; Shu'bah informed me; Qays ibn Muslim narrated to me. He said: I heard Tariq ibn Shihab say: I heard Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, say: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was resting his mount at al-Batha'. He said to me: 'With what did you enter ihram?' I said: 'Here I am, with an ihram like the ihram of the Prophet ﷺ.' He said: 'You have done well.' Then he commanded me, so I performed Tawaf of the Bayt and of al-Safa and al-Marwah.""
[Both al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih.]
[Sahih. al-Bukhari 1484. Muslim 1221]"
"9011","(9008) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Malik informed me from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr that 'Abd al-Malik ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham informed him that Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib al-Ansari informed him that his father informed him that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Jibril, peace be upon him, came to me and commanded me to instruct my Companions to raise their voices with the ihram or with the Talbiyah — or one of the two.""
This 'Abd al-Malik is Ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham.
[Sahih. Malik 736. Abu Dawud 1814. al-Tirmidhi 829]"
"9012","(9009) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us; al-Rabi' informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us, mentioning it, except that he said ""from"" (repeated) and said: ""And he commanded me to instruct my Companions, or those who are with me, to raise their voices with the Talbiyah or with the ihram"" — meaning one of the two.
And similarly, Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated it from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr.
[See the preceding hadith.]"
"9013","(9010) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ahmad ibn Shayban al-Ramli narrated to us. (H) And 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Bashran informed us; Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Hayyan narrated to us. Both said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Khallad from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Jibril came to me and commanded me to instruct my Companions to raise their voices with the ihram.""
And similarly, al-Humaydi and others narrated it from Sufyan. And Ibn Jurayj narrated it, saying: ""'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr wrote to me, mentioning it, but he did not mention the father of Khallad in his chain. The correct narration is that of Malik and Ibn 'Uyaynah from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr from 'Abd al-Malik from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib from his father from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" Thus said al-Bukhari and others.
[See the preceding hadith.]"
"9014","(9011) And al-Muttalib ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Hantab narrated it from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, who said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""Command your Companions to raise their voices with the Talbiyah, for it is the slogan of Hajj.""
Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi informed us, dictating; Abu Hamid ibn al-Sharqi informed us; Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Dhuhli informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; al-Thawri narrated to us from Ibn Abi Labid from al-Muttalib ibn Hantab, mentioning it.
[Sahih. Ibn Majah 2923. Ahmad 5/192]"
"9015","(9012) And similarly, Shu'bah narrated it from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Labid, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Jibril came to me…""
Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — he is al-Asamm — informed us; al-'Abbas al-Duri informed us; Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri informed us; Shu'bah narrated, mentioning it. (T) And similarly, Waki' said it from al-Thawri. And Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated it from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Labid and others from al-Muttalib ibn 'Abd Allah from Abu Hurayrah.
[Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
"9016","(9013) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Uthman and 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Labid both informed him from al-Muttalib ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Hantab, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Jibril, peace be upon him, commanded me to raise the voice with the ihram, for it is among the rites of Hajj.""
[Munkar in its chain. Ahmad 325]"
"9017","(9014) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed me. Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī and Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us. Hushaym narrated to us. Dāwūd narrated to us from Abū al-ʿĀliyah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by the Valley of al-Azraq and said, ""What valley is this?"" They said, ""The Valley of al-Azraq."" He said, ""It is as though I see Mūsā, peace be upon him, descending from the mountain pass, raising his voice in supplication to Allah, the Most High, with the talbiyah."" Then he came upon the mountain pass of Harshā and said, ""What mountain pass is this?"" They said, ""The mountain pass of Harshā."" He said, ""It is as though I see Yūnus ibn Mattā on a reddish-brown she-camel, curly-haired, wearing a woollen cloak, the reins of his she-camel made of palm-fibre rope, and he was reciting the talbiyah.""

Hushaym said: He meant palm-fibre. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 166. Ibn Mājah 2891]"
"9018","(9015) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me that Abū al-Walīd informed us. Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us. Surayj ibn Yūnus narrated to us. Hushaym narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and Surayj ibn Yūnus."
"9019","(9016) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us. My grandfather narrated to us. Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Fudayk informed me. Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān informed us from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yarbuʿ from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, ""Which deed is best?"" He said, ""The talbiyah raised loudly (al-ʿajj) and the shedding of blood (al-thajj).""

Thus did a group narrate it from Ibn Abī Fudayk. [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Ibn Mājah 2934]"
"9020","(9017) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us. Muḥammad ibn Hārūn al-Azdī narrated to us. Abū Nuʿaym Ḍirār ibn Ṣurad narrated to us. Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us from al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān from Ibn al-Munkadir from Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yarbuʿ from his father from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, ""Which Ḥajj is best?"" He said, ""The talbiyah raised loudly (al-ʿajj) and the shedding of blood (al-thajj)."" Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Sawwāq al-Balkhī also narrated it thus from Ibn Abī Fudayk.

Abū ʿĪsā said: I asked al-Bukhārī about it and he said: In my view it is mursal. Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir did not hear from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yarbuʿ.

I said: Then who mentioned Saʿīd in it? He said: It is an error. There is no mention of Saʿīd in it. I said to him: Ḍirār ibn Ṣurad and others narrated from Ibn Abī Fudayk this ḥadīth and they said, ""From Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from his father."" He said: It is nothing.

The Shaykh said: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal also said the same, according to what reached us from him. [Munkar al-isnād]"
"9021","(9018) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Sūsī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us. ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Kathīr narrated to us. My father narrated to me. Abū Ḥarīz Sahl, the mawlā of al-Mughīrah ibn Abī al-Ghayth ibn Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, narrated to us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we had not yet reached al-Rawḥāʾ when I heard the voices of the people, all of them hoarse from the talbiyah.

This Abū Ḥarīz is weak. ʿUmar ibn Ṣuhbān, who is also weak, narrated it from Abū al-Zinād from Anas ibn Mālik. [Munkar]"
"9022","(9019) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Al-Ḥusayn ibn Idrīs al-Anṣārī narrated to us. ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us. ʿUbaydah ibn Ḥumayd narrated to me. ʿUmarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us from Abū Ḥāzim from Sahl ibn Saʿd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no one who recites the talbiyah but that everything to his right and to his left of trees and stones responds with the talbiyah, until the earth is cut off from here and from there"" — meaning from his right and from his left.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Tirmidhī 828. Ibn Mājah 2921]"
"9023","(9020) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us. Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Hamadhānī narrated to us in Hamadhān. Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir narrated to me. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb narrated to me. Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to me from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbayd Allāh from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""No believer recites the talbiyah until the sun sets but that his sins set with it, until he returns as his mother bore him.""

ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: I asked al-Thawrī, ""Where did you get this from ʿĀṣim?"" He said: ""He came to us in Kufah during the time of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz and narrated to us.""

[Munkar. Ibn Mājah 2925. Ibn ʿAdī in al-Kāmil 5/227]"
"9024","(9021) He said: And ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar narrated to me from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbayd Allāh from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah from his father from the Prophet ﷺ.

[Munkar. Aḥmad 3/373]"
"9025","(9022) And it has also been narrated in this regard from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbayd Allāh from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Whoever spends a day reciting the talbiyah until the sun sets, his sins set with it, and he returns as his mother bore him.""

Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us dictating. Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us in Makkah. Abū Yaḥyā Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd ibn Ghālib narrated to us. Ḥammād ibn Khālid al-Khayyāṭ narrated to us. ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

[Munkar. See the one before it.]"
"9026","(9023) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us. Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us. Al-Rabīʿ informed us. Al-Shāfiʿī informed us. Saʿīd ibn Sālim narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that he used to recite the talbiyah while riding, while dismounting, and while lying down.

[Ḍaʿīf. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 573]"
"9027","(9024) 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would not recite the Talbiyah while performing Tawaf of the House of Allah. [Sahih. Muwatta Malik 749]

The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Imam al-Shafi'i (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding al-Safa and al-Marwah: It has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he would stand upon them and make supplication and say the Takbir, and that one should make supplication while performing Sa'i between the two. And I also prefer that I do what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did, i.e., without declaring the Talbiyah to be disliked."
"9028","(9025) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Matar informed us; Abu Khalifah informed us; Muhammad ibn Kathir informed us; Sufyan narrated from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): He stood on the side of al-Safa and recited the Talbiyah. So I said, ""I have been forbidden from reciting the Talbiyah."" He said, ""Rather, I command you to do it. The Talbiyah was a supplication that was answered, which Ibrahim (peace be upon him) answered."" [Sahih]"
"9029","(9026) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us among the last of those who narrated, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us.

(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Malik ibn Anas and others informed us that Nafi' narrated to them.

(And) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam informed us; Yahya ibn Yahya informed us, he said: I read to Malik from Nafi', from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): The Talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata laka wa al-mulka lā sharīka lak.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours, and the dominion, You have no partner."")

And 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar used to add to it:

""Labbayka labbayka wa sa'dayka wa al-khayru biyadayka labbayka wa al-raghba'u ilayka wa al-'amal.""

(""Here I am, here I am, and I am at Your service. All goodness is in Your hands. Here I am, and all desire is directed to You, and the deed is for You."")

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Bukhari 1474, Muslim 1184]"
"9030","(9027) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Husayn ibn Mansur informed us from his original book; Abu 'Abdullah al-Husayn ibn Ahmad ibn Mansur Sajjadah informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abbad al-Makki informed us; Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, and Nafi', the freed slave of 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, and Hamzah ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): When his mount stood upright with him at the Mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah, he entered into ihram and said:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata laka wa al-mulka lā sharīka lak.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours, and the dominion, You have no partner."")

He said: And 'Abdullah used to say, ""This is the Talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him).""

Nafi' said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar used to add to this:

""Labbayka wa sa'dayka wa al-khayru biyadayka labbayka wa al-raghba'u ilayka wa al-'amal.""

(""Here I am, and I am at Your service. All goodness is in Your hands. Here I am, and all desire is directed to You, and the deed is for You."")

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abbad al-Makki. [Sahih. Muslim 1184]"
"9031","(9028) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu Bakr ibn 'Abdullah informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed us; and Isma'il ibn Ahmad informed us; Muhammad ibn al-Hasan informed us; they both said: Harmalah ibn Yahya informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Yunus informed me from Ibn Shihab. Salim ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar informed me from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) entering into ihram with his hair matted, saying:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata laka wa al-mulka lā sharīka lak.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours, and the dominion, You have no partner."")

He did not add anything to these words. And 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and then when his mount stood upright with him at the Mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah, he would enter into ihram with these words.

And 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar used to say: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) would enter into ihram with the same ihram as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) with these words, and he would say:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka wa sa'dayka wa al-khayru fī yadayka labbayka wa al-raghba'u ilayka wa al-'amal.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, and I am at Your service. All goodness is in Your hands. Here I am, and all desire is directed to You, and the deed is for You."")

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Harmalah ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Muslim 1184]"
"9032","(9029) Abu 'Amr Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us; al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya informed us; 'Amr ibn 'Ali informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated from Sufyan.

(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz informed me; Abu 'Urubah informed us; Zakariyya ibn al-Hakam informed us; al-Firyabi informed us; Sufyan narrated from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah, from Abu 'Atiyyah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I know how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to recite the Talbiyah:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata lak.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours."")

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from al-Firyabi. Al-Bukhari said: Abu Mu'awiyah followed him (in narrating) from al-A'mash. And al-Bukhari said: Shu'bah said from al-A'mash, from Khaythamah. [Sahih. Bukhari 1475]"
"9033","(9030) Abu Bakr ibn Furak Muhammad ibn al-Hasan informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us; Yunus ibn Habib informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Shu'bah narrated from al-A'mash. He said: I heard Khaythamah narrating from Abu 'Atiyyah al-Wadi'i.

He said: I heard 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: By Allah, I know how the Talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was. Then I heard her reciting the Talbiyah:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata lak.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours."") [Sahih. See the preceding]"
"9034","(9031) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi informed us; Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr informed us; Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: We came to Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was in Banu Salamah, and we asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He mentioned the hadith and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out and we set out with him, until his she-camel stood upright at al-Bayda', and he entered into ihram with the declaration of Tawhid:

""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata laka wa al-mulka lā sharīka lak.""

(""Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed, all praise and grace are Yours, and the dominion, You have no partner."")

He said: And the people were adding words such as ""Dha al-Ma'arij"" and the like, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would hear them and say nothing to them. [Sahih. Muslim 1218]"
"9035","(9032) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Nasr Ahmad ibn Sahl al-Faqih in Bukhara informed us; Qays ibn Anif al-Bukhari informed us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us; Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated from his father, from his grandfather, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), in the story of the Hajj of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: And the people recited the Talbiyah: ""Labbayka Dha al-Ma'arij"" and ""Labbayka Dha al-Fawadil,"" and he (peace and blessings be upon him) did not find fault with any of them for anything. [Sahih. Al-Arwa' 4/202]"
"9036","(9033) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah informed me that 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl narrated to him from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Among the Talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was:

""Labbayka Ilaha al-Haqq.""

(""Here I am, O God of Truth."")

And he informed me with it in the Fawaid of Abu al-'Abbas, and he said from Abu Hurayrah that he used to say: Among the Talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was:

""Labbayka Ilaha al-Haqq, labbayk.""

(""Here I am, O God of Truth, here I am."") [Sahih. Sunan al-Nasa'i 2752, Ibn Majah 2920]"
"9037","(9034) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Yūsuf ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed me; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī narrated to us; Maḥbūb ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Dāwūd narrated from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered a sermon at ʿArafāt. When he said, ""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayka,"" he said, ""Indeed, the good is the good of the Hereafter."" [Ḥasan. Ibn Khuzaymah, Mustadrak al-Ḥākim 1/636. Ibn Abī Rūd: 470]"
"9038","(9035) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Saʿīd narrated from Ibn Jurayj; Ḥumayd al-Aʿraj informed me from Mujāhid that he said: The Prophet (ﷺ) used to raise his voice with the talbiyah, ""Labbayka Allāhumma labbayka."" He mentioned the talbiyah. He said: Until one day, when the people were dispersing from him, it was as though he liked the sound of it, so he added to it, ""Labbayka, indeed the life is the life of the Hereafter."" Ibn Jurayj said: And I think that was the day of ʿArafah. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Shāfiʿī 569]"
"9039","(9036) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī and Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn al-Azhar al-Mihrajānī informed us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Barāʾ narrated to us; al-Muʿāfā ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; al-Qāsim ibn Maʿn narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salamah or Ibn Abī Salamah, that Saʿd saw one of his nephews reciting the talbiyah at Dhī al-Maʿārij. Saʿd said: ""Indeed, he is the possessor of the ascents (Dhū al-Maʿārij), but this is not how we used to recite the talbiyah in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" Another narrator narrated it from al-Qāsim and said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Salamah. [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad 1/171. Abū Yaʿlā 724]"
"9040","(9037) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us; ʿIkrimah narrated from Abī Zuml, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The polytheists used to circumambulate the House and say, ""Labbayka, labbayka, you have no partner."" The Prophet (ﷺ) would say, ""Enough, enough."" They would say, ""Except a partner who belongs to You; You own him and what he owns."" And they would say, ""Your forgiveness, your forgiveness."" He said: Then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And Allah would not punish them while you are among them, and Allah would not punish them while they seek forgiveness} [Al-Anfāl: 33]. Ibn ʿAbbās said: There were among them two sources of security: the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) and seeking forgiveness. He said: The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) departed, and seeking forgiveness remained. {And they have no right that Allah should not punish them while they prevent [people] from al-Masjid al-Ḥarām, and they are not its guardians. Its guardians are only the righteous} [Al-Anfāl: 34]. He said: This is the punishment of the Hereafter, and that is the punishment of this world. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Naḍr ibn Muḥammad, from ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār, in abridged form, without their saying ""Your forgiveness"" to the end of it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1150]"
"9041","(9038) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Kāsib narrated to me. (Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, Abū al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī, informed us; Ibn Rustah narrated to us; Ibn Kāsib narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Umawī narrated that he heard Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad ibn Zāʾidah narrating from ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thābit, from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ), when he finished his talbiyah, would ask Allah for His pleasure and His forgiveness, and would seek refuge in His mercy from the Fire. Ṣāliḥ said: And I heard al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad say: One is commanded, when he finishes his talbiyah, to send blessings upon the Prophet (ﷺ). The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Aṣbahānī, and Ibn ʿAbdān did not mention the narration from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ṭabarānī 3721. Al-Shāfiʿī 574. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/238]"
"9042","(9039) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Makhlad narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: A woman should not ascend above al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, nor should she raise her voice with the talbiyah. Mawqūf. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/295]"
"9043","(9040) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: A man stood and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you command us to wear of garments for the muḥrim?"" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not wear shirts, nor trousers, nor turbans, nor hooded cloaks, nor sandals, unless one has no sandals, in which case he may wear leather socks cut below the ankles. And do not wear anything of the garments that has been touched by saffron or wars dye. And a muḥrim woman should not wear a face veil (niqāb) nor gloves."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ, from al-Layth ibn Saʿd. Al-Bukhārī said: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah, Juwayriyah ibn Asmāʾ, and Ibn Isḥāq followed him, meaning from Nāfiʿ, regarding the face veil and the gloves. As for the ḥadīth of Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah— [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 1741]"
"9044","(9041) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn Mihrān al-Jammāl narrated to us; Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Ḥafṣ, who is Ibn Mayṣarah, narrated from Mūsā, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that a man stood and called out to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), ""What do you command us to wear of garments in iḥrām?"" He mentioned the ḥadīth similarly to the ḥadīth of al-Layth, and added: He said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to command a woman to fasten her jilbāb to her forehead. ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak and a group also narrated it from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"9045","(9042) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade a woman from wearing a face veil (niqāb) and gloves while she is in iḥrām. As for the ḥadīth of Juwayriyah ibn Asmāʾ— [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"9046","(9043) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl al-Khuzāʿī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Hāshim al-Baghawī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ narrated to me; Juwayriyah ibn Asmāʾ narrated from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbd Allāh said: A man stood and called out to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), ""What do you command us to wear of garments when we enter iḥrām?"" He mentioned the ḥadīth similarly to the ḥadīth of al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
"9047","(9044) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us: ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham informed us: Abū Salamah informed us: Juwayriyyah narrated from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The woman in iḥrām shall not wear a niqāb, nor shall she wear gloves.""

As for the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — [Sound. See the preceding]."
"9048","(9045) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: my father informed us: Yaʿqūb, meaning Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd, informed us: my father informed me from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Nāfiʿ, the freed slave of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, informed me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbidding women, during their iḥrām, from wearing gloves and the niqāb, and garments touched with wars or saffron. After that, they may wear whatever they wish of types of garments — dyed cloth, silk, jewellery, trousers, a shirt, or leather socks.

It was also narrated by Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd al-Madanī from Nāfiʿ. [Sound. Abū Dāwūd 1827]."
"9049","(9046) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd al-Madanī narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The woman in iḥrām shall not wear a niqāb, nor shall she wear gloves.""

Abū Dāwūd said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, Mālik ibn Anas, and Ayyūb also narrated it from Nāfiʿ, as a mawqūf narration from Ibn ʿUmar: ""The woman in iḥrām shall not wear a niqāb, nor shall she wear gloves.""

The Shaykh said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated the ḥadīth up to his words: ""nor wars (a fragrant dye),"" and then he said: ""He (Ibn ʿUmar) used to say: 'The woman in iḥrām shall not wear a niqāb, nor shall she wear gloves.'"" [Sound]."
"9050","(9047) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us and said: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ said: ""She shall not wear a niqāb"" — this is the statement of Ibn ʿUmar, and it has been incorporated into the ḥadīth. [Sound]."
"9051","(9048) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl informed us: Abū al-Ashʿath informed us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Hishām ibn Ḥassān from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The iḥrām of a woman is in her face, and the iḥrām of a man is in his head.""

Thus al-Dāruwardī and others narrated it as a mawqūf narration from Ibn ʿUmar. [Sound. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/294]."
"9052","(9049) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muʾammil ibn Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yūnus informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: Ayyūb ibn Muḥammad al-Yamāmī, Abū al-Jamal, informed us. And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: Ayyūb ibn Muḥammad, Abū al-Jamal, a trustworthy narrator, narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no prohibition upon a woman except in her face.""

Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī said: ""I do not know of anyone other than Abū al-Jamal who raised this narration from ʿUbayd Allāh.""

The Shaykh said: Ayyūb ibn Muḥammad, Abū al-Jamal, is considered weak (ḍaʿīf) by the scholars of ḥadīth; Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and others have declared him weak. This ḥadīth has also been narrated through another unknown chain from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar as marfūʿ, but the preserved narration is mawqūf. [Denounced (Munkar). Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/294. Al-Ḍuʿafāʾ al-ʿAqīlī 1/116]."
"9053","(9050) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Muʿādh informed us: my father informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Yazīd al-Rishk from Muʿādhah from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The woman in iḥrām may wear whatever garments she wishes, except a garment touched with wars or saffron. She shall not wear a burquʿ (face veil), nor shall she cover her face with a lithām (lower face wrap). She may drape the garment over her face if she wishes."" [Sound]."
"9054","(9051) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: Hushaym informed us: Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated from Mujāhid from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""Riders would pass by us while we were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and we were in iḥrām. When they drew near us, one of us would drape her jilbāb from her head over her face. When they had passed us, we would uncover it.""

Abū ʿAwānah, Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl, and ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim also narrated it from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād. However, Ibn ʿUyyaynah contradicted them in what was narrated from him from Yazīd, for he said: from Mujāhid, who said: ""Umm Salamah said…"""
"9055","(9052) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Junayd al-Dāmaghānī informed us: Abū Usāmah informed me: ʿUmar ibn Suwayd al-Thaqafī informed me: ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah informed me that ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers, informed her and said: ""We would set out with the Prophet (ﷺ) towards Makkah, and we would anoint our foreheads with perfumed sukk at the time of iḥrām. When one of us perspired, it would flow upon her face, and the Prophet (ﷺ) would see it and would not forbid us."" [Sound. Abū Dāwūd 1830. Ibn Rāhawayh 1021]."
"9056","(9053) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ granted me permission to narrate from him, from Abū al-ʿAbbās, from al-Rabīʿ, from al-Shāfiʿī, who said: Saʿīd ibn Sālim informed us from Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah from his brother ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbaydah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, who both said: ""It is from the Sunnah that a woman should apply something of henna to her hands at the time of iḥrām, and she should not enter iḥrām while her hands are bare (unadorned).""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And I also love that for her.""

The Shaykh said: It has been narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār informed him from Ibn ʿUmar that he used to say: ""It is from the Sunnah that a woman should rub something of henna on herself on the evening of iḥrām, and wash her head with ghuslah (a cleansing preparation) containing no perfume, and she should not enter iḥrām in an unadorned state."" However, this is not preserved (as a reliable narration). [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 2/215]."
"9057","(9054) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād, Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us, al-Ḥārith ibn Manṣūr al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Qays narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) gave his Companions permission to visit the House (the Kaʿbah) on the Day of Naḥr (Sacrifice) at midday, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) visited it with his wives at night.

Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār narrated it from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān with his chain of narration, and she said: ""He departed (performed ifāḍah) at the end of his day.""

Abū al-Zubayr narrated from ʿĀʾishah and Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) delayed the ṭawāf on the Day of Naḥr until night. [Weak]"
"9058","(9055) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʾAṭāʾ informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Women are not required to perform saʿy between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah with ramal (brisk walking) around the House, nor the saʿy in the valley of al-Musīl.""

And we have received this from the jurists of the Tābiʿīn among the people of Madīnah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Shāfiʿī 611. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/295]"
"9059","(9056) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Zuhrī say: Sālim informed me from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The muḥrim (one in the state of iḥrām) shall not wear a shirt (qamīṣ), nor a turban (ʿimāmah), nor trousers (sāwīl), nor a hooded cloak (burnus), nor any garment dyed with saffron (zaʿfarān) or wars dye, nor leather socks (khuffayn) — except for one who cannot find sandals (naʿlayn). If he cannot find them, he should cut them so that they fall below the ankles."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 134. Muslim 177]"
"9060","(9057) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us, Abū Khaythamah, ʿAmr al-Nāqid, and Ibn Abī Isrāʾīl narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked: ""What should the muḥrim wear of garments?"" And he mentioned it with the same meaning.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Khaythamah and ʿAmr, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous]"
"9061","(9058) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Mālik ibn Anas and others informed us that Nāfiʿ narrated to them from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): ""What should the muḥrim wear of garments?"" He said: ""Do not wear shirts, nor turbans, nor trousers, nor hooded cloaks, nor leather socks — unless one cannot find sandals; then he may wear the leather socks and cut them below the ankles. And do not wear any garment that has been touched by saffron or wars dye."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous]"
"9062","(9059) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: I read it to Mālik, and he mentioned it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and others, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous]"
"9063","(9060) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʾAṭāʾ informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAwn informed us from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: A man stood from this door — meaning one of the doors of the Mosque of Madīnah — and said: ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), what should the muḥrim wear?"" And he mentioned it with a meaning similar to the ḥadīth of Mālik. In the narration of Juwayriyah from Nāfiʿ: A man stood and called out, saying: ""What do you command us to wear of garments when we enter iḥrām?"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous]"
"9064","(9061) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Masʿūd al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, al-Muqaddamī narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: A man called out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while he was delivering a sermon in that place — and Nāfiʿ pointed to the front of the mosque — and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what should the muḥrim wear of garments?"" He said: ""He shall not wear trousers, nor a shirt, nor a turban, nor leather socks — unless one cannot find sandals; then he should cut them and wear them below the ankles. And nothing of garments that has been touched by wars dye or saffron, nor a hooded cloak.""

The wording is that of al-Muqaddamī. In the narration of Sulaymān: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) what the muḥrim should not wear. He said: ""He shall not wear…"" and he mentioned it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, from Ḥammād, in an abridged form.

Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Ayyūb and added ""al-Qabāʾ"" (a long-sleeved garment) in it, and it is a sound, preserved narration from Sufyān al-Thawrī from Ayyūb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous]"
"9065","(9062) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us, Isḥāq — meaning Ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī — narrated from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. Sulaymān said: And ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz and Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, saying: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us. And Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyābī narrated to us — all of them from Sufyān, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar — that a man stood up before the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what should the muḥrim wear of garments?"" He said: ""He shall not wear a shirt, nor a turban, nor a hooded cloak, nor trousers, nor a qabāʾ, nor any garment touched by wars dye or saffron, nor shall he wear leather socks unless he cannot find sandals, in which case he should cut them below the ankles."" And with its meaning, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd al-ʿAdanī narrated it from Sufyān in al-Jāmiʿ, and with its meaning, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated it from Nāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous]"
"9066","(9063) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān — Abū al-Shaykh — informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Jārūd narrated to us, Abū Saʿīd al-Ashajj narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us. And Abū Bakr informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Bahlūl narrated to us, Ḥumayd ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade the wearing of shirts, hooded cloaks, trousers, and leather socks — unless one cannot find sandals — and he forbade wearing any garment dyed with saffron or wars dye, meaning the muḥrim. In the narration of al-Ashajj: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade the muḥrim to wear shirts and hooded cloaks, and then he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/232]"
"9067","(9064) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, among the last narrations, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Hārūn ibn Mūsā al-Zāhid informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, he said: I read to Mālik, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the one in iḥrām from wearing a garment dyed with saffron or wars, and he said: ""Whoever does not find two sandals, let him wear two khuffs and cut them below the ankles.""

In the narration of al-Shāfiʿī: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade. And the remainder is the same. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9068","(9065) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī informed us: Ādam informed us: Shuʿbah informed us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed us, from Jābir ibn Zayd, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us at ʿArafāt and said: ""Whoever does not find an izār, let him wear trousers, and whoever does not find two sandals, let him wear two khuffs.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghandar from Shuʿbah. Al-Bukhārī said: Ibn ʿUyaynah followed him, from ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1746. Muslim 1178]"
"9069","(9066) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, among the last narrations, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, that he heard ʿAmr ibn Dīnār saying: I heard Abū al-Shaʿthāʾ saying: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering a sermon, and he was saying: ""When the one in iḥrām does not find two sandals, let him wear two khuffs, and when he does not find an izār, let him wear trousers.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1746. Muslim 1178]"
"9070","(9067) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Bashshār informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us. He mentioned it with its chain and its meaning. He added: ʿAmr said: Ibn ʿAbbās did not mention cutting. And Ibn ʿUmar said: ""And let him cut them so that they are below the ankles."" I do not know which of the two ḥadīths abrogated the other. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous.]"
"9071","(9068) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Yazīd informed us: Sufyān informed us, from ʿAmr, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The one in iḥrām, when he does not find two sandals, let him wear two khuffs and cut them so that they are below the ankles.""

He said, and ʿAmr said: Look at which of them came first, the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar or the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās?

And others narrated it from Ibn ʿUyaynah, from ʿAmr, and said: Look at which of them came first. So ʿAmr ibn Dīnār reconciled them by one abrogating the other. And it is clarified in the narration of Ibn ʿAwn and others, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that that was in Madīnah before iḥrām. And it is clarified in the narration of Shuʿbah, from ʿAmr, from Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ — Jābir ibn Zayd — from Ibn ʿAbbās, that that was at ʿArafah, and that was after the account of Ibn ʿUmar.

As for al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, he said: I see that they should be cut, because that is in the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar, even if it is not in the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās, and both of them are truthful and precise. The addition of one over the other is not something the other failed to convey — either it escaped him, or he was uncertain in it and so did not convey it, or he was silent about it, or he conveyed it but it was not transmitted from him — for any of these reasons, as a difference. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous.]"
"9072","(9069) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī, and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Shabīb al-Fāmī informed us, they said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us: Zuhayr informed us: Abū al-Zubayr informed us, from Jābir, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever does not find two sandals, let him wear two khuffs, and whoever does not find an izār, let him wear trousers.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1179]"
"9073","(9070) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Saʿīd — that is Ibn Sālim — informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Hishām ibn Ḥujayr, from Ṭāwūs, who said: I saw Ibn ʿUmar performing saʿī at the House, having tied a garment around his belly. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9074","(9071) He said, and Saʿīd informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, that Nāfiʿ informed him: that Ibn ʿUmar had not tied the garment upon himself; rather, he had tucked its end into his izār. [Ḥasan. Shāfiʿī 546]"
"9075","(9072) And with this chain, al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Saʿīd informed us, from Muslim ibn Jundub, who said: A man came asking Ibn ʿUmar while I was with him, and he said: ""May I bring the two ends of my garment from behind me and then tie them while I am in iḥrām?"" ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""Do not tie.""

And with this chain, al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Saʿīd ibn Sālim informed us, from Ibn Jurayj: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man girded with a variegated rope, and he said: ""Remove the rope."" Twice. This is interrupted (munqaṭiʿ). It was also narrated by Ibn Abī Dhiʾb from Ṣāliḥ ibn Abi Ḥassān from the Prophet ﷺ, and it is also interrupted, except that one of them corroborates the other, then by what has preceded from the account of Ibn ʿUmar, then because when he tied it, it fell under the meaning of stitching (makhīṭ). [Very weak. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī 548]"
"9076","(9073) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ informed us: Ibn Abī Zāʾidah informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: We used to wear garments when we entered iḥrām as long as they had not become dusty, and we used to wear the soft leather (mumashshaq), which is a lining.

And it has been narrated to us from ʿIkrimah, the freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās: that the Prophet ﷺ changed his two garments at Tanʿīm while he was in iḥrām. Abū Dāwūd cited it in the Marāsīl. [Ḥasan. Ibn Khuzaymah 2689]"
"9077","(9074) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us, from Ibn ʿAjlān — Nāfiʿ informed me, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he was affected by cold while he was in a state of iḥrām, so I threw a burnus (hooded cloak) over him. He said: ""What is this?"" I said: ""A burnus."" He said: ""Remove it from me! Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the muḥrim from wearing a burnus?"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad Aḥmad 2/57]"
"9078","(9075) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us — Yaʿqūb informed us — his father informed us, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Nāfiʿ, the mawlā of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, informed me from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid women, in their state of iḥrām, from wearing gloves (quffāzayn), the veil (niqāb), and garments dyed with wars (a yellow dye) or saffron. After that, they may wear whatever they wish of coloured garments — dyed yellow (muʿaṣfar), silk (khazʾ), jewellery (ḥulī), trousers (sarāwīl), shirts (qamīṣ), or shoes (khuff). Abū Dāwūd said: ʿAbda and Muḥammad ibn Salama narrated this from Ibn Isḥāq, from Nāfiʿ, only up to his words ""and garments dyed with wars or saffron"" — they did not mention what follows. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1827. Musnad Aḥmad 2/22]"
"9079","(9076) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Qutayba informed us — Ibn Abī ʿAdī informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: I mentioned this to Ibn Shihāb, and he said: Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me that ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn ʿUmar — used to do that, i.e., he would cut the shoes for a woman in iḥrām. Then Ṣafiyya bint Abī ʿUbayd informed him that ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, informed her that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had granted women permission regarding shoes, so he abandoned that. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 1831. Aḥmad 6/35]"
"9080","(9077) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us — al-Rabīʿ informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Ibn ʿUyayna informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, that he used to issue fatwā to women, when they entered iḥrām, that they should cut their shoes — until Ṣafiyya informed him from ʿĀʾisha, that she issued fatwā to women that they should not cut them, so he ceased doing so. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Shāfiʿī 787]"
"9081","(9078) Abū Bakr informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us — al-Rabīʿ informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Saʿīd informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Ṣafiyya bint Shayba, that she said: I was with ʿĀʾisha when a woman from the women of Banī ʿAbd al-Dār, called Tamlaq, came to her and said: ""O Mother of the Believers, my daughter Fulāna has sworn that she will not wear her jewellery during the season (al-mawsim)."" ʿľisha said: ""Say to her: the Mother of the Believers adjures you by Allah that you must wear all your jewellery."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Musnad al-Shāfiʿī 805]"
"9082","(9079) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us — Abū al-Naḍr informed us — Muḥammad ibn Rāshid informed us, from ʿAbda ibn Abī Lubāba, from Ibn Bābāh al-Makkī, that a woman asked ʿľisha: ""What does a woman wear in her iḥrām?"" She said: ""She wears from her silk (khazʾ), her fine garments (bazz), her dyes (aṣbāgh), and her jewellery (ḥulī)."" [Ḥasan]"
"9083","(9080) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī in al-Kūfa informed us — Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghiraza informed us — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā and Abū Nuʿaym and Thābit al-ʿĀbid informed us, from Sufyān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the muḥrim from wearing a garment dyed with wars or saffron. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of Mālik from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, and Sālim and Nāfiʿ narrated it from Ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9084","(9081) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us — ʿĀrim informed us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd informed us, from Ayyūb, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: While a man was standing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at ʿArafa, he fell from his mount and it broke his neck — or he said ""killed him"" — and he died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in two garments. Do not apply perfume to him, and do not cover his head, for verily Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection, crying the talbiya."" Ḥammād said: I heard ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrating it from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, and I did not find anything objectionable in Ayyūb's ḥadīth. He said: ""Verily Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection, crying the talbiya."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Nuʿmān ʿĀrim, except that the ḥadīth of ʿAmr was not mentioned. He also narrated it from Musaddad from Ḥammād, as previously cited, in the Book of Funerals. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1206. Muslim 1206]"
"9085","(9082) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq informed us — Musaddad informed us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd informed us, from Ayyūb and ʿAmr, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that a man was standing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at ʿArafa and fell from his mount. Ayyūb said: ""It broke his neck,"" and ʿAmr said: ""It killed him,"" and he died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in two garments. Do not apply perfume to him, and do not cover his head, for verily Allah will raise him, crying the talbiya."" And ʿAmr said: ""Crying the talbiya (mulabbīyan)."" Ismāʿīl said: Musaddad said it thus, and ʿĀrim and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb differed from him, and they agreed that ʿAmr said: ""Crying the talbiya (yulabbī),"" and that Ayyūb said: ""Crying the talbiya (mulabbīyan)."" Muslim extracted it from Abū al-Rabīʿ from Ḥammād from both of them, as ʿĀrim narrated. And Ibn Jurayj and Sufyān ibn ʿUyayna narrated it from ʿAmr, as Ḥammād narrated: ""Do not cover his head"" — without any mention of the face. And it was narrated from Wakīʿ, from al-Thawrī, from ʿAmr, that he mentioned the face along with it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous ḥadīth]"
"9086","(9083) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Shaybānī informed us — my father informed us — Abū Kurayb informed us — Wakīʿ informed us, from Sufyān, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that a man's mount broke his neck while he was in a state of iḥrām, and he died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Wash him with water and lotus leaves, and shroud him in his two garments. Do not cover his face or his head, for verily he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection, crying the talbiya."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb. And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated it from Wakīʿ without mentioning the face. And likewise Muḥammad ibn Kathīr and ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd al-ʿAdanī narrated it from Sufyān without mentioning the face. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 120]"
"9087","(9084) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that Abū ʿAwānah narrated from Abū Bishr from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās: that a man was thrown and killed by his mount while we were in the state of iḥrām with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Wash him with water and lotus leaves, shroud him in his two garments, do not apply perfume to him, and do not cover his head, for Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection reciting the talbiyah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Nuʿmān, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Kāmil, both from Abū ʿAwānah. They also both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hushaym from Abū Bishr, without mention of the face. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1206. Muslim 1206]"
"9088","(9085) Shuʿbah narrated it from Abū Bishr on one occasion in agreement with Abū ʿAwānah and Hushaym. Shuʿbah said: ""Then he narrated to me afterwards and said: 'His head and face are to remain uncovered.'""

Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr as the group narrated it, without mention of the face.

Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ḥurrah narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās that the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Cover his face and do not cover his head.""

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated it to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, who said: ""And Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ḥurrah added it and mentioned it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9089","(9086) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah that he said: I saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, at al-ʿArj while he was in the state of iḥrām on a hot day, having covered his face with a velvet cloth of purple. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 714]"
"9090","(9087) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us that my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr Kushmard narrated to us that al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Yaḥyā, i.e. Ibn Saʿīd, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, who said: al-Furāfiṣah ibn ʿUmayr informed me that he saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, covering his face while he was in the state of iḥrām. [Ḥasan. Mālik 714]"
"9091","(9088) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim from his father that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, Zayd ibn Thābit, and Marwān al-Ḥakam used to cover their faces while they were in the state of iḥrām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 7/411]"
"9092","(9089) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Abī al-Zubayr from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The one in iḥrām may bathe, wash his garments, cover his nose from dust, and cover his face while sleeping. Ibn ʿUmar differed with them. [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14851]"
"9093","(9090) ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Najayd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: That which is above the chin of the head, the one in iḥrām must not cover it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 706]"
"9094","There is no ḥadīth under this chapter."
"9095","(9091) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us that Mālik narrated from Ḥumayd ibn Qays from Mujāhid from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""Perhaps your lice have troubled you."" I said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Shave your head and fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or sacrifice a sheep.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1719. Mālik 938]"
"9096","(9092) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl narrated to us that Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Walīd informed us that Mālik ibn Anas narrated from ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Jazarī from Mujāhid from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah: that he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the state of iḥrām and lice troubled his head. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to shave his head and said: ""Fast three days, or feed six poor persons—one mudd of barley for each poor person—or sacrifice a sheep. Whichever of these you do, it will suffice you.""

Al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Walīd al-Naysābūrī rendered it well from Mālik.

Ibn Wahb likewise narrated it from Mālik, and a group narrated it from Mālik without mention of Mujāhid in its isnād. The mention of barley is in the narration of al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Walīd and not in others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 938]"
"9097","(9093) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us in Kitāb al-Manāqib: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān—meaning Ibn ʿUyaynah—informed us, from ʿAbd al-Karīm, from Mujāhid, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad informed us: Abū Khubayb informed us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar informed us: Sufyān informed us, from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, and Ayyūb, and Ḥumayd, and ʿAbd al-Karīm, from Mujāhid, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah:

That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him at al-Ḥudaybiyyah before entering Makkah, while he was in iḥrām, and he was kindling a fire beneath his cooking pot, and lice were falling upon his face. He ﷺ said: ""Are these vermin of yours troubling you?"" He said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Then shave your head and give a farq—that being three ṣāʿs—as food to six poor persons, or fast three days, or offer a sacrifice (nusuk)."" And Ibn Abī Najīḥ said: ""Or slaughter a sheep."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿUmar.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, and al-Bukhārī recorded it through other chains from Ibn Abī Najīḥ and Ayyūb.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1201]"
"9098","(9094) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us. And Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, and Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Turk, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr Kushmard said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Khālid ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us, from Khālid, from Abī Qilābah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah:

That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him during the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah and said: ""Are the vermin of your head troubling you?"" He said: ""Yes."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Shave your head, then offer a sacrifice (nusuk), or fast three days, or give three ṣāʿs of dates as food to six poor persons.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And al-Shaʿbī narrated its meaning from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1201]"
"9099","(9095) And al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtaybah narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, who said: Vermin afflicted my head while I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, to the point that I feared for my eyesight. Then Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, revealed regarding me: {And whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of the head} [al-Baqarah: 196]—the verse. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called me and said to me: ""Shave your head and fast three days, or give a farq of raisins to six poor persons, or offer a sacrifice (shāh)."" So I shaved my head, then I offered a sacrifice.

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed him: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr informed us: Yaʿqūb informed us: My father informed us, from Ibn Isḥāq: Abān—meaning Ibn Ṣāliḥ—informed me, from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtaybah, and he mentioned it."
"9100","(9096) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed him: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī in al-Baṣrah informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī informed us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aṣbahānī, who said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿqil saying: I sat with Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah in this mosque—meaning the mosque of al-Kūfah—and I asked him regarding the statement of Allah, the Most High: {So the expiation is fasting, or charity, or a sacrifice} [al-Baqarah: 196]. He said: I was carried to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while lice were falling upon my face, and he ﷺ said: ""I did not realize that your distress had reached this extent. Do you have a sheep?"" I said: ""No."" He ﷺ said: ""Then fast three days, or give food to six poor persons—each poor person receiving half a ṣāʿ of food—and shave your head."" Kaʿb said: ""This verse was revealed specifically concerning me, and it is generally applicable to all of you.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim recorded it through another chain from Shuʿbah. And Ashʿath narrated from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿqil, from Kaʿb, regarding this ḥadīth, saying: ""Give three ṣāʿs of dates to six poor persons.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 4245. Muslim 1201]"
"9101","(9097) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī said: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Hammām ibn Yaḥyā informed us: ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rābāḥ informed us: Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah informed us, from his father:

That a man came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was at al-Jiʿrānah, wearing a shirt upon which there was the trace of perfume (khalūq)—Hammām said, or he said: the trace of saffron (ṣufrah)—and he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how do you command me to perform my ʿumrah?"" He said: Then Allah revealed unto the Prophet ﷺ. He said: He was covered with a garment. He said: And Yaʿlā used to say: ""I wish I could have seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while revelation was descending upon him."" He said: Then ʿUmar said: ""Would it please you to look upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while revelation is being sent down upon him?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: He lifted the edge of the garment, and I looked at him, and he had a snoring sound—Hammām said, I think he said: like the snoring of a young camel. Then when it was lifted from him, he ﷺ said: ""Where is the one who asked about the ʿumrah? Remove this shirt from yourself, and wash from yourself the trace of perfume""—or he said: the trace of saffron—""and perform in your ʿumrah as you perform in your ḥajj.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 197. Muslim 1180]"
"9102","(9098) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Muḥammad ibn Naṣr informed us: Shaybān ibn Farrūkh informed us: Hammām informed us, and he mentioned it with its chain and its meaning. He said: Then when it was lifted from him, he ﷺ said: ""Where is the one who asked about the ʿumrah? Wash from yourself the saffron""—or he said: the trace of perfume—""and remove your shirt, and perform in your ʿumrah what you would perform in your ḥajj.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Abī Nuʿaym and Abī al-Walīd, from Hammām, and they both recorded it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous reference]"
"9103","(9099) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar informed us: Sufyān informed us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā, from his father, who said:

A man came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was at al-Jiʿrānah, and I was with the Prophet ﷺ, wearing a shirt—meaning a jubbah—daubed with perfume (khalūq). He said: ""I have entered iḥrām for ʿumrah, and I am wearing this, and I am daubed with perfume."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""What would you do during your ḥajj?"" He said: ""I would remove these garments and wash this perfume from myself."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""What you would do during your ḥajj, do the same in your ʿumrah.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿUmar. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, and he also recorded it from the ḥadīth of Qays ibn Saʿd and Rābāḥ ibn Abī Maʿrūf, from ʿAṭāʾ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1180]"
"9104","(9100) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us: Muḥammad—meaning Ibn ʿUbayd—informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān informed us, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah al-Qurashī, who said:

I asked ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, to show me the Prophet ﷺ when the Qurʾān was being revealed to him. He said: While we were with him on a journey, a man came to him wearing a shirt upon which was a trace of saffron. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have entered iḥrām for ʿumrah, and the people are mocking me."" The Prophet ﷺ fell silent regarding him, and revelation was sent down upon him. He mentioned the ḥadīth. Then he ﷺ said: ""Where is the one who asked about the ʿumrah?"" The man stood up, and he ﷺ said: ""Remove this shirt from yourself, and whatever you would do in your ḥajj when you enter iḥrām, do the same in your ʿumrah.""

ʿAbd al-Malik shortened his chain and did not mention Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā in it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See the previous reference]"
"9105","(9101) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from Qatādah, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Yaʿlā ibn Munayyah, may Allah be pleased with him:

That the Prophet ﷺ saw a man wearing a shirt upon which was the trace of perfume or saffron, and he ﷺ said: ""Remove it from yourself, and perform in your ʿumrah what you perform in your ḥajj."" Qatādah said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ: We used to hear that he said: ""Tear it."" He said: ""This is corruption, and Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, does not love corruption.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭayālisī 1323]"
"9106","(9102) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Abū ʿAwānah informed us, from Abī Bishr, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah; and Hushaym, from al-Ḥajjāj, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā, from his father, with this story. He said: The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Remove your jubbah."" So he removed it from his head, and he continued with the ḥadīth.

[Abū Dāwūd 1820]"
"9107","(9103) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Mawhab al-Hamdānī narrated to us, from al-Layth, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, from Ibn Yaʿlā, from his father, with this report. He said in it: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to remove it by plucking and to perform ghusl two or three times, and he narrated the hadith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd 1821]"
"9108","(9104) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī and al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Qūhyār said, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he did not see any harm in the one in iḥrām smelling aromatic plants. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/232. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14600]"
"9109","(9105) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abū al-Zubayr: that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allah be pleased with him, being asked about aromatic plants—whether the one in iḥrām may smell them—and about perfume and oil. He said: No. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9110","(9106) And Abū ʿAbdullāh and Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, Yaḥyā informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Saʿīd informed us, from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he used to dislike the one in iḥrām smelling aromatic plants. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9111","(9107) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr—recited over them—and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī by dictation, said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, Abū Salamah al-Khuzāʿī informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Farqad, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that the Prophet ﷺ applied oil that was not perfumed while he was in iḥrām, meaning not scented. Ibn Yūsuf did not mention its explanation.

Imām Aḥmad said: and al-Aswad ibn ʿĀmir Shādhān narrated it from Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Farqad, from Saʿīd, from Ibn ʿUmar, and he mentioned it without explanation. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ḥilyah of Abū Nuʿaym 3/49]"
"9112","(9108) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Sufyān informed us, Ashʿath ibn Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ informed me, from Murrah al-Shaybānī, who said: We passed by Abū Dharr while we were in iḥrām and our feet had cracked, so he said: Apply oil to them. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9113","(9109) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us from his original, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghazwān Abū Nūḥ narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us.

And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānʾiʾ narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us, from Mujāhid, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, Allah, the Most High, boasts of the people of ʿArafāt to the people of the heavens, and says to them: Look at My servants—they have come to Me disheveled and dusty."" In the narration of Abū Nūḥ, he said: ""Look at these servants of Mine."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this. Aḥmad 2/305. Ibn Ḥibbān 3852]"
"9114","(9110) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Tamtām narrated to us, Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, from Ibrāhīm al-Khuzī.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firabrī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Khashram narrated to us, ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us, from Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Khuzī, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar informed me, who said: We sat with Ibn ʿUmar and we discussed the ḥajj. Then Ibn ʿUmar said: A man stood before the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""What is the Ḥājj?"" He said: ""The disheveled, unkempt one."" Another stood and said: ""What is the Sabīl?"" He said: ""Provisions and a mount."" Another stood and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which ḥajj is best?"" He said: ""Al-ʿajj and al-thajj"" (much raising of voice in talbiyah and shedding of blood in sacrifice).

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Mālinī. In the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān, he said: from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar al-Makhzūmī, from Ibn ʿUmar, and he said: ""al-ashʿath al-ghabir al-tafil"" (disheveled, dusty, unkempt). And the rest has the same meaning. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9115","(9111) Abū Bakr al-Mashshāṭ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Tawbah al-Ṣūfī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Najjār al-Amulī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb ibn ʿArabī narrated to us, Muʿtamir narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There is no harm in al-khabīṣ (a sweet made from dates and ghee) and al-khushknānaj al-muṣaffar (dried bread dyed yellow) being eaten by the one in iḥrām.

Al-Layth ibn Abī Sulaym is not strong (in ḥadīth)."
"9116","(9112) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with both of them: that she used to wear deeply dyed garments of saffron colour while she was in iḥrām, in which there was no saffron (zaʿfarān). Thus Mālik narrated it. Abū Usāmah, Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl, and Ibn Numayr contradicted him, narrating it from Hishām, from Fāṭimah, from Asmāʾ. Thus said Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 711]"
"9117","(9113) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; Abū Ubayd ibn Yūnus ibn Ubayd narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir al-Khazzāz narrated from Ibn Abī Mulaykah: that ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to wear garments dyed with light saffron while she was in the state of iḥrām. [Ṣaḥīgh for others. Ibn Abī Shaybah 24743]"
"9118","(9114) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Saʿīd ibn Sālim narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Abī al-Zubayr from Jābir that he heard him say: ""A woman should not wear perfumed garments, but she may wear garments dyyed with saffron, for I do not consider saffron to be perfume."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Shāfiʿī 542]"
"9119","(9115) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr from Abī Jaʿfar who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, saw upon ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar two garments dyed with saffron while he was in the state of iḥrām, so he said: ""What are these garments?"" ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I do not think anyone should teach us the Sunnah."" So ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, fell silent.

And it has been narrated from Nāfiʿ that the women of Ibn ʿUmar used to wear garments dyed with saffron while they were in the state of iḥrām. [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī 541]"
"9120","(9116) Abū Dāwūd narrated in al-Marāsīl from Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbaḥ from al-Walīd from ʿAlī ibn Ḥawshab who said: I heard Makhūl say: ""A woman came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, with a garment heavily dyed with saffron and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I intend to perform Hajj — should I enter iḥrām in this?' He said: 'Do you have other than it?' She said: 'No.' He said: 'Then enter iḥrām in it.'""

Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Dāwudī narrated to us; Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated it. [Weak. Abū Dāwūd in al-Marāsīl 147]"
"9121","(9117) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ that he heard Aslam, the freed slave of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, saw upon Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh a dyed garment while he was in the state of iḥrām, so ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: ""What is this dyed garment, O Ṭalḥah?"" Ṭalḥah said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, it is only clay (madar)."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Indeed, O group of people, you are leaders whom the people follow. If an ignorant man were to see this garment, he would say: 'Indeed, Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydullāh used to wear dyed garments during iḥrām.' So do not wear, O group of people, anything of these dyed garments."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 710]"
"9122","(9118) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Bakr narrated to us; Hishām narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbdullāh informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to me; my father narrated from Yaḥyā who said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me that Ibn Maʿdān informed him that Jubayr ibn Nufayr informed him that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ informed him, saying: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw upon me two garments dyed with saffron and said: 'Indeed, these are garments of the disbelievers, so do not wear them.'"" The wording is that of Abū ʿAbdullāh. And in the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān: ""That Khālid ibn Maʿdān narrated to him, and he said: 'Two yellow garments.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. (T) And ʿAlī ibn al-Mubārak and Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, similar to the narration of Muʿādh ibn Hishām from his father from Yaḥyā. And Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yāsār narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm and stated that that occurred while he was in the state of iḥrām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2077]"
"9123","(9119) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Tamtām narrated to us; ʿAyysh al-Raqqām narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith that Khālid ibn Maʿdān al-Kalāʿī narrated to him from Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Ḥaḍramī who said: ""I was sitting with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ in Bayt al-Maqdis or in the mosque when a man appeared whose garments were dyed with saffron. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr said: 'I entered iḥrām in a garment like this, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saw it upon me and forbade me from wearing it. Then I returned to my house and did something with it — I wish I had done something else.' I said: 'What did you do?' He said: 'I lit an oven and threw it into it.'""

And ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated it from his father from his grandfather and stated that there is no harm in that for women. [Ḥasan for others]"
"9124","(9120) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Hishām ibn al-Ghāz narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather who said: ""We descended with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, from Thaniyyat Adhākhir."" He mentioned the hadith regarding his prayer. He said: ""Then he turned toward me, and upon me was a wrap (raṭah) dyed with saffron, and he said: 'What is this wrap upon you?' I recognized what he disliked, so I went to my family while they were heating an oven, and I threw it into it. Then I came to him the next day and he said: 'O ʿAbdullāh, what happened to the wrap?' I informed him, and he said: 'Why did you not give it to some of your family members, for there is no harm in that for women.'"" The wording is that of Musaddad.

And ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to indicate that the prohibition was specific to him and not others. [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd 4066. Ibn Mājah 3603. Aḥmad 2/196]"
"9125","(9121) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥunayn from his father from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade me — and I do not say he forbade you — from wearing a gold ring, from wearing silk garments (al-qissī), from wearing garments dyed with deep saffron (al-mufaddam) and from reciting while in rukūʿ.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn ʿAbdullāh from Ibn Abī Fudayk. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2078]"
"9126","(9122) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Qādim narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mawhab, a Madinan, narrated from his uncle from Abū Hurayrah who said: ""ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, went out for Hajj, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar consummated his marriage with his wife and spent the night with her. Then he went out toward Mecca and came to the people while they were at Malal before they departed. ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him, and upon him were traces of perfume and a wrap dyed with deep saffron. He rebuked him and expressed disapproval, saying: 'You wear dyed garments when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, has forbidden them?' ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: 'The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, did not forbid you or him; rather, he only forbade me.' So ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, fell silent.""

This chain of narration is not strong. The ruling of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding specification in the authentic narration is not explicitly stated, and the hadith of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ regarding the prohibition of men from that is general. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
"9127","(9123) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrāk — informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar — informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb — informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Mahzam — informed me, Karīmah bint Hammām al-Ṭāʾiyyah, who said: We were in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām and ʿĀʾishah was there, so we sat near her. A woman said to her: ""O Mother of the Believers, what do you say about ḥinnāʾ (henna) and dye?"" She said: ""My beloved (the Prophet ﷺ) did not like its smell.""

It was also narrated by Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Karīmah with the same meaning regarding dyeing with ḥinnāʾ.

In this is an indication that ḥinnāʾ is not a perfume, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved perfume but did not like the smell of ḥinnāʾ. [Ḥasan. Ṭayālisi 1567. Abū Dāwūd 4164]"
"9128","(9124) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us, al-Shāfiʿī — informed us, Muslim ibn Khālid — from Ibn Jurayj — from ʿAṭāʾ, that he said: ""For (removing) a single hair, a mudd (of expiation) is due; for two hairs, two mudd; and for three or more, blood (i.e., a sacrificial animal) is required.""

And it was narrated to us from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and ʿAṭāʾ that they both said: ""For three hairs, blood (a sacrificial animal) is required, whether done forgetfully or deliberately — they are the same in this regard."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9129","(9125) Abū ʿAbdillāh — al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Fanjawayh al-Dīnawarī at Dāmaghān — informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥamdān ibn ʿAbdillāh — informed us, Yūsuf ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Māhān — informed us, Abū Ḥudhayfah — informed us, Sufyān — from Ayyūb — from ʿIkrimah — from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The muḥrim (one in the state of iḥrām) may enter the bathhouse, extract a tooth, and smell fragrant herbs. If his nail breaks, he should discard it. And he would say: 'Remove harm from yourselves, for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, does not do anything with your harm (i.e., He does not benefit from your suffering).'"" [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Dāraquṭnī 2/232]"
"9130","(9126) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhawayh al-ʿAdl — informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsā — informed us, al-Ḥumaydī — informed us, Sufyān — from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā — informed me, Nabīh ibn Wahb, who said:

ʿUmar ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Maʿmar complained of pain in his eyes due to an ailment while he was in the state of iḥrām, so he sent a message to Abān ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān asking him what he should use as treatment. Abān ibn ʿUthmān said to him: ""Apply a compress of aloeswood (ṣabr) to them, for I heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān narrating that from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: 'He should apply a compress of aloeswood (ṣabr) to them.'""

Muslim narrated it from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1204. Abū Dāwūd 1838]"
"9131","(9127) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār — informed us, Ziyād ibn al-Khalīl al-Tustarī — informed us, Musaddad — informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd — from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā — from Nabīh ibn Wahb, that ʿUmar ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Maʿmar complained of an eye ailment while he was in the state of iḥrām and wished to apply kohl to it. Abān ibn ʿUthmān instructed him to apply a compress of aloeswood (ṣabr) instead, and he asserted that ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to do that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1204]"
"9132","(9128) ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated it, saying: My father informed me, Ayyūb ibn Mūsā informed me, Nabīh ibn Wahb informed me, that ʿUmar ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Maʿmar's eyes became inflamed, and he wished to apply kohl to them, but Abān ibn ʿUthmān forbade him and instructed him to apply a compress of aloeswood (ṣabr). He narrated from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to do that.

Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported it to us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd al-Wārith, and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [See the one before it]"
"9133","(9129) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad — informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār — informed us, ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham — informed us, Abū al-Yamān — informed us, Shuʿayb — Nāʿif said: Ibn ʿUmar used to say: ""The muḥrim should not apply kohl containing any perfume, nor should he treat himself with it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9134","(9130) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us, al-Shāfiʿī — informed us, Saʿīd ibn Sālim — from Ibn Jurayj — from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā — from Nāʿif — from Ibn ʿUmar, that when his eyes became inflamed while he was in the state of iḥrām, he would instill a few drops of aloeswood (ṣabr) into his eyes. And he said: ""The muḥrim may apply any kohl he wishes when his eyes are inflamed, so long as it does not contain perfume."" Ibn ʿUmar was the one who said this. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Shāfiʿī 550. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14853]"
"9135","(9131) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar — informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad — he said: I found in my book from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh — my father informed me — Shuʿbah informed us — from Shumaysah, who said: My eye became inflamed while I was in the state of iḥrām, so I asked ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers, about kohl. She said: ""Apply whatever kohl you wish, except ithmid (antimony),"" or she said, ""except any black kohl."" She added: ""It is not ḥarām, but it is an adornment, and we dislike it."" She also said: ""If you wish, I will apply aloeswood (ṣabr) kohl for you,"" but I refused."
"9136","(9132) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh — informed me, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd — informed us, al-Qaʿnabī — from what he read to Mālik.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us among a group who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us, al-Shāfiʿī — informed us, Mālik — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥunayyin — from his father, that Ibn ʿAbbās and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah disagreed at al-Abwāʾ. Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""The muḥrim may wash his head,"" and al-Miswar said: ""The muḥrim may not wash his head."" So Ibn ʿAbbās sent me to Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī. I found him bathing between the two pillars (al-Qarnayn), screened by a garment. I greeted him, and he said: ""Who is this?"" I said: ""I am ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥunayyin. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās has sent me to you to ask how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wash his head while in the state of iḥrām."" Abū Ayyūb placed his hand on the garment and lowered it until his head became visible to me. Then he said to the person pouring water: ""Pour."" So he poured water on his head. Then he moved his head with both hands, bringing them forward and backward, and said: ""This is how I saw him ﷺ do it.""

The wording is that of al-Qaʿnabī. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah, both from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1743. Muslim 1205]"
"9137","(9133) Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qāḍī informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Saʿīd ibn Sālim narrated on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, ʿAṭāʾ informed me: that Ṣafwān ibn Yaʿlā informed him from his father Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah, who said: While ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) was performing ghusl behind a camel, and I was screening him with a garment, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Yaʿlā, pour water over my head!"" I said: ""Amīr al-Muʾminīn knows best."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) then said: ""By Allah, water only increases the hair in dishement."" So he mentioned the name of Allah and then poured water over his head. [Ḥasan. Al-Shāfiʿī 535]"
"9138","(9134) And Ahmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated on the authority of ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Jazarī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) would sometimes say to me: ""Come, let us compete in plunging into the water to see which of us can hold his breath the longest,"" while we were both in the state of iḥrām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Shāfiʿī 536]"
"9139","(9135) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Khamīruwayh informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr informed us, my father informed me, ʿUbayd Allāh narrated on the authority of Sālim, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar: that ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd plunged into the sea, competing with each other in submerging—each one dunking the other's head under the water—while ʿUmar was watching them, and he did not disapprove of that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9140","(9136) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibn Abī Yaḥyā narrated on the authority of Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he entered a public bath at al-Juḥfah while he was in the state of iḥrām, and he said: ""Allah does not care about our filth at all."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Shāfiʿī 1678]"
"9141","(9137) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh al-Aṣbahānī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Makhlad informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us, Abū Muʿāwiyah al-Ḍarīr narrated on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The one in iḥrām may smell fragrant herbs, enter a public bath, extract a tooth, treat a wound, and if his nail breaks, he may remove the irritation from it. [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Dāraquṭnī 2/232. Ibn Abī Shaybah 4600]"
"9142","(9138) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibn Abī Yaḥyā informed us: that al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām ordered that some dirt on his back be scratched, and it was scratched while he was in the state of iḥrām. [Very weak. Al-Shāfiʿī in al-Umm 3/310]"
"9143","(9139) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us, Ibn Jurayj narrated on the authority of Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said regarding the one in iḥrām scratching his head: ""With the inner side of his fingertips."" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14951]"
"9144","(9140) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥulwānī informed us, Khalaf informed us, Abū Shihāb narrated on the authority of Sulaymān al-Taymī, from Abī Mijlaz, who said: I saw Ibn ʿUmar scratching his head while he was in the state of iḥrām. I paid close attention to him, and he was scratching it with the tips of his fingertips. [Ḥasan]"
"9145","(9141) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik narrated on the authority of ʿAlqamah ibn Abī ʿAlqamah, from his mother, that she heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, being asked: ""May the one in iḥrām scratch his body?"" She said: ""Yes, let him scratch vigorously."" And ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""If my hands were tied and I could find no way to scratch except with my foot, I would scratch with my foot."" [Ḍaʿīf. Mālik 794]"
"9146","There are no hadiths under this chapter heading."
"9147","(9142) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Isḥāq al-Azraq narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd: that a woman asked Ibn ʿUmar, saying: ""May I wash my garments while I am in the state of iḥrām?"" He replied: ""Allah is not concerned with your dirt."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14850]"
"9148","(9143) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us, Abū al-Naḍr informed us, Abū Khaythamah informed us, Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir, who said: ""The muḥrim (one in the state of iḥrām) may take a bath and wash his garments if he wishes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn al-Jaʿd]"
"9149","(9144) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān narrated from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he looked into a mirror while he was in the state of iḥrām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Shāfiʿī 1679]"
"9150","(9145) And it has been narrated to us from Hishām ibn Ḥassān, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he said: ""There is no harm in one looking into a mirror while he is in the state of iḥrām."" Abū Bakr al-Aṣbahānī reported it to us, Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us, Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Hishām, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9151","(9146) And ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās: that he used to disapprove of the muḥrim looking into a mirror except on account of pain. Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr reported it to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī, and he mentioned it. ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī is not strong (in ḥadīth transmission), and the first narration is more authentic. [Munkar]"
"9152","(9147) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʔubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us, Musaddad informed us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ṭāwūs and ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while he was in the state of iḥrām. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 1836]"
"9153","(9148) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Asfaṭī informed us, Ibn Abī Uways informed us, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from ʿAlqamah ibn Abī ʿAlqamah, from al-Aʿraj, from Ibn Buḥaynah, who said: ""The Prophet ﷺ was cupped at Laḥy Jamal, on the middle of his head, while he was in the state of iḥrām."" [al-Bukhārī 1739. Muslim 1203]"
"9154","(9149) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, Abū Salamah al-Khuzāʿī and Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr al-Rāzī both said: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us with a similar chain of narration: that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while he was in the state of iḥrām. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr. [See the preceding]"
"9155","(9150) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, al-Haytham ibn Khārijah and al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā both said: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated from al-Nuʿmān, from ʿAṭāʾ, Mujāhid, and Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās: that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was cupped while he was in the state of iḥrām due to pain. And: ""Did the Prophet ﷺ use the siwāk while he was in the state of iḥrām?"" He said: ""Yes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭabarānī al-Kabīr 11500. Ibn Khuzaymah 2655]"
"9156","(9151) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us, al-Qaʿnabī narrated from what he read to Mālik. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us among others, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik informed us, from Nāfiʿ, the freed slave of Ibn ʿUmar, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, the brother of Banī ʿAbd al-Dār: that ʿUmar ibn ʿUbaydullāh intended to arrange the marriage of Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUmar to the daughter of Shaybah ibn Jubayr, so he sent a message to Abān ibn ʿUthmān to be present for it, while both of them were in the state of iḥrām. Abān disapproved of that and said: ""I heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'The muḥrim shall not marry, nor shall he be married, nor shall he propose.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1409. Abū Dāwūd 1841]"
"9157","(9152) And Abū Zakariyyā b. Abī Isḥāq al-Naysābūrī and Abū al-Qāsim Ṭalḥah b. ʿAlī b. al-Ṣaqr in Baghdad informed us, they both said: Abū Muḥammad Dalja b. Aḥmad al-Sijzī al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Muḥammad b. al-Ḥasan al-Nasāʾī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh b. Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us: Saʿīd b. Abī ʿArūbah narrated to us, from Maṭar, and Yaʿlā b. Ḥakīm, from Nāfiʿ, from Nuwayh b. Wahb, from Abān b. ʿUthmān, from ʿUthmān b. ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said: ""The one in a state of iḥrām shall neither marry, nor be married, nor propose."" He said, and Nāfiʿ said: Ibn ʿUmar used to say that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9158","(9153) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī b. Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan b. Muḥammad b. Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf b. Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Khaṭṭāb narrated to us: Muḥammad b. Sawāʾ informed us: Ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated it with its meaning, except that he said: ""And Ibn ʿUmar used to say this statement, though he did not attribute it to the Prophet, peace be upon him."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Khaṭṭāb Ziyād b. Yaḥyā, and he also recorded it from the ḥadīth of Ayyūb from Nāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9159","(9154) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid b. Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Yaḥyā b. al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Ayyūb b. Mūsā, from Nuwayh b. Wahb, from Abān b. ʿUthmān, from ʿUthmān, reaching the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The one in a state of iḥrām shall neither marry, nor propose."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9160","(9155) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn b. ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Zuhayr b. Ḥarb narrated to us: Sufyān b. ʿUyaynah narrated it. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr b. Ḥarb and others from Sufyān. And al-Ḥumaydī narrated from Sufyān, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""The one in a state of iḥrām shall neither marry, nor be married."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9161","(9156) And al-Shāfiʿī narrated from Sufyān and said: ""The one in a state of iḥrām shall neither marry, nor be married, nor propose."" And they both said that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said it. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me: ʿAlī b. Muḥammad b. Sakhṭawayh narrated to us: Bishr b. Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9162","(9157) And Saʿīd b. Abī Hilāl also narrated it from Nuwayh b. Wahb, from Abān, from ʿUthmān b. ʿAffān, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, who said: ""The one in a state of iḥrām shall not marry."" Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Bishr b. Aḥmad al-Isfarāʾinī informed me: Dāwūd b. al-Ḥusayn b. ʿUqayl narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik b. Shuʿayb b. al-Layth b. Saʿd narrated to me: my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, who said: Khālid b. Yazīd narrated to me, from Saʿīd b. Abī Hilāl, who narrated it. Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd al-Malik b. Shuʿayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9163","(9158) And Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad b. al-Ḥusayn b. al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us: ʿAbd Allāh b. Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb b. Sufyān narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAmr b. Dīnār narrated to us. He said: I said to Ibn Shihāb, ""Abū al-Shaʿthāʾ informed me, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Prophet, peace be upon him, married while he was in a state of iḥrām."" Ibn Shihāb said: ""Yazīd b. al-Aṣamm informed me that the Prophet, peace be upon him, married Maymūnah while he was ḥalāl, and she was his maternal aunt."" He said: I then said to Ibn Shihāb, ""Do you set a Bedouin who urinates on his heels as a proof against Ibn ʿAbbās, when she is also the maternal aunt of Ibn ʿAbbās?"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Numayr from Sufyān up to his words, ""he married her while he was ḥalāl."" And Yazīd b. al-Aṣamm did not say it on his own authority; rather, he narrated it from Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1410. Abū Dāwūd 1844.]"
"9164","(9159) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh and ʿAbd Allāh b. Aḥmad al-Nasawī informed me, they both said: al-Ḥasan b. Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr b. Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Yaḥyā b. Ādam narrated to us: Jarīr b. Ḥāzim narrated to us: Abū Farāzah narrated to us, from Yazīd b. al-Aṣamm, who said: Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, married her while he was ḥalāl. She said: ""And she was my maternal aunt and the maternal aunt of Ibn ʿAbbās."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr b. Abī Shaybah. And Maymūn b. Mihrān also narrated it from Yazīd b. al-Aṣamm, from Maymūnah, may Allah be pleased with her. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See previous.]"
"9165","(9160) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ṭayyib Muḥammad b. ʿAbd Allāh al-Shuʿayrī informed us: Muḥammad b. ʿIṣām narrated to us: Ḥafṣ b. ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me: Ibrāhīm b. Ṭahmān narrated to us, from al-Ḥajjāj b. al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Walīd b. Zarwān, from Maymūn b. Mihrān, from Yazīd b. al-Aṣamm, from his maternal aunt Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, the wife of the Prophet, peace be upon him, that she narrated to him: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon me, married her as a ḥalāl man and consummated the marriage as a ḥalāl man; he married her at Sarif."" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
"9166","(9161) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad b. al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAlī b. al-Ḥasan al-Darābjardī narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Ḥammād, who is Ibn Zayd, narrated to us, from Maṭar, from Rabīʿah, from Sulaymān b. Yasār, from Abū Rāfiʿ, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, married Maymūnah as a ḥalāl man and consummated the marriage with her as a ḥalāl man, and I was the messenger between the two of them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Aḥmad 6/396. Dārimī 1825. Ibn Ḥibbān 4130.]"
"9167","(9162) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik narrated from Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥuṣayn from Abū Ghaṭfān ibn Ṭarīf al-Murrī, who reported that his father Ṭarīf married a woman while he was in the state of iḥrām, so ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb, may Allah be pleased with him, annulled his marriage. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik 773]"
"9168","(9163) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Sājī informed us; Bundār narrated; Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān narrated from Maymūn al-Murāʾī from al-Ḥasan from ʿAlī, who said: ""Whoever marries while in the state of iḥrām, we shall take his wife away from him."" [Ḍaʿīf. al-Kāmil of Ibn ʿAdī: 6/415]"
"9169","(9164) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Abū Khalīfah narrated; al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Sulaymān, i.e. Ibn Bilāl, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The one in the state of iḥrām shall not contract marriage; if he does marry, his marriage shall be annulled."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9170","(9165) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Aṣbahānī narrated; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān narrated; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Qudāmah ibn Mūsā from Shawdhab, a freedman of Zayd ibn Thābit, that he married while in the state of iḥrām, so Zayd ibn Thābit separated them. And we have narrated concerning this from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9171","(9166) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Abū al-Qāsim al-Ṭabarānī narrated; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated; al-Firyābī narrated; Sufyān narrated from Qudāmah ibn Mūsā, who said: ""I married while I was in the state of iḥrām, so I asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, and he said: 'They shall be separated.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9172","(9167) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated; ʿAffān narrated; Hammām narrated from Qatādah from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, that a man married while in the state of iḥrām, so the people of Madīnah unanimously agreed that they should be separated. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9173","(9168) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Maryam narrated; al-Firyābī narrated; Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr from Abū Ḥāzim from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever performs Hajj of this House and does not engage in sexual intercourse and does not commit sin returns as on the day his mother gave birth to him."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Firyābī, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Thawrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1724. Muslim 1350]"
"9174","(9169) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated; Yaʿlī ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Al-Rfath (sexual intercourse) means sexual relations; al-fusūq means acts of disobedience against Allah, such as hunting or other sins; and al-jidāl means cursing and disputing."" It has also been narrated in this book from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that he said: ""Al-rfath means sexual intercourse; al-fusūq means acts of disobedience; and al-jidāl means argumentation."" [Ḥasan li-ghayrih. Ḥākim 2/303]"
"9175","(9170) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated; Abū ʿĀmir narrated; Sufyān narrated from Khuṣayf from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās: ""Al-rfath means sexual intercourse; al-fusūq means cursing; and al-jidāl means that you argue with your companion until you anger him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih. Abū Yaʿlā 2709. Ibn Abī Shaybah 13225]"
"9176","(9171) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṭāwūs narrated from his father from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding His statement {There shall be no sexual intercourse, no disobedience, and no disputing during Hajj} [al-Baqarah: 197]. He said: ""Al-rfath means approaching women for sexual intercourse; al-fusūq means disobedience to Allah; and al-jidāl means disputing with people."" [Ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrih]"
"9177","(9172) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʬAṭāʾ informed us, saying: Ibn Jurayj said: Abū al-Zubayr informed me, saying: I heard Ṭāwūs say: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr say: ""It is not permissible for the one in iḥrām to engage in ʿirāb."" He said: So I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: ""What is ʿirāb?"" He said: ""It is sexual intercourse (al-taʿarruḍ), meaning intercourse."" [Hasan. Tafsīr al-Ṭabarī 2/274]"
"9178","(9173) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Zakariyyā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ziyād ibn Ḥuṣayn, from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, who said: ""I was walking with Ibn ʿAbbās while he was in iḥrām, and he was reciting rajaz poetry about camels, saying:

'And they walk with us softly (hamīsan)…'

So I said to him: 'Are you engaging in rafath while you are in iḥrām?' He said: 'Rafath is only that by which women are intended.' The other hemistich of the verse, which was dropped, was:

'If the bird speaks truly, we shall do lamīs (i.e., intercourse).'""

Al-Thawrī and others narrated it from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ḥākim 2/303. Ibn Abī Shaybah 14492]"
"9179","(9174) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Hushaym informed us: ʿAwf narrated from Ziyād ibn Ḥuṣayn, from his father, who said: ""Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) dismounted from his mount and began driving it while reciting rajaz, saying:

'And they walk with us softly (hamīsan); if the bird speaks truly, we shall do lamīs (intercourse).'

He mentioned intercourse explicitly, without using allusion. So I said: 'O Abū al-ʿAbbās, you say rafath while you are in iḥrām?' He said: 'Rafath is only that by which women are intended.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
"9180","(9175) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, who said: ""When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) heard the camel-rider (ḥādī) singing, he would say: 'Do not allude to women (in your songs).'"" Wakīʿ and al-Zubayrī also narrated it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9181","(9176) Abū al-Ḥusayn informed us: ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibn Bishr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, i.e., ibn Mahdī, narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, who said: ""Ibn ʿUmar used to forbid the camel-rider (ḥādī) from alluding to women while in iḥrām."" Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān and a group also narrated it. And Allāh knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
"9182","(9177) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Shaybānī in al-Kūfah narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, who said: ""We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performing Hajj, and the caravan of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the caravan of Abū Bakr were one. We stopped at al-ʿArj, and our provisions were with Abū Bakr's servant. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat down, and ʿĀʾishah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā) sat beside him, and Abū Bakr (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) sat beside the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the other side, and I sat beside my father, waiting for his servant and his caravan to come to us. Then the servant appeared walking, but his camel was not with him. Abū Bakr (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) said to him: 'Where is your camel?' He said: 'I lost it last night.' Abū Bakr (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) stood up and began striking him, saying: 'You lost a single camel, and you are a man!' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did no more than smile and say: 'Look at this muḥrim and what he is doing!'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Khuzaymah 2679. Ḥākim 1/623]"
"9183","(9178) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr, from Nāfiʿ ibn Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, from Abū Shurayḥ al-Khuzāʿī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him treat his neighbor well. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others, from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from another route from Abū Shurayḥ, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5673. Muslim 48]"
"9184","(9179) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ṣafwān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Dunyā narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and Abū Khaythamah both said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: Nāfiʿ informed me that Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) passed by a group of people who were in iḥrām, and among them a man was singing. He said: ""Beware! May Allah not hear you! Beware! May Allah not hear you!"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī al-Dunyā in Dhamm al-Malāhī 74/17]"
"9185","(9180) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥāfiẓ in Hamadhān narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān narrated to us: Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī. Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām informed me that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam informed him that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth informed him that Ubayy ibn Kaʿb al-Anṣārī informed him that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, there is wisdom in some poetry.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5793. Abū Dāwūd 5010]"
"9186","(9181) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm, i.e., ibn Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Poetry is speech; its good is like good speech, and its bad is like bad speech."" This is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). [Ḍaʿīf. Shāfiʿī 1687]"
"9187","(9182) Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm — Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn — Usāmah ibn Zayd — Zayd ibn Aslam — from his father: ʿUmar heard a man singing in a desolate tract of land and said: ""Singing is the provision of the traveller."" [Ḥasan for other than this chain. Ibn Abī Shaybah 13956]"
